(window.MUSEUM_MATCH_T = window.MUSEUM_MATCH_T || {})["en"] = {"ui":{"lead":"Great games, one move at a time. Follow famous games on the board while Musowl explains each move.","all":"All","k_chess":"Chess","k_shogi":"Shogi","k_go":"Go","k_xiangqi":"Xiangqi","side_r":"Red","res_r":"Red won","back":"Back to Great Games","start":"Start","prev":"Back one move","next":"Forward one move","end":"End","play":"Play","pause":"Pause","moves":"Moves","move":"Move {n}","startpos":"Start","nextnote":"Next note (move {n})","quiet":"No note for this move.","guide":"Musowl","keys":"← → step through the moves","result":"Result","r_event":"Game","r_date":"Year","r_place":"Place","side_w":"White","side_b":"Black","side_s":"Sente","side_g":"Gote","side_B":"Black","side_W":"White","res_w":"White won","res_b":"Black won","res_d":"Draw","res_s":"Sente won","res_g":"Gote won","credit":"The moves are the public record of each game. The notes are by the museum’s Musowl.","caps":"Captured","hand":"In hand","pnote":"Previous note","nnote":"Next note","plies":"{n} moves","about":"About this game","ev":"Evaluation","even":"Even","evby":"Read by this site’s engine (a rough guide): the first player’s chance of winning","r_dp":"When & where","k_reversi":"Reversi","pass":"Pass","discs":"{n} discs","k_checkers":"Checkers","taken":"Captured"},"opera":{"t":"The Opera Game","w":"Paul Morphy","b":"Duke Karl II of Brunswick and Count Isouard","ev":"Played in a box at the Paris Opera (the two allies consulting)","d":"1858","pl":"France (Paris)","lead":"The young American champion against two noblemen, during a performance at the opera: seventeen moves. Bring the pieces out fast, give them up without a second thought, and finish with very few. No game appears in more chess books.","c":{"0":"Paris, 1858. Twenty-one-year-old American Paul Morphy had been beating Europe's strong players one after another. That evening, in a box at the opera, the Duke of Brunswick and Count Isouard took Black together and played him, talking over each move. It is said Morphy wanted to finish quickly so he could watch the stage. These seventeen moves are full of the basics: bring pieces out fast, keep your king safe. Morphy was born in New Orleans, in the American South. His father was a lawyer who became a judge on the state's Supreme Court, and Morphy also studied law at university. But when he graduated he was still too young to work as a lawyer, so in the meantime he set off on a chess journey to Europe.","1":"White pushes the king's pawn two squares. It eyes d5 and e5 in the centre and opens paths for the bishop and queen. That night's venue was the Italian opera house in Paris (the Salle Ventadour). The opera being performed is said to have been Rossini's The Barber of Seville, or perhaps Bellini's Norma; the sources disagree, and it is still not certain today.","2":"Black also plays e5. The pawns face each other in the centre, and White's pawn cannot go further. On this 1858 trip, Morphy caused a sensation at the Café de la Régence in Paris by playing eight opponents at once while blindfolded, and beating them. In December of the same year he also won a match against Anderssen, thought to be Europe's best, with 7 wins, 2 losses and 2 draws.","3":"The knight comes to f3 and attacks the e5 pawn. A good move: it develops a piece and makes Black think about defending. The start 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 is, almost 200 years later, still one of the most common beginnings of all, used by everyone from beginners to world champions.","4":"Black guards e5 with d6. This is called the Philidor Defence. It is solid, but it makes the path of the bishop on f8 a little narrow. Philidor, who gave it its name, was an 18th‑century Frenchman: a chess master and also a musician who composed eleven operas. He recommended this move in a book he published in 1749. Isn't it a fun coincidence that the Opera Game begins with a defence named after an opera composer? Today it is thought of as solid but a little cramped.","5":"White plays d4, bringing another pawn to the centre to hit e5. Black must decide whether to take or defend. Against the Philidor Defence, hitting the centre at once with 3.d4 like this has always been, and still is, the main line.","6":"Black's bishop goes to g4. It presses the knight on f3, which stands in line with White's queen on d1. If the knight moves, the queen can be taken, so the knight cannot go after e5. However, this 3...Bg4 has long been considered a poor move. Later world champions Steinitz and Fischer both judged it weak. In today's opening theory, the usual choices are 3...exd4, taking the pawn, or 3...Nf6, developing the knight.","7":"White takes on e5 with the pawn. If Black took back at once with the d6 pawn, White would trade queens and then the knight would grab the e5 pawn. So Black first wants to get rid of the knight on f3.","8":"Black takes the knight with the bishop. Now e5 can be won back safely. But giving up the bishop that had just come out for the knight leaves Black behind in development. One of the Black players, the Duke of Brunswick, was the former ruler of the Duchy of Brunswick in Germany. He was driven out of his land during the upheavals of 1830, and afterwards lived mostly in Paris and London. All over Europe, people gossiped about his luxury and his huge collection of jewels.","9":"White takes back with the queen. White's queen is already working near the middle, while Black has hardly brought out any pieces. The Duke loved chess. A newspaper report from the year before even says he made such a noise playing chess at the same Italian opera house, in the middle of a performance, that he was asked to be quiet.","10":"Black takes back with a pawn, and the material is even. But White is ahead in pieces that are out and ready. The other player, Count Isouard, was a French nobleman whose family owned a château at Vauvenargues in the south of France. Even two grown men putting their heads together were no match for the 21-year-old.","11":"Bishop to c4. Together with the queen on f3 it aims at f7. Only the king guards f7, so it is a weak square; taking it would be mate.","12":"Black brings the knight to f6 and blocks the f-file. Without this, queen takes f7 would be mate. Defending while developing is the right answer.","13":"The queen swings to b3 and attacks f7 and b7 at the same time. A move with two threats is strong, because the opponent cannot defend both.","14":"Black plays the queen to e7 to guard f7, letting b7 go. But the queen now blocks the bishop on f8. In fact, Black had no good alternative to Qe7. For example, analysts have shown that defending with Qd7 lets White take on b7 and win a rook as well.","15":"Morphy does not take the pawn on b7; he develops a knight instead. Taking would send the queen far away. Choosing fast development over a pawn is typical of Morphy. It is said that if White had taken on b7, Black could check with the queen on b4 and force a queen trade, and White's attack would have fizzled out.","16":"Black plays c6, covering d5 and b5 and opening a road for the queen to b6.","17":"Bishop to g5 pins the knight on f6. If the knight moves, the queen on e7 behind it can be taken. Black's pieces find it harder and harder to move.","18":"Black plays b5 to chase the bishop from c4. It also gains room on the queen's side, but it opens a gap for White's pieces to jump in. This 9...b5 is said to be the move that lost, but even later analysis has found no move here that clearly saves Black. Black's troubles had really begun much earlier, with 3...Bg4.","19":"The knight is given up to take on b5! Instead of retreating the bishop, Morphy keeps attacking. With Black's king still in the middle, this is very dangerous for Black.","20":"Black takes the knight and is a knight ahead. But Black's king is still on e8, and White's pieces keep gathering.","21":"Bishop to b5, check! Black's king is still stuck in the middle. White has two pawns for the knight and comes straight at the king.","22":"Black blocks with the knight on d7. But this knight is pinned: if it moves, the black king is in check. White will now aim at d7.","23":"White castles queenside. The king is safe, and at the same time the rook arrives on d1 to aim at d7. One move does both defence and attack: a superb move.","24":"Black brings the rook to d8 to guard d7. Black still cannot castle, and the rook on h8 and bishop on f8 are doing nothing.","25":"The rook is given up to take on d7! White hands over one rook to remove one of Black's defenders.","26":"Black takes back with the rook. Now this rook on d7 is pinned too: if it moves, the bishop on b5 checks the black king.","27":"The other rook comes to d1 and attacks d7 again. Now more white pieces attack d7 than black pieces defend it.","28":"Black's queen goes to e6, offering a queen trade to push White's queen away from b3, and hoping to help d7. But it is too late.","29":"The bishop takes on d7 with check. White removes Black's defenders one by one.","30":"Black takes back with the knight. With the knight gone from f6, the diagonal from the bishop on g5 to d8 is open. That diagonal will matter at the very end.","31":"The queen is given up on b8, with check! This is the most famous move of the game. Black must take with the knight, and when the knight leaves d7 the d-file opens.","32":"Black takes with the knight. Now no black piece guards d8.","33":"Rook to d8, checkmate! The bishop on g5 supports d8, and the black king is boxed in by its own pieces. White gave away queen, rook and bishop, and finished with just a rook and a bishop. Develop quickly, keep your king safe, and attack while the enemy king is stuck in the middle: this famous game shows all these important ideas."}},"reti1910":{"t":"Réti's Mate","w":"Richard Réti","b":"Savielly Tartakower","ev":"A casual game (not a tournament)","d":"1910","pl":"Austria (Vienna)","lead":"Two future grandmasters, a casual game in Vienna, eleven moves. White gives up the queen and finishes with a double check: two pieces checking at once. The mating pattern is named after White: Réti's Mate.","c":{"0":"Vienna, 1910. White is twenty-year-old Richard Réti; Black is Savielly Tartakower. Both went on to become top players and are known for bringing new ideas to chess. This was not a tournament game but a casual one, played for a small stake. It is short, but it shows clearly why you should get your king safe early. Réti was born in the town of Pezinok, in today's Slovakia, and his father was an army doctor. He had come to Vienna to study mathematics at the university. Tartakower was born in Rostov-on-Don in Russia; he studied law at the universities of Geneva and Vienna, and people called him 'Doctor'.","1":"White pushes the king's pawn two squares. Taking the middle of the board makes it easier for the pieces behind to come out. The bishop and queen now have open paths. According to a newspaper chess column of the time, the stake was 10 crowns, and the game was played with a clock, at a pace of 15 moves per hour.","2":"Black plays c6. Instead of going to the centre at once, Black gets ready for d5 to hit the pawn on e4. This solid opening is called the Caro-Kann Defence. It is named after two 19th‑century players: Horatio Caro, who was active in Berlin, and Marcus Kann. Caro published an article analysing this move in a chess magazine in 1886.","3":"White puts the other pawn on d4 too. Two pawns stand side by side in the centre, and White has plenty of space. Black will try to break them up. In the spring of this year, 1910, Halley's Comet came close to the Earth, and people said the Earth would pass through its long tail. Some, afraid of poisonous gas in the tail, even rushed to buy 'anti-comet pills'.","4":"As planned, d5. The pawn challenges e4: \"I can take you.\" White must decide whether to guard it, push it or trade it.","5":"White brings out a knight to guard e4. It develops a piece and defends at the same time, so no time is wasted. Later, Réti became a leader of the new 'hypermodern' idea – controlling the centre from a distance with pieces instead of filling it with pawns – and his name lives on in the Réti Opening, which starts 1.Nf3. But in this game he still plays in the old way, lining up pawns on e4 and d4.","6":"Black takes the pawn on e4. By trading centre pawns, Black makes White's centre smaller.","7":"White takes back with the knight, which now stands in the middle of the board. A central knight reaches many squares and is a strong piece. Up to here, this is the main line of the Caro-Kann, still the most played today.","8":"Black brings out a knight to face the one on e4. Bishop to f5 is often played here, but Black invites a knight trade. Tartakower later became famous for his many witty sayings, such as 'The winner of the game is the player who makes the next-to-last mistake.'","9":"The queen comes out early to d3 to guard the knight on e4. Usually you should not bring the queen out too soon, but White has a plan. Remember that this queen stands on the d-file, in front of Black's king. Normally White trades knights here, so 5.Qd3 is an unusual move.","10":"Black plays e5, offering a pawn to open lines for the bishop and queen. It is an active move that starts a fight in the centre. However, opening the centre before castling also made it a risky move for Black's king. In fact, records show that a queen sacrifice very similar to the one coming up had already been played before this game.","11":"White takes the pawn on e5. White accepts the gift, but this also gives Black's queen more room to move.","12":"Check! Black's queen on a5 aims diagonally at the king on e1. At the same time it plans to win back the e5 pawn: two jobs in one move. White must block.","13":"White blocks the check with the bishop on d2. The queen could take the bishop, but White's queen guards it well.","14":"Black wins the pawn back, so material is equal again. And this queen looks straight down the e-file at the knight on e4 and the king behind it. If the knight moves, the king is in check, so the knight is stuck. Black seems about to win it.","15":"White castles queenside. The king leaves the e-file, so the knight is free again. At the same time a rook comes to d1: the rook on d1, the queen on d3 and the square d8 next to Black's king are all on one line. This is White's trap.","16":"Black takes the knight on e4 and seems to win a piece. But Black's king is still on e8 and has not castled. White's pieces are lined up on the d-file, and the black king is unprotected. This move, which loses the game, is the turning point.","17":"Queen to d8, check! White gives away the strongest piece for nothing. The only black piece that can take it is the king. By letting it be taken, White drags the king onto the dangerous square d8.","18":"Black must take with the king; there is no other way to stop the check. The king is pulled out to d8, with the rook on d1 waiting behind.","19":"The bishop moves from d2 to g5 with check, and at the same time it opens the way for the rook on d1, which checks along the d-file too. Two pieces checking at once is a double check. You cannot stop it by taking one piece or by blocking, so the king must run.","20":"The king runs to c7. If it went to e8 instead, the rook would come to d8 and mate. Wherever the king goes, White has a finishing move ready.","21":"Bishop to d8: checkmate on the diagonal against the king on c7! The bishop is guarded by the rook on d1, so the king cannot take it. Squares like b8, c8, b7 and c6 are blocked by Black's own pieces, and the rook watches d6 and d7. In just eleven moves, White gave up the queen and won with two small pieces. The game shows how dangerous it is to grab material before castling. Some records say Black resigned one move earlier."}},"immortal":{"t":"The Immortal Game","w":"Adolf Anderssen","b":"Lionel Kieseritzky","ev":"An informal game during a break in the London 1851 tournament","d":"1851","pl":"United Kingdom (London)","lead":"London, during the first international chess tournament: a game played in a break. White gives up a bishop, both rooks and finally the queen, and mates with the three small pieces left. Black is said to have resigned before the end; the last few moves may be the finish White showed.","c":{"0":"London, June 1851. That year the world’s first international chess tournament was being held in London. White is Adolf Anderssen, a mathematics teacher from Germany; Black is Lionel Kieseritzky, who taught chess at the Café de la Régence in Paris. This was not a tournament game but a relaxed game played during a break. Kieseritzky printed the moves in his own magazine and spread them, and the game later came to be called “the Immortal Game”. Let’s watch a battle typical of its time, full of pieces thrown into the attack. Musowl will be your guide. In May of that year, a huge building of iron and glass, the 'Crystal Palace', stood in London's Hyde Park, and the Great Exhibition, gathering new inventions and goods from all over the world, had opened. Chess players thought, 'Let's have a world event for chess too', and this international tournament was held at Staunton's call.","1":"White pushes the pawn in front of the king two squares. It now watches the central squares d5 and f5, and the paths for the bishop and queen are open. Then as now, it is the most popular first move. Anderssen was born in what is now Wrocław in Poland (then Breslau, in Germany). His father taught him chess when he was nine. In everyday life he taught mathematics at a school, and he was also known as a composer of chess puzzles (problems). In this tournament he beat Kieseritzky in the first round and went on to win the whole event.","2":"Black also plays e5. The pawns meet head-on and Black does not give up the centre. Black’s bishop and queen now have open paths too. Kieseritzky was born in Tartu, in today's Estonia. He was originally a mathematics teacher too, but he moved to Paris and made his living from chess, giving lessons or playing games for five francs an hour. So this was a game between two maths teachers!","3":"f4! White offers a pawn for free: the “King’s Gambit”. If Black takes it, Black’s e5 pawn leaves the centre and White can more easily push a pawn to d4 as well. In this era, plans like this that rush into attack were hugely popular. The weak point is that the space in front of White’s king is opened up. The King's Gambit was one of the most popular openings of the 19th century. Today it is seen as a somewhat risky choice and is rarely played in serious games between top players.","4":"Black accepts the pawn. The pawn on f4 blocks the road of White’s bishop on c1 and also watches the squares e3 and g3. Black is a pawn ahead. The name 'Immortal Game' was given in 1855 by the Austrian player Ernst Falkbeer.","5":"White brings the bishop out to c4. The square f7 next to Black’s king is weak, guarded only by the king. White aims at it so an attack can start at any moment. Playing 3.Bc4 here instead of 3.Nf3 is the form of the King's Gambit called the 'Bishop's Gambit'.","6":"Check! Because White pushed the f-pawn, a diagonal road from h4 to e1 was open. If White blocks with g3, the pawn on f4 simply takes it. White has to move the king.","7":"The king steps aside to f1. Now White can no longer castle and cannot tuck the king away safely. On the other hand, Black’s queen has come out early, so White can now chase it while bringing out pieces. Even though he lost, Kieseritzky sent the moves to his friends in Paris and printed them in La Régence, the magazine he edited. He cared more about a wonderful game than about who won.","8":"Black offers a pawn for free too. With b5 Black lures the c4 bishop away from the diagonal that aims at f7. This move was later named the “Bryan Countergambit”, and it was an attacking move Kieseritzky liked to use. Today, though, most players think this 4...b5 is not a sound move. It is lively, but it does not give enough in return for the pawn.","9":"White takes the pawn. The bishop has left c4, so it no longer aims at f7. In return White has won a pawn back, and the count is even again.","10":"Black’s knight goes to f6. It attacks the e4 pawn and also helps guard the king, a fine square. The race to bring out pieces has begun.","11":"White’s knight comes out to f3 as well. It develops a piece and threatens the queen on h4 at the same time, a very satisfying move. The queen has to run, so White has effectively gained a move.","12":"The queen drops back to h6. From here it watches the h-file and sees the rook on h1 behind White’s h2 pawn. It is still a safe square.","13":"With d3, White firmly guards the e4 pawn that was under attack. At the same time the road for the c1 bishop opens. A calm move that wastes nothing.","14":"Black’s knight goes to h5. It guards the f4 pawn and plans to jump to g3. A knight jump to g3 would give check and hit the h1 rook; if White takes with the h2 pawn, the h-file opens and the queen on h6 can capture the h1 rook. A rather sharp plan.","15":"White’s knight goes to h4. This knight blocks the h-file, so even if Black’s knight jumps to g3 the h1 rook can no longer be taken. It also prepares to jump to f5 and threaten the queen on h6.","16":"Black’s queen goes to g5, attacking the knight on h4. White has to decide whether to defend the knight or move it somewhere.","17":"The knight jumps to f5. Protected by the e4 pawn, it is a very strong post. From here it attacks the g7 pawn and keeps an eye on the squares h6 and d6. White’s pieces are closing in on Black’s king.","18":"With c6 Black tries to chase away the bishop on b5. If the bishop runs, Black can grow the centre with pawns. A normal, sensible move.","19":"But White does not save the bishop and plays g4!, threatening the knight on h5. White is willing to lose a bishop in order to chase Black’s pieces around and gain time for the attack: a bold idea. The g4 pawn is protected diagonally by the queen on d1. Later analysis judges this 10.g4 to be a bit too much. Even so, in the actual game its energy put Black under more and more pressure.","20":"The knight, attacked on h5, goes back to f6. Black decides that taking White’s bishop can wait. The c6 pawn is still attacking the bishop on b5.","21":"The rook goes to g1. It guards the g4 pawn and gets ready to fight on the g-file. The bishop on b5 is still left to be taken. White believes that chasing Black’s queen matters more than one bishop. This 11.Rg1 is still highly praised as a purposeful sacrifice, deliberately letting the bishop be taken.","22":"Black takes the bishop. In material, Black is now a whole bishop ahead, a big lead. But apart from the queen and a knight, Black’s pieces are still on their starting squares. White’s attack starts from here. The strong German player Hübner analysed that taking the bishop here was Black's big mistake, and that pushing the pawn to h5 instead would have been better.","23":"h4! White chases Black’s queen on g5 with a pawn. Just by pushing pawns, White keeps shrinking the places where Black’s queen can stand.","24":"The queen escapes to g6. It still has some squares, but it is starting to be surrounded by White’s pawns and knight.","25":"With h5 White chases the queen once more. Every time a White pawn advances, Black’s queen can only retreat.","26":"The queen goes back to g5. The knight on f5 watches h6 and the other squares are blocked, so this is almost the only place left. Black’s queen is slowly being cornered.","27":"White’s queen goes to f3. Next, if the c1 bishop takes the f4 pawn, the queen on g5 will be attacked and have almost nowhere to run. It is a move that sets a trap to catch the queen.","28":"Black brings the knight back to g8 to free the escape square f6 for the queen. A knight that was developed returning to its starting square is a sign that Black is in trouble. In the race to bring out pieces, White is far ahead.","29":"The bishop captures the f4 pawn, winning a pawn back while chasing the queen on g5. White’s bishop has finally joined the fight as well.","30":"The queen escapes to the square it prepared, f6. From here it looks diagonally all the way to the b2 pawn and can also aim at the a1 rook behind it.","31":"White’s knight goes to c3. The knight on c3 also blocks the diagonal from the f6 queen to b2. Next it plans to jump to d5 and threaten the queen on f6.","32":"Black brings the bishop out to c5 and attacks the rook on g1. At last Black has brought out a bishop and joined the attack. Should Black go grab pieces, or defend the king? It was an important moment for Black.","33":"The knight goes to d5! It threatens the f6 queen, and there is also a jump to c7 that would attack the king and rook at once. But because the knight left c3, the road from Black’s queen to b2 is open again. White does not try to protect either the g1 rook or the b2 pawn. White is ready to let pieces be taken in order to attack Black’s king.","34":"Black’s queen takes the b2 pawn and now aims at the a1 rook. The idea is to grab lots of pieces and win on numbers. But the queen is getting farther and farther away from its own king.","35":"The bishop goes to d6! Now White leaves both rooks to be taken. In return the bishop controls f8 and e7, blocking the escape routes of Black’s king. White chose not the piece count but weaving a net to catch Black’s king. This is the turning point of the game.","36":"Black takes the rook on g1. Black is now ahead by a bishop and a rook. However, later studies say another move was better here, and that Black had a stronger defence.","37":"e5! A quiet pawn move, but it is one of the deciding blows. This pawn blocks the diagonal from the b2 queen to g7 and also controls f6. Black’s queen can no longer come back to help the king, and the g8 knight cannot come out to f6. White now threatens to take on g7 with the knight, giving check, and then mate with the bishop on c7.","38":"Black takes the other rook too, with check. The whole first rank from a1 to f1 was open. Black has taken both rooks and is far ahead in material.","39":"The king steps to e2. White’s only big piece left is the queen, plus two knights and a bishop. Even so, around Black’s king White has far more pieces than Black.","40":"Black brings the knight out to a6 to guard c7. It is meant to stop the mate where the knight takes g7 with check, the king runs to d8, and the bishop finishes on c7. It is said that Kieseritzky resigned around here, and that the rest of the moves were shown by White.","41":"The knight takes the g7 pawn with check! The knight on g7 attacks the king on e8. The bishop on d6 controls e7 and f8, so the king has few places to go.","42":"The king has to run to d8. It can no longer stay on e8 or go to e7 or f8; d8 was the only square left.","43":"White sacrifices the queen on f6 with check! Even the last big piece is given away for free. It checks diagonally from f6 through e7 to d8, and Black has no choice but to take this queen.","44":"Black takes the queen with the g8 knight. The king has nowhere to run, so there is no other move. But this knight moving away completes the mating pattern.","45":"The bishop goes to e7. Checkmate! The knight on d5 controls e7 and c7, the knight on g7 controls e8, and Black’s own pieces block c8 and d7. White gave up a bishop, two rooks and even the queen, and won with just three small pieces. Black still has a queen and rooks to spare, but none of them arrive in time. It teaches us that how hard your pieces are working matters more than how many you have, and it is one of the most famous games in the history of chess. The name is said to have been given a few years later by the Austrian master Falkbeer."}},"mimiaka":{"t":"The Ear-reddening Game","w":"Honinbo Shusaku","b":"Inoue Gennan Inseki","ev":"A game in Osaka (played over three days)","d":"1846","pl":"Japan (Osaka)","lead":"Seventeen-year-old Shusaku (4-dan) took on Gennan (8-dan), one of the strongest players of the day. When Shusaku played move 127, Gennan’s ears are said to have turned red — hence the name. There was no komi then; Black won by 2 points.","c":{"0":"Osaka, 1846. Black is 17-year-old Shusaku (4-dan); White is Inoue Gennan Inseki (8-dan), about 48, one of the top players of the day who had once fought Honinbo Jowa for the post of Meijin Godokoro. The day before, Shusaku had started with two handicap stones, as their ranks required, but Gennan soon saw his strength and stopped that game; this one was played with Shusaku simply taking Black. There was no komi in those days, so only the territory on the board counts. Let us follow this three-day game move by move. Go in the Edo period was run by four “houses”: Honinbo, Inoue, Yasui and Hayashi. Each house was paid by the shogun’s government to play and teach go. Shusaku belonged to the Honinbo house and Gennan to the Inoue house, so this game also carried the pride of two houses. In the same year 1846, the American commodore Biddle sailed two warships into Uraga Bay to ask for trade, and the shogunate said no, seven years before Perry came. In Europe, the planet Neptune was discovered about two weeks after this game.","1":"Black 1 is on the 3-4 point at top right (R16). It balances corner territory and room to extend along the sides. Shusaku liked 3-4 points when he had Black. Shusaku was born in 1829 on Innoshima, an island in the Seto Inland Sea (now part of Onomichi, Hiroshima). His father served the Asano family, lords of Mihara Castle. He was very strong at go from early childhood, and in 1837, at about eight, he went to Edo to become a pupil of Honinbo Jowa.","2":"White 2 takes the 3-4 point at top left (D17). In the opening the corners come first, because territory is easiest to make there. The two sides share out the empty corners.","3":"Black 3 is the 3-4 point at bottom right (Q3). It is lined up with R16 so that the right side between the two corners can later become a big Black area.","4":"White 4 (P17) approaches Black’s top-right corner. If Black got one more move there, the corner would become very solid, so White acts first. Black can answer, or play another big point instead.","5":"Black 5 does not answer but takes the 3-4 point at bottom left (C4). Three 3-4 points at moves 1, 3 and 5 is a pattern often seen in Shusaku’s games with Black. Dealing with White’s P17 can wait. This pattern of Black 1, 3 and 5 later came to be called the “Shusaku opening”. Shusaku did not invent it, but he won so many games with it that it took his name. It is the most famous opening for Black from the days without komi.","6":"White 6 (C14) is on the left side. Approaching Black’s bottom-left corner would be normal, but Gennan builds on the left side together with D17. It is a slightly unusual move that blocks Black’s C4 from spreading up the left side. Gennan was born in 1798. He longed to become Meijin Godokoro, the highest post in go, and fought Honinbo Jowa over it for years. When Jowa retired in 1839 he tried again, but he lost the first game of a challenge match against Shuwa of the Honinbo house and gave up. So this is also a great player who never became Meijin testing the young hope of the Honinbo house.","7":"Black 7 (E4) closes the bottom-left corner with a second stone. The corner is now almost surely Black’s. By defending before White could approach, Black also limits what White 6 can do on the left.","8":"White 8 (R5) approaches Black’s Q3. With black 3-4 points at both ends of the right side, White needs a foothold there. A long fight in the bottom right begins.","9":"Black 9 (Q15) defends the top right. Placed below White’s P17, it strengthens the corner and pushes P17 towards the narrow top edge. Black wants to keep the right side on its side. Black 9 is the famous move later called “Shusaku’s kosumi” (a diagonal step). Instead of answering P17 directly, Black steps diagonally to guard the corner and the right side. Shusaku is said to have remarked that as long as the board stays the same size, this kosumi will never be a bad move. In the komi era it was sometimes called too slow, but in recent years go AI has also rated it highly, and it has been looked at again.","10":"White 10 (O4) is the taisha: instead of a simple move, White caps Black’s stones diagonally from a little distance. It has so many variations that an old saying speaks of “the taisha’s hundred changes”, and it was Gennan’s speciality. How will 17-year-old Shusaku handle his opponent’s favourite weapon? Study of the taisha began around the 1820s and flourished in the Tenpo era (1830s–40s). The Inoue house is said to have kept secret research on it: eleven years earlier, in the “Blood-vomiting Game”, Gennan’s pupil Akaboshi Intetsu played an Inoue new move inside the taisha. The new line Gennan plays in this game is judged an even result and is still a joseki today.","11":"With 11 (Q5) Black attaches to White’s R5 and accepts the fight. A quiet defence was possible, but Shusaku chooses the line with many variations. A reading contest follows.","12":"White 12 (Q4) wedges between Black’s stones, trying to separate Q3 from Q5 and weaken one of them.","15":"With 13 (P4) and 15 (P3), Black firmly connects the lower stones. White 14 (R4) dug into the corner, so the corner territory is now contested. Black prefers to stay connected and strong even if it gives up a little of the corner. This is still the path of the taisha joseki that players of the day had studied hard. Both sides know the shapes well, so even this tricky sequence goes along without hesitation.","17":"With White 16 (P5) and Black 17 (Q6), the stones grow upwards. Black pokes its head out towards the right side, while White presses from above to keep Black in.","19":"White 18 (R3) adds a stone in the corner to make life, and Black 19 (O3) holds White back on the bottom side. Black’s plan: let White have the corner, but become strong outside along the bottom and right.","20":"White 20 (P6) caps Black’s Q6 from above, trying to shut Black low on the right side. If Black cannot get out upwards, its right-side stones will only just live, with little to gain. Three years after this game, from 1849, Shusaku took part in the castle games (oshirogo), the official games played at Edo Castle, and left a record of 19 wins in 19 games. But in 1862, when cholera spread through Edo, he nursed the sick in the Honinbo house, caught it himself and died at 34.","21":"With 21 (Q7) and 23 (Q8), Black crawls up the right side looking for a way out, while White 22 (P7) presses alongside. Black’s long chain on the third line is low and makes little territory, but it is clearly alive.","24":"White 24 (N4) seals Black’s O3–P3 stones on the bottom from outside. Black’s stones now face two directions, the right side and the bottom, and protecting both at once is getting hard.","25":"Black 25 (N3) extends one step along the bottom. Later study says another move here, one using a ladder, would have been better, so this is called Black’s small mistake in the game. Black’s bottom stones stay low and easy to surround, which gives White a chance to strike back. Even Shusaku could go astray inside his opponent’s favourite pattern. With this move, Black stepped a little off the well-studied path of the taisha joseki.","26":"White 26 (P8) caps Black’s right-side stones from above. Black cannot get out upwards on the right, and is in danger of being shut in on the bottom too.","28":"White 28 (M3) attaches directly to Black’s N3 — a severe move. It stops Black from spreading along the bottom and takes away living space, while making Black add more stones. Gennan punished Black’s small slip at 25 at once.","30":"After Black 29 (M2), White 30 (L2) goes round underneath as well. Black’s bottom stones can get out neither outwards nor sideways. To live they must connect to the bottom-right corner, and a capturing fight over the corner begins.","31":"Black 31 (M4) is an important move. Instead of rushing into the corner fight, Black first creates a point White must answer. It prepares for the ko that is sure to come.","33":"With 33 (R2) the capturing race in the bottom-right corner begins. White 32 (L3) made White safe on the bottom, so Black must go for White’s corner stones. If Black captures them, its bottom stones live; if not, they are in danger.","35":"White 34 (S2) and Black 35 (S1): fine moves on the first and second lines in the corner. Each side reduces the other’s liberties (empty points around a group), trying to capture first.","36":"White 36 (N2). Before starting the ko, White first plays one move around Black’s bottom stones. Even if Black wins the ko, it will gain a little less — a well-timed move.","40":"White 40 (R1) captures a stone, and at last it is a ko. In a ko the side that was just captured may not retake at once. Black must first play a threat elsewhere that White has to answer, and only then retake. The fight is decided by who has more threats.","41":"Black 41 (O5) is the first ko threat. Ignoring it would put White’s bottom-right stones in trouble, so White answers with 42 (N5), and Black retakes with 43 (R2). It costs a little, but it is necessary here.","44":"White 44 (S7) is White’s ko threat. Played beside Black’s right-side stones, it makes Black answer at 45 (S8), and White retakes at 46 (R1). Each threat settles a little more of the board.","47":"Black 47 (M5), White 48 (N6), Black 49 (R2): another threat, another retake. Around White’s bottom stones, Black is slowly adding stones on the outside.","50":"White 50 (R7) cuts into Black’s right-side stones — a big ko threat. Normally Black would answer, but Black puts the ko first. It becomes a large trade: White gains on the right side, Black gets the bottom-right corner.","51":"Black 51 (P2) captures a stone and wins the ko. The bottom-right corner becomes living Black territory, and the bottom stones are saved. In return White continues with 52 (R8) to split Black’s right-side stones in two.","53":"Black 53 (R10) is an inventive move few would find. It comes to the aid of the upper part of Black’s split right-side stones from beyond White’s stones. Had Black answered simply, White would have taken a big share of the right side.","56":"White 54 (S10), Black 55 (S11), White 56 (S9): White makes life along the right edge, while Black holds it from above and keeps the upper half of the right side.","59":"After Black 57 (M6) and White 58 (N7), Black 59 (R11) connects the right-side stones firmly. Black’s right side is now safe from top to bottom. The big trade so far looks roughly even.","60":"White 60 (S3) is a big move. It reduces Black’s corner territory while strengthening White’s stones, and it also lets White play 62 (M1) next as a move Black must answer.","63":"White 62 (M1) captures a stone and Black 63 (O1) connects: the long bottom-right fight is over for now. White has territory on the bottom; Black has a thick wall on the right. Partly because of Black 25, White is thought to have done well so far. But Shusaku’s thick wall will later work hard in the centre.","65":"White 64 (G3) extends to the left part of the bottom to make White’s bottom area even bigger. Black 65 (G5) caps it from above so White’s territory cannot grow upwards. The pattern becomes clear: White takes territory, Black takes outside strength.","66":"White 66 (C9) is on the left side. Together with White 6 (C14), it builds a White framework there too. White is gathering territory along the bottom and the left.","67":"Black 67 (L17) is on the top side. With the thick wall behind it, Black aims for a large framework — a wide area likely to become territory — from the top towards the centre. It also pincers and attacks White’s P17.","69":"White 68 (J17) goes to the left of L17 to split Black’s top framework. Black 69 (Q17) attaches to White’s P17, attacking it while strengthening the corner.","70":"White 70 (O10) goes between Black’s right-side wall and the top framework. It stops Black’s framework from joining up through the centre and tries to link with White’s stones on the right.","72":"White 72 (P13) is a superb move. Placed just above Black’s wall, it makes a White home right in the middle of Black’s framework. Even if Black chases it, White can escape lightly. Black’s hopes for the top shrink with this one move.","73":"Black 73 (P12) blocks below P13 so it cannot connect to White’s right-side stones. White 74 (Q18) then goes into the top-right corner to trim Black’s corner territory as well.","77":"After Black 75 (R18) defends the corner, White 76 (O13) and Black 77 (N11) follow. Black caps White’s centre stones, trying to gain territory while attacking. From here to around move 100, Gennan does not make a single slack move and keeps dodging Black’s attacks.","79":"White 78 (N10) connects White’s centre stones, and Black 79 (O16) firms up the top. Black did not gain much by attacking, but holds the top territory securely.","80":"With 80 (Q12) and 82 (Q13), White links stones right beside Black’s wall. Now that White has a sound shape inside Black’s framework, Black’s top-right territory will be smaller than hoped.","83":"Black 83 (H3) goes into White’s bottom territory, which had grown too large. White 84 (H2) and Black 85 (J2) follow: a close fight over whether Black can live inside or escape outside.","87":"White 86 (J3), Black 87 (H4), White 88 (K2): Black links the invading stones to G5 above and brings them out. White’s bottom territory is trimmed, and Black adds to its central thickness.","89":"Black 89 (F17) approaches White’s D17 at top left, putting a black stone on the left of the top side and stopping White from spreading along it. White 90 (M17) and 92 (N17) try to make White’s stones live between Black’s on the top.","93":"With 93 (M18) and 95 (N16), Black squeezes White’s top stones from above and below. White can only live low on the first and second lines, and even then makes very little territory.","96":"From White 96 (R14) a sharp little fight starts beside Black’s top-right corner. White splits Black’s wall from the corner to weaken Black’s stones. Black answers at 97 (Q14), and White pushes along the right edge with 98 (R12) and 100 (S12).","101":"With 101 (T12) and 103 (T13), Black descends to the first line at the right edge, narrowing White’s living space. Edge moves look small, but here they decide life and death.","104":"White 104 (Q10) hits a weak spot in Black’s right-side wall. After Black 105 (Q11) and White 106 (P11), Black goes to capture with 107.","107":"Black 107 (P10) captures a white stone. Black’s right-side wall is now solidly connected and safe. White 108 (O11) and 110 (P9) head for the centre to protect White’s stones near the wall.","112":"From White 112 (L18), White’s top stones hurry to make life. With 113 (K18), 115 (K17) and 117 (K19), Black surrounds them so they cannot get out.","118":"White 118 (M19) captures a stone and White’s top group lives. Living inside Black’s top framework cuts Black’s territory a lot. Gennan’s play so far has been excellent.","120":"Black 119 (D18) probes White’s top-left corner and White answers with 120 (C17). Black is not trying to take the corner, but to firm up the left part of the top and create a chance to add territory.","122":"Black 121 (F15) tidies Black’s shape from the top towards the centre. White 122 (C6) plays beside Black’s bottom-left corner to trim its territory bit by bit.","124":"Black 123 (G2), White 124 (J1), Black 125 (F3): the border on the left of the bottom is settled. White captures a stone there and keeps its territory safe.","126":"White 126 (J5). White is now thick on the bottom and ready to push into the centre. With territory on the bottom and left and a living group at the top right, White leads. Most of the professionals watching are said to have thought White was ahead. Black cannot catch up by ordinary play.","127":"This is the famous ear-reddening move, Black 127 (K11). This one stone in the middle of the board is said to work in four directions at once. Upwards it widens Black’s framework; downwards it stops White’s thickness from reaching the centre; to the right it supports Black’s wall; to the left it blocks White’s expansion. One move doing four jobs. From here the game, thought to favour White, slowly starts to tip towards Black.","128":"When move 127 was played, a doctor watching nearby noticed that Gennan’s ears had turned red. He knew little about go, but he is said to have read it as a sign that Gennan was shaken, and guessed that Black would win. While the professionals thought White was better, only the doctor’s eye caught the turning point. White 128 (E18) defends the top-left corner.","131":"After Black 129 (F18) firms up the left of the top, Black 131 (D10) moves into White’s left-side framework. With stone 127 holding the centre, Black can safely invade White’s territory.","136":"With 133 (E10) and 135 (D12), Black’s left-side stones slip between White’s and take shape. White 136 (J10) comes into the centre to weaken the effect of stone 127; White cannot leave the centre to Black.","140":"Black 137 (H7) caps White’s bottom stones, and White 138 (G10) and 140 (F8) try to link White’s stones on the left of the centre. In the centre the stones are tangled, and both sides have weaknesses.","143":"White 142 (E2) slips into Black’s bottom-left corner. Black 143 (D7) and 145 (D6) connect Black’s stones while eyeing both White’s left-side stones and the white invader in the corner.","147":"Black 147 (K10) extends straight down from stone 127, cutting White’s centre stones into left and right. Here the single stone of 127 begins to show its real power.","150":"White 148 (K9), Black 149 (L9), White 150 (J11): White tries to connect its centre stones, Black cuts them off from the bottom. White 150 lands right next to stone 127, pushing into Black’s centre too.","155":"From Black 151 (J9) to Black 155 (K12), Black squeezes White’s centre stones from both sides. Black 155 connects above stone 127, joining Black’s centre stones with the top. Black has taken the initiative in the centre.","160":"After White 156 (C5) and Black 157 (D5), White plays 158 (J12) and 160 (H8). White protects its centre stones while also eyeing a weakness in Black’s bottom-left stones. The game is still close; neither side can relax.","162":"Black 161 (B8) goes underneath White’s left-side stones. White 162 (D13) caps Black’s left-side stones from above to make them suffer.","167":"After strengthening the centre with 163 (G12) and 165 (J13), Black 167 (C2) is an accurate move defending the bottom-left corner. Had Black ignored it, White’s stone at 142 (E2) could have threatened the corner. Black removes the danger first and builds a hard-to-lose position.","169":"White 166 (E17) and 168 (F16) try to cut Black’s stones at the top left. Black answers with 169 (G16) and 171 (G17), connecting the top stones firmly.","175":"Black 175 (G4) captures a white stone. Black’s stones survive inside White’s bottom area, so White’s territory there is smaller than it first looked. The gap is slowly closing.","180":"White 176 (M11), Black 177 (L13), White 178 (J6), Black 179 (M10), White 180 (M13): on the right of the centre too, White puts stones into Black’s framework to reduce it. Black answers while gathering the whole centre into its own territory.","187":"From Black 183 (K13) to 187 (M14), Black presses down on the white stones in the centre and keeps them away from the top. White fights to live there, but Black’s wall is thick and the fight is hard for White.","195":"From White 190 (G14) to 194 (M9), White widens the escape routes of its centre stones. Black 195 (L10) cuts between White’s centre stones to break their shape.","199":"With Black 199 (K7) the centre fight calms down. White’s centre stones manage to live, but Black gained territory along the way and the game now leans towards Black. Next comes the endgame — fixing the borders — where one or two points decide everything.","208":"Black 203 (E19), 205 (F2), White 208 (R19): endgame moves along the edges are played from the biggest down. In the endgame it is important to play moves the opponent must answer first.","212":"With 212 (D19) and 216 (F19), White captures black stones at the top edge, and Black makes up for it elsewhere with 213 (G11) and 217 (G19). Neither side gives an inch.","225":"Black 225 (L6) captures a white stone; Black keeps trimming White’s territory in the middle of the bottom. The last big fight, a ko in the centre, is approaching.","228":"White 228 (N12) captures a stone and creates a ko in the centre. For more than 80 moves the sides will capture and recapture at M13 and N13. Each ko threat causes a small trade somewhere on the board. This long, long ko will decide the game.","236":"White 236 (T8) captures a stone in the middle of the ko. Some later analysts think that ending the ko here instead would have kept the margin smaller. In a long ko, which trades you choose decides the last point or two.","262":"With 262 (T1) and 266 (S4), White captures black stones in the bottom-right corner. Meanwhile Black has taken back elsewhere, and the ko goes on with gains and losses piling up on both sides.","275":"Black 275 (T14) captures two white stones; this trade at the top-right edge favours Black. Adding up the trades during the ko, Black is just keeping its small lead.","299":"Black 299 (J16) captures four white stones at once. White’s threat at the top is gone and Black’s top is safe. Black has come through the last big crisis of the ko.","311":"Black 311 (M13) finally fills the ko. The centre ko that lasted more than 80 moves is over. Through all the trades it caused, Black has kept the lead. Only small endgame moves remain.","321":"From Black 317 (N1) to 321 (M2), the last borders on the bottom are fixed. Both sides keep playing accurately to the end, trying to gain even one more point.","325":"The game ends after 325 moves, and the count shows Black ahead by 2 points. Black stumbled a little in Gennan’s favourite taisha early on, and White was thought to be better up to the middle game. Shusaku turned the tide with the single stone of move 127, came through the long ko and held on to a tiny lead. Gennan is said to have remarked later that Shusaku’s strength was no less than 7-dan. This game made Shusaku’s name, and he later became heir to the Honinbo house. The taisha in the opening, the ear-reddening move 127 and the long final ko — these three are the highlights of this great game."}},"evergreen":{"t":"The Evergreen Game","w":"Adolf Anderssen","b":"Jean Dufresne","ev":"A casual game (not a tournament)","d":"1852","pl":"Germany (probably Berlin)","lead":"Anderssen, of the Immortal Game, played this other masterpiece a year later. Just as Black threatens mate, White quietly moves one rook, then gives up the queen and finishes with a double check. It is called \"evergreen\" because it never fades.","c":{"0":"Germany, 1852. White is Adolf Anderssen, who had won the London tournament the year before and was thought to be the strongest player in the world. Black is his friend Jean Dufresne, who later wrote a chess handbook that many people read. It was a relaxed game, not a tournament game; it is thought to have been played in Berlin, but there is no clear record. It is another great game, born one year after “the Immortal Game”. The name is said to come from the later champion Steinitz, who praised it as “the evergreen in Anderssen’s laurel crown”. Let’s look at it together with Musowl. Steinitz wrote those words in 1879, the year Anderssen died, in a newspaper article remembering him.","1":"White pushes the pawn in front of the king two squares. It now watches d5 and f5 in the centre, and the roads for the bishop and queen are open.","2":"Black also plays e5. It faces White’s pawn in the centre and does not give way. In this same year, 1852, Anderssen also published his second book of chess puzzles (problems). After winning the tournament the year before, he had become the most watched player in Europe.","3":"White’s knight goes to f3. It develops a piece and attacks Black’s e5 pawn. Black has to protect this pawn.","4":"Black brings the knight to c6 to protect the e5 pawn. Defending while developing a piece: a move that wastes nothing.","5":"White’s bishop goes to c4. It aims at f7 next to Black’s king. f7 is a weak square, guarded only by the king.","6":"Black likewise brings the bishop to c5 and aims at White’s f2. Both sides have the same shape; so far it is a very calm start. Up to here this is the 'Italian Game' (Giuoco Piano, meaning 'quiet game'), a beginning recorded from very early times.","7":"b4! White offers a pawn for free: the “Evans Gambit”. It is said to have been invented by a British sea captain named Evans, and it was very popular in this era. If Black takes with the bishop, White pushes pawns to c3 and d4, gaining time while taking control of the centre. William Davies Evans was born in Wales. As a young man he joined the navy, and later he became captain of a ship carrying the mail. He is said to have thought up this gambit in the mid-1820s and made it famous by beating the strong player McDonnell in London. He was also an inventor, who came up with a system of three-coloured lights so that ships would not crash into each other at night.","8":"Black takes the pawn with the bishop. It is a move that accepts what is offered, but because the bishop has moved, White can now chase it and seize the initiative.","9":"With c3 White threatens the bishop on b4. At the same time, it prepares the next move, d4.","10":"The bishop drops back to a5. From a5 it looks diagonally all the way to e1 and eyes the c3 pawn from the side.","11":"d4! White pushes a pawn into the centre. It strikes at Black’s e5 pawn and tries to make the middle of the board White’s. White’s pieces can now come out one after another. Giving up one pawn to gain time and the centre: that is the whole idea of the Evans Gambit.","12":"Black takes the d4 pawn. Black is now two pawns up. But Black has not castled yet, and few of Black’s pieces are out.","13":"White castles. The king is tucked safely into the corner, and the rook can soon join the fight. Even two pawns down, White is far ahead in the speed of developing pieces.","14":"Black plays d3. The pawn steps forward one square and drives a wedge into White’s camp. This pawn becomes a small thorn that gets in the way of White’s pieces. Other moves are also well studied in this position, but Black chose a slightly unusual one. This 7...d3 was a favourite of Dufresne. It stops White's knight from coming out to c3 and makes White spend a move winning the pawn back. Today, though, it is thought to be inferior, and 7...Nge7 and other moves are normally played.","15":"White’s queen goes to b3. Together with the bishop on c4 it aims at f7: a frightening setup. Since only the king guards f7, the threat of taking on f7 with the bishop with check is becoming serious.","16":"Black brings the queen to f6 to guard f7. While protecting f7 the queen also eyes White’s kingside: a hard-working square.","17":"e5! White chases away Black’s queen with a pawn. This pawn also blocks the road of Black’s knight to f6, cramping Black’s pieces.","18":"The queen escapes to g6. From here it aims down the g-file at g2 in front of White’s king, and it also keeps an eye on e4. The e5 pawn looks as if it can be taken, but analysis shows that if Black takes it, a rook comes to e1 with a pin and Black loses material.","19":"The rook goes to e1 and firmly protects the e5 pawn. White’s rook has now joined the fight too. However, recent analysis with computers suggests that Rd1 might have been better here.","20":"Black develops the knight to e7. Next Black would like to castle and make the king safe. The knight went to e7 instead of f6 because the e5 pawn controls f6.","21":"The bishop goes to a3! There is a straight diagonal road from a3 toward f8. This bishop aims at the knight on e7 and also looks at f8 behind it. If the knight moves, the road to f8 opens, so it has become hard for Black to castle on the kingside. A very important move that keeps Black’s king trapped in the middle. Computers also suggest Qd1 or Re3 here, but Ba3 is a clear and powerful move for a human to understand.","22":"With b5 Black offers a pawn and threatens the bishop on c4. It is a counterattack to pull White’s queen away from f7 and shake things up.","23":"White takes the pawn with the queen. The queen protects the bishop on c4 while still aiming near Black’s king.","24":"Black’s rook comes out to b8 and threatens the queen on b5. The rook has started working on the open b-file.","25":"The queen goes to a4. It attacks the bishop on a5, and along the diagonal from a4 to e8 it pins the c6 knight to Black’s king. If this knight moves, the king would be in check, so it cannot move.","26":"The bishop escapes from a5 to b6. It is back on its old diagonal, aiming toward f2.","27":"White develops the knight to d2. The last piece left at home comes out, and next it plans to jump to e4 and approach Black’s king.","28":"Black develops the bishop to b7 to protect the pinned knight on c6. The long diagonal from b7 is blocked by the c6 knight for now, but if the knight moves, the bishop will be able to aim toward f3 and g2.","29":"The knight goes to e4! From the middle of the board it threatens to jump to f6 or d6 with check. Black’s king is still in the centre and in danger.","30":"Black’s queen goes to f5. It aims at both the e4 knight and the f3 knight. The idea is to make the knights step aside and then go after White’s king.","31":"The bishop takes the pawn on d3. The pawn that was Black’s thorn is gone. What’s more, this bishop is looking at the f5 queen from behind the e4 knight: if the knight moves, the bishop will take the queen.","32":"The queen escapes to h5. From here it aims through g4 at the knight on f3. The plan is to take f3 with the queen next and use the g-file too, to mate White’s king.","33":"The knight jumps to f6 with check! It also attacks the queen on h5: a fork. So as not to lose the queen, Black decides to take this knight.","34":"Black takes the knight with the g7 pawn. The g-file is open now, so Black can aim at White’s king with the rook on g8.","35":"White recaptures on f6 with the e5 pawn. This pawn threatens the knight on e7, and since the e-file is open, the rook on e1 now looks through the e7 knight at Black’s king. The f6 pawn also controls the squares g7 and e7.","36":"Black’s rook goes to g8. On the open g-file it aims at g2 in front of White’s king. If the queen takes f3 next, g2 will be attacked by both the queen and the rook, and Black looks set to mate White.","37":"The rook goes to d1! Black has a mating threat, yet White quietly moves a rook. Between d1 and d7 stands only the bishop on d3. If the bishop moves, the rook will look straight at the square next to Black’s king. It is a deeply calculated move that aims to finish first with a series of checks, and it is the most famous move of this game. However, later studies say there were other moves for both White and Black, and it is still being examined today.","38":"Black’s queen takes the knight on f3. If it takes g2 next, it is mate together with the rook. White now has to win by giving check after check.","39":"The rook takes the knight on e7 with check! Even giving up a piece, White keeps chasing Black’s king.","40":"Black recaptures with the c6 knight. But because the knight left c6, the diagonal from the a4 queen to d7 is now open. It is said that even if Black moved the king instead of taking with the knight, White’s attack would go on.","41":"White sacrifices the queen on d7 with check! Through the diagonal that has just opened, the queen jumps right in front of Black’s king. This is the decisive blow of the Evergreen.","42":"Black takes the queen with the king. Running to f8 would allow the queen to take the e7 knight with mate, so taking was the only choice.","43":"The bishop moves to f5: double check! The bishop checks the king on d7 from f5, and the rook on d1, uncovered by the bishop, also checks along the d-file. When two pieces give check at once, you cannot defend by blocking or capturing; the king must run.","44":"The king runs to e8. If it ran to c6 instead, the bishop would come to d7 and it would be mate.","45":"The bishop comes to d7 with check. This time it is right next to the king on e8. This bishop is protected by the rook on d1, so the king cannot take it.","46":"The king goes to f8. Running to d8 would also lose: the a3 bishop would take the e7 knight with mate.","47":"The a3 bishop takes the knight on e7: checkmate! The d7 bishop controls e8, the f6 pawn controls e7 and g7, and Black’s own pieces block f7 and g8. The f6 pawn, the bishop that had not moved since it came to a3, and the rook on d1: every piece worked perfectly at the end. Black was aiming at g2 with the queen and rook, but was one move too late. Reading the opponent’s threat and then finishing first with a chain of checks: this game is a model of how to attack."}},"lasker1912":{"t":"The King Hunt","w":"Edward Lasker","b":"George Alan Thomas","ev":"A quick casual game at the City of London Chess Club","d":"1912","pl":"United Kingdom (London)","lead":"A fast, casual game at a London club. White gives up the queen and chases Black's king all the way across the board, from g8 to g1. The opening moves are written in slightly different orders in different records, but the position after move 10 is the same.","c":{"0":"October 1912, the City of London Chess Club. White is Edward Lasker, a young engineer just arrived from Germany; Black is George Alan Thomas, later British champion. It was a fast, casual game. It is famous as a \"king hunt\": Black's king is chased from g8 all the way to g1 at the other end of the board. Lasker later moved to America and became well known for his chess books. According to the records, the date was 29 October. It is described as a 'five-minute' game, but not like today's five-minute blitz: the rule was that neither player could use more than five minutes more than the opponent.","1":"White starts with d4, the queen's pawn. It covers e5 and c5 and opens the way for the bishop on c1. Lasker was born in Kępno, in today's Poland (then Germany). He studied mechanical and electrical engineering at the technical university in Berlin, had only just come to London that year, and joined this club in October. He was a distant relative of the world champion Emanuel Lasker, and people often mix the two up.","2":"Black plays e6, building a solid position and preparing f5 or d5. Thomas was born in Istanbul. He was not only a chess player but also a great badminton player, winning 21 titles in all at Britain's biggest championships (the All England). The Thomas Cup, the world team competition in badminton, is named after him. He also did well in tennis at Wimbledon – an amazing all-round sportsman.","3":"Knight to f3. It covers e5 and helps guard the king's side: the most natural square. In April of this year, 1912, the great liner Titanic struck an iceberg and sank on its first voyage from Britain to America.","4":"Black plays f5, controlling e4, like the Dutch Defence. But moving a pawn in front of the king makes Black's king a little weaker. With the order 1.d4 e6 2.Nf3 f5, the game has entered a Dutch Defence. Books give slightly different move orders for this game, but this order is thought to be the one in Lasker's own score sheet.","5":"The other knight comes to c3, getting ready for e4 to hit the f5 pawn.","6":"Black brings a knight to f6. It controls e4 even more and prepares to castle.","7":"Bishop to g5, aiming at the knight on f6. That knight is an important defender of Black's king, so White wants to remove it early.","8":"Black brings the bishop to e7 to guard the knight, and gets closer to castling.","9":"White trades the bishop for the knight. The knight that guarded Black's king is gone.","10":"Black takes back with the bishop. Now this bishop on f6 takes over the job of guarding the king's side.","11":"e4! White hits the f5 pawn and gains space in the centre. Lines open, and White's pieces can move more freely.","12":"Black takes the pawn on e4. If not, White would push e5 and drive Black's pieces back.","13":"The knight takes back and sits in the centre, eyeing the bishop on f6 and the squares g5 and d6. Two years later, in 1914, Lasker moved to America and worked as an engineer. In the 1920s he invented a breast pump, a machine that helped save the lives of babies born too early.","14":"Black plays b6, preparing to bring the bishop to b7 to aim down the long diagonal at the knight on e4.","15":"Knight to e5, a strong central square that also eyes g4 and f7. Both white knights are now close to Black's king's side. Lasker also loved Go and is known as one of the people who spread Go in America. In 1934 he wrote a book on Go and Gomoku, and he was later given an award from Japan for promoting Go.","16":"Black castles. The king is on g8. But with the f-pawn gone, there are many gaps around the king. After this game, Thomas went on to become British chess champion in 1923 and 1934.","17":"Bishop to d3. For now its own knight on e4 is in the way, but behind it lies the pawn on h7. The moment the knight on e4 moves, this bishop will aim straight at h7.","18":"Black's bishop goes to b7 and aims down the long diagonal at the knight on e4, making White's pieces less free.","19":"Queen to h5, aiming at the pawn on h7, which only Black's king defends. White's queen, bishop and knights all point at Black's king.","20":"Black puts the queen on e7. It guards the bishop on f6 and gets ready to go to c5, but Black misses White's idea. Moves like h6 or taking the knight on e5 were needed. This is the turning point.","21":"The queen is given up on h7, with check! White hands over the strongest piece for nothing. Lasker saw the whole path of chasing the king to mate.","22":"Black must take with the king. The king is pulled out to h7.","23":"The knight takes the bishop on f6 with check. At the same time e4 is empty, so the bishop on d3 also checks the king on h7: a double check. It cannot be blocked, so the king must run.","24":"The king goes to h6. On h8, the other knight would jump to g6 and mate. The king is pushed further and further forward.","25":"The other knight jumps from e5 to g4 with check. It attacks the king on h6 and drives it further forward.","26":"The king goes to g5, leaving its own camp and walking toward White's side.","27":"Check with the h-pawn! Even the pawns join the hunt. The king has to keep moving forward.","28":"The king goes to f4. It has crossed the middle of the board and is entering White's camp.","29":"Check again with the g-pawn. Two pawns now block the way back for the king.","30":"The king goes to f3, right in the middle of White's pieces.","31":"The bishop drops back from d3 to e2 with check. The king is pushed even deeper.","32":"The king goes to g2. White's own king on e1 is not far away.","33":"Rook to h2, check. At last the rook joins in.","34":"The king finally reaches g1. It started on g8 and has walked to the first row at the other end of the board.","35":"White's king steps from e1 to d2. This opens the way for the rook on a1, which now checks the king on g1 along the first row: checkmate! Castling queenside would also have mated, but Lasker moved the king. In a quick game, after giving up the queen, White chased the king for fourteen moves into the deepest corner of White's own camp. A very rare game."}},"toketsu":{"t":"The Blood-vomiting Game","w":"Akaboshi Intetsu","b":"Honinbo Jowa","ev":"Played at the residence of Lord Matsudaira of Suo (over four days)","d":"1835","pl":"Japan (Edo)","lead":"A game born of the struggle for Meijin Godokoro, the highest post in Japanese go. Inoue Gennan Inseki sent his pupil Akaboshi Intetsu to face the Meijin, Jowa. After four days of hard fighting Intetsu lost, and he is said to have coughed up blood at the board.","c":{"0":"Edo, 1835. This game was played at a go gathering in the residence of Lord Matsudaira of Suo, a senior minister. Black is Akaboshi Intetsu (7-dan) of the Inoue house; White is Honinbo Jowa, holder of Meijin Godokoro, the highest post in go, which he had won in a struggle with Inoue Gennan Inseki. Unhappy with that, Gennan sent his best pupil, Intetsu, planning to argue that a Meijin who lost to a 7-dan did not deserve the title. The game lasted four days and left one of the most famous — and saddest — stories in go history. In this year 1835, Halley’s Comet, which returns about every 76 years, was passing near the Earth, and that autumn the writer Mark Twain was born in America. In the go world, it was a time when the four houses — Honinbo, Inoue, Yasui and Hayashi — fought over the single post of Meijin Godokoro.","1":"Black 1 on the 3-4 point at bottom left (C4). Intetsu starts calmly, showing he will play his own game even against the Meijin. Intetsu was born in 1810 in Kikuchi, in Higo (now Kumamoto). At twelve he went to Edo and joined the Inoue house, and from about fifteen he was taught by Gennan. In 1833 he wrote a book of life-and-death problems, “Genran”, and in 1834 he reached 7-dan: the great young hope of the Inoue house.","2":"White 2 (Q3), Black 3 (R16), White 4 (E16): one stone goes into each corner. White 4 is on the high 4-5 point, valuing outward spread over corner territory. Jowa, born in 1787, was the 12th Honinbo and became Meijin Godokoro in 1831. His go was tremendously powerful, and he is called the “Later Saint”, beside the great master Dosaku, the “Earlier Saint”. For a long time people said he outwitted Gennan to become Meijin, but documents found in recent years have led some to say that story should be looked at again.","5":"Black 5 (P17) encloses the top-right corner with a second stone. Black makes the top right solid to prepare for the fights ahead.","6":"White 6 (E3) already approaches Black’s bottom-left corner. Jowa is aggressive from the start and gives Black no time to settle quietly. E3 is the “small knight’s approach” to a 3-4 point, one of the most common approaches from the Edo period to today.","8":"Black 7 (R5) approaches White’s Q3, and White 8 (R9) pincers it from above. White wants to make Black’s stone weak on the right side and turn it into a target. From R5 to R9 there are three empty points between, so White 8 is a “three-space pincer”.","9":"Black 9 (R11) pincers White 8 back from above. With Black’s top-right corner solid, Black can turn the tables and attack White on the right side.","10":"White 10 (P4) defends the bottom-right corner, leaving the right-side fight for later and making its own corner solid first.","11":"Black 11 (J3) goes to the middle of the bottom side, between White’s E3 and P4, so that White cannot take a big area there. From E3 to J3 there are also three empty points, so Black 11 is also a “three-space pincer” against E3: Black answers the approach to its corner by pinching it.","12":"White 12 (D6) presses down on Black’s bottom-left corner from above. Together with E3, it tries to wrap up Black’s C4. The biggest fight of the game begins here. D6 is a large knight’s move from White’s E3. This is the taisha, a move played when a small knight’s approach is pinched from three spaces away, and this game is known as an example of a taisha arising from a three-space pincer. The taisha was a shape the Inoue house had studied in secret, so Jowa walked straight into his opponents’ specialty. Eleven years later, in the “Ear-reddening Game”, Gennan would play the taisha too.","13":"Black 13 (E4) attaches right between White’s stones and splits them before Black can be surrounded. Black accepts the fight head-on.","16":"White 14 (D4), Black 15 (D5), White 16 (D3): White digs under Black’s corner while Black grows upwards. With stones cut on both sides, each has weak groups — a difficult fight. This is inside the taisha joseki, said to have “a hundred variations”. Which path each side picks here changes the whole fight that follows.","19":"Black 17 (C5), White 18 (E5), Black 19 (F4): Black holds White’s E5 from below and keeps White’s stones split. White’s bottom-left stones must either live in the corner or escape outward.","20":"White 20 (C3) slips under Black’s corner, eating its territory while trying to live there. Black 21 (B3) and White 22 (B2) continue the close exchange inside the corner.","22":"White 22 (B2) drops to the second line in the corner to shape White’s life there. Black’s corner is now smaller, but Black’s outside stones are stronger.","23":"Black 23 (C7) goes up the left side and holds White’s upper stones (like D6) from below. White 24 (G2) extends along the bottom, giving the corner stones a way to connect and live.","26":"Black 25 (H4) caps White’s bottom stones, and White 26 (E6) strengthens the upper white stones. The lower white group wants territory, the upper one an exit to the centre — and Black is eyeing both.","27":"Black 27 (B4) blocks above White’s corner stones, narrowing their living space to prepare later attacks.","28":"White 28 (C9) reaches far along the left side, trying to give the upper white stones a base to live on. It is often called an over-extension — a fierce move very typical of Jowa. Black now sets out to punish it.","31":"Black 29 (D8), White 30 (D9), Black 31 (E8): Black cuts in between White’s C9 and the upper white stones, splitting White in two. White’s C9–D9 on the left and D6–E6 in the centre are now separated.","33":"Black 33 (C2). This is said to be a new line that the Inoue house had studied in secret. It squeezes White’s corner stones and at the same time attacks White’s floating stones in the centre — two aims at once. A “secret move” prepared to defeat the Meijin, and from here Black’s advantage becomes clear.","36":"White 34 (D2), Black 35 (B1), White 36 (F3): White hurries to connect its bottom stones and make life. Meanwhile White’s centre stones get no help and become Black’s target.","38":"White 38 (A2) is a first-line move that secures eye space for White’s corner stones. Once the lower white group is settled, White can focus on the upper fight.","40":"From Black 37 (G5) to White 40 (G7), White’s centre stones run upwards while Black chases closely alongside, strengthening its own stones as it goes.","42":"Black 41 (H7), White 42 (G8): White runs upwards into the centre and Black follows from outside. The further White goes up, the firmer Black’s bottom and left become.","43":"Black 43 (E9). White’s C9–D9 on the left are surrounded and in trouble, and White’s centre stones are not yet alive either. Up to here Intetsu has played splendidly and is thought to have taken a clear lead. Against the Meijin, the Inoue plan seemed to be working.","45":"White 44 (H8) runs the centre stones upwards, and Black 45 (D11) extends up the left side, blocking above C9–D9. White’s left-side stones are losing their way out both upwards and towards the centre.","48":"Black 47 (H5), White 48 (F5): White tries to link its centre and bottom stones, while Black tries to pry them apart. For White, every connection counts.","50":"Black 49 (C12) makes the left side even stronger, and White 50 (C16) turns to the top-left corner. White leaves the hard bottom-left fight for now to gain elsewhere.","53":"Black 51 (M3) reinforces the bottom, and White 52 (Q6) presses Black’s R5 on the right. Black 53 (G17) goes into the top side to stop White’s top left from growing.","57":"After White 54 (R6) firms up the right side, Black 55 (D17), White 56 (C17), Black 57 (E17): Black plays right above White’s top-left corner, trimming it while spreading along the top.","58":"White 58 (C14) spreads from the top-left corner along the side towards Black’s left-side stones. White’s top left and Black’s left side now meet head-on.","60":"When Black 59 (J8) caps White’s centre stones, White 60 (G4) captures two black stones. White’s bottom-left stones finally get some breathing room — a struggling White takes what it can.","63":"Black 61 (O3) adds a stone on the right of the bottom, White 62 (J17) goes to the top, and Black 63 (F16) holds White’s top left from outside. Sparks of fighting spread all over the board.","66":"White 64 (M17), Black 65 (B13), White 66 (B14): Black slips under White’s stones at the left edge while White tries to settle the top-left group. White’s C9–D9 are still surrounded.","68":"White 68 (B11). The next few white moves, together with move 80, became famous as Jowa’s brilliant moves — so remarkable that people said ghosts had shown them to him. B11 looks for a path linking White’s surrounded C9–D9 on the left with the top-left group. From here, the struggling White hits back.","70":"Black 69 (D10) tries to stop the connection, but White 70 (E12) ignores the left side and takes a key point in the centre first. Hidden resources remain for White on the left, so there is no need to answer at once. Taking back the initiative while letting the attack slide — a Meijin’s move.","72":"After Black 71 (F10) presses White’s centre, White 72 (R13) turns to the right side. White pauses the centre fight to stop Black’s top-right framework from growing.","76":"Black 73 (P11), White 74 (R15), Black 75 (Q16), White 76 (O14): White slips beside Black’s top-right corner and places stones linking the right side with the centre — groundwork for splitting Black’s stones.","78":"Black 77 (P9), White 78 (Q9): White blocks the link between Black’s right-side stones (R5, P9) and the black stones above. Will Black connect, or be cut apart? A tense moment.","80":"White 80 (Q10) makes an “empty triangle”, a clump of three stones usually considered bad shape. Yet it became the base for splitting Black’s right-side stones in two and attacking them. Valuing function over looks, it is often shown as the classic bad-looking but excellent move — the last of the famous three. From here White’s splitting attack begins in earnest, and the game slowly starts to lean towards White.","83":"After Black 81 (S6) and White 82 (S7), Black 83 (R3) goes into White’s bottom-right corner. Under pressure on the right, Black seeks both life and territory by invading White’s corner.","86":"Black 85 (S2), White 86 (R4): Black reduces White’s liberties from the first line, White holds Black from inside the corner. Both are counting how many moves each group has before capture.","88":"From White 84 (R2) to White 88 (Q2), a close capturing race goes on along the first and second lines of the corner. Who captures first? One move can make the difference.","91":"Black 91 (Q4) captures a white stone and gets one step ahead in the corner race. But White is preparing to wrap Black’s stones from outside.","95":"White 92 (N4), Black 93 (R7), White 94 (Q7), Black 95 (O4): White goes round Black’s outside from the bottom, while Black tries to connect on both the right and the bottom. The bigger Black’s group grows, the harder it is to save.","98":"White 98 (M4) wraps Black’s bottom stones from outside. Black has no quick safe shape either in the bottom-right corner or towards the centre.","101":"From White 96 (N3) to Black 101 (O5), black and white stones grow side by side from the bottom towards the centre — a pushing match where each holds the other from one side.","104":"After Black 103 (P2), White 104 (R4) captures a black stone. In the bottom-right corner race, White has taken a step back.","106":"Black 105 (N2), White 106 (S5) captures a black stone. White holds the bottom-right corner; Black’s stones cannot live there and must run outward.","107":"Black 107 (Q11) protects Black’s upper right-side stones: with the lower group in trouble, Black at least makes the upper one safe. White 108 (M2) and Black 109 (O1) continue a small capturing fight at the bottom edge.","113":"After White 110 (J4), Black 111 (K2) and White 112 (H3), Black 113 (K6): Black’s bottom stones poke out towards the centre looking for life. They must secure an exit before White closes the net.","116":"White 114 (L5), Black 115 (L3), White 116 (L6): White puts a lid over Black’s stones and narrows the exit to the centre. Black cuts White’s bottom stones while trying to get out.","120":"Black 117 (K7), White 118 (L7), Black 119 (J9), White 120 (N9): Black leaps out into the centre and White runs ahead to block from the right. It is now a race over whose stones weaken first.","125":"With White 122 (J2) and Black 123 (K3), Black’s bottom stones just manage to connect. Black 125 (N11) tries to link the upper right-side group with the centre stones.","128":"White 126 (O7), Black 127 (N8), White 128 (L11): White goes round above Black’s centre stones too, pressing them from above and below. The splitting plan prepared since move 80 is taking shape.","132":"From Black 129 (M10) to White 132 (K10), the two sides cut each other in the centre. Black tries to break White’s ring; White closes Black’s exits one by one.","137":"From Black 133 (L8) to White 136 (P10), the centre gets even more tangled. Black 137 (N13) jumps far upwards, trying to link the centre stones with Black’s top.","138":"White 138 (K8) captures a stone and a ko begins in the centre. For about 30 moves the sides take and retake at K8 and L8. This ko will decide the fate of Black’s centre stones.","143":"From Black 139 (K9) to Black 143 (J6), threats and retakes continue. Black 143 captures a white stone and eases the pressure on Black’s bottom stones.","148":"From White 144 (K5) to White 148 (K8), ko threats and retakes continue at the bottom and centre. With each threat, White gains a little around Black’s bottom stones.","152":"White 152 (E11) is a ko threat, hitting a weak point in Black’s left-side stones. Throughout the ko, White keeps finding moves all over the board that Black has to answer.","158":"From Black 155 (H2) to White 158 (C6), ko threats in the bottom and bottom left cause small trades. White 158 threatens Black’s bottom-left corner, and Black defends with 159 (B6).","162":"Black 161 (G1), White 162 (F1): captures happen at the bottom edge too. During the ko, small fights spread to every corner of the board.","167":"After White 166 (K8) retakes again, Black 167 (M1) does not continue the ko but plays elsewhere on the bottom. White thus wins the centre ko and can connect its centre stones. Black gains something on the bottom instead, but its centre stones are now in trouble.","170":"After White 168 (K11) and Black 169 (H10), White 170 (P15) heads for the top right. White settles the centre for now and aims at the big black group there, which is not yet fully alive.","175":"From Black 171 (L13) to Black 175 (K14), Black extends towards the top to link the top-right group with the centre stones. White 174 (O17) narrows Black’s living space near the top-right corner.","178":"White 178 (J14) jumps into the middle of the top to split Black’s stones left and right. The splitting attack prepared since move 80 now begins in earnest. Black must defend both sides at once — a very hard position.","182":"From Black 179 (K15) to White 182 (G14), White presses Black from outside on the left of the top as well. Black’s top-left stones also have to look for living space.","190":"From Black 183 (F14) to White 190 (J13), the two sides cut each other fiercely on the left of the top. White keeps placing stones between Black’s, breaking up Black’s shape.","193":"Black 193 (J11) captures a white stone; Black fights hard to connect and find life. But Black’s stones on the right of the top are still uneasy.","199":"From White 194 (S14) to Black 199 (A16), White holds Black’s top-right corner from outside while Black works at the top-left edge too. Which big group weakens first? A key moment before the end.","201":"Black 201 (R14) captures a stone and a new ko begins on the right side at R14 and R15. Whether Black’s top-right group lives depends on this ko.","206":"From White 202 (E14) to White 206 (M13), White plays ko threats while slowly tightening the grip on Black’s centre stones. Even during the ko, White’s overall net is nearly closed.","213":"From Black 207 (L17) to Black 213 (K18), Black fights to connect on the top. White reaches down to the first and second lines to rob Black of eyes — the empty points a group needs to live.","222":"White 222 (L14) captures a stone. Black’s top stones remain split in two, and saving both is now very hard. The Meijin’s splitting attack is finally paying off.","225":"Black 225 (R15) resolves the right-side ko and the top-right group manages to live. But meanwhile White has taken the middle of the top, and overall White is still ahead.","232":"Black 229 (P14) and White 232 (Q14) start another small ko in the top right. Black keeps looking for chances to the end, but White answers carefully.","239":"With 237 (F12) and 239 (E13), Black captures back on the top left. Even so, it does not make up for what was lost in the middle of the top, and the overall gap does not shrink.","244":"Black 241 (P18), White 242 (P19), Black 243 (P14), White 244 (N12): small moves continue to the end, but White plays the important centre points one by one. The game is almost decided.","246":"After White 246 (L8), Intetsu resigned. Early on he took a big lead with the Inoue house’s secret line, but Jowa hit back with his brilliant moves from 68 and the ugly-but-excellent move 80, kept splitting and attacking Black’s stones and finally turned the game around. As the stones were being cleared, Intetsu is said to have coughed up blood onto the board. He had probably been ill for some time, and he died not long afterwards. For Jowa, who kept the Meijin title, it is called the greatest win of his life, and Gennan’s plan failed. It is still told today as the game young Intetsu played with his life."}},"deepblue1997":{"t":"Deep Blue Wins","w":"Deep Blue (IBM computer)","b":"Garry Kasparov","ev":"Game 6 of the rematch with IBM's Deep Blue (a six-game match)","d":"1997","pl":"United States (New York)","lead":"World champion Kasparov against Deep Blue, a chess computer built by IBM, in a six-game match. With the score level after five games, the last game was over in only 19 moves. It was the first time a computer beat the world champion in a full match of several games.","c":{"0":"New York, 11 May 1997. World champion Garry Kasparov against Deep Blue, a chess computer built by IBM, in a six-game match. Kasparov had won their match the year before, but in this rematch against the stronger Deep Blue the score was 2½–2½ after five games. In the last game, Deep Blue had White. The whole world watched to see whether a human or a computer was stronger. Kasparov was born in 1963 in Baku, in today's Azerbaijan. In 1985, aged 22, he beat Karpov and became the youngest world champion up to that time. The prize for this match was 700,000 dollars for the winner and 400,000 dollars for the loser.","1":"White pushes the king's pawn two squares, taking the centre and opening paths for the pieces. Deep Blue grew out of a chess machine that Taiwanese-born researcher Feng-hsiung Hsu and his friends began building as students at Carnegie Mellon University in the USA. It was later greatly developed at IBM, and it is said that it could examine hundreds of millions of positions per second.","2":"Kasparov plays c6, getting ready for d5 to hit e4: the Caro-Kann Defence. It is solid, and players often chose it against computers. In fact it was unusual for Kasparov to choose the Caro-Kann with c6. Normally he preferred openings that led to sharper fights.","3":"White brings the other pawn to the centre too. Two pawns stand side by side. In July of this year, 1997, Hong Kong, which Britain had governed, was handed back to China. It was a year of big changes in the world.","4":"Black plays d5 and hits e4. The fight for the centre pawns begins.","5":"The knight goes to c3 and guards e4. A natural move: develop and defend.","6":"Black takes the pawn on e4, trading centre pawns to make White's centre smaller.","7":"The knight takes back. White's knight now stands in the middle of the board.","8":"Black puts a knight on d7. Then, when the other knight goes to f6, Black can take back with this knight if White trades knights. This 4...Nd7 line is called the Steinitz Variation; because Kasparov's long-time rival Karpov liked it so much, it is also known as the Karpov Variation.","9":"The knight jumps to g5, a sharp plan aiming at f7 and e6. White attacks early, before Black's king side is settled. This 5.Ng5 was a fairly new idea at the time. It deliberately breaks the basic rule 'don't move the same piece twice in the opening' in order to target the weak f7 square. Kasparov himself had played it several times with White.","10":"Black brings the knight to f6. Now more pieces guard the king.","11":"Bishop to d3. It aims diagonally at h7 and points toward Black's king side.","12":"Black plays e6 to strengthen the defence and open the way for the bishop on f8. But the e6 pawn also becomes a target for White's knight.","13":"The other knight goes from g1 to f3. White is ready to castle.","14":"Black plays h6 to chase the knight on g5. But in this position a knight sacrifice on e6 was already known for White. People found it strange that a master like Kasparov chose this, and some think he mixed up the move order by accident. This is the turning point. The usual book line is 7...Bd6, and only after 8.Qe2 does Black play h6. Besides the view that Kasparov played h6 one move too early, Deep Blue's creator Hsu has written that he may have chosen it on purpose, to confuse the computer.","15":"The knight is given up on e6! In return, White breaks the pawns around Black's king and stops Black from castling. Deep Blue is said to have played this move from the opening moves it had stored in advance. This knight sacrifice had already appeared in several games between strong players, with good results for White. Today's strong engines also rate it as the best move, even without any opening preparation.","16":"Black doesn't take at once but puts the queen on e7. On the e-file it lines up on the knight on e6 and White's king behind it. If the knight moves, the king is in check, so the knight cannot escape. Many commentators thought this pin was a mistake; some suggest it would have been better to take the knight straight away.","17":"White castles and moves the king away. Now the knight on e6 is no longer pinned. White does not mind if the knight is taken.","18":"Black takes the knight and is ahead in material. But the pawns around Black's king are broken, and the king is poorly protected.","19":"Bishop to g6, check. With the f7 pawn gone, the diagonal reaches the king on e8.","20":"The king goes to d8. Black can no longer castle, and the king is stuck near the middle. Black's pieces get in each other's way around the king.","21":"The other bishop goes to f4, eyeing c7 and b8 and squeezing the space around Black's king even more.","22":"Black plays b5, making room on the queen's side to bring the bishop to b7 and give the king a way out. This 11...b5 was the first new move of the game, not seen in earlier games. This is where the known opening theory ended.","23":"a4 hits the pawn on b5. White wants to open lines on the queen's side so the rook on a1 can join the attack.","24":"Black brings the bishop to b7, aiming down the long diagonal at g2. Black's pieces are finally starting to move.","25":"Rook to e1. On the e-file it eyes the e6 pawn and the queen on e7 behind it. All of White's pieces have joined the attack.","26":"Black's knight goes to d5, a strong central square, attacking the bishop on f4 and protecting e6.","27":"The bishop drops back to g3. It keeps aiming at c7 and b8, keeping the pressure on Black's king.","28":"Black's king goes to c8, a little safer, and lets the rook on d8 move.","29":"White takes the pawn on b5. The a-file opens, and the rook on a1 now eyes the pawn on a7.","30":"Black takes back with a pawn. But fewer and fewer pawns are left around Black's king.","31":"Queen to d3, eyeing the b5 pawn and the square f5. White's pieces slowly surround Black's king.","32":"Black brings the bishop back to c6 to guard the b5 pawn.","33":"Bishop to f5! It can be taken for free. But if a pawn takes it, the e-file pawn is gone, and the rook on e1 reaches the queen on e7 directly.","34":"Black takes with a pawn. Now nothing stands on the e-file between e1 and e7.","35":"The rook takes the queen on e7. White has given a knight, a bishop and a rook, but has won the strongest piece, the queen.","36":"Black takes back the rook with the bishop. Material is about even, but Black's king has almost no shelter.","37":"c4! The pawn hits both b5 and d5, and Black's defenders start to fall apart. White's queen is ready to join the attack at any moment. Kasparov resigned here, probably feeling his king was too open to stop the attack. Only 19 moves. It was the first time a computer beat the world champion in a full match of several games: a historic moment. After this, using computers to study and train became normal in chess."}},"seigen1933":{"t":"The 3-3, Star Point and Tengen Game","w":"Go Seigen","b":"Honinbo Shusai","ev":"Commemorative game with the Meijin (over three months)","d":"1933–1934","pl":"Japan (Tokyo)","lead":"Nineteen-year-old Go Seigen (5-dan) played the Meijin, Honinbo Shusai. With Black he opened on the 3-3 point, then a star point, then the very centre (tengen) — breaking every rule of the day and astonishing the go world. With many adjournments the game lasted over three months, and White won by 2 points.","c":{"0":"Tokyo, autumn 1933. Black is 19-year-old Go Seigen (5-dan); White is the 59-year-old Meijin, Honinbo Shusai. Go Seigen was born in Fujian, China, and came to Japan at 14 as a child prodigy. That year he and his friend Kitani Minoru were spreading a new way of playing called “Shin Fuseki” (New Opening), which valued power in the centre more than territory in the corners, and it had become the talk of the go world. Shusai was the last Meijin to hold the Honinbo name by inheritance, and stood at the very top of go. Everyone wanted to see how the new generation would challenge the old master. The game was adjourned many times (stopped and continued on a later day) and lasted more than three months. There was no komi in those days, so only the territory on the board decides the result. The game began on 16 October 1933 at the Kajibashi Inn in Kyobashi, Tokyo. Go Seigen had won the Japan Go Championship run by the Yomiuri Shimbun newspaper, and this game against the Meijin was his prize. In the Edo period the shogun’s government supported the go houses; by now newspapers were holding the big games and reporting them in their daily pages. In the wider world, Hitler had become chancellor of Germany that January, and on 17 October, the day after the game began, Einstein arrived in America. During the game, on 23 December, Japan’s crown prince (later Emperor Akihito) was born.","1":"Black 1 is the 3-3 point in the top right (R17), where the third lines from both edges cross. One stone there almost surely secures the corner territory. In return it has little power towards the outside, and at the time it was thought “not a move to play first”. Against the Meijin, Go Seigen began with a 3-3 right away. Times have changed: today the 3-3 point is an ordinary move even early in the game.","2":"White 2 is the 3-4 point (komoku) in the top left (C16), where the third and fourth lines cross. It is the most basic opening move, played since the Edo period. The Meijin showed no surprise and chose his usual move. Shusai, born in 1874 as Tamura Yasuhisa, was the 21st Honinbo and became Meijin in 1914. He was the last Honinbo to inherit the title through the house. In 1936 he handed the name “Honinbo” over to the Nihon Ki-in, and from this came the Honinbo tournament, where players win the name by strength. His retirement game in 1938 was against Kitani Minoru, Go Seigen’s friend, and it is told in the novel “The Master of Go” by the writer Kawabata Yasunari.","3":"Black 3 is the star point in the bottom left (D4), where the two fourth lines cross. Unlike the 3-3, it values spreading outwards more than territory. Diagonally opposite the 3-3, Black placed a stone of a quite different character. Go Seigen was born in 1914 in Fuzhou, Fujian, China. In 1928, at fourteen, he came to Japan at the invitation of the professional Segoe Kensaku, helped by the businessman Okura Kishichiro and by Inukai Tsuyoshi, who later became prime minister.","4":"White 4 takes the bottom-right corner with another 3-4 point (Q3). White plans to take the corners one by one and get ahead in territory. Whatever new things Black tries, the Meijin’s plan does not waver.","5":"Black 5 goes to the very centre of the board: tengen (K10). Tengen is equally close to every side, so it can lend strength to a fight in any direction. But it hardly becomes territory, so almost no one played it in the opening. 3-3, star point, tengen: with three stones Black displayed three ideas side by side — corner territory, outward spread, and central power. This daring opening caused a sensation, and it is said that some people even called it disrespectful to the Meijin. Shin-fuseki, the “new opening”, took shape that summer of 1933, when Go Seigen and Kitani Minoru studied together at Jigokudani hot spring in Nagano. In the autumn ranking games the two played it and finished first and second, and in 1934 the book “A Revolution in Go: The New Opening” came out and sold 100,000 copies. Tengen on move 1 had in fact been tried before: in 1670, Shibukawa Harumi (Yasui Santetsu), who was also an astronomer, is said to have opened on tengen against Honinbo Dosaku in a castle game at Edo Castle, and lost by 9 points. The shin-fuseki boom calmed down within a few years, but its idea of valuing the centre was handed on to later players.","6":"White 6 (Q16) is a “shoulder hit”, leaning diagonally on Black’s 3-3 stone in the top right. White wants to shut Black in low in the corner and build a thick wall outside. This is the standard way to meet a 3-3, and the Meijin went straight for the starting point of Black’s new opening.","8":"Black 7 (R16) crawls and White 8 (Q15) extends. Black widens its living space in the corner while White stretches its wall towards the right side. Black does not push outwards here because it has clearly given the 3-3 stone the job of “corner territory”.","9":"Black 9 (P18) jumps along the second line on the top side, widening the corner territory and making a firm living shape. Now the top right is clearly divided: the corner is Black’s territory, the outside is White’s thickness.","10":"White 10 (R6) sits where it works with both the wall in the top right (Q16, Q15) above and the stone in the bottom right (Q3) below. It is a calm, large move aiming to make the whole right side White’s domain.","11":"Black 11 (K4) is the middle of the bottom side, on the fourth line right below tengen. Lined up vertically with K10, it aims at a large framework from the bottom side up into the centre. All of Black’s stones stand high, showing clearly that Black values influence over territory.","12":"White 12 (M3) approaches from the bottom-right corner, landing on the third line just right of Black’s K4. It is a move to get a foothold early so that Black cannot enclose the bottom side. Left alone, Black might play around here next and turn the bottom side into a big territory.","13":"Black 13 (D10) takes the middle of the left side. With D4 and D10 on the left and K4 and K10 in the centre, Black’s stones now spread like a net over the lower-left half of the board. Not answering White 12 at once and taking a big point first is very much the spirit of the New Opening.","14":"White 14 (E16) adds a second stone in the top-left corner, enclosing it (a shimari). The Meijin is not distracted by Black’s new style and keeps making corners surely his own. By now the plans are sharply split: White has two corners and the right side, Black two corners and the centre. Traditional go valued the order “corners first, then sides, and the centre last”. Shusai plays just as that idea says, while Black, in the new-opening way, looks to the centre first. Two eras’ ideas are colliding on a single board.","15":"Black 15 (M5) caps White’s M3 from above. Black wants to press White down low and build a wall outside. The idea: even if White gets some territory on the bottom side, Black turns that into power in the centre.","18":"White 16 (O3) widens the foothold towards the White stone at Q3, and with Black 17 (L3) and White 18 (M4) the stones clash on the bottom side. White makes a living shape while securing the third line; Black presses from above. Both sides keep to their own plans.","20":"White 20 (C12) slides from the top-left enclosure down the left side along the third line. It approaches Black’s D10 and stops the whole left side from becoming Black’s. The Meijin’s method was to take territory bit by bit from the edges before Black’s framework could grow. In this game Shusai was allowed to adjourn on his own turn, and there were as many as 13 adjournments. Some criticised this, saying he could talk things over with his pupils during the breaks, and from Shusai’s retirement game in 1938 the “sealed move” came into use: when play stops, the next move is written down and sealed in an envelope.","21":"Black 21 (G10) sits exactly between D10 on the left side and K10 at tengen. Black’s stones now stand in a row at fourth-line height, forming a big wall from the left side into the centre. Black ignores White’s territory and keeps widening its central influence.","22":"White 22 (F3) approaches Black’s star point D4 in the bottom left from the bottom side (a kakari). White already has stones in the bottom right and on the bottom side, so making territory here too would give White the whole bottom edge. It shaves the lowest rim of Black’s big framework.","25":"Black 23 (F5) caps White’s F3; when White 24 (J3) extends right along the bottom, Black 25 (G4) presses from above again. White on the third line, Black on the fourth and fifth: the pattern of this game — White takes territory, Black takes walls — repeats here too.","26":"White 26 (C3) is the 3-3 point inside Black’s star point D4 in the bottom left. Slipping in right under the star stone, White goes for the corner territory. A star point has strong outward power, but its weakness is that it cannot keep the corner when the 3-3 is invaded. This 3-3 invasion under a star point is still a common start of star-point joseki in the opening today.","28":"Black 27 (C4) blocks from outside and White 28 (D3) makes a living shape in the corner. Black gives the corner territory to White and in return gains a thick wall facing the centre: C4, D4, F5, G4. Looking over the opening, White has territory in the top-left, bottom-right and bottom-left corners and on the bottom side, while Black has the top-right corner and central influence. How much of that central influence becomes territory will decide the game from here on.","29":"Black 29 (O16) is played on the top side, to the left of White’s wall in the top right (Q16, Q15). It keeps the wall from working along the top side and widens Black’s top-right corner along the top edge. It stands at a sensible distance, not too close to White’s thickness.","30":"White 30 (Q12) extends down the right side from the top-right wall and makes a base (a foothold for life). Now the top-right White stones and R6 can link up more easily, and the right side starts to look like White territory.","31":"Black 31 (Q10) lands on the right-side star point, right between White’s Q12 and R6. It stops White from turning the whole right side into territory, and it also works together with Black’s central influence. From here the right side becomes a battlefield where Black and White stones mix.","33":"White 32 (O15) attaches under Black’s O16, shaking the root of Black’s top-side stone. Black 33 (N15) blocks from the left, closing White’s road to the left of the top side and the centre. By keeping White’s stones from getting out, Black wants the top side as its sphere of influence.","35":"When White 34 (N14) extends downwards, Black 35 (O14) cuts between White’s O15 and N14. Cut apart, White’s stones split into two, and each becomes weak. Black makes White fight inside Black’s own sphere, hoping to profit while attacking.","37":"After White 36 (O13), Black 37 (P15) captures White’s O15 — the first prisoner of the game. White 38 (P14) is answered by Black 39 (O15), connecting, and Black’s top-right stones are now firmly joined to the top side. White lost a stone but has poked out into the centre with O13, N13 and N14.","40":"White 40 (O10) jumps far into the centre from the O13 stone, landing between Black’s Q10 on the right side and tengen. White now has a foothold inside Black’s central framework. For Black, there is also the pleasure of attacking these White stones inside its own sphere.","41":"Black 41 (P7) is played low on the right side, pressing White’s R6 from above and working with Q10. Now White’s right-side stones, Q12 above and R6 below, are squeezed by Black and hard to connect. Black is trying to shrink White’s territory on the right side as well.","43":"After White 42 (N13) connects and settles the central White stones, Black 43 (L15) extends left along the top side. Black’s top-side wall grows longer, and the area from the top side into the centre becomes Black’s sphere. Black does not rush the fight and first enlarges its own domain.","44":"White 44 (H17) extends from the top-left enclosure along the top side, stopping Black’s top-side influence before it can grow further. Here too the Meijin places a stone on the side, where territory is easy to make, before Black’s framework spreads. Another rim of Black’s big framework has been taken by White.","45":"Black 45 (R7) attaches right on top of White’s R6 on the right side. This starts the first big fight of the game. Black aims to cut R6 off from the White stones above (Q12) and split White’s right side in two. White does not back down either, and from here a tricky shape of mutually cut stones continues from the right side into the bottom right.","48":"White 46 (S7), Black 47 (S6), White 48 (S8). White places stones on the second line below and above Black’s R7 to gain strength around it. Black’s S6 goes round to the lower right of White’s R6, taking away the space R6 needed to live easily along the edge. Each side is poking at the other’s weak points.","52":"Black 49 (S5), White 50 (Q7), Black 51 (R8), White 52 (R9). White’s Q7 cuts in between Black’s R7 and P7, and with Black R8 and White R9 the stones end up mutually cut. On the right side both sides’ stones remain cut, a hard fight in which whoever is left with a weak group first will suffer.","55":"Black 53 (Q8), White 54 (Q6), Black 55 (Q9). Black extends to Q8 and Q9, linking R7 and R8 to Q10 above. White’s Q6 gathers the lower White stones R6 and Q7 into one group. Black’s right-side stones are now one chain, but they are surrounded by White and do not have many liberties.","58":"White 56 (R10), Black 57 (O8), White 58 (Q11). With R10 and Q11 White joins the upper right-side stone (Q12) with R9 and S8 along the edge. Black’s O8 strengthens the central side and blocks White’s way into the centre. White’s right side links up and down, Black links to the centre, and the fight calms for a while.","59":"Black 59 (R4) now invades the bottom-right corner, going into the territory of White’s Q3. It also aims to link with Black’s S5 and S6 above, threatening the footing of White’s R6, Q6 and Q7. For White it becomes a hard choice: protect the corner territory, or protect the stones outside.","63":"White 60 (P6), Black 61 (O6), White 62 (O5), Black 63 (N5). With P6 and O5 White goes to join its right-side stones to those below, and Black presses from above and from the left with O6 and N5. Through O6, Black brings its bottom wall (M5) close to its right-side stones P7 and O8. White chose to protect the stones outside, leaving Black inside the bottom-right corner.","66":"With White 64 (P4) White firmly joins the stones that came down the right side to Q3 below. Black 65 (R2) and White 66 (R3) continue the tussle inside the corner. Black is trying to make room to live in the corner on the low first and second lines.","69":"Black 67 (S3), White 68 (Q2), Black 69 (S2). Black connects its stones along the edge of the bottom-right corner and makes a living shape. White’s bottom-right corner became much smaller than expected, but in return White’s stones are thickly connected outside (P4, P6, Q6). Black cut into territory, White strengthened the outside: a result both sides can accept.","70":"White 70 (N6) cuts in between Black’s O6 and N5, at the seam between Black’s bottom-side and right-side stones. The aim is to split Black’s stones and weaken one part. Black 71 (O7) connects at once, keeping the right-side Black stones together.","74":"After White 72 (M7) and Black 73 (M8), White 74 (L4) pushes in between Black’s K4 and L5 on the bottom side, trying to leave Black’s L3 stranded among White’s bottom stones. Black 75 (N4) and White 76 (N3) follow, and White’s bottom stones join the White stones on the right along the third line.","78":"Black 77 (J4) links Black’s bottom wall to the left, and White 78 (K3) blocks the way out for Black’s L3. The L3 stone now has just one liberty (L2). Black gives it up and puts first the long wall facing the centre: J4, K4, L5, M5, N5, N4. Rather than clinging to a small stone, Black protects the big plan.","79":"Black 79 (L9) is played below and to the right of tengen (K10), making the central Black stones thicker still. Black’s bottom wall, the right-side stones from O6 to Q10, tengen, and the left-side stones from D10 to G10 now ring a huge central framework. White earns territory in the corners and on the sides; the question is how much of the centre Black can turn into territory. The position seems close to even, and the game now enters the middle-game fighting.","80":"White 80 (C6) climbs from the bottom-left White corner up the left side and places a stone on the third line beside Black’s wall. It narrows Black’s left side and will also serve as a foothold for an invasion there later. Black 81 (E4) mends the gap between D4 and F5/G4, making the wall one piece.","82":"White 82 (C10) invades by attaching to the left of Black’s D10, on the edge side. If the whole left side became Black’s territory, Black’s central framework would be very large. White will not allow that and tries to live at the far left end of Black’s formation.","85":"Black 83 (C9), White 84 (D9), Black 85 (C8), White 86 (D8). Black blocks under White’s C10 from the edge side, while White extends D9 and D8 on the inner side of Black’s wall. Each side is pushing the other against the edge. Neither side’s stones can be called alive yet.","88":"Black 87 (C11) is a severe move that cuts between White’s C10 and C12. White 88 (B10) drops towards the edge, widening the space for the C10 stone to live. Black’s aim is to cut off the invading White stones and attack them.","91":"Black 89 (B11), White 90 (D11), Black 91 (E10). Black extends the cutting stone with B11, but White’s D11 presses on the head of Black’s D10 from above. Black’s E10 links D10 towards tengen and protects the left-side wall. Black’s cutting stones B11 and C11 are caught between White stones and running short of liberties.","96":"With White 92 (B12), Black’s two stones B11 and C11 are down to one liberty. Black does not save them; Black 93 (D7) instead blocks under White’s D9 and D8. White 94 (E11), Black 95 (F10) and White 96 (C7) follow, and on the left side both sides are now trying to capture the other’s stones. Black boldly chose capturing over rescuing.","98":"After Black 97 (A9), White 98 (A11) captures the two Black stones B11 and C11. White makes a living shape on the edge of the left side, and Black’s left-side territory is smaller than expected. In return, after Black 99 (E8) White’s D9 and D8 have only one liberty (E9), and Black can capture them whenever it likes. Two stones lost, two stones won: the left side ended roughly even.","100":"White 100 (B4) widens White’s bottom-left corner while pressing under Black’s C4. With B4 and, soon, C5, White’s corner stones C3 and D3 link up with C6 and C7 on the left side. The White stones from the bottom left up the left side become one strong group.","101":"Black 101 (S14) slides from Black’s top-right corner down the right side, reducing the top part of White’s right-side territory (Q12, Q11). While White 102 (C5) secures the bottom left, Black goes one step further with 103 (R13), and White 104 (S12) just about protects the right-side territory. Bit by bit, the borders between the two sides’ territories are being fixed.","105":"Black 105 (K17) is played in the middle of the top side, to the right of White’s H17. It stops White’s top-side territory from spreading further right and works with Black’s top-side stones L15 and N15. The top side looks set for a fight over the border: White on the left, Black on the right.","106":"White 106 (G3) now attaches on the bottom side, right under Black’s G4. White wants to fill the gap between F3 and J3 with its own stone and firm up the bottom-side territory. Black 107 (E3) goes in between White’s D3 and F3 to cut. It may look small, but this exchange decides where the borders of territory lie.","108":"White 108 (E6) is played just above Black’s bottom-left wall, a little above D4 and E4. Working with White’s C5 and C6 on the left side, it pokes at the back of Black’s wall. Black 109 (F6) immediately presses from above, stopping White from coming out into the centre.","113":"White 110 (E2), Black 111 (F2) and White 112 (D2) continue the tussle for stones on the bottom edge, and Black 113 (H3) cuts in between White’s G3 and J3. It tries to break White’s bottom-side stones apart and shrink White’s territory. Black is trying to reduce, even a little, the White territory under its own wall.","116":"Black 115 (H4) connects H3 to the wall, and White 116 (G2) links F3 and G3 from below. Black’s F2 stone is down to one liberty (F1) and is all but captured. Though poked at its gaps, White’s bottom side has held together as one.","117":"Black 117 (F17) invades on the third line of the top side, between White’s top-left enclosure (C16, E16) and White’s H17. It cuts into White’s top-side territory and, if possible, aims to split White’s stones and attack them. A new fight begins on the left half of the top side.","120":"White 118 (J17) strengthens the top-side stones, and Black 119 (D17) goes even deeper into the top-left corner. White 120 (C17) protects the corner. Black is trying to take the very top of White’s corner, around the second line. White guards the corner while getting ready to squeeze Black from outside.","124":"Black 121 (G16), White 122 (H15), Black 123 (G15), White 124 (K15). With G16 and G15 Black extends towards the centre to separate the top-left White stones from White’s H17. With H15 and K15 White links its top-side stones towards the centre and threatens Black’s K17 on the top side. In this top-side fight, Black and White stones keep poking their heads out in turn.","127":"Black 125 (H16), White 126 (J16), Black 127 (H14). Black presses on White’s H15 from above with H16 and from below with H14. The H15 stone is surrounded and has only one liberty left (J15). Black takes the upper centre for itself while weakening White’s stones one by one.","130":"White 128 (L16), Black 129 (M15), White 130 (L17). With L16 and L17 White shuts Black’s K17 in on the top side and gives its top-side stones a living shape. Black’s K17 can hardly move any more. The result: Black gains the upper centre, White keeps the top-side territory.","133":"Black 131 (P13), White 132 (P12), Black 133 (Q13). Black presses the base of White’s right-side stones and cuts White’s top-right stones Q16, Q15 and P14 off from White’s Q12 on the right side. Those top-right White stones are surrounded by Black and in a painful shape. The top-right corner is becoming fairly secure as Black territory.","135":"White 134 (E7) steps from the bottom left into the inside of Black’s wall, and Black 135 (E9) captures White’s two stones D9 and D8. Black had earlier lost two stones on the left side, so this wins back the same number. Black’s left-side stones are now safe, and Black’s territory from the left side into the centre is becoming clearer.","137":"White 136 (F7), Black 137 (G7). White lines up E6, E7 and F7 above Black’s bottom-left wall, trying to come out into the centre, and Black stops it with G7. Black’s central framework is being shaved bit by bit from below too. White does not charge in deeply; its plan is to shrink the edges one by one.","138":"White 138 (N18) goes in on the second line on the right part of the top side, to the left of Black’s P18 and O16. It shrinks Black’s top-right territory from the top-side direction. Later White 148 (O18) pushes further right, and Black’s top-side territory is shaved little by little.","139":"Black 139 (C18) now invades on the second line above White’s top-left corner. Above White’s corner (C16, C17, E16) there is still room for Black to live. If Black lives here, White’s top-left territory will shrink a lot. It is a bold move, trying to live right next to White’s thick stones.","145":"White 140 (E17), Black 141 (E18), White 142 (D16), Black 143 (D18), White 144 (G18), Black 145 (B17). Black lines up C18, D18 and E18 on the second line and goes deep into the corner with B17. White firms up the outside with E17 and D16, and stops Black from spreading right with G18. Above White’s corner, a delicate sequence continues as Black tries to live in a narrow space.","150":"White 146 (B16), Black 147 (B18), White 148 (O18), Black 149 (P19), White 150 (F18). Black gathers its stones above the top-left corner, and White tightens from outside with moves such as F18. White’s top-left corner is now only the part below the line from B16 to E16. Black has kept its stones alive inside White’s area and greatly reduced White’s top-left territory. Meanwhile White has also shaved Black’s territory in the top right with O18, and the fine exchanges go on.","152":"White 152 (G12): White finally goes inside Black’s central framework. G12 lies between White’s left-side stones (E11, D11) and the White stones above, in a gap in Black’s thickness. If White lives here or links up with the White stones around it, Black’s centre will not become territory. From here comes the biggest climax of the game.","153":"Black 153 (K14) is played right under White’s top-side stone K15, blocking the road by which White could link G12 to the top-side stones. At the same time it is a broad road joining Black’s central stones above and below. Black wants to split White into top-side and central parts and attack one of them.","156":"White 154 (J15) rescues White’s H15 on the top side, and after Black 155 (F14) comes White 156 (H11). White puts another stone in the centre and, working with G12, widens its foothold inside Black’s centre. Black’s centre is being seeped into, bit by bit, by White from above and from the left.","159":"White 158 (E5) pokes at the inside of Black’s bottom-left wall. If White went on to play F4, Black’s wall would be cut between E4 and F5. So Black 159 (F4) connects solidly. Before the central fight, Black mends the weak points of its wall one by one.","160":"White 160 (H8): the most famous move of the game. In the wide space at the bottom of Black’s central framework, surrounded by Black stones such as G7, G10, K10 and L9, White places a single stone. This stone is said to aim at several gaps in the links between Black’s stones, and Black cannot guard them all at once. With this move as a trigger, White starts to work its way into Black’s central territory one point after another. A rumour spread that a pupil, not Shusai himself, had found the move, but nothing about that is certain.","161":"Black 161 (J8) is played to the right of H8, stopping White from spreading below tengen, between it and Black’s bottom wall. It is a normal reply that guards the place that looks biggest. But White’s next aim lay somewhere else.","162":"White 162 (K12) now lands above tengen (K10). Working with White’s G12 and H11, it tries to split Black’s centre into an upper and a lower part. A shake-up below with H8, then straight round to the top: each time Black guards one spot, White touches another weak point — a skilful sequence.","165":"Black 163 (F12) is played to the left of G12, trying to separate White’s G12 from the White stones on the left side. White 164 (F11) seeks a connection, and Black 165 (H10) blocks under White’s H11 so that White’s central stones cannot come out downwards. In the centre, Black and White stones are tangled, each trying to cut the other.","168":"White 166 (J14), Black 167 (J12), White 168 (J11). With J14 White extends the top-side stones downwards, and with J11 it links to the central White stone H11. Black’s J12 is a stone that cuts White’s upper and lower parts. The centre has become a very intricate fight in which many stones are cut and connected.","172":"Black 169 (J13), White 170 (K13), Black 171 (K11), White 172 (F13). With F13 White reaches towards the White stones on the left, trying to link its central stones with the White stones on the left side and top left. What used to be Black’s central thickness is turning into a road for White’s stones to pass through.","175":"Black 173 (L14) rescues the single Black stone at K14, then White 174 (J10) and Black 175 (J9). White’s J10 enters right next to tengen on the left, and Black blocks from below with J9. White stones have reached the point just left of tengen (K10), and Black’s central territory keeps getting smaller.","178":"White 176 (H12) connects the central White stones, and after Black 177 (G14) comes White 178 (N9). This time White enters the right part of the centre, between Black’s L9 and O8. Black’s centre has been entered by White from both left and right, and it no longer looks likely to become a big territory. White does not miss Black’s weak points on the other side of the board either.","180":"After Black 179 (D14) protects the top-left Black stones, White 180 (N8) extends further down. With N9 and N8 White tries to separate Black’s right-side stones from O6 to O8 from Black’s central stones L9 and M8. Black’s right-side stones are surrounded by White and short of liberties, and this is becoming a new weak point.","186":"After exchanges on the right edge with Black 181 (T7) and White 182 (T9), Black 183 (N7) blocks under White’s N8; after White 184 (D13) and Black 185 (M11) comes White 186 (P8). It is a sharp move cutting between Black’s P7 and Q8. Black’s five stones O6, O7, O8, P7 and N7 on the right side are down to one liberty (O9), and the five Black stones from R7 to Q10 have only two. With one move White aimed at two Black groups at the same time.","189":"Black 187 (M6) captures White’s N6, and after White 188 (O9), Black 189 (L7) captures White’s M7. Now the five Black stones from O6 to P7 gain liberties and are saved by linking with the Black stones on the bottom side. Black has captured two White stones, but meanwhile, with O9, White has reached round to the other Black group on the right side.","192":"After White 190 (F8) and Black 191 (G8) comes White 192 (P10). Black’s five stones R7, R8, Q8, Q9 and Q10 on the right side are down to a single liberty, P9. Even if Black plays P9 and captures White’s P8, White can play back at P8 at once and capture the whole lot (a “snapback”), so these five stones can no longer be saved. Starting from the cut at White 186, White made a big gain on the right side. Part of the influence Black built towards the centre has become not territory but White’s prey.","197":"After Black 193 (R12), White 194 (S11), Black 195 (C14) and White 196 (E14) comes Black 197 (G11). White’s five central stones G12, H12, H11, J11 and J10 are down to a single liberty, G13. Black wants to capture White in the centre and win back the loss on the right side. Whether White’s central stones survive will decide the game.","198":"White 198 (E12) captures Black’s F12. Now White’s five central stones also have a liberty at F12, and they can link to the White stones on the left either through F12 or through G13. If Black blocks one, White connects through the other, so White’s central stones can no longer be captured. The White stones that split Black’s thickness have survived: this is the scene that decided the game.","203":"With Black 199 (E15), White 200 (E13), Black 201 (D15) and White 202 (B14) the upper left side is settled too, and from Black 203 (B3) the game enters the yose (the endgame stage in which the borders of territory are fixed). Black’s B3 enters White’s bottom-left corner on the first and second lines, trying to shave even a little off White’s territory. From here, fine moves over one or two points follow.","206":"Black 205 (Q1), White 206 (P1), Black 207 (R1), White 208 (P3). On the first line of the bottom-right corner Black shaves a little off White’s territory, and White firmly protects its corner. In the endgame, a single move on the first line makes a difference of one or two points. Even with the big fights over, neither the Meijin nor Go Seigen relaxes.","214":"With Black 209 (B5) and White 210 (A3), White captures Black’s B3; with Black 211 (A6), White 212 (B6), Black 213 (A7) and White 214 (A5), White captures Black’s B5 as well. On the bottom-left edge White takes two stones and fully protects its territory. Black also trims White’s territory higher up the left edge (A6, A7), so each side gains a little.","220":"Black 215 (S13), White 216 (N11), Black 217 (R11), White 218 (M12), Black 219 (L12), White 220 (M10). On the right side of the centre too, White advances with N11, M12 and M10, making the territory left in Black’s centre even smaller. The Black territory that was supposed to spread around tengen is now just a thin strip.","224":"With Black 221 (S10), White 222 (S9), Black 223 (T12) and White 224 (T10), White captures Black’s S10 on the right edge. White’s right-side territory has been completed without giving up a single point. Even when Black tries to win something back, White answers carefully and shows no gap at all.","232":"After Black 225 (B13), White 226 (A15), Black 227 (C13), White 228 (A13), Black 229 (F15), White 230 (G17) and Black 231 (F16) comes White 232 (C15). With this C15, Black’s large group of 18 stones stretching from the left of the top side into the centre is down to a single liberty, G13. While playing endgame moves on the left side, White has created an unexpected danger for Black’s big group. Black must act at once.","233":"Black 233 (L13) captures White’s two stones K12 and K13. Now Black’s big group gains liberties at K12 and K13, links up with the central Black stones and is safe. It was a close call, but by taking these two stones Black protected the big group. Capturing it would have won the game outright for the Meijin, but Go Seigen did not miss the danger.","246":"White 234 (N17), Black 235 (L11), White 236 (F9) … White 244 (G13), Black 245 (F19), White 246 (G19). With G13 White links its central stones to F13, and with F19 and G19 the border on the top edge is settled as well. Only small endgame moves worth one or two points remain. Counting the territory on the board, the difference is tiny.","249":"After White 248 (A16), Black 249 (M13) puts White’s M12 in atari (one move from capture), and White 250 (N12) connects. The last border in the centre is fixed too. Black 251 (R5) tidies up Black’s shape in the bottom right, and only a few moves remain.","252":"With White 252 (M9) the game ends. White won by 2 points. With the new opening of 3-3, star point and tengen, Black, Go Seigen, built great influence in the centre. But the Meijin, Shusai, took solid territory in the corners and on the sides, and in the late middle game, from move 152, he entered Black’s centre. Shaking the centre from move 160 at H8, and turning the cut at P8 on move 186 into the capture of five Black stones on the right side, were decisive, and Black’s central influence did not become as much territory as hoped. In the no-komi era Black, moving first, was thought to have the advantage, so winning with White shows the Meijin’s true strength. On the other hand, by playing the Meijin to within just 2 points at 19, Go Seigen made the New Opening leave a deep mark on the go world. Later, in ten-game matches, Go Seigen beat the top players of the day one after another and became the leading player of the Showa era."}},"century1956":{"t":"The Game of the Century","w":"Donald Byrne","b":"Bobby Fischer","ev":"The third Rosenwald Trophy tournament (Rosenwald Memorial)","d":"1956","pl":"United States (New York)","lead":"Thirteen-year-old Bobby Fischer beats Donald Byrne, one of America's strongest masters. On move 17 Fischer lets his queen be taken for nothing, then wins piece after piece back, and at the end his small pieces hunt down White's king. Fischer later became world champion.","c":{"0":"October 17, 1956: a game from the Rosenwald Trophy tournament (the Rosenwald Memorial), held at the Marshall Chess Club in New York. It was a strong event for invited top American players, and Bobby Fischer, playing Black, was only 13 years old, the youngest player there. White is Donald Byrne, 26, winner of the 1953 U.S. Open and one of the leading American masters of the time. Fischer’s overall result in the tournament was modest, but this one game made his name known around the world. In the magazine Chess Review, Hans Kmoch called it “The Game of the Century”, and the name is still used today. Let’s follow it move by move with Musowl and see how a 13-year-old boy beat a grown master.","1":"White plays the knight to f3. It controls the central squares e5 and d4 and helps guard the king’s side: a very calm start. White does not commit any pawns yet, so many different set-ups are still possible. Later, Byrne represented the USA at three Chess Olympiads and earned the International Master title in 1962.","2":"Fischer also plays his knight to f6. It controls e4 and d5. Like White, he keeps his plans flexible and waits to see what his opponent does. Fischer was born in Chicago on 9 March 1943. His family moved to Brooklyn in New York, and when he was six, he and his sister Joan, five years older, learned the rules from the instructions that came with a chess set they had been given.","3":"White plays c4. This queen’s-side pawn attacks the d5 square from the side. The idea is to make it harder for Black to put a pawn on d5 and to gain space in the centre. In November of this year, 1956, the first Olympic Games in the Southern Hemisphere were held in Melbourne, Australia.","4":"Black plays g6. Next the bishop will go to g7 and look at the centre along the long diagonal. Fischer loved this kind of set-up from a young age: it holds back at first and then strikes back hard. When Fischer was seven, after losing to a master in a simultaneous exhibition, he joined the Brooklyn Chess Club and hardly ever missed its Friday evenings. At twelve he also joined the Manhattan Chess Club and grew stronger very quickly.","5":"White brings the other knight to c3. It controls d5 and e4 even more, and later White can line up pawns in the centre with d4 and e4.","6":"The bishop goes to g7. For now it stands behind the knight on f6, but as soon as the knight moves, the long diagonal from h8 to a1 opens up. Black is also ready to castle.","7":"White plays d4. With pawns on c4 and d4, White takes the centre. If Black answers with d5, the game becomes an opening called the Grünfeld Defence.","8":"Black castles. The king moves to safety on g8, and the rook on f8 comes closer to the centre. Young Fischer makes his king safe first, before the fighting starts. After this, Fischer improved at tremendous speed: he won the US Championship that started the next year, and in 1958, at 15, he became the youngest grandmaster in history up to then.","9":"White plays the bishop to f4. It eyes c7 and b8 along the diagonal and also controls e5. But White’s king is still in the middle on e1, and White is not yet ready to castle.","10":"Black plays d5 and hits the pawn on c4 in the centre. Now it is a Grünfeld Defence. Black’s plan is to let White have the centre for a while and then attack it later. Although the game began with 1.Nf3 and a different move order, it has now reached the 5.Bf4 Grünfeld (the line classified as D92). The name comes from Ernst Grünfeld, the Austrian master who made this defence popular in the 1920s.","11":"White’s queen comes out early to b3. It puts more pressure on d5 and also aims at the pawn on b7 down the b-file. It is a strong move, but a queen that comes out early can easily be chased around by enemy pieces.","12":"Black takes the pawn on c4. The pawn that captured now attacks the queen on b3 diagonally, so White has to take it back. In this way Black removes one of White’s central pawns.","13":"White’s queen recaptures on c4. The queen now stands near the middle of the board and looks at d5 and f7. But this queen will be chased by Black’s pieces again and again.","14":"Black plays c6. It controls d5 and b5, so White’s queen and knight cannot go there. It also prepares b5 later to chase the queen.","15":"White plays e4. Two pawns, on d4 and e4, now stand side by side in the centre. Just from the look of it, White has lots of space and seems to be playing comfortably.","16":"Black plays the knight to d7. From here it plans to jump to b6 and chase the queen. It also helps prepare the pawn move e5 to hit White’s centre. Analysts consider this 8...Nbd7 a little slow; in later games players preferred lines such as 8...b5 followed by bringing the queen to a5.","17":"White plays the rook to d1. It supports the pawn on d4 from behind and makes the d-file strong. But because the rook moved before White castled, the king is still stuck in the middle on e1.","18":"The knight jumps to b6 and attacks the queen on c4. White’s queen has to move again. Black is bringing pieces forward while making White waste moves.","19":"The queen escapes to c5. From c5 it eyes the pawn on e7 and also tries to hold back Black’s queen’s side. But the queen is now far forward on the board, in a place where Black’s pieces can easily attack it. Analysts say Qd3 would have been better than this Qc5. On c5 the queen is easily hit by Black's knight jumping to a4 or e4.","20":"The bishop goes to g4 and attacks the knight on f3. Behind the knight is the rook on d1, so if the knight moves, the rook will be taken: this is called a pin. The knight on f3 guards important squares like d4 and e5, so now White can hardly move it.","21":"White plays the bishop to g5. It attacks the knight on f6 and tries to stop Black from moving that knight and playing e5. But White still has pieces at home and has not castled. Many people say it would have been better to play the bishop to e2 and get ready to castle. In a later game, in 1991, White played 11.Be2 here and got a slightly more comfortable position.","22":"The knight goes to a4! This is the first big surprise of the game. The knight on a4 attacks both the queen on c5 and the knight on c3. If White takes it with the c3 knight, Black takes the pawn on e4 with the f6 knight and attacks the queen on c5 and the bishop on g5 at the same time. White’s king would still be in the middle under attack, so White cannot take the knight. It is said that, after this move, people gathered around the board to watch.","23":"White does not take the knight and moves the queen to a3 instead. From there it still eyes the pawn on e7 along the diagonal. White picked the way of defending that does the least damage.","24":"The knight takes the knight on c3. That knight was the piece that guarded the pawn on e4. So Black has now removed the defender of e4.","25":"White recaptures with a pawn. The material is equal, but White’s pawns on c3 and d4 are now a little loose. And nothing is guarding the pawn on e4 any more.","26":"The knight takes the pawn on e4! From e4 it attacks the pawn on c3 and the bishop on g5. White’s neat pawns in the centre have broken up, the e-file is opening, and the space in front of White’s king in the middle is now wide open.","27":"White takes the pawn on e7 with the bishop. From e7 it attacks Black’s queen on d8 and the rook on f8 at the same time: a double attack. White wins back the pawn and attacks Black’s big pieces. A strong counterattack.","28":"Black moves the queen to b6. It is a bold move that says: you can have the rook on f8. If White takes the rook, Black recaptures with the bishop, and that bishop then chases the queen on a3. Black thinks that White’s king still being in the middle matters more than a rook. The queen on b6 also eyes the pawn on d4.","29":"White does not take the rook and brings the bishop to c4 instead. This empties f1, so White can castle next move and take the king to safety. White also knows that king safety is the biggest problem.","30":"The knight takes the pawn on c3! From here it attacks the rook on d1. If White takes this knight with the queen, Black can bring a rook to e8 and attack the bishop on e7 and the king behind it in a straight line. Black’s pieces are gathering one after another around White’s king.","31":"White plays the bishop to c5 and attacks Black’s queen on b6. If Black simply moves the queen away, White can castle next move. But because the bishop left e7, the e-file is now open for Black’s rook.","32":"The rook moves to e8: check along the e-file! The bishop on e7 has gone to c5, so the e-file was open all the way from e8 to e1. Before saving the queen, Black first goes after White’s king.","33":"The king goes to f1. Now White can never castle. The rook on h1 is also stuck behind the king and has trouble getting out. Black has gained a moment, but the queen on b6 is still attacked by the bishop on c5.","34":"The bishop goes to e6! This is the most famous move of the game: Black lets the queen be taken for nothing. The bishop on e6 attacks White’s bishop on c4, and taking it will be check. If White takes on e6 with the c4 bishop, Black checks with the queen from b5, keeps checking with the knight, and at the end the king is surrounded by its own pieces and cannot move: a “smothered mate”. Fischer wrote that moving the knight to b5 instead would have lost to White taking the pawn on f7. A 13-year-old boy had looked far ahead, well past giving up his queen.","35":"White takes Black’s queen. If you only count pieces, White is far ahead. But now Black’s pieces will chase White’s king and win back piece after piece.","36":"The bishop takes White’s bishop on c4, with check! From c4 it aims through d3 and e2 at the king on f1. Black’s counterattack begins here.","37":"The king escapes to g1. Black’s rook controls the e-file, so there is almost nowhere else to go. On g1 the king is shut in by the rook on h1 and the pawn on g2 and can hardly move.","38":"The knight jumps to e2 with check. The king has to go back to f1. Black ties White’s hands with checks while getting ready for the next capture.","39":"The king goes back to f1. Now the knight on e2 stands in front of the bishop on c4. If this knight moves, the bishop behind it will give check to the king on f1: a “discovered check”.","40":"The knight takes the pawn on d4: discovered check! Because the knight moved, the bishop on c4 now checks the king on f1. When you are in check, you must deal with the king first, no matter what the knight is doing.","41":"The king goes to g1. White keeps getting checked, so there is no time to do anything else.","42":"The knight jumps to e2 again with check. The king has to go back to f1. Black can give check again and again, and each time looks for a chance to win something.","43":"The king goes to f1 again. White can only repeat the same moves. This pattern of checking, grabbing a piece and coming back is called a “windmill”, because it goes round and round.","44":"The knight moves to c3: another discovered check! The bishop on c4 checks the king on f1, and at the same time the knight on c3 attacks the rook on d1. White is in check, so the rook cannot be saved. The windmill is ready to grab another piece.","45":"The king goes to g1. Black could keep checking, but here Black stops for a moment to win back White’s bishop on b6.","46":"The pawn takes the bishop on b6. Now the bishop that took Black’s queen is gone too. Because the a7 pawn moved, the a-file is open, and the rook on a8 attacks White’s queen on a3. Count the pieces: Black has two rooks, two bishops and a knight; White has a queen, two rooks and a knight. Black can also take the rook on d1 next, so Black is getting back more than the queen was worth.","47":"White’s queen escapes to b4. From here it attacks the bishop on c4, the knight on c3 and the pawn on b6 all at once: a sharp escape. White is trying hard to win something back.","48":"The rook goes to a4! It chases the queen again. The rook on a4 is guarded by the knight on c3, and once the queen moves away, it also guards the bishop on c4. White’s queen cannot take any of Black’s pieces and has to run.","49":"White’s queen takes the pawn on b6. At least White wins back one pawn. But meanwhile it is the c3 knight’s turn to take the rook on d1.","50":"The knight takes the rook on d1. Black has two rooks, two bishops and a knight against White’s queen, rook and knight, and is clearly ahead. White’s rook on h1 is still shut in.","51":"White plays h3. This makes an escape square on h2 for the king. Once the king goes to h2, the rook on h1 can finally come out. It also stops Black from threatening White’s back rank forever.","52":"The rook takes the pawn on a2. White has no pawns left on the queen’s side, and nothing can stop Black’s pawns there any more. The rook has entered White’s second rank, where it can also aim at f2 near White’s king.","53":"The king moves to h2. Now the rook on h1 can finally move freely along the first rank. White is trying somehow to bring the remaining pieces into the game.","54":"The knight takes the pawn on f2. From f2 it attacks the rook on h1 and the pawn on h3. Another pawn near White’s king is gone.","55":"White moves the rook to e1 to escape and offers to trade it for Black’s rook on the e-file. The idea is to remove one of Black’s attacking pieces.","56":"Black trades rooks. When you have more pieces, trading usually helps you. Before taking back on e1 with the knight, White first gives check with the queen.","57":"The queen moves to d8 with check. With the rook on e8 gone, the eighth rank was open all the way to Black’s king on g8. White puts in this check first and plans to take back the rook afterwards.","58":"Black blocks the check with the bishop on f8. The bishop comes next to the king, and the defence becomes solid.","59":"The knight takes back the rook on e1. White has a queen and a knight; Black has a rook, two bishops and a knight. Adding up the strength of the pieces, Black is clearly better.","60":"The bishop goes to d5. It eyes the f3 square and the pawn on g2, and from behind along the diagonal it also guards the rook on a2. Black is gathering the pieces one by one on good squares where they support each other.","61":"The knight goes back to f3. Guarded by the pawn on g2, it is the only piece defending White’s king. With the queen and knight, White is trying to hold out against Black’s attack.","62":"The knight goes to e4. It steps into the centre and comes closer to White’s king. Next Black wants to play the f8 bishop to d6 and give check to the king on h2.","63":"White’s queen goes to b8. It attacks the pawn on b7 and, along the eighth rank, pins the f8 bishop to the king on g8. If the bishop moved, the king would be in check, so this stops Black’s plan of checking with the bishop.","64":"Black plays b5. The pawn moves two squares and escapes from the queen’s attack. On b5 it is guarded by the pawn on c6. One day Black may also push this pawn forward.","65":"White plays h4. The h-pawn goes forward, hoping to break up the pawns around Black’s king with h5 if possible. White does not have much left to do.","66":"Black plays h5 too. This stops White’s pawn and keeps the shape around Black’s king solid. Black is in no hurry and plays safely.","67":"The knight goes to e5. From the centre it attacks f7. Also, if the knight jumped to d7, the pinned bishop on f8 would be attacked by two pieces, the queen and the knight. This is White’s last hope of a counterattack.","68":"The king goes to g7. It frees the f8 bishop from the pin and also guards it. Now the bishop can move freely. Black is ready for the final attack.","69":"The king goes to g1. On h2 it could be checked by the freed bishop from d6, so it seems White stepped away first. But there are almost no defending pieces near White’s king.","70":"The bishop moves to c5: check! From c5 it aims through d4, e3 and f2 at the king on g1. Now Black’s small pieces begin to hunt White’s king.","71":"The king goes to f1. White’s king looks for a way out towards the left side of the board. But Black’s rook and bishops are waiting there, and the king is slowly driven to the other side of the board.","72":"The knight jumps to g3 with check. The king is driven to e1. The knight also blocks the king’s way back to the right side of the board.","73":"The king goes to e1. It cannot go back to f2 or g1, so it can only run to the left. Black’s net is slowly getting tighter.","74":"The other bishop comes out to b4 with check. From b4 it aims through c3 and d2 at e1. The king is driven further left, to d1.","75":"The king goes to d1. White’s queen and knight are on the far side of the board and cannot come to help the king.","76":"The bishop moves from d5 to b3 with check. From b3 it aims through c2 at d1. This bishop will later have an important job: guarding the square of the final rook move.","77":"The king goes to c1. There are almost no squares left to run to. One by one, Black’s pieces are closing the squares around White’s king.","78":"The knight jumps to e2 with check. From e2 it attacks c1. The king can only escape to b1.","79":"The king goes to b1. It has been chased almost into the corner of the board.","80":"The knight jumps to c3 with check. From c3 it attacks b1 and also controls d1. The king has to go back to c1.","81":"The king goes to c1. This will be its last square. The knight on c3 covers b1 and d1, and the rook that is about to arrive will cover b2 and d2.","82":"The rook moves to c2: checkmate! The rook on c2 is guarded by the bishop on b3, so the king cannot take it; the knight on c3 covers b1 and d1, and the rook covers b2 and d2. Three pieces, a rook, a bishop and a knight, make the mating net, and the other bishop helped chase the king here. White’s king was hunted from g1 all the way to c1 on the other side of the board, and White’s queen could do nothing to the end. Byrne could have resigned earlier, but it is said he played on to checkmate out of respect for the boy’s brilliant play. The knight leap on move 11, the decision to give up the queen on move 17, the “windmill” that won the pieces back, and a mating net woven by small pieces: after this game, Fischer became U.S. champion at 14 and world champion in 1972."}},"oshiro1849":{"t":"Shusaku’s First Castle Game","w":"Honinbo Shusaku","b":"Yasui Sanchi IX","ev":"Castle Game (the yearly game at Edo Castle)","d":"1849","pl":"Japan (Edo)","lead":"The Castle Games were the yearly showcase where players of the official go houses played at the shogun’s castle. Twenty-year-old Shusaku made his debut this year and won with Black by 11 points — the start of a perfect record of 19 wins in 19 Castle Games.","c":{"0":"Edo, 1849. Black is 20-year-old Honinbo Shusaku; White is Yasui Sanchi, the ninth head of the Yasui house. The Castle Games were official games that players of the go houses (the four families Honinbo, Inoue, Yasui and Hayashi) played for the shogun, once a year at Edo Castle. It was the grandest stage for a house player, and a loss could stain the name of his house. In practice the game was often played beforehand in another residence and only replayed at the castle, or so it is said. Shusaku was born on the island of Innoshima in Bingo (today Onomichi, Hiroshima). The year before, he had been named heir of the Honinbo house, and this was his first Castle Game. From here he would play 19 Castle Games and win all 19, never losing once. There was no komi in those days, so only the territory on the board decides the result.","1":"Black 1 (R16) is the komoku in the top right. Komoku is the point where the third and fourth lines from the corner cross, a move that makes it easy to take the corner as territory. With Black, Shusaku often began with komoku, and the opening later called the “Shusaku style” was also built from komoku. With no komi, Black, who moves first, wins simply by keeping a small lead safely to the end. Shusaku knew this well and played calm go that never forced things. In the Shusaku style, Black’s seventh move, a diagonal step from a komoku called the “Shusaku kosumi”, is famous, and today’s go AIs have also re-evaluated it as a good move. In this game too, at move 45, Black plays a kosumi from the top-right komoku.","3":"White 2 (R4) is the komoku in the bottom right, and Black 3 (D17) the komoku in the top left. Black now holds both corners along the top. The top side between those two corners becomes a place Black can later turn into territory. White does not take the remaining corner at once; with the next move he approaches one of Black’s corners instead. Playing two komoku in a row is the same idea as the start of the later Shusaku style. By the way, in the spring of this year, 1849, the ukiyo-e artist Katsushika Hokusai, famous for “Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji”, died in Edo.","4":"White 4 (C15) approaches Black’s D17 in the top left (a kakari). It is the “small knight’s approach”, coming in at a knight’s-move distance, and it stops Black from enclosing the corner widely. Before taking the empty bottom-left corner, White goes to disturb Black’s plan for the top. Black can choose to answer quietly or to pincer. White’s Yasui house was one of the four go houses. The second head, Yasui Santetsu, later took the name Shibukawa Shunkai and is also known as the astronomer who made the first calendar created in Japan (the Jōkyō calendar).","5":"Black 5 (C11) pincers the approaching white C15 from below. With three empty points between, on the third line, it is a “three-space low pincer”. It stops White from spreading down the left side and making a base (a foothold for living). Pincering instead of answering quietly is an active choice that gives White no easy time. In the Shusaku style, move 5 would also be a komoku in another corner, but in this game Black answers the approach at once with a pincer, and here the game leaves the Shusaku-style pattern. By the way, the name “Honinbo” comes from the small temple at Jakkō-ji in Kyoto where the first head, Honinbo Sansa, lived.","6":"White 6 (F16) approaches D17 from the top side as well. With C15 on the left and F16 on the right, White now sandwiches Black’s corner stone from both sides (a double approach). Instead of answering the pincer directly, White puts pressure back on Black’s corner. Black will have to split one of the white stones off. The double approach is a shape found in joseki books, and the sequence that follows depends on which white stone Black turns toward. As a child, Shusaku is said to have had his talent recognized by the Asano family, lords of Mihara Castle (today Mihara, Hiroshima), and to have studied go with their support.","7":"Black 7 (D15) pokes out between the two white stones. Played right next to C15, it keeps C15 and F16 from joining hands. If Black did not play here, White could play around D15 and shut Black’s corner into a small space. From here the stones start to collide in the top left.","9":"White 8 (D14) blocks the head of D15, and Black 9 (E15) extends sideways so two black stones stand in a row. D15 and E15 now have plenty of liberties, so there is no danger of a quick capture. The row also works as a wall that keeps White’s F16 apart from the white stones in the top left. In 1837, at the age of eight, Shusaku went to Edo and became a pupil of the Honinbo house under Honinbo Jowa. Jowa is said to have praised him as “the greatest go talent in 150 years”.","12":"White 10 (C16), Black 11 (E17), White 12 (C17). White lines up stones along the edge and takes the top-left corner and the left side. In return for giving up the corner, Black has D17, E17, D15 and E15 facing the top. White’s F16 on the top is almost surrounded by black stones and has been left on its own. It is a result both sides can accept. This result closely resembles a joseki in which, after a double approach, the corner goes to White and the outside to Black. Across the seas, in this year of 1849 people were flocking to California in search of gold (the Gold Rush), and in Europe the composer Chopin died.","13":"Black 13 (J17) makes a wide extension along the top. The idea is to use the outside stones from the top left and turn the top side into Black’s area. It also closes off the right of White’s F16, which makes that stone even harder to move. F16 stays on the board to the end, but as a dead stone inside Black’s territory. In the same year, 1849, the British warship Mariner entered Edo Bay, carried out surveys and left. It was a time when foreign ships were coming near Japan more and more often; four years later Perry’s “black ships” would arrive.","14":"White 14 (P17) approaches Black’s R16 in the top right from the top side (a kakari). It stops, from the right, Black’s plan to make the whole top into a big area with J17 and R16. Black would like to answer here, but Shusaku chooses another place next. Shusaku had become heir the year before, in 1848, and is said to have married a daughter of his teacher’s house at that time.","15":"Black 15 (C5) goes to the last empty corner, the bottom left (a mokuhazushi). Black does not answer White’s P17 at once and takes the open corner first. A corner is easy to turn into territory with few stones, so in the opening it is a very big place. Black judged that R16 would not get into trouble even if the top right waited a little. Now all four corners of the board are filled. The mokuhazushi stands a little toward the outside of the corner and was often used in Edo-period go. The players of the go houses received stipends from the shogunate, and the Castle Game was the most important place to show their skill.","16":"White 16 (E4) is an approach that comes straight at Black’s C5. It keeps Black from enclosing the bottom-left corner easily, and stones begin to collide here too. White will now line up stones outside the corner and build a shape facing the bottom side. Around this time the shogunate was ordering stronger coastal defences, in preparation for the foreign ships coming near Japan.","19":"Black 17 (D3), White 18 (E3), Black 19 (D2). Black places stones low in the corner and comes down to the second line, securing the corner territory firmly. White lines up E4 and E3 outside and gains power toward the bottom side. Black takes territory, White takes outside strength. Making a small but certainly living shape first is Shusaku’s solid way of playing.","20":"White 20 (C7) closes in on Black’s corner from above. It stops Black from coming up the left side and aims to make the left side White’s area. Black’s corner is not fully alive yet, so Black needs to get his head out upward.","23":"Black 21 (C6), White 22 (D7), Black 23 (E5). After stretching upward, Black plays right on top of White’s E4 and blocks White’s head. E4 and E3 now find it hard to come out upward, and Black’s corner stones gain a way to spread outward. The stones are tangled together, and the shape in the bottom left is gradually settling.","24":"White 24 (C13) extends White’s left-side stones down from C15. It strengthens White’s top-left stones and at the same time presses Black’s move-5 stone C11 from above. C11 is now caught between white stones above and below, and Black must decide whether to treat it lightly or to save it.","26":"Black 25 (E6) stretches upward to strengthen the corner stones, and White 26 (E7) blocks its head. The bottom left is settling into a shape where Black has the corner territory and White an outside wall of C7, D7 and E7. White’s wall keeps watch over both the left side and the bottom side.","27":"Black 27 (F15) is played right below White’s lone F16 on the top. F16 has its way out downward blocked and is left with only three liberties (empty points next to it). Black’s stones on the top also join into one solid group, and the top side area becomes clearer. A quiet move, but an important one that leads to the later territory on top.","28":"White 28 (G7) jumps two points right from E7 and extends the outside wall. White is spreading his influence (power that is not yet territory) outside the bottom left, looking at the bottom side and the centre. Black’s left-side stone C11 is close to this white power and starts to feel a little lonely.","29":"Black 29 (F11) jumps two points right from C11 and gives the left-side black stone a partner. It keeps C11 from being attacked on its own and also becomes a foothold for heading into the centre. At the same time it limits White’s power from below from spreading widely into the centre.","30":"White 30 (H4) extends two points to the right along the bottom from E4 and E3. White begins to build an area on the bottom side and firms up the outside of Black’s bottom-left corner. The left half of the board is now coloured in: Black has the corners and the top, White the left and bottom sides. Next Black heads for White’s bottom-right corner.","32":"Black 31 (O3) approaches White’s R4 in the bottom right from a little further away (a large knight’s approach). White 32 (L3) at once pincers that black stone from the left. L3 also looks to connect with White’s H4 on the bottom, and White would like the whole bottom side as his area. Black’s O3 is caught between White’s corner and L3, and Black must decide how to play here.","33":"Black 33 (R3) attaches right below White’s komoku R4, on the 3-3 point (the point on the third line from both edges). Instead of running away with the pincered O3, Black goes into White’s corner to break up White’s shape. If White blocks, Black will line up stones on the outside. It is the start of Shusaku’s plan: give the corner territory to White and gain outside thickness (a strong wall) in return.","36":"White 34 (S3) blocks R3 from the corner side, and Black 35 (Q3) stretches left toward O3. White 36 (S2) guards the bottom of the corner. Black’s R3 and Q3 now lead to the outside of White’s corner, while White chooses to live inside the corner. From here Black will advance upward along the outside of the white stones.","37":"Black 37 (Q5) jumps one point up from Q3. It holds down the outside (left side) of White’s R4 and goes to stop White from spreading up the right side. If this continues, White can only crawl upward along a line close to the right edge. Black is getting ready to build a wall.","42":"White 38 (R5), Black 39 (Q6), White 40 (R6), Black 41 (Q7), White 42 (R7). White crawls up the third line from the right edge one step at a time, while Black lines up stones on the fourth line just outside. Crawling along the third line gives White some territory, but it is thin and small. The black stones on the fourth line, on the other hand, grow into a strong wall facing the centre and the bottom side.","43":"With Black 43 (Q8), a thick black wall now stretches up the right side from Q3 to Q8. White has the bottom-right corner and a thin strip of territory along the right edge, while Black has a big outward-facing power. This wall will later become the base of a large black area on the right, and it also glares at White’s L3 on the bottom. It shows clearly the way Shusaku liked to play: give up territory to gain thickness. The result so far seems slightly easier for Black to play.","44":"White 44 (L17) plays into the top between Black’s J17 and White’s P17. The idea is to stop, in advance, Black making a huge area on the top to go with the wall on the right. White’s right-side stones are not yet capped from above, but White judged the size of the top more important. Rather than attack this stone at once, Black first sets the top right in order.","45":"Black 45 (Q15) steps one point diagonally down from R16 (a kosumi). Here Black finally answers White’s approach at P17. It strengthens the corner stone and at the same time turns toward the right side, aiming to work together with the Q8 wall below. A well-thought-out move that guards the top-right corner while keeping the right side wide in view.","48":"White 46 (P15), Black 47 (P14), White 48 (O15). White bumps into Q15 and makes shape on the top side, while Black stretches downward toward the right side. White’s stones in the top right (P17, P15, O15) put down roots on the top, and Black gets stones that head for the right side. Each side has moved in the direction it wanted.","49":"Black 49 (R8) blocks the head of the white stones that have crawled along the third line (a hane). White’s R4–R7 can no longer extend up the right side. White’s bottom-right stones must now live in a small space. Black’s wall now runs from Q3 to R8 and becomes even stronger, and it grows likely that the upper right side will be Black’s area. You could say Shusaku left White’s invasion at move 44 alone for a while just to play this move.","50":"White 50 (R18) slides in under Black’s top-right corner on the second line. Linked to White’s P17, it trims a little off Black’s corner territory. Black’s R16 and Q15 now have a smaller corner, but they are looking down toward the right side instead. White has made his living shape on the top clear.","51":"Black 51 (P13) stretches down from P14 and sets up a large area on the right side. With P13 and P14 above and the Q8–R8 wall below, the shape seems to enclose a wide space on the right. If White does not go in, it will become a big black territory. White has to enter the right side somewhere. From here, when and where White enters Black’s area becomes the focus of the game.","52":"White 52 (D10) plays in under Black’s C11 and F11 on the left side. With the white wall in the bottom left at his back, White goes to attack Black’s still-weak left-side stones. C11 and F11 are only two points apart and not yet connected. White wants to split and attack them and widen his left-side area.","56":"Black 53 (D11), White 54 (E11), Black 55 (E12), White 56 (E10). Black connects to C11 with D11, and White cuts between C11–D11 and F11 with E11. Black 55 puts E11 in atari (one move from capture), but White connects to E10 and gets away. Now Black is split into three: C11–D11 on the left, F11 on the right and E12 above. For a while the left side becomes a difficult place where weak stones of both sides are tangled.","57":"Black 57 (B14) slides in on the second line under White’s top-left stones (C13–C17). It goes to take away the room those white stones need to live (the space to make eyes). It also aims to widen the space where Black’s C11 and D11 can live along the left edge. Now White’s own stones are weaker too, so White must fight while defending.","60":"White 58 (D12), Black 59 (C12), White 60 (D13). White wedges between Black’s stones with D12, but Black puts it in atari with C12, and White connects with D13 to the white stones above. Black’s C11, C12 and D11 become one group, and White’s top-left stones come together with D12–D14. Both sides have connected their stones and tidied up their shapes for now.","63":"Black 61 (B13), White 62 (C14), Black 63 (B10). Black lines up B13 and B14 on the left edge, and White connects with C14, making his top-left stones solidly one. Black 63 places a stone on the left edge below C11, stopping White from coming in from underneath. Black’s left-side stones have now secured room to live along the edge. Next Black turns his eyes to the bottom side a little further away.","64":"White 64 (F12) puts Black’s E12 in atari. E12 has only one liberty left at E13, and F11 is down to two liberties. Black does not save E12; it stays inside White’s territory to the end as a sacrificed stone. Shusaku made a clear-cut decision: playing a bigger place is worth more than saving one stone.","65":"Black 65 (G3) plays in between White’s E3 and H4 on the bottom side. It enters the area White is trying to build there and aims to split the white stones in two. E4 and E3 sit outside Black’s bottom-left corner, and H4 is in the middle of the bottom side, so if they are cut apart both become a bit weaker. After defending on the left, Black at once strikes back inside White’s area.","70":"White 66 (H3), Black 67 (G4), White 68 (G2), Black 69 (F2), White 70 (H2). White gets under the black stones with H3 and G2, and Black stands up and then reduces G2’s liberties with F2. White connects with H2 and gathers his stones on the right of the bottom (H4, H3, H2, G2) into one group. Black’s G3 and G4 have pushed in between the white stones, but they still have no base and are not strong.","71":"Black 71 (G5) stretches one more point up from G4. Black’s three stones G3–G5 now stand in a vertical line between White’s E4–E3 on the left and H4–H2 on the right. The two white groups now find it hard to join, but these three black stones are also surrounded by white and are not yet safe. Later these black stones become the seed of a big fight.","72":"White 72 (F13) adds a stone above F12 to tidy its shape. F12 was a weak stone with only two liberties, so White reinforced it before Black could aim at it. White’s stones running from the top left down the left side can now come out toward the centre more easily. Next Black turns to White’s L17 on the top.","75":"Black 73 (K17) attaches right onto White’s L17, and White 74 (L16) stands downward, making a shape to escape into the centre. Then Black 75 (H14): Black pushes his top area firmly down toward the centre. This stone also sits where it hinders White’s left stones (F12, F13) from linking with the white stones on the top (L16, L17). It aims to kill two birds with one stone: widen the top and keep the opponent’s stones apart.","76":"White 76 (P11) finally jumps into Black’s area on the right side. It lands between P13–P14 above and the Q8 wall below, the place Black most wants as territory. Whether this white stone lives or is captured will change the territory count a great deal. With the strong wall on the right, Black is the attacker, but trying too hard to capture could open a weakness somewhere. The next few dozen moves are a big turning point of this game.","77":"Black 77 (Q11) attaches right next to White’s P11. Answering from the side of his own wall, Black stops White from making a base (a place to live) along the right edge. This leaves White only the way out into the centre, where Black has few strong stones. Black chose a sensible attack: chase White out while guarding territory. Had Black attacked from the centre side, White could have lived along the right edge and Black’s territory would have shrunk a lot.","80":"White 78 (Q10) hanes below Black’s Q11, and Black 79 (Q12) stretches upward, making a shape that links toward P13. White 80 (N10) jumps out into the centre from P11 with a knight’s-move shape. White gives up living on the right edge and runs to the centre to break out of Black’s net. Black’s right-side territory is protected, but whether Black can capture the white stones is not yet clear.","81":"Black 81 (R10) goes round below White’s Q10, toward the right edge. Q10 is left with only two liberties (P10 and Q9) and can no longer put down roots on the right edge. Black firmly encloses the right-side territory all the way to the edge, while turning Q10 into a weak stone he can capture whenever he likes. In fact, Black does capture it later, at move 233.","84":"White 82 (J14) bumps into the right of Black’s H14, and Black 83 (H15) stretches upward, making an escape route toward the black stones on the top. White 84 (H13) holds down the bottom of H14. With J14 White looks to link with L16 on the top, and with H13 with F13 on the left. Black’s H14 and H15 are squeezed by white stones, but they are close to the black stones above and are in no immediate trouble.","85":"Black 85 (N12) is played above White’s N10 and P11, which are running into the centre. It goes to stop those stones from moving up and linking with White’s O15 and P15 on the top. At the same time it widens Black’s area from the top right down the right side even more. A skilful way to gain territory while attacking. White now looks for an escape route to the left, through the centre.","87":"White 86 (B9) is played below Black’s B10 on the left edge. It tries to narrow the space Black’s left-side stones have to live along the edge. Black 87 (B15) stretches the other way, upward, widening the room for those black stones to live. Black now has enough length up and down along the left edge. White’s top-left stones, too, can now only go outward.","90":"White 88 (M12) bumps into the left of Black’s N12, and Black 89 (M13) holds the top, leaving M12 with two liberties. White 90 (L12) stretches left and escapes. White is heading left through the centre, looking for a way to link with his J14 and L16 stones on the top. Black presses White from above while firming up his territory on the top and top right.","91":"Black 91 (J7) jumps into the space between the bottom side and the centre. Black boldly shows himself between White’s wall at G7 and White’s H4 and L3 on the bottom. It hinders White from building a big area from the bottom up to the centre, and also aims to join hands with Black’s wall on the right. It enters White’s area, but with friendly black stones around, it is not a reckless invasion.","94":"White 92 (J15) closes in on Black’s H14 and H15 from the right, cutting them down to three liberties. Black 93 (H16) stretches upward and secures an escape route close to the black stones on the top. White 94 (N11) connects to N10 in the centre to tidy his shape and at the same time reduces Black’s N12 to two liberties. In the centre, white and black stones are now pressing each other in a complicated tangle.","99":"Black 95 (M8), White 96 (L9), Black 97 (H7), White 98 (J9). Low down, Black places M8 and H7 and widens the group of black stones around J7. White places L9 and J9 in the centre, going over the top of the black stones and hindering their connections. Then Black 99 (S8): Black extends his wall to the right edge and completely blocks the way for White’s bottom-right stones to spread up the right side. The centre is tangled, but Black’s territory on the right keeps steadily firming up.","101":"White 100 (S17) goes into Black’s top-right corner, near the edge just right of R16. White reduces the room for Black’s corner to make eyes and hopes to break the corner if possible. Black 101 (S16) holds it from below, leaving S17 with three liberties. Without panic, Black guards the corner’s shape firmly.","106":"White 102 (N3), Black 103 (N2), White 104 (M2), Black 105 (N4), White 106 (M3). On the bottom, White attaches right to the left of Black’s O3, and Black places stones below and above it, putting N3 in atari. White connects with M3 and makes L3, M3, N3 and M2 on the bottom into one strong group. Black’s O3 has also gained friends at N4 and N2 and is safe near the wall on the right. A quiet exchange in which both sides firm up the border on the bottom.","112":"Black 107 (B8), White 108 (A9), Black 109 (A10), White 110 (C10), Black 111 (B12), White 112 (C8). On the left edge, Black goes in below White’s B9, and White squeezes the black stones with A9 and C10. White 110 was a sharp move that cut both B10–A10 and C11 and its friends down to two liberties, but Black connects with B12, gathering the main left-side stones into one and removing the big danger. White 112 reduces Black’s B8 to two liberties and keeps Black’s edge stones small.","116":"Black 113 (T5), White 114 (T4), Black 115 (T7), White 116 (T2). Black places stones on the very edge of the board right next to White’s bottom-right stones, trying to reduce White’s room for eyes. White guards the edge with T4 and T2 and makes sure of a living shape in the corner. Even if Black cannot capture, this keeps White’s territory small and prepares for the yose (the endgame of settling borders).","121":"Black 117 (H9) bumps into White’s J9 in the centre, making White’s central stones heavier. White 118 (Q14) is played below Black’s Q15 in the top right, trying to cut the black stones apart. Black 119 (R17) cuts the corner stone S17 down to two liberties, and when White 120 (S18) connects, Black 121 (R15) firmly joins Q15 to R16. White’s aim is gone, and Q14 becomes a stone left inside Black’s territory. Black’s top-right stones are now one strong group.","122":"White 122 (G6) is played right on top of Black’s G5. It goes to cut the link between Black’s stones on the bottom, G3–G5, and Black’s H7 and J7 in the centre. G3–G5 were stones that had pushed in between white stones, and they are surrounded by white. If their link to the outside is cut here, these black stones will find it very hard to live. From here the fiercest fight of the game begins, from the bottom left across the bottom side.","128":"Black 123 (H5), White 124 (J5), Black 125 (K5), White 126 (H6), Black 127 (J6), White 128 (J4). Black looks for an exit to the right with H5 and K5, but White blocks the way with J5 and H6. Black 127 put J5 in atari, but White connects with J4 and makes his stones on the bottom one large group. Now Black’s four stones G3, G4, G5 and H5 have almost lost their way out.","131":"Black 129 (G8) is played above White’s G7, G6 and H6, cutting them to two liberties. White 130 (F7) connects them to the white wall on the left and removes the worry. Then Black 131 (M5): Black does not tend the four stones below right away, but lines up K5 and M5 on the right part of the bottom side, building his own shape above White’s L3. The idea is to take a big place first rather than cling to weak stones.","134":"White 132 (E2), Black 133 (D4), White 134 (F3). White goes round below Black’s corner in the bottom left, and Black strengthens his corner with D4 while cutting White’s E4, E3 and E2 down to three liberties. White 134 is a severe move: it reinforces the white stones on the left, puts Black’s F2 in atari and also cuts Black’s four stones G3–H5 down to two liberties. In the bottom left, Black’s corner, White’s stones and Black’s four stones are all entangled, in a struggle over which group has how many liberties.","137":"Black 135 (L8) is played below White’s L9 in the centre, making White’s central stones heavier. White 136 (D6) wedges in between Black’s corner stones C6 and E6 in the bottom left. If Black did not answer, his corner stones would be cut in two and the corner itself would be in danger. Black 137 (D5) connects at once, making eight corner stones into one group with eight liberties. Black chose to protect the big corner group rather than the four stones on the bottom.","138":"White 138 (F5) puts Black’s four stones G3, G4, G5 and H5 in atari. Their last liberty is F4, but Black cannot play there: everything around it is white, so the stone would have no liberties of its own. White can play F4 and capture the four stones whenever he likes. In other words, these black stones can no longer be saved. In exchange for protecting his bottom-left corner, Black has given up the four stones on the bottom. But Shusaku does not mourn; with the next move he heads for a big place in the centre.","139":"Black 139 (M9) bumps into the right of White’s L9 in the centre. L9 drops to two liberties (K9 and L10), and White has to spend a move helping his central stones. Black lost four stones on the bottom, but meanwhile he firmed up his corner and gained the move to attack White in the centre. In this trade, giving up stones to take a big place elsewhere, Black is thought not to have lost much. Calm trades like this were one of Shusaku’s strengths.","145":"White 140 (B4), Black 141 (B3), White 142 (B2), Black 143 (C3). White places stones on the edge of Black’s bottom-left corner to reduce Black’s room for eyes. Black answers calmly with B3 and C3, gathering ten corner stones into one group with seven liberties. Next, White 144 (B16) hanes above Black’s B15 on the top-left edge, narrowing the eye space of Black’s left-side stones from above, and Black 145 (A15) stops it on the edge. Both black groups still have plenty of room to live.","151":"White 146 (B6), Black 147 (B5), White 148 (A5), Black 149 (B7), White 150 (A7), Black 151 (A6). On the left edge, White puts stones between Black’s corner and Black’s B7, and a small, detailed fight begins. Black 151 plays A6, captures the single white stone B6 and at the same time puts both A5 and A7 in atari. This is a ko shape. In a ko, stones can be taken back and forth again and again, but you may not retake at once; you must first play somewhere else.","154":"White 152 (B11) is a ko threat: a threatening move elsewhere that the opponent must answer, played in order to retake the ko. It put Black’s B10 and A10 in atari, so Black 153 (A11) answers by capturing that white stone. Now White can retake with 154 (B6), capturing the black stone on A6. Play a ko threat, make the opponent answer, then retake: that is how a ko fight works. Black’s B7 and B8 are in atari again.","157":"Black 155 (A3) does not continue the ko but firms up the corner. White 156 (A8) captures Black’s two stones B7 and B8, but Black 157 (A4) puts both White’s B4 and A5 in atari. As a result of this ko, White has taken two black stones on the left edge, and Black has made his big bottom-left corner safe. White’s B4 and A5 are left behind inside Black’s corner and later become the seed of another small ko.","162":"White 158 (L10) extends White’s L9 in the centre, which was short of liberties, and saves it. Black 159 (H11) strengthens Black’s stones on the left of the centre, and White 160 (N13) puts Black’s N12 in atari. Black does not save N12; instead, 161 (N14) puts White’s N13 in atari, and White 162 (O12) captures N12. In return for handing over one stone, Black firmly connects his top-right stones with N14 and gains a shape that holds down White’s O15 and P15 on the top. Shusaku keeps making decisions that do not cling to small stones.","167":"Black 163 (O14) lines up below White’s O15 and P15 on the top, cutting them to three liberties. White 164 (N17) defends, as if to connect the white stones on the top. Black 165 (R2) goes into the eye space of White’s corner in the bottom right, White 166 (S1) answers, and Black 167 (O2) connects O3, O2 and N2 on the bottom. The big fights have calmed down, and the game is moving into the stage of settling borders all over the board.","171":"White 168 (O8) heads down from White’s central stones and peeps into Black’s area in the bottom right. Black 169 (O7) closes the way below it, and with White 170 (N8) and Black 171 (N7), whenever White stretches sideways, Black lines up below. White has widened the shape of his central stones, but Black has not given up a single step of his area from the bottom right to the bottom side. Black’s M8, L8 and M9 also have five liberties and are not yet in danger.","179":"From here on it is yose. White 172 (K18), Black 173 (J18) and White 174 (L19) settle the border on the top; Black 175 (D18) in the top left; and White 176 (D1), Black 177 (C1), White 178 (E1) and Black 179 (C2) on the bottom-left edge. In yose, players compare how many points each move gains and play the biggest places first. Black 177 put D1 in atari, White connected with E1, and with Black 179 the black corner is firmly closed. Solid yose continues, with neither side making a big mistake.","185":"White 180 (C18) and Black 181 (J19) are also yose on the top. White 182 (K4) cuts Black’s K5 down to two liberties on the bottom, and Black 183 (L4) wedges between White’s stones on the bottom, going to reduce the liberties of L3, M3, N3 and M2. White 184 (M14) put Black’s M13 in atari, but Black does not save it; with 185 (N15) he chooses instead to cut White’s O15 and P15 on the top down to two liberties. Black valued attacking the opponent’s weak stones more than a small stone.","189":"White 186 (T16), Black 187 (T15) and White 188 (T17) settle the border on the top-right edge. Black 187 put T16 in atari, White connected with T17, and White’s stones in the top-right corner are left in a small living shape. Then Black 189 (R14): it puts the white stone Q14, left inside, in atari, removing any power of that white stone inside Black’s right-side territory. Black’s big territory from the top right down the right side is now nearly settled.","192":"White 190 (H10) wedges between Black’s H9 and H11 on the left of the centre. H9 drops to two liberties, and there is a danger that Black’s central stones may be cut apart. Black 191 (G10) puts H10 in atari, and White 192 (J10) connects and escapes. On the left of the centre, black and white stones remain cut into each other, and neither side is completely safe yet.","199":"Black 193 (O17) is quietly placed between White’s P17 and N17 on the top. When White 194 (O18) puts it in atari, Black 195 (O16) stretches and in turn puts White’s O15 and P15 in atari. White 196 (P16) connects to save them and at the same time puts Black’s O16 and O17 in atari, but Black 197 (N16) connects and escapes, leaving White’s N17 with two liberties. When White 198 (M17) defends, Black 199 (M15) puts M14 in atari. On the top, Black puts white stones in atari one after another while connecting his own, and pushes into White’s area.","204":"White 200 (L14) connects and saves M14. Black 201 (M1) plays on the very edge of the bottom, cutting White’s L3, M3, N3 and M2 down to three liberties. When White 202 (L1) puts M1 in atari, Black 203 (N1) connects, and the black stones join the black stones on the right. White 204 (K2) tidies the shape of White’s stones on the bottom so Black cannot get in. The border on the bottom has been settled in a way that gains Black a little at a time.","208":"Black 205 (Q18) is played above White’s corner stones R18, S18, S17, T17 and T16 in the top right, cutting them to three liberties. When White 206 (P18) connects the white stones on the top and leaves Q18 with two liberties, Black 207 (Q17) connects and gathers the top-right black stones. White 208 (Q19) plays on the very top edge and keeps the living shape of his corner stones. The top-right corner is settled: White lives small, and Black firmly surrounds it.","211":"Black 209 (J12) places a stone in the centre to strengthen the links between Black’s central stones. White 210 (G14) approaches Black’s H14, H15 and H16 from the left, leaving them four liberties. Black 211 (G15) connects with Black’s stones on the top and cuts G14 down to two liberties. The black stones reaching from the top into the centre have now become one large group.","219":"From White 212 (F9) to Black 219 (G11), Black and White chase each other on the left of the centre. With Black 213 (G9), White 214 (K8), Black 215 (K7), White 216 (J8) and Black 217 (H8), each side reduces the other’s liberties. White 218 (F10) put Black’s F11 in atari, but Black 219 (G11) connects, and Black’s central stones become one large group of twelve. It is a close exchange in which both the black and the white central stones must watch their liberties to survive.","226":"White 220 (D19), Black 221 (E19) and White 222 (C19) are yose on the top-left edge. Then Black 223 (P10): it puts White’s Q10 on the right side in atari and also cuts P11 down to two liberties. White 224 (O10) in turn puts Black’s P10 in atari, Black 225 (P9) stretches and escapes, and White 226 (P8) cuts Black’s P9 and P10 down to two liberties. On the right side it has become a tight reading battle: will Black capture Q10, or must he protect his own P9 and P10?","233":"Black 227 (M10) is played between White’s N10, O10, N11 and L9, L10 in the centre, reducing the liberties of both. From White 228 (J11) to Black 231 (J13), on the left of the centre, Black puts H13 in atari while gathering his central stones into one large group of fifteen. White 232 (O9) put Black’s P9 and P10 in atari, but now Black 233 (Q9): it captures the single white stone Q10, and P9 and P10 jump to nine liberties, safe at a stroke. Q10, a weak stone ever since move 81, is finally captured here, and Black’s territory on the right side is protected in every corner.","238":"White 234 (G13) connects and saves H13. Black 235 (O13) puts N13 in atari, and when White 236 (N12) connects, White’s central stones become a large group of thirteen. Black 237 (K13) links his central stones further, and White 238 (L13) captures the single black stone M13. In the centre, both the big white group and the big black group now clearly have living shapes. What remains are small yose and ko.","242":"Black 239 (A6) captures the single white stone A5, and the left edge becomes a ko again. This was the stone Black had put in atari with A4 at move 157. White 240 (D16) is a ko threat: it cuts Black’s D17, E17 and D18 in the top left down to three liberties and threatens to cut the black stones apart. Black 241 (E16) answers by connecting, gathering the black stones on the top into one. White 242 (A5) now retakes the ko.","245":"Black 243 (P12) is a ko threat that puts White’s P11 on the right side in atari. But White 244 (A6) ignores it and connects at A6, ending the ko on the left edge. Black does not take P11 at once; with 245 (A16) he places a stone on the top-left edge, reducing White’s top-left stones to five liberties. He probably judged this bigger than capturing one stone. White later connects P11 with 266 (O11).","248":"White 246 (A17) cuts Black’s stones on the top-left edge to four liberties. Then Black 247 (F8): a sharp move that cuts White’s F9, F10, E10, D10, E11 and C10 on the left down to three liberties. White 248 (E8) connects them to the white wall in the bottom left and protects them. Black’s attack is parried, but by making White spend a move on defence, Black gets to the next yose first.","253":"Black 249 (M4) cuts White’s L3, M3, N3 and M2 on the bottom down to two liberties. White 250 (R1) reduces the liberties of Black’s R2, R3 and Q3 on the bottom-right edge, and Black 251 (Q1) gets in below that white stone. White 252 (Q4) cuts Q3, R3 and R2 down to two liberties, and Black 253 (P4) answers by reducing the liberties on the side of White’s R4. In the bottom-right corner it has become a close race in which White and Black take away each other’s liberties one by one.","259":"White 254 (P7) goes inside Black’s wall in the bottom right, and Black 255 (P6) stops it. White 256 (M11) cuts Black’s M10, M9, M8 and L8 in the centre down to three liberties. Then Black 257 (S7): it cuts White’s R4–R7 and Q4 in the bottom right down to three liberties. White 258 (S5) put Black’s T5 in atari, but with Black 259 (S6) six white stones are in atari (their only liberty left is S4). Late in the game, White’s bottom-right stones are suddenly in unexpected danger.","263":"White 260 (T6) captures Black’s T5 and gives the white stones more liberties. A ko now appears on the bottom-right edge. Black 261 (K19) is a ko threat that puts White’s K18 on the top in atari, and White 262 (L18) answers by connecting. When Black 263 (T5) retakes the ko, the six white stones are in atari again. If White loses this ko he will lose a lot of stones in the bottom right, so he must defend with his next move.","267":"With White 264 (S4) connecting, White’s bottom-right stones live with two eyes at T3 and T1. Black 265 (J16) and White 266 (O11) take small points, Black 267 (T6) fills in where the ko was, and the game ends after 267 moves. Black wins by 11 points. Shusaku had the thick wall he built in the bottom right by making White crawl along the third line (up to move 43) at his back, and set up a big area on the right side. From move 76 White went into the right side and ran into the centre; the white stones did live, but Black, while chasing them, firmed up his territory on the top and the right, and at move 233 he finally captured Q10, the stone he had been aiming at since move 81. In the bottom left Black gave up four stones, but in return he protected his corner and got to the big places in the centre first. No great adventures: he attacked his opponent’s stones and naturally increased his territory, a truly Shusaku-like way to win. From this game on, the 20-year-old Shusaku would build a record still told today, 19 wins in 19 Castle Games, until his early death in 1862, while still in his thirties."}},"shilin":{"t":"The Shilin Guangji game","w":"Red (name not recorded)","b":"Black (name not recorded)","ev":"The everyday encyclopedia Shilin Guangji: “a game won after giving the first move, with same-side cannons”","d":"13th century","pl":"China (Song–Yuan)","lead":"This record comes from the Shilin Guangji, an everyday encyclopedia compiled by Chen Yuanjing around the end of the Song dynasty and the start of the Yuan. It is said to be one of the oldest complete xiangqi game records that survive. It is a model game in which the stronger side lets the other move first, and in only nine moves Black’s horse traps Red’s chariot.","c":{"0":"This record is more than 700 years old, from the time between the Song and Yuan dynasties. It appears in the games chapter of the encyclopedia Shilin Guangji, and it is said to be one of the oldest complete xiangqi games we still have. The players’ names are not recorded. The title says the stronger player “gave the first move” to the weaker one, so this is a lesson showing how Red, who moves first, loses. Red is at the bottom and starts. Xiangqi is thought to have taken almost its present form, with the river and the cannons, in the Song dynasty, especially the Southern Song. Chen Yuanjing, who compiled the Shilin Guangji, lived at the end of the Southern Song. Later, at the end of the 13th century, the Mongol Yuan dynasty overthrew the Song. While dynasties were changing, a book for everyday life already carried a model xiangqi game.","1":"Red moves the right cannon to the middle file. This is the Central Cannon, aimed straight at Black’s general, and still one of the strongest openings today. It shows that this opening was already played 700 years ago.","2":"Black answers with a cannon to the middle too, so the two cannons face each other. When both sides move the cannon on the same side, it is called Same Direction Cannons. Cannon against cannon has been a popular fight for centuries. Bringing the cannon from the opposite side to the opponent is called \"Opposite Direction Cannons\", and old records are full of games with both Same and Opposite Direction Cannons. It was an age that loved fast fights where the cannons clash right away. In professional games today, Black usually answers the Central Cannon with the \"Screen Horses\", and Same Direction Cannons is said to be less common than it used to be.","3":"Red jumps a horse out to guard the middle soldier. That soldier is under attack from Black’s cannon, so it needs a defender. The horse is also getting ready to come forward.","4":"Black does the same and guards the middle soldier with a horse. Both sides are building a firm base first.","5":"Red moves the right chariot one step sideways onto an open file. The chariot is the strongest piece on the board, so it is good to get it working early.","6":"Black brings out the other horse and leaves the chariots at home for now. The plan is to bring pieces forward first. But a chariot that comes out late can be hard to use in the attack later. Model games in old books often show a mistake to avoid. This one teaches that even if Black is slow to bring out the chariots, Black can still win by using the other pieces well.","7":"Red’s chariot rushes across the river into Black’s camp. It wants to threaten Black’s pieces, but Red’s other pieces are still at home, so this attack is a bit hasty. Bringing out the left horse or chariot first was also possible.","8":"Black pushes the soldier on file 3 one step. This opens a path for the horse so it can jump forward later. Black is almost inviting Red’s chariot to come.","9":"Red’s chariot takes a soldier and now eyes the black horse beyond it. Winning a piece and threatening another looks great. But the chariot has run far ahead with no friends around it. A chariot alone in the enemy camp is easy to chase.","10":"Black’s horse jumps forward. It escapes from the chariot and moves toward Red’s camp at the same time — two jobs with one move. This horse will be the star of the game.","11":"Red pulls the chariot back one step to attack the horse from the side. A chariot can capture from far away, so the horse cannot rest. Still, this move does not help Red develop any other piece.","12":"Black’s horse goes further forward and crosses the river into Red’s camp. Each time it runs from the chariot, it gets closer to Red’s general.","13":"Red pushes a soldier to open a path for Red’s own horse. The idea is to bring that horse out and trade it for Black’s horse. But this gives Black time to go even further.","14":"Black lifts an elephant and attacks Red’s chariot. Using a defending piece to chase the enemy chariot away is very efficient. Red must move the chariot again, and Black gains another move.","15":"Red’s chariot slides across and takes another soldier. Red is now two soldiers up, but this is the move that loses. The chariot is now far from its general and cannot come back to defend. Red needed a move to guard against the horse, such as bringing the chariot home.","16":"Black’s horse jumps deeper and attacks Red’s chariot. And there is more: if the horse jumps once again, it will check Red’s general and at the same time attack the chariot in Red’s corner. One horse has made two threats at once.","17":"Red chases the horse with the chariot. But the horse can no longer be stopped. Around Red’s general there is nobody left to help.","18":"The horse leaps in with check! It also attacks the chariot in Red’s corner, so when the general steps away, the chariot will be lost. Only nine moves, but the game teaches what happens to a chariot that runs ahead alone, and why pieces should fight together. Players 700 years ago were thinking about the same things on the same board."}},"qima13":{"t":"The Thirteen-Move Horse Sacrifice (Juzhongmi)","w":"Red (name not recorded)","b":"Black (name not recorded)","ev":"Juzhongmi, Volume 1, Game 1","d":"1632 (published)","pl":"China (Ming)","lead":"This is the very first game in the Juzhongmi (“The Secret in the Tangerine”), a xiangqi book published by Zhu Jinzhen of the Ming dynasty in 1632. Red lets Black capture a horse for free, rushes in while Black is busy, and mates in thirteen moves. Under the name “The Thirteen-Move Horse Sacrifice” it is still loved almost 400 years later.","c":{"0":"This is Game 1 of the Juzhongmi, a book printed in 1632, near the end of the Ming dynasty. Together with the later Plum Blossom Manual, it is called one of the two great classics of xiangqi. This is not a real match but a model game written to teach a plan, so there are no players’ names. Red is at the bottom and moves first, and the game is over in just thirteen moves. 1632 was the 5th year of the Chongzhen era of the Ming. Rebellions were breaking out across the land, just 12 years before the Ming fell (1644). In Japan it was the early Edo period, 25 years after the oldest shogi record, Sōkei against Sansa. The name \"Ju Zhong Mi\" (Secrets inside the Tangerine) is said to come from a Tang-dynasty tale: when a huge tangerine was cut open, old men were found inside enjoying a board game.","1":"Red moves the right cannon to the middle. This is the Central Cannon, staring straight at Black’s general. The Juzhongmi is famous for studying how to attack with this opening. In this book the author, Zhu Jinzhen, wrote down move by move how to attack with the Central Cannon. The later Plum Blossom Manual (Meihua Pu), compiled by Wang Zaiyue in the Qing dynasty, is known for the opposite: answering the Central Cannon with the \"Screen Horses\". Ju Zhong Mi for attack, Meihua Pu for defence: these two books are the roots of today's Central Cannon openings.","2":"Black also moves the cannon on the same side to the middle. This is Same Direction Cannons, where the cannons clash head-on. Each side is aiming at the other’s middle soldier.","3":"Red jumps a horse out to guard the middle soldier. It protects the soldier the black cannon is aiming at, and the horse starts to work too.","4":"Black also guards the middle soldier with a horse. So far both sides have the same shape.","5":"Red moves the right chariot just one step forward. Now it can run sideways next move. A chariot that moves sideways like this is called a “horizontal chariot”, and it can quickly swing to either wing.","6":"Black moves the chariot one step sideways so it can drive straight up the board. This is a “straight chariot”. Horizontal chariot against straight chariot — two different ways of using the chariot face off. \"Same Direction Cannons, horizontal chariot against straight chariot\" is a standard Same Direction Cannons shape that appears again and again in the old manuals. Even today it is one of the first branches you meet when you study this opening.","7":"Red’s horizontal chariot swings over to a file on the left. That file runs right beside Black’s advisor, where Black’s defence is thin. Red is getting ready to break in from here.","8":"Black’s straight chariot crosses the river and goes deep into Red’s camp. Black wants to fight back with an attack of its own. But it is a move that hurries to attack before defending.","9":"Red’s chariot drives deep into Black’s camp. It stands on the elephant’s “eye”, the point an elephant must pass through, so Black’s elephant cannot move. Right next to the advisor, it is now very close to the general.","10":"Black brings the other horse out to the edge. Part of the idea is to stop Red’s chariot from sliding in sideways.","11":"Here is the horse sacrifice! Red lifts the other chariot one step. This leaves the horse in the left corner with no defender, so Black’s cannon can take it for free. But in return for one horse, Red gains time to bring both chariots into the attack quickly.","12":"Black’s cannon takes Red’s horse. It looks like Black has won a piece. But this cannon is now far from home and cannot help defend. It is said that strengthening the defence by moving up an advisor, instead of capturing, was better here.","13":"Red’s cannon moves forward. Using Black’s middle cannon as a “screen” to jump over, it now aims at Black’s horse beyond it. The cannon is a strange piece that captures by jumping over exactly one piece.","14":"Black retreats the horse to escape. But that horse was the important piece guarding the middle soldier. By running away, it leaves a hole in Black’s centre. Black had other defences, but this was the worst choice.","15":"Red’s cannon takes the middle soldier — check! With Black’s horse gone, the centre was wide open. From here Red’s attack rushes forward.","16":"Black moves an advisor up to block in front of the general. This is the only good way to stop the check.","17":"Red’s second chariot arrives on the same file. Two chariots lined up on one file are extremely strong. The plan is to break into the back of Black’s camp.","18":"Black’s general steps to the side. Otherwise it would be stared at straight down the file by Red’s two chariots.","19":"Red’s front chariot takes Black’s advisor — check! This chariot will be captured by the other advisor at once, but that is part of the plan. Red throws away a chariot to smash the guards around Black’s general. What a bold move!","20":"Black takes the chariot with the advisor. In pieces, Black is now far ahead. But the general’s house is even emptier than before.","21":"Red’s last chariot moves to the general’s file — check again. Black’s general has almost no pieces left to protect it.","22":"Black puts a cannon in front of the general to block the check. Putting a piece in between like this is called interposing.","23":"Red’s chariot takes that cannon — check again. The chariot keeps capturing Black’s pieces while chasing the general into a corner.","24":"Black’s general can only go back to the middle. Every other point is covered by Red’s pieces.","25":"Finally Red’s cannon lines up in the middle, two cannons on one file — check. The front cannon is the screen and the back cannon hits the general: a “double cannon” mate. Black’s general has nowhere to go, and Red wins. By giving up just one horse, Red won in thirteen moves. For 400 years this game has been teaching that time and active pieces can matter more than the number of pieces."}},"sokei1607":{"t":"The oldest shogi record that survives","w":"Ōhashi Sōkei (I)","b":"Hon'inbō Sansa","ev":"The oldest game on record","d":"June 1607","pl":"Japan (the place is not recorded)","lead":"Of all shogi games written down move by move, this is the oldest one we still have. Sente is Sōkei, who later founded the Ōhashi house of shogi masters. Gote is Sansa, founder of the Hon'inbō house of Go, who was also a strong shogi player. Sōkei wins in 133 moves.","c":{"0":"Welcome, I am Musowl. This game was played in 1607, just as the Edo period was beginning. Sente, Ōhashi Sōkei, would later be called the first Meijin, the top shogi master of the land. Gote, Hon'inbō Sansa, founded the Hon'inbō house of Go and was very good at shogi too; the two often played in front of important people. Eight of their even games survive, and Sōkei is said to have won seven of them. More than 400 years later, we can still replay every move. 1607 was the fourth year after Tokugawa Ieyasu set up his government in Edo. Having handed the title of shogun to his son Hidetada, Ieyasu began building a castle in Sunpu (today's Shizuoka City) that very year. This game comes from the moment Japan was moving from the age of warring states into the Edo period.","1":"P-7f. This opens the bishop's diagonal, and it is still the most common first move today. So shogi already began this way 400 years ago.","2":"P-3d. Gote opens the bishop's diagonal too. Now the two bishops face each other across the middle of the board. Sansa was born in Kyoto in 1559 and became a Buddhist monk at the age of eight. \"Hon'inbō\" was the name of the small hall he lived in at Jakkōji temple, and it became the name of his Go house. It is said that Oda Nobunaga once praised him as a true master.","3":"S-4h. Sente lifts the right silver. It is getting ready to bring the silver forward. Sōkei was born around 1555 and is said to have come from a family of Kyoto townspeople. In 1603 he presented a book of tsume-shogi problems he had made to the Emperor. It is said to be the oldest tsume-shogi collection on record.","5":"P-4f. Sente pushes the pawn on file 4 to make room for the silver. It goes together with S-4g next.","6":"S-4b. Gote also lifts a silver. Defenders are gathering on Gote's file 4. Five years after this game, in 1612, Ieyasu began paying the best Go and shogi players a stipend in rice. This was the start of the \"iemoto\" houses that made shogi their profession, and Sōkei's Ōhashi house went on for the whole Edo period.","10":"R-4b. Gote moves the rook to file 4. Today this is called the Fourth File Rook, one of the Ranging Rook plans where the rook swings to the left. People were already playing it 400 years ago. So this game is what we now call an \"opposing\" game: a Static Rook Sente against a Ranging Rook Gote. Four hundred years later, the Fourth File Rook is still a favourite of both professionals and amateurs. It is fun to see a popular plan already in the very oldest record.","11":"R-4h. Sente's rook goes to file 4 too, so both rooks face each other there. Sente will move this rook many more times, looking for a good place to attack. In today's words, Sente's rook stands on the \"Right Fourth File Rook\" square. The rook faces Gote's Fourth File Rook head-on and attacks straight up file 4, and it is still played today as one way to break the Fourth File Rook.","12":"B-3c. The bishop steps up to add strength to file 4. Gote has made file 4 very solid.","16":"K-6b. Gote moves the king to the left. In Ranging Rook, the king usually hides on the side away from the rook. The farther the king is from the fighting, the safer it is.","17":"K-6h. Sente moves the king to the left as well. It will go on to 7h and then 8g. In those days, named castles such as today's Mino Castle had probably not yet taken a clear shape. You can see both players feeling their way as they guard their kings.","19":"K-7h. The king reaches file 7. The next goal is to gather golds and silvers around it to build a castle.","21":"G-5h. The right gold comes toward the king. The gold is a great defender, and having one close by makes the king feel safe.","24":"S-5c. Gote brings a silver to file 5. With silvers on 5c and 4c, the center is solid too.","29":"S-6g. Sente's silver arrives near the king. With the silver on 6g and the gold on 7h, a wall is forming around the king.","30":"G-6c. Gote lifts a gold to thicken the front of the king. Both players are building their camps slowly and carefully.","33":"P-8f. Sente pushes the pawn on file 8 to make a place for the king. It is preparing K-8g.","35":"K-8g. The king goes to 8g. It is not a castle we use today: the king sits high, near the edge. We can see how players of that time thought about defence.","36":"K-8b. Gote's king settles on 8b. Each king is now tucked away deep in its own corner.","39":"N-7g. The knight jumps up so that it can leap to 6e at any time. Sente wants the castle's pieces to help in the attack too.","41":"R-3h. Sente shifts the rook to file 3. Gote's file 4 is too strong, so Sente is changing the place to attack. At that time there were no books yet that wrote down the set ways to play the opening (joseki). Masters were still finding good shapes one game at a time. Later players of the Ōhashi and Itō houses studied records like this one and grew the joseki.","42":"R-3b. Gote at once moves its rook to file 3 as well. Wherever Sente's rook goes, Gote's rook follows.","43":"P-2e. Now Sente pushes the pawn on file 2. The plan is to bring the rook to file 2 and attack there.","44":"B-5a. Gote pulls the bishop back. It rearranges its pieces so that other pieces can meet an attack on file 2.","45":"R-2h. The rook is back on its starting file 2. File 4, file 3, file 2: Sente's rook has made a long journey.","46":"R-2b. Gote's rook again follows to file 2. Wherever Sente sends its rook, Gote meets it on the same file. This 'rook chase' is the highlight of the opening of this game.","47":"P-6e. Now Sente starts the fight on file 6, where no rook stands. Instead of attacking where the rooks stare at each other, Sente strikes where Gote is thinner. The middle game begins. Starting a fight on a file where the enemy rook is not is an idea still used in opposing games today. The master of 400 years ago had already noticed it.","49":"Px6d. Sente pushes the pawn and takes. The idea is to lure Gote's gold forward and loosen the defence around the king.","50":"Gx6d. Gote takes back with the gold. The gold has moved a little away from its king, so a small gap opens in Gote's defence.","51":"P-4e. Next Sente pushes the pawn on file 4. It shakes Gote's defence on file 4 as well.","53":"N-3g. The other knight jumps up to join the attack. Sente's attackers are moving forward one by one.","54":"P*6e. Gote drops a pawn on 6e, the square where Sente's knight wants to jump. A pawn is cheap, but it is very useful for blocking an enemy piece's path.","56":"P-7e. Gote strikes back. It pokes at the file right above Sente's king. If the pawns above a king are broken, the king becomes hard to defend.","57":"Px7e. Sente takes with the pawn. If not, Gote's pawn would push on to 7f.","58":"Gx7e. Gote takes back with the gold, which now stands on 7e. Gote is bringing the gold forward to press on Sente's king.","59":"P*7f. Sente drops a pawn to block the gold. This pawn guards the head of the king.","61":"R-6i. Sente's rook travels all the way to the bottom of file 6. It wants to add the rook's power to the fight on file 6. Later this rook will be taken by Gote's silver.","63":"Nx6e. The knight from 7g jumps and takes the pawn on 6e. The knight Sente prepared long ago finally gets to work.","64":"Nx6e. Gote takes back with its own knight. The knights are traded.","66":"P-8e. Gote presses on file 8 too. It tries to strip away the pawns in front of Sente's king one by one.","68":"P*7g. Gote drops a pawn on 7g, right next to the king. If Sente takes it, the taking piece is pulled out of place; if not, the pawn will promote. It is a common trick to break a defence with a small pawn.","69":"G-7i. Sente steps the gold back out of the way. It chooses to keep its defensive shape instead of taking the pawn now.","70":"N*7e, check. Gote drops a knight. The knight reaches the king on 8g by jumping over other pieces. Gote's attack is getting strong.","72":"Sx7e. After Sente's pawn takes the knight, Gote takes back with the silver and moves forward. Gote's attacking pieces are now right next to Sente's king.","74":"P*8f, check. Gote drops another pawn right in front of the king on 8g. The king cannot stay there any longer.","75":"K-9h. Sente's king escapes to 9h on the edge. It steps away from danger and rebuilds its defence.","77":"Px7e. Now Sente's pawn on 7f takes the silver on 7e. Sente removes the centre of Gote's attack and gets a chance to strike back.","79":"S*7f. Sente drops a silver to clash with the gold on 7e. It guards the king's head and tries to drive the gold away.","81":"Sx7f. Silver and gold have been traded. Sente is cutting down Gote's attackers one at a time.","83":"Px6e. The pawn on 6f takes the knight on 6e. Gote loses another attacker, and Sente's pieces in hand grow.","84":"S*7h. Gote drops a silver that aims at the rook on 6i. Sente will lose the rook, but in the meantime it hurries its own attack.","85":"P*9b. Now Sente's counterattack begins. A pawn dropped on 9b, near Gote's king, pulls the lance forward. Moving the pieces around the king creates gaps.","86":"Lx9b. Gote takes the pawn with the lance. But now the lance stands on 9b, and it becomes a target for the next knight.","87":"N*8d. Sente drops a knight that attacks both the lance on 9b and the gold on 7b. Hitting two things at once is just what a knight does best.","88":"B-5a. Gote pulls the bishop back so that it aims along the diagonal at the knight on 8d. Gote wants to take the knight and stop the attack.","89":"N*7d, check. Sente drops its other knight, and it checks Gote's king. A knight can check by jumping over other pieces, so it is hard to defend against.","90":"K-8c. Gote's king steps up. Moving forward also brings it near Sente's knights.","91":"Nx9b+. The knight on 8d takes the lance and promotes, becoming a piece that moves like a gold. Sente has broken into the area near Gote's king.","92":"Kx9b. Gote takes the promoted knight with the king. Gote's king now stands on the edge at 9b.","93":"Bx7g. Sente's bishop takes the pawn on 7g. The bishop has not moved all game, but now it starts working. It will play a big part later.","94":"Sx6i+. Gote's silver takes Sente's rook and promotes. Losing a rook is a big loss, but Sente judges that its attack on the king is faster.","95":"Gx6i. Sente takes back the promoted silver with the gold. Sente's defending gold is still standing firm.","97":"P*9c, check. A pawn is cheap, but dropped right in front of a king it becomes very strong.","98":"K-8a. Gote's king retreats to 8a. Its escape routes are getting fewer.","99":"Bx8f. The bishop takes the pawn on 8f. Along its diagonal it now sees deep into Gote's camp. It is preparing the next big move.","101":"G*9b, check. The gold on 9b and the pawn on 9c work together to drive Gote's king off the edge.","103":"B-5c+. The bishop on 8f flies all the way to 5c and promotes into a horse. A horse moves like a bishop plus one step in any direction, a very strong piece. Sente's big piece has broken in close to Gote's king.","104":"K-6a. Gote's king runs on to file 6. It is being pushed from the edge toward the middle of the board.","105":"L*6d. Sente drops a lance. A lance moves straight forward as far as it likes, so it watches the whole of file 6.","106":"P*6b. Gote drops a pawn to block the lance's path.","107":"+Bx4c. The horse takes the silver on 4c. Gote loses another defender.","108":"N*5b. Gote drops a knight to strengthen its defence. The position is hard, but Gote keeps fighting.","109":"K-8g. Here Sente moves its own king up to 8g once. It seems to be giving the king more room before Gote strikes back.","110":"R*8i, check. Gote strikes back, dropping a rook to give check. Gote is trying to catch Sente's king too.","112":"P-7g+, check. The pawn promotes into a tokin and gives check. A tokin moves like a gold, yet if it is captured the opponent only gets a pawn.","114":"Rx6i+. The rook takes the gold and becomes a dragon. Sente's king has lost its defenders, and the game becomes a race: who catches the other king first?","115":"S*5c. Sente drops a silver to tighten the net around Gote's king. Pressing the enemy king even when your own king is in danger is the heart of such a race.","116":"S*6f, check. Gote drops a silver and checks. Sente's king must run.","117":"K-8f. The king escapes to 8f. Sente's king has plenty of room upward.","119":"K-9e. The king goes on to 9e at the edge. Gote's pieces can hardly reach it here. Sente's king is safe for now, so Sente can focus on attacking.","120":"P-6c. Gote pushes a pawn to defend. But Gote's defence is no longer enough.","121":"S*6b. Here Sente begins the finish. It drops a silver right into Gote's camp to take the pieces guarding the king.","122":"Bx6b. Gote takes the silver with the bishop.","123":"Sx6b+. The silver on 5c takes the bishop and promotes. Gote loses a big piece.","125":"Nx6b+. The knight on 7d takes the gold and promotes as well. Trading piece for piece, Sente strips Gote's defence bare.","126":"Kx6b. Gote takes with the king. But there are almost no defenders left around Gote's king.","127":"B*5c, check. Sente drops the bishop it just captured. Using captured pieces right away is what makes shogi so exciting.","129":"G*8d, check. The gold closes the king's escape from above.","131":"P*7c, check. Gote's king has no choice but to go back deeper.","133":"Lx6c+. The lance takes the pawn on 6c and promotes. Seeing that a piece coming to 6b next would leave the king no escape, Sansa admitted defeat here. Sōkei wins in 133 moves. The highlights were the 'rook chase' of the opening, the middle-game tug of war with pawns and knights, and the ending, where Sente gave up its rook to race at the king. It shows how the ideas of a master 400 years ago still connect to the shogi of today."}},"hu1960":{"t":"Hu Ronghua, aged 15, beats Yang Guanlin","w":"Yang Guanlin","b":"Hu Ronghua","ev":"5th National Xiangqi Individual Championship, round 3","d":"28 October 1960","pl":"Beijing","lead":"Yang Guanlin had been national champion three times. Facing him with Black was Hu Ronghua, a 15-year-old playing his first national tournament. Hu gave up a cannon early to seize the initiative, then won a long endgame. Hu went on to win the tournament and then ten national titles in a row, so this game is remembered as the moment the crown passed from the old champion to the new.","c":{"0":"This is round 3 of the 5th National Xiangqi Individual Championship, held in Beijing in 1960. Red is Yang Guanlin. He won the first championship in 1956 and three of the four tournaments so far — the strongest player of the time. Black is Hu Ronghua from Shanghai, only 15 years old and playing his first national tournament. Everyone expected Red to win, but this game would change xiangqi history. In 1960 China was going through very hard times: after the policy called the \"Great Leap Forward\", there was not enough food. Because of this, it is said that the national individual championship was not held the next year (1961). The national xiangqi individual championship itself was still new; it had begun only four years earlier, in 1956.","1":"Yang moves the right cannon to the middle. The Central Cannon aims straight at Black’s general, and it was the opening Yang had studied most deeply. The Central Cannon was already the star of Ju Zhong Mi more than 300 years earlier; it is the oldest attacking start in xiangqi. Yang Guanlin was a player who polished it further with modern study.","2":"Hu jumps a horse out to guard the middle soldier. Bringing the horse out early prepares both defence and attack. Hu Ronghua was born in Shanghai in 1945. He learned xiangqi from his father and sharpened his play against grown-ups at street-side xiangqi stalls. It is said his family was too poor to buy wooden pieces, so he played with pieces made of paper. He joined the Shanghai team in 1959 and, at first, is said to have lost more than a hundred games in a row.","3":"Red also jumps a horse out, guarding the middle soldier while bringing pieces forward. Both sides make the centre safe first.","4":"Black pushes the soldier on file 7 early. It holds back Red’s horse and opens a path for Black’s own horse. In this position most players bring out a chariot first.","5":"Red moves the right chariot one step sideways onto an open file.","6":"Black also moves a chariot sideways. Both sides want their strongest piece, the chariot, working early.","7":"Here Red pushes a soldier. Many players would send the chariot across the river, but Yang is steering toward the cannon set-up he liked best.","8":"Black’s cannon advances to the river and stands right in front of Red’s right chariot. This plan, called “left cannon blocks the chariot”, shuts the chariot in at home. It was a bold idea to stop Yang from reaching his favourite set-up. Shutting in the Central Cannon side's chariot with a cannon on the river is one of Black's ideas for fighting the Central Cannon. Since the days of the old manuals, Red's attacks and Black's defences have been studied bit by bit like this.","9":"Red brings out the other horse, developing the left side and preparing to strike back.","10":"Black lifts an elephant to strengthen the defence. Elephants cannot cross the river; their job is to guard the general’s house.","11":"Red’s cannon dives deep into Black’s camp, takes a horse and gives check! It trades a cannon for a horse. Trading a cannon for a horse early often costs time, but here it is said to be a fair move.","12":"Black recaptures the cannon with the chariot. Material is level again, and Black’s chariot has come out naturally.","13":"Red brings out the left chariot too. Red would like to push the black cannons away and get the chariots moving.","14":"Black pushes the other cannon to the river as well, blocking Red’s left chariot. Now both of Red’s chariots have a cannon sitting in front of them. Red is getting cramped.","15":"Red moves the horse toward the river. Red has two threats in mind: taking the middle soldier with the horse, or pushing a soldier to attack Black’s cannon.","16":"Black lifts an advisor to guard the centre. This stops the horse from taking the middle soldier with check.","17":"Red pushes the soldier. That opens the path the soldier was blocking, so now Red’s horse can capture Black’s cannon. The cannon is under attack.","18":"This is the most famous moment of the game. Black does not save the cannon. Instead the chariot moves up to attack Red’s horse. Hu gives up a cannon to keep the attacking shape. His opponent was a three-time champion and he was only 15, yet he chose energy over material — and the spectators were amazed.","19":"Red’s horse takes Black’s cannon. Black is a piece down. In return, Black’s chariot is now pressing on Red’s chariot and horse from the side.","20":"Black’s other cannon takes the middle soldier — check! Black’s counterattack begins. Red has to lift an advisor to protect the general.","21":"Red lifts an advisor to block the check. But Black’s cannon is still sitting deep inside Red’s camp.","22":"Black’s chariot takes Red’s chariot. When the cannon moved away a moment ago, the chariot’s path opened up.","23":"Red takes back with the horse — a chariot for a chariot. Black is still a piece down, but Black’s pieces are now working better.","24":"Black’s horse advances to the river. One idea is to go after Red’s horse next.","26":"Black’s cannon slides across and stops right beside Red’s horse. A xiangqi horse cannot move in a direction if a piece stands right next to it on that side (the horse’s “leg”). With its leg blocked by the cannon, Red’s horse can hardly move.","28":"Black pushes the middle soldier. Later this soldier will become an important attacker in Red’s camp.","30":"Black’s horse takes a soldier and slips into Red’s camp. Little by little, Black wins back material.","32":"Black’s horse comes back to the middle. It avoids being traded for Red’s horse and takes a fine central point.","34":"Black’s cannon steps back one point. It is getting ready to slide across and attack Red’s horse.","36":"Black’s cannon slides over and, using Black’s own chariot as a screen, attacks Red’s horse. A cannon captures by jumping over exactly one piece.","38":"Black’s horse jumps out and attacks Red’s chariot. By moving away, it also takes away the screen Red’s cannon needed to attack Black’s chariot.","40":"Red’s cannon gave check, so Black drops the advisor back to block. Red is fighting back hard.","42":"Black’s cannon takes Red’s horse! Black wins back the cannon given up in the opening. Material is level, Black has an extra soldier, and all three of Black’s attacking pieces stand across the river. Black is now clearly better.","44":"Black pushes a soldier forward and offers it for free. If Red takes it, a hidden plan becomes possible — a move that looks far ahead.","45":"Red takes the soldier with an elephant. Now Red must watch out for Black’s plan.","46":"Black offers the horse as well. If Red’s horse takes it, Black’s cannon can give check while uncovering an attack on Red’s chariot.","47":"Red does not take the horse and pulls its own horse back instead. Yang, an old champion, does not fall into traps easily.","48":"Black’s chariot slides sideways and frees itself from the pin. Now the chariot can move freely again, for attack or defence.","50":"Black’s cannon moves to the middle, staring straight at Red’s general — a very strong post.","52":"Black’s horse jumps forward again. Cannon and horse are working together in the centre.","56":"Black’s horse goes all the way to the edge and eyes the side of Red’s general. A horse on the edge has fewer moves, but here it has the important job of threatening the general’s shelter.","58":"Black’s middle soldier moves further forward, pressing on Red’s camp. Once a soldier crosses the river it can also move sideways, so it becomes much stronger.","60":"The cannons are traded off. Red’s cannon took Black’s cannon, and Black’s soldier took Red’s cannon back. Black’s soldier is now close to Red’s camp.","62":"Black’s chariot takes one of Red’s elephants. Red’s defence loses another piece.","64":"Black’s horse gives check! Red blocks the horse’s leg with the chariot. In xiangqi you can stop a horse’s check by putting a piece right next to the horse.","66":"Black’s horse steps back for now. It keeps giving checks while looking for its best square.","70":"Black’s horse checks again. Red’s chariot cannot leave the general’s side, so it cannot join the attack.","72":"Black’s horse drops back to the edge. It keeps Red’s chariot tied down while Black plans the next step.","76":"Black’s chariot takes Red’s other elephant too. Red’s general is becoming very lonely.","82":"Black’s chariot takes Red’s horse. Red’s hard-working horse is traded off here.","83":"Red takes back Black’s horse with the chariot. With the horses traded, the board is much clearer. Now it is a fight of chariot and soldiers on both sides.","90":"Black’s soldier takes one of Red’s advisors! A soldier is a small piece, but deep in the enemy camp it is strong enough to trade itself for an advisor.","91":"Red takes the soldier back with the other advisor. A soldier for an advisor — Red’s general is even less protected.","92":"Black’s chariot takes a Red soldier. In the endgame, the number of soldiers left decides who wins.","98":"Black’s chariot takes another soldier. Red has only a few soldiers left. Step by step, Black is heading for an easier winning position.","99":"Red’s chariot takes a Black elephant in return. The two chariots are trading blows, taking one piece each.","104":"Black starts pushing the soldier on the edge. Bringing this soldier deep into Red’s camp is Black’s road to victory.","112":"Black’s chariot goes behind the soldier to protect it. A chariot and a soldier together make a very strong team.","115":"Red’s chariot takes one of Black’s soldiers. Black has one soldier left. Red has not given up yet.","123":"Red pushes the soldier forward. But it is said that pulling the chariot back to guard the general was better here. This move became the turning point of the game.","124":"Black’s soldier steps sideways toward Red’s general. A soldier moves only one step at a time, but the closer it gets to the general, the more dangerous it becomes.","126":"Black’s chariot drops back and attacks Red’s soldier. Black must keep that soldier in check, or Red could fight back with it.","134":"Red’s soldier has reached the far end of the board, but Black’s chariot has been chasing it all the way. A soldier that reaches the far end can no longer go forward, only sideways.","136":"Black’s chariot takes Red’s last soldier. Black has a chariot, a soldier and two advisors; Red has a chariot and one advisor. With an extra soldier, Black’s win is now clearly in sight.","140":"Black’s chariot gives check! Red’s general can only step back.","144":"Black’s soldier advances again and is now right next to Red’s general. With soldier and chariot working together, Red’s general is no longer safe.","148":"Black’s general also steps sideways. In the endgame the general is a fighting piece too, controlling where the enemy pieces can go.","150":"Black’s chariot drops back, ready for both defence and attack. It is getting the chariot ready for the best square for the final blow.","152":"Black’s chariot enters file 4, and Red can no longer hold. The game is decided, and 15-year-old Hu Ronghua has won. A bold cannon sacrifice in the opening, teamwork of horse and cannon in the middlegame, and precise play through a long endgame — this game had all three. Hu won the tournament and went on to be national champion ten times in a row, from 1960 to 1979."}},"soei1790":{"t":"Demon Sōei beats the Meijin in an even game","w":"Ōhashi Sōei (VI)","b":"Ōhashi Sōkei (IX)","ev":"Oshiro Shogi (the yearly game at Edo Castle)","d":"1790","pl":"Japan (Edo)","lead":"Ōhashi Sōei, nicknamed 'Demon Sōei', played this even game against the reigning Meijin, Ōhashi Sōkei IX, at the castle games. That year the two alternated even games and lance-handicap games, and this even game is remembered as a rare masterpiece. Sōei won in 121 moves.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. The castle games (Oshiro Shogi) were a great yearly event, where the shogi houses played before the Shōgun. Sente, Ōhashi Sōei, was so strong that people called him 'Demon Sōei'; he later became the ninth Meijin. Gote, Ōhashi Sōkei IX, had become Meijin only the year before. So this is the Meijin against the next Meijin. Sōei spread the idea of building a solid defence first and attacking after, close to how shogi is played today, and he is even called the father of modern shogi. 1790 was the time when Matsudaira Sadanobu, a senior minister under the shogun Tokugawa Ienari, was carrying out the \"Kansei Reforms\". People were told to stop spending on luxuries, and that year the rules on publishing books became stricter too. Even so, the castle games went on. The 8th shogun, Yoshimune, is said to have fixed the castle-shogi day on 17 November, and today's Shogi Day in Japan comes from it.","1":"P-7f. The first move opens the bishop's diagonal. From here Sōei builds a plan to attack with his right-side pieces. Sōei was born in 1756, a son of Sōjun, the 5th head of the Ōhashi branch house. As a small child he was sent to live with another family, but it is said he was brought back home after being recognised as a born master, and he went on to inherit the house.","3":"P-2f. Sente pushes the pawn in front of the rook. Pushing the rook's pawn early to press the opponent was one of the ideas Sōei valued.","4":"P-4d. Gote pushes a pawn that closes its bishop's diagonal. It avoids a bishop trade and prepares for a slow game. Gote, Sōkei IX, was born in 1744. He first played in the castle games at 12 and is said to have beaten his uncle, Itō Kanju. The year before this game (1789), at the age of 46, he took over the Meijin title, which had been empty for 27 years.","5":"S-4h. Sente lifts the right silver so it can be used both to attack and to defend.","7":"P-5f. Sente pushes the centre pawn so that silvers and golds can come forward more easily.","9":"P-3f. Sente pushes the file-3 pawn too, opening a road for the knight to jump. It prepares N-3g.","11":"P-2e. Sente pushes the rook's pawn further. Now Gote must guard file 2 with the bishop or a silver. Pushing the pawn in front of the rook and trading it for the enemy pawn is called the \"rook-pawn exchange\". Sōei is said to have shown clearly that this trade is an advantage. It is still the most basic idea in Static Rook openings today.","12":"B-3c. Gote guards file 2 with the bishop. Without it, Sente's rook would break through on file 2 after P-2d.","15":"G-5h. Sente brings a gold closer to the king and starts to build its defence.","17":"N-3g. The knight jumps up and Sente's attacking shape is ready. From here the knight can leap to 4e or 2e to reach Gote's camp. Sōei is also known for winning, as a young man (in 1779), with a new plan called the \"Bird Catcher\". He did not stick to fixed shapes; he liked to invent new ways to play.","20":"R-5b. Gote moves its rook to the centre file 5. Today this is called the Central Rook. The aim is to shake Sente's camp from the middle. Now the game is an \"opposing\" one: Static Rook Sente against Ranging Rook (Central Rook) Gote. Sōkei IX is said to have often tried new plans such as Ranging Rook. The Central Rook is still a popular plan, played today under names like \"Cheerful Central Rook\".","22":"P-5e. Gote at once pushes its file-5 pawn into Sente's. As Central Rook players like to, it starts the fight in the middle. Putting the rook in the middle and pushing the file-5 pawn straight away is the basic move of the Central Rook. It is a direct way of attacking that still lives on in today's Central Rook.","23":"Px5e. Sente takes the pawn. If not, Gote's pawn would take the pawn on 5f.","24":"S-6d. Gote brings a silver forward, aiming to win back the pawn on 5e.","25":"P-5d. Sente pushes the captured pawn on even further. Placing it right in front of Gote's rook makes Gote take it, which moves Gote's pieces out of their best squares.","26":"Rx5d. Gote takes the pawn with the rook. Gote's rook has come out to 5d and is now strong in the centre.","27":"B-7i. Sente pulls the bishop back. Stepping back once lets it work later along a different diagonal. This bishop will later come out to 2d.","28":"K-4a. Gote moves its king to 4a. With a Central Rook, the king usually hides on the right side.","29":"P*5f. Sente drops a pawn to stop Gote's rook from advancing along file 5.","30":"P*5e. Gote drops a pawn too and clashes again on file 5. The tug of war in the centre goes on.","32":"Px5f. Gote's pawn takes Sente's pawn and bites into Sente's camp.","34":"S-5e. Gote brings the silver out to take control of file 5.","35":"Gx5e. Sente takes the silver with the gold. A gold and a silver are about to be traded.","36":"Rx5e. Gote takes back the gold with the rook. After this gold-for-silver trade, the fight in the centre calms down. Sente now has a silver in hand, and Gote has a gold.","37":"P*5f. Sente drops a pawn to block file 5, so Gote's rook cannot come any further.","38":"R-5b. Gote pulls its rook back. The fight in the centre is over for now.","39":"G-5h. Sente lifts its other gold and makes the area around the king firmer.","41":"K-6i. Sente's king starts moving left, away from the centre where the fighting happened.","43":"K-7h. The king reaches 7h. From here Sente will build a castle with golds and silvers. Sōei was a player who believed in defending first and attacking second.","44":"R-8b. Gote returns its rook to file 8. Now that the centre is quiet, it plans to attack on file 8 instead.","45":"S*7g. Sente drops the silver from its hand in front of the king. It prepares in advance for Gote's attack on file 8.","47":"P-1f. Sente pushes the edge pawn. An advanced edge pawn helps later, whether for an edge attack or as an escape route for the king.","49":"P-1e. The edge pawn goes one step further. Sente is preparing carefully.","51":"P-2d. Here Sente starts its attack, pushing the rook's pawn it has been advancing all along. Sente's offensive begins.","53":"Bx2d. The bishop that retreated to 7i flies all the way along the diagonal to 2d. Now we can see why Sente pulled the bishop back on move 27.","54":"Bx2d. Gote takes the bishop with its own bishop. The bishops are traded.","55":"Rx2d. Sente takes back with the rook, which now stands on 2d glaring at Gote's camp. Each side now has a bishop in hand.","56":"P*2c. Gote drops a pawn to block the rook's path.","57":"R-2f. Sente pulls the rook back. It doesn't push too far, and brings the rook back to safety.","59":"P-4f. Sente pushes the file-4 pawn to prepare the next attack. After file 2, the plan is to attack on files 3 and 4.","60":"R-8d. Gote lifts its rook so it can also move sideways.","61":"P-3e. Sente pushes the file-3 pawn, getting ready to clash with Gote's pawn on 3d.","63":"P-4e. Sente pushes its file-4 pawn into Gote's. Sente shakes Gote on several files at once: 3, 4 and 5.","65":"P-5e. Sente pushes the file-5 pawn forward too, driving a wedge into the middle of Gote's defence.","66":"P*5c. Gote drops a pawn to guard file 5.","67":"Px3d. Sente's pawn takes the pawn on 3d. Later this pawn will reach 3c and promote, pressing Gote's king.","68":"P-8f. Gote strikes back, pushing its file-8 pawn to start a fire near Sente's king.","69":"Sx8f. Sente takes with the silver. The silver from 7g steps forward and defends while keeping the king safe.","70":"B*4d. Gote drops the bishop from its hand. Along its diagonals it watches Sente's camp, ready to defend and attack.","71":"R-2i. Sente pulls the rook all the way back. It escapes the bishop's line and still watches file 2.","72":"P-6e. Gote pushes the file-6 pawn to shake the area near Sente's king.","74":"P*3f. Gote drops a pawn that attacks the knight on 3g.","75":"B*6f. Sente drops its bishop from hand. Along the diagonal it attacks the rook on 8d. It defends and attacks a big piece at the same time: two birds with one stone.","76":"R-8a. Gote runs the rook away. With just one bishop move, Sente has pushed Gote's rook all the way back to the bottom.","77":"Nx4e. The knight jumps and takes the pawn on 4e. Next it aims to jump into 3c or 5c.","78":"G*4f. Gote drops a gold that presses on the knight from behind.","79":"P-3c+. The pawn from 3d advances to 3c and promotes into a tokin. A Sente piece has broken in near Gote's king.","80":"Nx3c. Gote takes the tokin with its knight.","81":"Nx3c+. Sente's knight on 4e takes that knight and promotes. Trading piece for piece, Sente thins out the defenders around Gote's king.","82":"Bx3c. Gote takes back the promoted knight with the bishop.","83":"S-5g. Sente moves the silver up to 5g to clash with Gote's gold on 4f, to drive away the gold that came so close.","85":"Gx5g. Silver and gold have been traded. Sente has neatly cleared away the Gote piece that came into its camp.","86":"P-7e. Gote pushes its file-7 pawn, aiming at the head of Sente's king.","87":"N*4e. Sente drops a knight again. This knight attacks the bishop on 3c.","88":"B-2d. Gote moves the bishop away to 2d.","89":"P*3c. Sente drops a pawn to drive a wedge near Gote's king.","90":"G-2b. Gote steps the gold back to defend.","91":"P-5d. Sente pushes the file-5 pawn further, luring Gote's silver to 5d.","92":"Sx5d. Gote takes the pawn with the silver. But this leaves 4c empty, which links to Sente's next moves.","93":"Rx2d. This is the turning point. Sente takes Gote's bishop with the rook. Even if the rook is lost, Sente judges it can rush at Gote's king in the meantime.","94":"Px2d. Gote takes the rook with a pawn. Sente has given up its rook but won a bishop, an attacking piece, for its hand.","95":"B*3b, check. Sente drops the bishop. Following Rx2d, this is the real start of Sente's attack.","96":"K-5a. Gote's king escapes to 5a.","97":"Bx5d+. The bishop takes the silver on 5d and promotes into a horse. Since 4c was empty, the bishop could reach it straight along the diagonal. The empty square left by Sx5d on move 92 pays off here.","98":"Px5d. Gote takes the horse with a pawn. Sente has given away all its big pieces, but Gote's defending silver is gone too.","99":"P*5c. Sente drops a pawn right in front of Gote's king as a foothold. Next it aims to promote on 5b.","100":"R*4h. Gote strikes back, dropping the rook it won into Sente's camp. The game becomes a race: whose attack is faster?","101":"S-5h. Sente pulls the silver back to defend, blocking the rook from sliding sideways toward the king.","102":"B*5f, check. Gote drops a bishop. From 5f it reaches the king on 7h diagonally through 6g.","103":"K-8h. Sente steps the king down, out of the bishop's line. With this defensive move it makes sure it won't lose the race.","104":"Bx4e. Gote's bishop takes the knight.","105":"Px5b+. The pawn promotes into a tokin and takes Gote's gold. A Sente piece is now right beside Gote's king.","106":"Kx5b. Gote takes the tokin with the king.","107":"P*5c, check. Pawns can be dropped again and again, so Sente can keep checking like this (but you may not have two of your own unpromoted pawns on the same file).","108":"K-6b. Gote's king escapes to 6b.","109":"S*6d. Sente drops a silver to close the king's escape routes.","110":"S*7d. Gote drops a silver too, to defend.","111":"P-5b+. Sente makes a tokin and attacks the king from behind as well.","112":"K-7b. Gote's king goes to 7b. Step by step, it is being chased toward the edge.","113":"Sx7c+. The silver takes the knight on 7c and promotes. Another of Gote's defenders is gone.","114":"Kx7c. Gote takes the promoted silver with the king.","115":"G*6d, check. From here Sente uses the pieces in its hand to hunt down Gote's king.","116":"K-8c. Gote's king goes to 8c.","117":"Gx7d, check. The gold takes the silver on 7d with check, stripping away the defenders around Gote's king.","118":"Kx7d. Gote takes the gold with the king. The king has been dragged out toward the middle of the board.","119":"Sx7e, check. The silver on 8f takes a pawn with check. The silver that stepped up to defend on move 69 joins the attack at the end.","120":"K-6c. Gote's king escapes to 6c.","121":"S-6d, check. The silver on 7e moves to 6d with check. Sente still has a gold, a silver and a knight in hand, and seeing that the king could not escape, Sōkei admitted defeat here. Sōei wins in 121 moves. After the fight in the centre, Sente first built a firm castle, then widened the attack across files 2, 3 and 4, and finally gave up the rook to rush the king. It is a masterpiece between Meijin that shows Sōei's shogi of 'defend first, then attack'."}},"soho1856":{"t":"Sōho the Saint and his far-seeing bishop","w":"Amano Sōho","b":"Itō Sōin (VIII)","ev":"Oshiro Shogi (the yearly game at Edo Castle)","d":"1856","pl":"Japan (Edo)","lead":"Amano Sōho, called the 'Saint of Shogi', played this castle game against Itō Sōin VIII, a future Meijin, and it became Sōho's last castle game. On move 33 he dropped a bishop into the corner of his own camp, B*1h, a move remembered in shogi history as a masterstroke. Sōho wins in 111 moves.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. Amano Sōho played near the end of the Edo period, and he was so strong that people called him 'a true 13-dan' and the 'Saint of Shogi'. But he was not born into one of the shogi houses, so he could never become Meijin. Gote, Itō Sōin VIII, was Sōho's junior in the same school and later became Meijin. This castle game of 1856 was the last castle game Sōho ever played. Sōho is said to have been a master of the bishop, so keep an eye on the bishops in this game. 1856 was three years after Commodore Perry's \"black ships\" came to Japan. That year the American Townsend Harris arrived in Shimoda, and the first American consulate in Japan was set up in a temple there called Gyokusenji. This castle game comes from a time when the end of the Edo period was near and the country was starting to change fast.","1":"P-7f. The first move opens the bishop's diagonal. Sōho's bishop will be the star of this game. Sōho was born in 1816 and was known in daily life as Tomejirō. He learned shogi as a pupil of the Ōhashi main house, but his rank never went above 7-dan. It is said that the 13th Meijin, Sekine Kinjirō, later honoured him as the \"Saint of Shogi\". Sōho died in 1859, three years after this game, at the age of 44.","3":"P-2f. Sente pushes the pawn in front of the rook. Sōho liked fast openings, pushing the rook's pawn early and getting his pieces moving.","4":"P-5d. Gote pushes the centre pawn. We can see a plan coming: the rook will swing to file 5 for a Central Rook. Static Rook for Sente against Central Rook for Gote: the same \"Static Rook vs Central Rook\" opposing game as the one between Sōei and Sōkei IX some 60 years earlier. Ranging Rook was played often in the Edo castle games too.","7":"S-4h. Sente lifts the right silver to prepare for both attack and defence.","11":"P-2e. Sente pushes the rook's pawn further. Next it plans to clash on 2d.","12":"S-6d. Gote brings a silver forward. The idea is to advance it toward 5e and attack in the centre.","13":"P-2d. Sente pushes the pawn into Gote's to trade off the rook's pawn. It is a natural way to start a fight, still common today. The rook-pawn exchange is the idea Ōhashi Sōei is said to have shown to be an advantage about 60 years earlier. By Sōho's time it had become a normal part of Static Rook play.","15":"Rx2d. The rook takes the pawn. Sente gains a pawn in hand and the rook comes forward. Trading the rook's pawn gives you an extra pawn in hand and makes the rook easier to use.","16":"R-5b. Gote moves its rook to file 5. With a Central Rook it plans to strike back in the middle.","17":"K-6i. Sente moves the king to the left, away from the centre where the Central Rook will attack.","19":"R-2h. The rook goes back home. Having won a pawn, Sente calmly pulls the rook back.","20":"P*2c. Gote drops a pawn to close the gap on file 2.","21":"Bx2b+. Here Sente trades bishops. After a bishop trade, both sides hold a bishop in hand that can be dropped anywhere. From now on, Sōho shows his skill in where he drops his bishop. Trading bishops early, keeping one in hand and fighting over where to drop it was a way of playing Sōho loved. A bishop in hand can be dropped anywhere on the board, so it can become very strong if it is used well.","22":"Sx2b. Gote takes back with the silver. Each side now has a bishop in hand.","24":"P-5e. Gote pushes its centre pawn into Sente's. It is a typical Central Rook attack.","26":"Sx5e. Gote's silver comes all the way to 5e. Gote's pieces are getting strong in the centre.","27":"P*5d. Sente drops a pawn deep into Gote's camp. The pawn on 5d blocks the front of Gote's rook and gets in the way of Gote's pieces.","29":"B*5c. Sente drops its bishop into Gote's camp. The idea is to let it run wild inside Gote's position.","30":"B*4d. Gote drops its bishop too, clashing with Sente's. It is a defensive move to get rid of Sente's bishop.","31":"Bx4d+. Sente's bishop takes Gote's bishop and promotes.","32":"Sx4d. Gote takes the horse back with the silver. The bishops have been traded again, and Sente once more has a bishop in hand.","33":"B*1h. This is the famous moment. Sōho drops his bishop in the corner of his own camp, on 1h. Along its diagonal it looks all the way across the board into Gote's camp. For now it supports the pawn on 5d from behind, and later, when the diagonal opens, it will do great work: a 'far-seeing bishop'. It is called one of the masterstrokes of shogi history, though some later Meijin think it may have been a clever way out of a difficult spot.","34":"G-4b. Gote brings a gold over to firm up the area around the king.","35":"S-5g. Sente lifts a silver to prepare for the fight in the centre.","37":"S-5f. The silver moves further forward, aiming at the centre squares 5e and 4e.","39":"R-5h. Sente moves the rook to the centre. Sente too gathers strength on file 5, in a Central Rook shape.","41":"P-6f. Sente pushes the file-6 pawn to make a path for its silver and bishop.","43":"K-7i. The king goes deeper to the left. Even Sōho puts the king in order before attacking.","45":"G-5i. Sente brings a gold near the king to strengthen its defence.","46":"P-8e. Gote pushes its file-8 pawn, preparing to attack from near Sente's king.","47":"B-2g. The bishop on 1h steps up one square. Shifting its line slightly, it now eyes 4e and 3f.","49":"S-4e. The silver advances to 4e and presses on Gote's camp. The bishop on 2g supports this silver from behind.","50":"P*5e. Gote drops a pawn to block the centre.","52":"P-8f. Gote starts a fight on file 8, pushing a pawn into the pawn near Sente's king.","54":"P-7e. Gote pushes the file-7 pawn into Sente's too. It is trying to break several of the pawns in front of Sente's king.","56":"Sx4e. Gote's silver takes Sente's silver on 4e.","57":"Bx4e. The bishop on 2g takes back the silver on 4e. The bishop's diagonal comes in handy here.","58":"S-4d. Gote lifts a silver to chase the bishop on 4e.","59":"B-1h. The bishop goes back to the corner at 1h again. Chased by the silver, it returns to its far-seeing spot. Watch how this bishop works at the end.","60":"S*5f. Gote drops a silver and attacks in the centre.","61":"Sx5f. Sente takes with its silver.","62":"Px5f. Gote takes back with a pawn, which now reaches 5f.","63":"Rx5f. Sente takes the pawn with the rook. The rook works in the centre.","64":"S*5e. Gote drops another silver to chase Sente's rook.","65":"R-5h. The rook steps back to where it was.","66":"Sx6f. Gote's silver takes the pawn on 6f and comes closer to Sente's king. Gote's attack goes on.","67":"P*6g. Sente drops a pawn so the silver cannot come in any further.","68":"P*7g. Gote drops a pawn on 7g, near Sente's king. It wants to move Sente's pieces around and make a gap.","69":"Nx7g. Sente takes the pawn with the knight. Later this knight will jump to 6e and join the attack.","70":"Sx7e. Gote's silver takes the pawn on 7e and closes in on the head of Sente's king.","71":"G-6h. Sente brings a gold over to guard the king.","72":"P*7f. Gote drops another pawn, aiming at the knight. Gote's attack is getting sharp.","73":"N-6e. Here Sente strikes back. The knight jumps to 6e and aims at 5c in Gote's camp.","74":"S-6d. Gote pulls the silver back to aim at the knight on 6e.","75":"S*5c. Sente drops a silver deep into Gote's camp. The pawn on 5d and the knight on 6e both support it. The pawn Sente dropped on 5d long ago is useful here.","76":"Gx5c. Gote takes the silver with the gold.","77":"Px5c+. The pawn on 5d takes the gold and promotes into a tokin.","78":"Sx5c. Gote takes the tokin with the silver.","79":"Nx5c+. The knight on 6e takes that silver and promotes. The captures go on, and Gote's camp grows emptier and emptier.","80":"Sx5c. Gote takes the promoted knight with the silver. But now Sente's pawn on 5d is gone, so the diagonal of the bishop on 1h is open all the way to 6c.","81":"Bx6c+. At last the bishop on 1h moves. It flies down the diagonal, takes the pawn on 6c and becomes a horse. The 'far-seeing bishop' dropped on move 33 does its big job here, 48 moves later. It is a true bishop's move, running from one edge of the board to the other.","82":"S*7g. Gote drops a silver to press on Sente's king. Gote keeps attacking too.","83":"P*2d. Sente drops a pawn on file 2 to shake the area near Gote's king.","84":"Px2d. Gote takes it with a pawn.","85":"P*2e. Sente drops another pawn. It aims at Gote's pawn on 2d and tries to pry open file 2.","86":"B*3c. Gote drops a bishop to strengthen its defence.","87":"P*5e. Sente drops a pawn to hold back the movements of Gote's silver and bishop.","88":"Sx7h+. Gote's silver takes Sente's gold and promotes.","89":"Gx7h. Sente takes back with the gold.","90":"G*7g. Gote drops a gold that presses on Sente's king. The fighting near Sente's king is fierce too.","91":"S*4e. Sente drops a silver. This silver aims at 3d, near Gote's king.","92":"R-6a. Gote moves its rook to file 6 to attack Sente's horse.","93":"+B-7d. The horse moves away to 7d. A horse is strong: it helps in its own camp and in the enemy's.","94":"Px2e. Gote's pawn takes the pawn on 2e.","95":"Sx3d. The silver takes the pawn on 3d. Sente's silver is right in front of Gote's king.","96":"B-2d. Gote moves its bishop to 2d to prepare for Sente's attack.","97":"S*4f. Sente drops another silver. It cuts the diagonal of the bishop on 2d and limits what that bishop can do.","98":"G*3c. Gote drops a gold to defend.","99":"Sx3c+. The silver on 3d takes the gold and promotes.","100":"Kx3c. Gote takes the promoted silver with the king. Gote's king has come out to 3c, where there are few defenders.","101":"S*3e. Sente drops another silver. It attacks the bishop on 2d and also blocks the king's escape.","102":"N*8g, check. Gote drops a knight to check. It is a last stand.","103":"Gx8g. Sente takes the knight with the gold.","104":"Gx8g. Gote takes back with the gold. But Sente's king is still not caught.","105":"Sx2d, check. The silver takes the bishop with check. From here Sente hunts Gote's king all at once.","106":"Kx2d. Gote takes the silver with the king.","107":"S-3e, check. The silver on 4f moves to 3e with check, pulling Gote's king out of its camp.","108":"Kx3e. Gote's king takes the silver and comes out to 3e. When a king comes out toward the middle with no defenders, it is in great danger.","109":"B*4f, check. The bishop again, right to the end. Sente drops the bishop from its hand with check. In Sōho's game, the bishops played a starring role from beginning to end.","110":"K-4d. Gote's king escapes to 4d.","111":"G*3d, check. Seeing that the king could no longer escape, Sōin admitted defeat here. Sōho wins in 111 moves. B*1h on move 33 did great work 48 moves later with Bx6c+, and the game was decided by a bishop check too. It is a masterpiece that shows Sōho's way of placing a piece while looking far into the future."}},"sakata1913":{"t":"Sakata Sankichi beats Sekine with the Side Rook","w":"Sakata Sankichi","b":"Sekine Kinjirō","ev":"A game sponsored by the Osaka Asahi newspaper","d":"14 July 1913","pl":"Japan (Osaka)","lead":"Sakata Sankichi of Osaka and Sekine Kinjirō, who later became the 13th Meijin, were rivals all their lives. This is an even game they played in Osaka in the summer of 1913. Sakata attacked with the 'Side Rook', moving his rook to file 3, and won a long battle of 143 moves.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. Sakata Sankichi was born in Sakai near Osaka, learned shogi almost entirely by himself, and became the strongest player in western Japan. Sekine Kinjirō was the top player in Tokyo and later became the 13th Meijin. They had played each other many times since they were young, more than 30 games in all: a famous rivalry. In April of this year, Sakata went to Tokyo for the first time and played Sekine. The words 'my silver is crying' are said to come from that game, which was a lance-handicap game. This July game is an even game against Sekine, who had come to Osaka. 1913 was the second year of the Taishō era: the Emperor Meiji had died, and this was the first summer of the new age. That same month (July), near Osaka, the \"Takarazuka Choral Troupe\" was formed, which grew into today's Takarazuka Revue. A game sponsored by a newspaper was also something new: it did not exist in the Edo period, when the shogi houses ran the game.","1":"P-7f. The first move opens the bishop's diagonal. From here Sakata builds a plan full of his own ideas. Sakata was born in 1870 into a poor family in Sakai that made the tops of straw sandals. It is said he left primary school after about half a year and was never good at reading and writing. He is said to have learned shogi at about 12 by watching grown-ups play on benches by the street.","3":"P-5f. Sente pushes the centre pawn, preparing to bring a silver up to 5g. Gote, Sekine Kinjirō, was born in 1868. As a young man he travelled around the country to sharpen his skill, and eight years after this game, in 1921, he became the 13th Meijin at the age of 53.","5":"P-6f. Sente pushes the file-6 pawn too and closes the bishop's diagonal. It avoids a bishop trade and builds its position slowly.","9":"S-6g. A silver moves up in front of the king and starts a defensive wall.","11":"B-7g. The bishop steps up behind the pawn on 6f. Using the bishop to defend gives a shape close to the Fortress castle. Building with the bishop on 7g, a silver on 6g and a gold on 7h leads toward the Fortress (Yagura), the classic castle of Static Rook against Static Rook. In the Shōwa and Heisei eras that followed, the Fortress became one of the most played plans in professional shogi.","13":"S-4h. Sente lifts the other silver too, ready to use it in the attack.","16":"K-4b. Gote, Sekine, moves his king toward the right side.","17":"G-7h. A gold comes toward the king to make the defence firmer.","19":"P-3f. Sente pushes the file-3 pawn. This is where Sakata's plan begins.","21":"R-3h. Sente moves the rook to file 3. This is the 'Side Rook'. Instead of file 2, the rook stands on the file next to it, file 3, to attack there. Sakata often used unusual plans like this. The Side Rook is still played now and then in Static Rook games, as a plan to attack file 3, close to the enemy king, from early on. Sakata's name also lives on in the name of an opening: the \"Sakata Opposing Rook\".","23":"K-6i. Sente's king goes left, away from file 3, where the attack will be.","26":"K-3b. Gote's king goes to 3b. But that is right next to file 3, which Sente's rook is aiming at.","27":"P-3e. The Side Rook attack begins. Sente pushes the file-3 pawn into Gote's and tries to break through on file 3 with the rook.","28":"Px3e. Gote takes with the pawn.","29":"Rx3e. The rook takes the pawn. Sente's rook comes out to 3e and glares at the area near Gote's king. Sente has also won a pawn.","30":"B-3a. Gote pulls the bishop back to prepare to defend file 3.","31":"R-3h. Sente pulls the rook back. Having won a pawn, it calmly brings the rook home.","32":"P-8f. Gote strikes back on file 8.","34":"Bx8f. The bishop that went back to 3a comes all the way along the diagonal to 8f and takes the pawn. A clever use of the bishop's long line.","35":"Bx8f. Sente takes the bishop with its bishop.","36":"Rx8f. Gote takes back with the rook. The bishops have been traded.","37":"P*8g. Sente drops a pawn to chase Gote's rook.","38":"R-8b. Gote's rook goes back home. Each side now has a bishop in hand.","39":"S-4f. Sente brings a silver forward to prepare for fights in the centre and on file 4.","43":"K-7i. Sente tucks the king in deeper and puts its defence in order.","47":"K-8h. The king settles on 8h, with golds and silvers gathered around it.","51":"P-5e. Sente pushes the centre pawn into Gote's. From here the real fighting starts.","52":"Px5e. Gote takes with the pawn.","53":"B*6c. Sente drops its bishop into Gote's camp. The idea is to let it run wild inside Gote's position.","55":"R-5h. Sente swings the rook to file 5 to join the fight in the centre.","56":"B*9b. Gote drops its bishop too. Along its diagonal it stares at Sente's camp.","57":"B-3f+. The bishop on 6c comes back along the diagonal and promotes as it leaves the enemy camp, becoming a horse on 3f. Back in its own camp, it can both defend and attack. A horse in your own camp is a very reliable defender.","58":"P-4e. Gote pushes a pawn to chase the silver on 4f.","59":"S-5g. The silver steps back to 5g.","60":"Px7f. Gote's file-7 pawn takes the pawn in front of Sente's king.","61":"Sx7f. Sente takes back with the silver.","62":"S-5d. Gote brings a silver to the centre to firm up its defence.","63":"+B-3g. The horse steps back to guard Sente's camp.","64":"R-7b. Gote swings its rook to file 7, aiming at the head of Sente's king.","65":"P*7e. Sente drops a pawn to block the rook's path.","66":"G-4d. Gote lifts a gold.","68":"K-4c. Gote's king climbs to 4c. It is an unusual shape, with the king close to the middle of the board. This king position will matter a lot in the race later on.","69":"G-6g. Sente lifts a gold next to the silver on 5g, building a defensive wall.","71":"N-7g. The knight jumps up, ready to join the attack.","73":"P-6e. Sente pushes its file-6 pawn into Gote's to shake Gote's camp.","75":"P*5f. Sente drops a pawn to firm up the centre.","76":"P-3f. Gote pushes its file-3 pawn and pokes at Sente's horse.","77":"+Bx3f. The horse takes the pawn.","78":"Px5f. Gote's pawn takes Sente's pawn and presses into the centre.","79":"Sx5f. Sente takes back with the silver.","80":"P*5e. Gote drops a pawn to block the silver's path.","81":"Sx6e. The silver takes the pawn on 6e and approaches Gote's camp from the side.","82":"P*6d. Gote drops a pawn to chase the silver.","83":"Sx5d. The silver takes the silver on 5d, removing one of Gote's defenders.","84":"Gx5d. Gote takes back with the gold.","85":"P*6e. Sente drops another pawn and keeps attacking on file 6.","86":"P*3e. Gote drops a pawn to chase Sente's horse.","87":"+B-3g. The horse goes back to 3g. It is an important defender of Sente's camp, so Sente does not force it forward.","88":"S-4d. Gote lifts a silver to guard the king.","89":"P*5f. Sente drops a pawn into Gote's pawn on 5e, trying to pry open the centre.","91":"Px6d. The pawn on 6e takes the pawn on 6d. A Sente pawn has made its way into Gote's camp.","92":"P-4f. Gote pushes its file-4 pawn and strikes back.","95":"P-6c+. The pawn promotes into a tokin. A tokin moves like a gold, yet if it is captured the opponent gets only a pawn.","96":"Gx6c. Gote takes the tokin with the gold.","97":"P*6f. Sente drops a pawn to fill a gap in its own camp.","98":"N-3c. Gote jumps its knight up to help defend.","99":"S*6e. Sente drops a silver from its hand. This silver is aiming at the gold on 5d.","100":"P*6d. Gote drops a pawn to try to stop the silver.","101":"Sx5d. The silver takes the gold on 5d. Another defender of Gote's king is gone.","102":"Gx5d. Gote takes back with the gold.","103":"P*3d. Sente drops a pawn and pokes at the area near Gote's king.","104":"Kx3d. Gote's king takes the pawn and steps out to 3d. The king is coming further forward.","105":"P*3f. Sente drops a pawn to hold down the front of Gote's king.","106":"S*4d. Gote drops a silver to protect the king.","107":"R-3i. Sente swings the rook back to file 3. Gote's king is on file 3, so the Side Rook is back at work.","108":"K-4c. Gote's king steps back to 4c.","109":"Px3e. The pawn on 3f takes the pawn on 3e. A Sente pawn moves right up close to Gote's king.","110":"P*3h. Gote drops a pawn that attacks the rook on 3i.","111":"G*3d, check. Sente drops a gold from its hand and drives Gote's king further.","112":"K-5c. Gote's king escapes to 5c.","113":"R-5i. Attacked by the pawn on file 3, the rook moves away to 5i.","115":"+B-2f. The horse steps aside to 2f. If Gote's silver came to 4f, it would hit the horse on 3g, so this seems to be dodging in advance.","116":"Sx4f. Gote's silver takes the pawn on 4f and comes closer to Sente's camp.","117":"Gx3c. The gold takes the knight. Sente shaves away Gote's pieces one by one.","119":"G-3d. The gold goes back to 3d, a square that blocks the king's escape.","121":"N*7d. Sente drops a knight. It aims at 8b, where Gote's rook is, and at 6b.","122":"R-4b. Gote runs the rook away to file 4.","123":"P*4d. Sente drops a pawn to pull Gote's defending gold out of place.","124":"Gx4d. Gote takes the pawn with the gold.","125":"Gx4d. Sente takes the gold with its gold.","126":"Rx4d. Gote takes back with the rook.","127":"Gx5f. The gold takes the pawn on 5f.","128":"Sx5f. Gote takes the gold with the silver.","129":"Rx5f. Sente takes back the silver with the rook. The trades go on, and the game reaches its final stage.","130":"S*5e. Gote drops a silver to chase Sente's rook.","131":"R-5i. The rook steps back.","132":"P*5h. Gote drops a pawn to chase the rook again.","133":"R-6i. The rook goes to 6i. Gote keeps chasing Sente's rook with pawns.","134":"K-5d. Gote's king comes out all the way to 5d. A king in the middle of the board has few defenders around it and is easy to target.","135":"+B-3f, check. The horse moves to 3f and gives check to the king on 5d along the diagonal through 4e. The horse that had guarded Sente's camp all this time joins the attack here.","136":"G*4e. Gote drops a gold to block the horse's line.","137":"G*6e, check. Sente drops a gold and closes in from right beside the king.","138":"Px6e. Gote takes the gold with a pawn.","139":"Sx6e, check. The silver on 7f takes that pawn with check. The silver that had stood in front of the king since the middle game joins the attack.","140":"K-4c. Gote's king goes back to 4c.","141":"+Bx4e. The horse takes the gold on 4e. Sente keeps taking the pieces around Gote's king.","142":"Rx4e. Gote takes the horse with the rook.","143":"S*5d, check. Seeing that the king could no longer escape, Sekine admitted defeat here. Sakata wins in 143 moves. He attacked on file 3 with the Side Rook, defended his camp with the horse through a long middle game while shaving away Gote's pieces little by little, and at the end caught Gote's king after it had come forward. It is a stubborn, determined game, just like the self-taught Sakata. After his death, Sakata was given the titles of Meijin and Ōshō."}},"nanzenji1937":{"t":"The Nanzen-ji Showdown: Sakata's Edge Pawn","w":"Kimura Yoshio","b":"Sakata Sankichi","ev":"Special match sponsored by the Yomiuri Shimbun","d":"February 1937","pl":"Japan (Nanzen-ji temple, Kyoto)","lead":"Sakata Sankichi had been cut off from the Tokyo shogi world for years after calling himself Meijin. In this game, at the age of 66, he returned to the big stage. His opponent was 31-year-old Kimura Yoshio, who would soon become the first Meijin chosen by strength. They played for 7 days at Nanzen-ji temple in Kyoto, with 30 hours of thinking time each. On his very first move, Sakata pushed the pawn on the far edge, and the whole country was astonished.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. Sakata Sankichi was born in Sakai near Osaka and became the great master of western Japan by teaching himself almost everything. At the end of the Taishō era he named himself 'Meijin' on his own, so the Tokyo shogi world cut ties with him, and for a long time he could not play Tokyo professionals officially. Later, Sekine Kinjirō stepped down as Meijin, and a new system began in which the Meijin would be decided by strength (the Meijin-sen). As part of that story, the Yomiuri newspaper arranged a game between Sakata and Kimura Yoshio, the leading candidate for the new title. The game was played from February 5 to 11, 1937, over 7 days at Nanzen-ji temple in Kyoto. Each player had an amazing 30 hours of thinking time, something unthinkable today.","1":"P-7f. The most ordinary first move, opening the bishop's diagonal. Kimura Yoshio was born in Honjo, Tokyo, and grew up as a disciple of Sekine Kinjirō. He valued the 'straight' style of piling up correct moves, and in this game too he sets up calmly and solidly.","2":"P-9d. This is the famous 'Sakata's edge pawn'. Gote is already one move behind, so by the common sense of the time, pushing the pawn on the very edge on move 2 was like throwing away another move. People say Sakata used this move to show his pride after years without a stage. The writer Oda Sakunosuke later wrote in one of his works how moved he was when he read about this move in the newspaper.","3":"P-5f. Kimura pushes the central pawn. Since his opponent has lost a move, he wants to take wide space in the center and grab the initiative. Kimura later wrote, in his own words, that the edge pawn surprised him greatly, but that he quickly started thinking about what his opponent intended.","4":"P-3d. Sakata opens his bishop's diagonal too. So far Gote has pushed the pawns on file 9 and file 3. Whether the edge pawn will ever be useful is one of the things to watch in this game. 1937, the year of this game, was also the year the war between Japan and China began, in July. Newspapers gave big shogi matches lots of space, and this one was called 'the match of the century'.","5":"P-5e. Kimura pushes the file-5 pawn one more step and takes 'the position on file 5'. Taking a position means advancing a pawn to the fifth rank and claiming the area around it. If you control the center, your opponent's pieces have fewer places to go.","6":"P-4d. A move that blocks his own bishop on purpose. Sakata meets the central pawn on 5e with a pawn on 4d and prepares to build his camp slowly. Blocking your own bishop like this is an idea often seen later in 'ranging rook' openings.","7":"S-4h. The right silver comes up to support the file-5 position. A position you have taken can easily collapse, so supporting it from behind with a silver is important.","8":"S-3b. Sakata also lifts a silver. This silver plans to go to 4c and then 3d, working on file 3. The next month, Sakata also played Hanada Chōtarō at Tenryū-ji temple in Kyoto, and that time he pushed the pawn on the opposite edge, P-1d, on move 2. Hanada won that game in 169 moves.","9":"S-5g. The silver now stands right behind the pawn on 5e. The file-5 position is firmly supported, and the 'central pawn position' setup is complete. The Meijin-sen, which decides the Meijin by strength, began when Sekine Kinjirō decided in 1935 to step down. At the time of this game, the league to choose the first Meijin was going on, and Kimura was leading it.","10":"S-4c. The silver comes to 4c. It watches the 5d square and keeps Sente's file-5 pawn from pushing further. Sakata's life later became the play 'Ōshō' by Hōjō Hideji, and became widely known through songs and films too.","11":"P-2f. Now Kimura pushes the pawn in front of his rook. On top of the central position, he prepares an attack on file 2 as well: a greedy and powerful plan. In even games of that time, battles where neither side moved the rook ('static rook' against 'static rook') were common, and Kimura is playing in that mainstream style.","12":"P-3e. Sakata stretches his file-3 pawn. Against Kimura's file-2 attack, he takes a position on file 3 to push back.","13":"P-2e. The rook's pawn moves forward again. Soon it will poke at the pawn on 2d, so Gote must decide how to use his bishop.","14":"B-3c. The bishop steps up to help guard file 2. Sakata will use the bishop for defense on file 2 and is getting ready to move his rook next.","15":"K-6h. Kimura starts moving his king to the left. A battle seems likely on the right side, so the basic idea is to move the king away to the other side.","16":"R-2b. Sakata swings his rook to file 2. Because it faces the opponent's rook directly, this opening is called the 'Opposing Rook'. Edge pawn plus Opposing Rook is a bold plan to meet the opponent's rook head-on, very much in Sakata's style. In even games between top players at that time, ranging rook openings were a minority, which also shows Sakata's individuality.","17":"P-4f. Pushing the file-4 pawn opens a path for the silver and gold to move forward. Kimura aims for a thick formation from the center to the right.","18":"G-3b. Sakata brings his left gold next to the rook, preparing for the fight on file 2. In the Opposing Rook, guarding the side of the rook with a gold is a common shape.","20":"S-3d. The silver advances to 3d and supports the pawn on 3e. Gote's pieces are moving forward bit by bit on files 3 and 2.","21":"K-7h. The king moves one more square to the left. Kimura's king is getting farther away from the right side, where the battle will happen.","22":"G-6b. Sakata lifts the gold from 6a to 6b. It is preparation for castling his king on the left side.","24":"K-6a. Sakata's king goes to 6a. In ranging rook openings, the king is usually castled on the side opposite the rook, toward files 7 and 8, and Sakata starts moving his king in that direction.","25":"G-4g. Kimura lifts his right gold to 4g. It supports the pawn on 4f and the 3f square, and the idea is to make the gold join the fight on the right. This setup values attack and central thickness more than the king's defense.","26":"P-2d. At last Sakata starts the fight. In the Opposing Rook, the basic plan is to clash pawns on file 2 like this and make the two rooks glare at each other.","27":"Px2d. Kimura takes the pawn. If he didn't, Gote would play P-2e and take it, and Gote's rook file would open first.","28":"Bx2d. The bishop takes back. It comes out to 2d and watches the right side of Sente's camp.","29":"P-5d. Kimura pushes the central pawn even further. The idea is to use the file-5 position to counterattack from the center; if Gote takes with the pawn, Sente's silver and bishop start working in the center. Because Gote's bishop has gone to 2d, the center has become a little thin.","30":"N-3c. Sakata doesn't take the pawn on 5d. Instead he jumps his knight to 3c and adds another attacking piece. His idea is to leave the center for now and hurry his attack on the right.","31":"P-3f. Kimura pushes the file-3 pawn, aiming to clash with Gote's pawn on 3e. The idea is to remove Gote's file-3 position and open a road for his own gold and silver.","32":"P*2f. Sakata drops a pawn from his hand on 2f. If Gote next plays P-2g+, the file 2 where Sente's rook stands will break open. It is a sharp move that stabs into the opponent's camp with a single light pawn.","33":"Px3e. Kimura ignores the pawn on 2f for now and takes the pawn on 3e. He probably thought that pushing a pawn to file 3 here would tie up Gote's bishop and silver.","34":"Bx3e. The bishop takes back the pawn on 3e. Since it is protected by the silver on 3d, it can't be captured right away for free.","35":"G-3f. Kimura lifts his gold and confronts the bishop. It offers a gold-for-bishop trade; if they trade, Sente gets a bishop in hand and also gains time to take Gote's pawn on 2f.","36":"K-7b. Sakata leaves the bishop for now and moves his king to 7b. He decides to move the king to a slightly safer place before the fighting gets fierce.","37":"Px5c+. Kimura's pawn on 5d captures and promotes, creating a tokin. It is a harsh move that promotes a pawn right next to Gote's king.","38":"Gx5c. Gote takes with the gold. This pulls Gote's gold to 5c, so the gold that was guarding the king has shifted out of place.","39":"P*2e. Kimura drops a pawn on 2e. It cuts the connection between Gote's pawn on 2f and the rook on 2b and leaves the pawn on 2f isolated. If he captures that pawn later, Sente's rook will be free.","41":"G-5i. Kimura pulls back his left gold to strengthen the defense around his king. It is a calm move that adds one defensive move before the race of attacks begins.","43":"Gx3e. Here the gold-for-bishop trade happens. Kimura gets a bishop, and Gote gets a gold in hand. The bishop is a major piece that works from far away, so it will be a trump card later when aiming at the king.","44":"Sx3e. The silver takes back the gold. Gote's silver comes out to 3e and presses toward Sente's camp.","45":"P*3d. Kimura drops a pawn that hits the knight on 3c. If he can play P-3c+ next, a hole will open in Gote's defense.","46":"Nx2e. Sakata jumps his knight while capturing the pawn on 2e. The knight escapes from the pawn's attack and at the same time comes closer to Sente's camp.","47":"P-4e. Kimura pushes the file-4 pawn, and together with the pawn on 3d it restricts Gote's pieces. A wall of Sente pawns is forming from the center to the right.","48":"R-2d. Gote's rook floats up to 2d. It works sideways, aims at the pawn on 3d, and joins the attack.","49":"Px4d. Kimura captures the pawn on 4d and moves forward again. If he gets to play P-4c+ next, Gote's camp will crumble badly.","50":"P*5e. Gote drops a pawn on 5e, right in the path of Sente's bishop. It is a lure: by making Sente take it with the bishop, Gote shifts the bishop's position.","51":"Bx5e. Kimura takes with the bishop. The bishop comes out to 5e in the center and watches far across the board.","52":"Gx4d. Sakata's gold from 5c captures the pawn on 4d and stops Sente's pawn attack. At the same time, the gold is now attacking the bishop on 5e.","53":"S-4f. Kimura lifts the silver to 4f and ties it to the bishop on 5e. It protects the bishop while adding the silver to the front line.","54":"Gx5e. Sakata takes the bishop with the gold. Sente could take back with the silver on 4f, but Kimura chooses a different move. He probably thought that taking the piece at the center of Gote's attack was more important than recapturing on 5e.","55":"Sx3e. Kimura does not recapture the gold on 5e; instead he takes Gote's silver on 3e. With a bishop traded for a silver, Gote is a little ahead in material. Even so, it means a lot that Sente's silver has come out near Gote's rook and knight.","56":"R-2a. The rook, which was attacked by the silver, retreats to 2a. It stays safe while still watching file 2.","57":"B*7g. Kimura drops the bishop he got onto 7g. The 7g square protects Sente's king, and from there the bishop also looks along a long diagonal through the center deep into Gote's camp. It is a drop square that helps both defense and attack.","58":"G*4e. Gote drops his gold from hand on 4e. Lining up two golds on 5e and 4e, he controls 5f and 4f in the center: a powerful move.","59":"P*5c. Kimura drops a pawn on 5c. If he plays P-5b+ next, a tokin will be born right next to Gote's king.","60":"P*5a. Sakata drops a pawn on the bottom rank to guard 5b. A defensive pawn like this is called a 'bottom pawn'. It is a quiet but important move that stops the opponent's tokin attack.","61":"S-4f. Kimura pulls the silver back to 4f, hitting Gote's golds on 4e and 5e. If a gold comes out to take it, Sente can use the time to push his attack on the right side.","62":"Gx4f. The gold from 5e takes the silver. Gote keeps gaining material, but in exchange his gold goes deep, close to Sente's camp.","63":"P-3c+. The pawn on 3d promotes and becomes a tokin. At last, Sente's pieces break into the right side of Gote's camp.","64":"R-2d. Gote floats his rook to 2d to keep the tokin in check from the side.","65":"+Px3b. The tokin captures the gold on 3b. A tokin is as cheap as a pawn but moves like a gold, and if it is captured the opponent gets only a pawn. Taking a gold with a tokin is a very happy gain for the attacker.","66":"R-3d. The rook comes out to 3d and presses on the right side of Sente's camp. Sakata also joins the race of attacks.","67":"+P-4b. The tokin moves even closer to the king. A tokin moves only one step at a time, but it surely closes in on Gote's king, as the saying 'a tokin is slow but sure' goes.","68":"P-7d. This makes an escape route for Gote's king. Before the tokin arrives, Sakata opens file 7 so the king can run upward.","69":"Rx2f. Kimura finally captures the pawn on 2f with his rook. The rook comes out to the sixth rank, works widely sideways, and also looks at the gold on 4f. The rook that couldn't move for so long has come back to life.","70":"R-3i+. Sakata drives his rook deep into Sente's camp and promotes it to a dragon. A dragon moves like a rook and can also move one square diagonally: a strong piece that threatens Sente's bottom rank.","71":"S*6a. Kimura drops a silver for free, giving check. By making Gote take with the king, he pulls the king back to the bottom rank and prepares to drop a gold there. Making the king's shape worse even at the cost of a piece is one of the standard techniques for catching the king.","72":"Kx6a. The king takes the silver. The king steps down to 6a, and fewer defenders are around it.","73":"G*8b. Kimura drops a gold on 8b. It blocks the escape route on the left of the king and also aims at the knight on 8a and the 7a square.","74":"S-7a. The silver steps back to stop the gold's attack.","75":"Rx4f. Here the rook captures the gold on 4f. Between attacking moves, Kimura wins pieces back one by one, recovering what Gote had taken earlier.","76":"Sx8b. Gote's silver also takes the gold on 8b. Both sides are grabbing each other's pieces in a fierce race.","77":"Rx4e. The rook takes the other gold on 4e as well. Now Sente is clearly ahead in material. The rook works sideways along the fifth rank and watches the area in front of Gote's king.","78":"K-7b. The king climbs to 7b and gets a little distance from the tokin and silver.","79":"+P-5b. The tokin advances again, closing in on Gote's king step by step.","80":"S*7a. Gote drops a silver from hand on 7a to strengthen the area under the king.","81":"G*6b, check. The king can only run upward and is being chased out into open space.","82":"K-7c. The king escapes to 7c. The escape route made by P-7d on move 68 is now useful.","83":"Rx2e. The rook captures the knight on 2e. Between checks, Kimura collects pieces one by one and saves them for the final attack.","84":"G*5f. Sakata drops a gold on 5f. It blocks the sideways path of Sente's rook and also puts pressure on Sente's camp: a stubborn move.","85":"G*6f. Kimura also drops a gold and attacks the gold on 5f. It goes after Gote's gold while also guarding above his own king: two birds with one stone.","86":"P-8d. Gote pushes the file-8 pawn, opening more room for the king to run.","87":"Gx5f. The gold takes the gold on 5f. Sente has more and more pieces, and Gote has fewer and fewer attacking pieces.","88":"B*4g. Sakata drops his bishop from hand on 4g, aiming at the gold on 5f. He never gives up and keeps looking for counterattacks.","89":"R-6e. The rook swings to 6e. It watches file 6 and aims to attack Gote's king from above.","90":"Sx6b. The silver takes the gold on 6b and clears an attacker near the king.","91":"+Px6b. The tokin takes back the silver. Even if a tokin is taken, the opponent gets only a pawn, so it is a reliable piece you can keep pushing forward.","92":"S*5d. Gote drops a silver and attacks the rook on 6e. It tries to chase the rook away and make the area above the king thicker.","93":"G*7b, check. Gote's king can only run to 8c now.","94":"K-8c. The king goes to 8c. It has been chased right next to the pawn on 9d, the edge pawn pushed on move 2.","95":"B-5e. The bishop on 7g comes out to 5e. Its diagonal watches 6d and 7c near Gote's king, and together with the rook, gold, and tokin there is no defense left. Sakata admitted defeat here, and Kimura won in 95 moves. The edge pawn on move 2 could not be put to use in this game, and later Naitō Kunio wrote that this was one of the greatest games in the history of shogi. In December of that year, Kimura became the first Meijin chosen by strength, and Sakata's edge pawn is still told as a legend marking the turning point of an era."}},"sokan1725":{"t":"Sōkan's brilliant knight drop N*3e","w":"Itō Sōkan III","b":"Ōhashi Sōmin (later Ōhashi Sōyo IV)","ev":"Castle shogi (the yearly game at Edo Castle)","d":"17th day of the 11th month, 1725 (Kyōhō 10)","pl":"Japan (Edo Castle)","lead":"A castle game between Itō Sōkan III (20), who would later become the 7th Meijin, and Ōhashi Sōmin (17), heir of the Ōhashi branch house and later Ōhashi Sōyo IV. Sente's Yagura castle meets Gote's wall of two silvers. Sōkan's knight drop N*3e on move 81 is a famous move that the later 15th Meijin Ōyama Yasuharu is said to have called 'a brilliant move'. Sōkan won in 101 moves.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. Today we watch a castle game from the middle of the Edo period, in 1725 (Kyōhō 10). Castle shogi was a yearly game at Edo Castle, played before the shōgun by the masters of the three shogi houses: the Ōhashi main house, the Ōhashi branch house and the Itō house. The 8th shōgun, Tokugawa Yoshimune, is said to have fixed it on the 17th day of the 11th month, and today's 'Shogi Day' (17 November) comes from this. Sente, Itō Sōkan III, was 20 and had taken over the Itō house only two years before, when his father Sōin II died. Gote, Ōhashi Sōmin, was 17 and would later take the name Ōhashi Sōyo IV. Watch this battle between two young heirs of the shogi houses.","1":"P-7f. This opens the bishop's diagonal and is the most common first move. In this year Arai Hakuseki died in Japan, and Peter the Great died in Russia. Yoshimune's 'Kyōhō reforms' were under way, and in Edo shogi was enjoyed by samurai and townspeople alike.","2":"P-3d. Gote opens its bishop's diagonal too. It is said that as a boy Sōkan loved swordsmanship and martial arts more than shogi. He is said to have thrown himself into shogi after his elder brother Indatsu died at 15 in 1712.","3":"P-2f. Sente pushes the pawn in front of the rook to prepare an attack on file 2. This start, P-7f, P-3d, P-2f, is still one of the most common openings today.","4":"P-4d. Gote pushes the pawn in front of its bishop and closes the diagonal again. It avoids a bishop trade and builds its camp slowly. In the castle games of this era, this P-4d, with Gote choosing to defend, appears very often.","5":"P-2e. Sente pushes the rook's pawn one more step. Next, P-2d would trade pawns on file 2, so Gote needs a move to stop it.","6":"B-3c. The bishop rises to guard the square 2d. Stopping the rook-pawn trade like this was Gote's normal defence in those days. Because the bishop is used for defence, Gote's game becomes a slow one.","7":"S-4h. Sente lifts the right silver. The silver is useful in attack and in defence, and it first heads for the centre.","8":"S-3b. Gote lifts a silver too and starts building the king's defence.","9":"P-5f. Sente pushes the centre pawn to make a path for the silver to 5g.","10":"S-4c. Gote's silver goes to 4c, and soon a second silver will stand beside it on 5c. Two years earlier, in the 1723 castle game between the same two players, Sōmin answered the same opening by moving his rook to 4b: a Fourth-File Rook. This time he leaves the rook on file 8 and fights with a Static Rook.","11":"P-4f. Sente pushes the pawn on file 4 so the pieces on the right can move easily. Sōkan is said to have played his first castle game at the age of 11, and won it.","12":"G-3b. Gote lifts a gold to strengthen the bishop on 3c and the king's defence.","13":"S-5g. The silver comes to the centre. This silver will move from square to square and become the leader of the attack.","14":"P-5d. Gote pushes its centre pawn too, preparing to bring a silver to 5c.","16":"S-6b. The left silver moves as well. Gote aims for a shape with two silvers side by side on 4c and 5c. This wall of silvers belongs to the family of shapes later called 'Gangi' (the 'wild-geese' formation).","17":"G-5h. Sente lifts a gold and starts building its king's castle.","18":"S-5c. Now the two silvers stand side by side. The silvers on 4c and 5c guard the centre, and Gote's camp has become very solid.","19":"K-6h. Sente moves its king to the left. Keeping the king away from the right side, where you will attack, is the basic rule of Static Rook play.","21":"P-6f. Sente pushes the pawn on file 6. This opens the way for the silver to come to 7g and build a 'Yagura castle'.","22":"P-8d. Gote pushes its rook's pawn and prepares to attack toward Sente's king.","25":"S-7g. The left silver goes to 7g. With the silver on 7g, a gold coming to 6g and a gold on 7h, the king will be wrapped in a Yagura castle. Yagura has been played since the Edo period and is still one of the most famous castles today.","28":"K-4a. Gote moves its king to the right. Each side castles its king on the opposite wing, so the fighting will happen far from each king.","29":"G-6g. The gold rises, and the Yagura shape is almost complete.","31":"B-7i. Sente pulls the bishop back. This empties 8h for the king, and the bishop will later go to 6h to attack along a diagonal.","33":"K-8h. The king enters the Yagura. From here on, how solid each king is will decide the game.","35":"G-7h. The other gold joins in, and the Yagura castle is complete: silver 7g, gold 6g, gold 7h and king 8h. A sturdy shape.","36":"N-7c. Gote jumps its left knight to aim at 6e. A knight is just the piece for attacking the silver of an enemy Yagura.","37":"S-4h. Sente pulls the silver from 5g back to 4h. The plan is to swing this silver round to the right, via 3g to 2f, and attack on files 2 and 3. It looks like a detour, but it is an important way of gathering attackers.","38":"R-8d. Gote lifts its rook one square so it can also move sideways: a 'floating rook'.","41":"S-3g. The silver reaches 3g. Sente is now ready to clash pawns on file 3 and start the attack.","43":"P-3e. The attack begins. Sente pushes the pawn on file 3 into Gote's, to break up the defence in front of the silver on 4c and the bishop on 3c.","44":"Px3e. Gote takes the pawn.","45":"S-2f. The silver goes to 2f. Next it wants to take on 3e with the silver and push it forward.","46":"S-3d. Gote lifts a silver to guard the pawn on 3e. The two sides' silvers glare at each other on file 3.","47":"R-3h. The rook swings to file 3. Sente gathers silver and rook on file 3 to break through there.","50":"P-6e. Gote starts a counter-attack on the other side. It pushes the pawn on file 6 into Sente's, aiming at the silver and gold of the Yagura. Both sides now attack on the side they like best: a lively fight.","51":"Px6e. Sente takes the pawn.","52":"Nx6e. Gote takes with the knight from 7c, and the knight lands on 6e. This knight aims at the silver on 7g and the square 5g, a very unpleasant piece for Sente.","53":"Sx3e. Sente does not give in either: the silver takes the pawn on 3e and comes forward. Leaving Gote's knight alone and putting its own attack first is a bold move.","54":"Sx3e. Gote's silver takes the silver.","55":"Rx3e. The rook takes back. The silvers have been traded and Sente's rook has come out to 3e.","56":"P*3d. Gote drops a pawn to block the rook.","57":"R-3h. The rook goes back home. Where Sente will use the silver it gained is something to look forward to.","58":"P-7e. Gote pushes the pawn on file 7 to strike at the head of Sente's Yagura. Together with the knight on 6e, Gote's attack is gathering near Sente's king.","59":"S-6f. Sente lifts the silver from 7g to 6f and hits the knight on 6e. It is a move that defends while trying to remove an attacking piece.","60":"S-6d. Gote lifts a silver to protect the knight on 6e.","61":"P*3e. Sente drops a pawn on file 3 again, aiming at the pawn on 3d.","62":"Px7f. Gote's pawn advances and takes Sente's pawn on 7f. The pawn in front of Sente's Yagura is gone, and the area around the king becomes a little draughty.","63":"Px3d. Sente takes in the pawn on 3d. This pawn now stands close to Gote's king and will do great work later.","64":"S-7e. Gote's silver goes to 7e. It hits the silver on 6f and presses further into Sente's camp.","65":"P-4e. Sente pushes the pawn on file 4 into Gote's pawn on 4d. It chips away, little by little, at the pawns that defend Gote's camp.","66":"Sx6f. Gote's silver takes the silver on 6f. Checked with this site's shogi AI, Gote, Sōmin, has the easier game around here. The knight on 6e and the silver on 7e are pressing on Sente's king, and balancing defence and attack is hard for Sente.","67":"Gx6f. Sente takes back with the gold. The gold comes to the centre and glares at the knight on 6e.","68":"S*7e. Gote drops a silver from its hand to aim at the gold on 6f.","69":"Gx6e. Sente's gold takes the knight on 6e. Sente removes Gote's attacking knight and gains material.","70":"P-8f. Gote pushes the pawn on file 8 to strike at the head of Sente's king.","71":"Gx7e. The gold takes the silver on 7e. Sente's gold is doing wonderful work.","72":"Bx7e. Gote takes back the gold with the bishop from 4b. This bishop aims at Sente's camp along a long diagonal.","73":"Bx8f. Sente's bishop from 6h takes the pawn on 8f. It meets Gote's bishop head-on and offers a bishop trade.","74":"Bx8f. Gote trades the bishops.","75":"Px8f. Sente takes back with the pawn. Through these captures Sente has gained silvers and a bishop, and this site's shogi AI now sees the game as roughly level again. But Gote also has a bishop in hand and comes at Sente's king.","76":"B*6f. Gote drops the bishop to aim at the area around Sente's king. This bishop points toward the king on 8h and is the base for the next attack.","77":"P*7g. Sente drops a pawn to cut the bishop's line.","78":"P*8g. Gote drops a pawn on 8g, pressing on the head of Sente's king.","79":"Gx8g. Sente takes the pawn with the gold. But because the gold moved to 8g, the square 7h has become empty.","80":"S*6g. Gote drops a silver right next to Sente's king. If Gote follows with something like S-7h+, Sente's king will be in real danger. Normally this is the moment to think about defending.","81":"N*3e. This is the highlight of the game, Sōkan's famous move. Instead of defending, he dropped a knight on 3e, near Gote's king. Next the knight threatens to jump into Gote's camp with N-4c+ or N-2c, and it cannot be taken because the rook on 3h guards it from behind. What is more, Sente's pawn on 3d means Gote cannot chase it away with a pawn. With his own king in danger, Sōkan found an attack one step faster than his opponent's, and the later 15th Meijin Ōyama Yasuharu is said to have called it 'a brilliant move'.","82":"G-4b. Gote pulls the gold from 4c back to 4b to prepare for the knight's jump.","83":"S*4c. Sente drops a silver from its hand on 4c, aiming at the gold on 3b. The knight on 3e reaches 4c, so this silver is supported from behind.","84":"Px7g+. Gote attacks too. It takes the pawn on 7g, makes a tokin and presses on Sente's king.","85":"Nx7g. Sente takes the tokin with the knight.","86":"P*7f. Gote drops a pawn to attack the knight on 7g. Gote is trying to strip away the pieces around Sente's king one by one.","87":"Sx3b+. Sente's silver takes the gold on 3b and promotes. One of the golds guarding Gote's king is gone.","88":"Gx3b. Gote takes back the promoted silver with the gold from 4b.","89":"B*5c. Sente drops a bishop from its hand on 5c. Because the gold on 4b has moved to 3b, the diagonal from 5c through 4b to 3a is open, so this is check. Gote has to block it with something.","90":"S*4b. Gote drops a silver to block the bishop's line.","91":"S*4c. Sente drops another silver on 4c, aiming once more at the gold on 3b.","92":"G*5b. Gote drops a gold, which attacks the bishop on 5c and strengthens the defence.","93":"N-2c (no promotion). The knight dropped on 3e on move 81 finally jumps in. Not promoting is the key: an unpromoted knight still reaches 3a, so this is check. A promoted knight moves like a gold and would not reach 3a, so it would not be check.","94":"K-2b. Gote's king escapes to 2b.","95":"Sx3b+. The silver on 4c takes the gold on 3b and promotes.","96":"Kx3b. Gote takes the promoted silver with the king. The king has come out to 3b and has almost no defenders left.","97":"G*3c. A gold drop with check, driving Gote's king out.","98":"Nx3c. Gote takes the gold with the knight.","99":"Px3c+. The pawn on 3d takes the knight and becomes a tokin. The pawn Sente took in on move 63 does great work here.","100":"Sx3c. Gote takes the tokin with the silver.","101":"B-3a+. The bishop on 5c jumps to 3a, promotes to a horse and gives check. Here Sōmin admitted defeat. Even when his own king was in danger, Sōkan did not turn to defence; from N*3e he chased Gote's king down in one go. Three years later, in 1728, Sōkan became the 7th Meijin at 23, and in 1734 he presented his tsume-shogi collection 'Shōgi Musō' to the shogunate. The loser, Sōmin, later took the name Ōhashi Sōyo IV and fought the Meijin Sōkan many times. The N*3e born from this battle of two young players is still told as a move that impresses professional players today."}},"alekhine1930":{"t":"Alekhine's Gun","w":"Alexander Alekhine","b":"Aron Nimzowitsch","ev":"San Remo international tournament, round 3","d":"January 1930","pl":"Italy (San Remo)","lead":"The world champion Alekhine squeezes his opponent's camp little by little until he wins. At the end White lines up two rooks and the queen one behind another on the c-file, a formation later called “Alekhine's gun”. Black resigns before losing any piece, because every one of his pieces has run out of good moves. Some records continue the game for a few more moves before it ends.","c":{"0":"January 1930, San Remo, a seaside town in Italy. This is round 3 of a 16-player round robin. White is Alexander Alekhine, world champion since he beat Capablanca in 1927. Black is Aron Nimzowitsch, the thinker whose 1925 book My System spread new ideas about chess. Alekhine won this tournament with an amazing 14 points from 15 games (13 wins, 2 draws). At the end of this game you will see White's “gun” lined up on the c-file.","1":"White plays e4. It takes a central square and opens lines for the queen and bishop: the most common first move since long ago. Alekhine was born into a wealthy family in Moscow, left Russia after the revolution, and later became a French citizen.","2":"Black plays e6: this is the French Defence. The name is said to come from a correspondence match between London and Paris around 1834, which the Paris side won with this move. Nimzowitsch was a master of this defence.","3":"White plays d4. The pawns on e4 and d4 build a big wall in the centre. In the French, White's theme is how to keep these two pawns supported.","4":"Black plays d5. He hits the e4 pawn at once and makes White choose: take, defend, or push ahead. This is the basic French position.","5":"The knight goes to c3. It is the most natural way to defend the e4 pawn. Black now chooses a move that ties this knight down.","6":"Black plays ♗b4: the Winawer Variation, named after the 19th-century Polish master Szymon Winawer. The bishop pins the c3 knight against the king and weakens its guard of e4. Tying down the opponent's pieces was a plan Nimzowitsch loved most.","7":"White plays e5. The pawn steps forward so that Black's knight cannot come to f6. White's pawns on d4 and e5 now form a diagonal “pawn chain”. In My System, Nimzowitsch taught that you should attack such a chain at its base.","8":"Black plays c5. Just as his book says, he attacks d4, the base of the chain. This is Black's most common counterattack in the French.","9":"White plays ♗d2. Today 5.a3 is more common, asking the bishop to take or retreat, but White calmly offers a bishop exchange instead. Later this line also became known under Bogoljubow's name. White wants to fight without spoiling his pawn structure.","10":"Black plays ♘e7. The knight will go on to c6 or f5, a typical French setup. The f6 square is covered by the e5 pawn, so the knight takes this road.","11":"White plays ♘b5! An unusual move: the knight hits the bishop and heads for d6. The d6 square is protected by the e5 pawn and no black pawn can chase a piece away from it: a “hole”. Alekhine loved to make opponents think hard with surprising moves away from the usual theory.","12":"Black plays ♗xd2+. Before his bishop is taken, he trades it for White's bishop. With this, Black gives up the piece that was pinning along the diagonal.","13":"White plays ♕xd2. The queen recaptures and lands on a square where it works well. White keeps his plan of aiming at d6.","14":"Black castles. The king moves to safety on g8. With the centre closed, Black first makes his defence firm.","15":"White plays c3. It supports the d4 pawn and also prepares b4 later. White has not castled yet, but since the centre is closed his king is in no hurry.","16":"Black plays b6, preparing to bring the c8 bishop to a6. In the French this bishop is blocked by its own pawns and is called the “bad bishop”. Black wants to trade it for one of White's good pieces.","17":"White plays f4. It gives the e5 pawn a second defender and weakens Black's counter-push f6. White has fixed the centre very solidly.","18":"Black plays ♗a6. The idea is to trade the bad bishop for the b5 knight or the f1 bishop. It is a logical move, typical of Nimzowitsch.","19":"White plays ♘f3. The last knight comes out to support d4 and e5. White still does not agree to the bishop trade.","20":"Black plays ♕d7. The queen also aims at the b5 knight. If Black takes with ♗xb5 next, White must recapture with a pawn or the bishop.","21":"White plays a4. If ♗xb5, then axb5 opens the a-file and grabs lots of space on the queenside. The tournament began on 16 January this year and lasted until 4 February. It was a big stage for Alekhine as the new world champion.","22":"Black plays ♘bc6. The other knight comes out and eyes b4 and d4. Black does not hurry the bishop trade and guards the centre with numbers.","23":"White plays b4! He pushes the queenside pawns and widens the battle there. Opening lines on the side where you have more pieces makes your advantage grow.","24":"Black plays cxb4. If he does not take, White pushes further with b5 or c5, so he takes and the lines open.","25":"White plays cxb4. Now the c-file is open from top to bottom. This file will become the stage of the whole fight. White's b4 pawn also controls c5 and a5.","26":"Black plays ♗b7, pulling the bishop back. Perhaps he thought that trading on b5 would give White strong play on the a- and c-files. But White's knight heads straight to its next target.","27":"White plays ♘d6! The knight lands in the d6 hole it was aiming for. No black pawn can drive it away. It eyes the b7 bishop and sits like a giant in the middle of Black's camp.","28":"Black plays f5. He stops the kingside pawns and prevents White's f4-f5 attack. But now the e6 pawn can never move, and Black's camp becomes even more frozen.","29":"White plays a5. He pushes again and shakes the b6 pawn. White keeps squeezing Black on the queenside.","30":"Black plays ♘c8. The knight heads over to trade itself for the d6 knight. White's powerful knight is the piece Black hates most.","31":"White plays ♘xb7. Before he can be traded, White takes Black's good bishop himself. Now Black has lost the piece that guarded the light squares.","32":"Black plays ♕xb7, recapturing with the queen. The material is equal, but White's bishop can now attack Black's weak light squares.","33":"White plays a6! The pawn hits the queen and freezes the a7 pawn. Squares like b7 and c6 are becoming White's.","34":"Black plays ♕f7. The queen retreats to the kingside. There is little room left for her on the queenside.","35":"White plays ♗b5. The bishop travels along the light squares and stares at the c6 knight. Black will have to use many pieces to guard c6.","36":"Black plays ♘8e7. The other knight defends c6. Black's pieces are being gathered on passive squares, one after another.","37":"White finally castles. With the centre closed there was no need to hurry. With the king safe, the rook can join the fight.","38":"Black plays h6. He makes an escape square near the king in case White attacks on the kingside. But it is not a real plan.","39":"White plays ♖fc1. The rook enters the open c-file. From now on White's pieces gather on the c-file one by one.","40":"Black plays ♖fc8. Black also puts a rook on the c-file to guard c6 and c7. The tug of war over the c-file begins.","41":"White plays ♖c2. The rook steps up one square to make room for another piece behind it. The plan of stacking pieces on the file is becoming clear.","42":"Black plays ♕e8. The queen also comes to guard c6. Black now defends c6 with two knights, the queen and a rook.","43":"White plays ♖ac1. The second rook comes to the c-file too. Two white rooks are now stacked on it.","44":"Black plays ♖ab8. His rook goes to the b-file and eyes the b4 pawn. Black is looking for a way to hit back on the queenside.","45":"White plays ♕e3. The queen moves aside for a moment to make a path to the back of the rooks. From here White slowly rearranges his pieces.","46":"Black plays ♖c7. The rook on c7 guards c6 once more. It prepares for White's stacking on the c-file.","47":"White plays ♖c3. The front rook steps out to c3. Now c2 and c1 are free, so the queen can come behind.","48":"Black plays ♕d7. The queen stays near c6 and c7 to keep the number of defenders. Almost every black piece is now working to guard c6.","49":"White plays ♖1c2. The back rook moves up to c2. In front is c3, behind it c2, and the empty c1 square is for the queen.","50":"Black plays ♔f8. The king walks toward the queenside to join the defence. Black is so hard pressed that even his king must become a defender.","51":"White plays ♕c1. The rook on c3, the rook on c2 and the queen on c1 now stand in a column on the c-file. This is the formation later called “Alekhine's gun”. Historians found that the name only appears in books from the 1990s, and earlier Russian books are said to have called it “Alekhine's arrangement”. When rooks and queen are stacked on one file, the pieces behind can recapture if the front one is taken, so they push on a square with great force.","52":"Black plays ♖bc8. Black also puts his last rook on the c-file to guard c6 and c7. By counting, everything is defended, but Black's pieces can barely move.","53":"White plays ♗a4. The idea is b5 next, attacking the c6 knight. The knight stands in front of the c7 rook, so if it runs away, the c3 rook takes on c7.","54":"Black plays b5. He gives up a pawn to stop White's b5. Any other move would let White win the knight with b5.","55":"White plays ♗xb5. He takes the pawn, and the bishop again eyes c6 and e8. White is a pawn up and the squeeze goes on.","56":"Black plays ♔e8. The king heads to d8 to protect the c7 rook. Black has nothing better than to bring his king toward the dangerous centre.","57":"White plays ♗a4. The bishop goes back and again threatens to push a pawn to b5. This time the b4 pawn is free to go to b5.","58":"Black plays ♔d8. The king now protects c7. So if b5 comes, the knight can leave c6.","59":"White plays h4: just one pawn step, a waiting move. But Black has almost no move left. If the queen or a knight moves, c6 cannot be held; if a rook moves, the c-file collapses; if the king moves, c7 becomes weak. It is said that his few pawn moves would soon run out too, and Nimzowitsch resigned here. A position where having to move is itself a loss is called “zugzwang”. After the tournament Alekhine put no fewer than seven games from it into his collection of best games."}},"ck1992g8":{"t":"Chinook beats Tinsley","w":"Chinook (computer)","b":"Marion Tinsley","ev":"Game 8 of the Man vs. Machine World Checkers Championship (a 39-game match)","d":"19 August 1992","pl":"United Kingdom (London)","lead":"A 39-game match between Marion Tinsley, the human champion who had hardly lost a game in over 40 years, and Chinook, a program built at the University of Alberta in Canada. In game 8, Chinook beat Tinsley for the first time. It is said to be the first time a computer beat a human world champion in a world title match of any mind sport. Tinsley still won the match, 4 wins to 2 with 33 draws.","c":{"0":"19 August 1992, the Park Lane Hotel, London: game 8 of the 39-game Man vs. Machine World Checkers Championship. In checkers Black moves first; Black's men start on squares 1–12 at the top and White's on 21–32 at the bottom. Here Chinook has Black and the champion Marion Tinsley, a mathematician, has White. The dark squares are numbered 1 to 32, and a move is written as its start and end, like 11-15. A capture uses an x, and a multiple jump lists every square it lands on.","1":"Chinook goes from 11 to 15. It is the most popular first move, heading for the centre, and has long been nicknamed \"Old Faithful\". Chinook was begun in 1989 at the University of Alberta in Canada by a team led by Jonathan Schaeffer. In the opening it played from a book of lines collected from human games.","2":"White goes from 23 to 18, right up against the man on 15, meeting it head-on. The opening 11-15 23-18 is called the \"Cross\". Tinsley was born in Ohio, USA, in 1927, and was a mathematics professor whose research was in combinatorics.","3":"Black plays 10-14, so the first three moves are 11-15 23-18 10-14. To keep games from ending in draws so often, tournaments often draw the first three moves by lot, the \"three-move restriction\", and these three moves are one of its ballots. The US championship has used the three-move restriction since 1934.","4":"White jumps 18x11 over the man on 15. In checkers you must capture when you can. This is White's only move, so far both sides are on a forced path.","5":"Black recaptures 8x15 and the count is even again. 7x16 was also possible, but this site's engine reads it as clearly worse. The man from 8 comes up and holds the centre square 15 once more.","6":"White goes 22-17. One opening book gives 26-23 here as the main line, reaching the same position as another opening (10-14 22-18 11-15). Tinsley chose 22-17 instead and left the main line. The table used for this match is said to be the one used in the 1986 Kasparov–Karpov world chess championship.","7":"Black goes 14-18, one step into White's camp, aiming at the gap by 22 and 23. The man on 18 is a thorn for White, who now has to think about how to drive it away.","8":"White plays 24-19, letting Black capture so that men are exchanged. The exchange removes Black's man on 15 from the centre and tidies up White's formation.","9":"Black takes 15x24. A capture is compulsory, so there is no other move.","10":"White recaptures 28x19. 27x20 was possible too, but the engine reads it as worse. The man from 28 now stands on 19 and holds the centre again.","11":"Black goes 7-11, bringing a back man forward to support 18 and 15. In checkers it matters to keep the men together so they protect each other.","12":"White plays 17-14, a bold move deep into the hole left by Black's 10. The man on 14 digs into Black's camp and ties down the men on Black's left.","13":"Black goes 11-16, spreading to the right and eyeing White's man on 19. With this move the engine's reading tips slightly towards Black.","14":"White goes 19-15, driving a second wedge into the middle of Black's camp. White's men on 14 and 15 block the paths of Black's men.","15":"Black plays 4-8, moving up a back man to stop White's man on 15 from reaching 11. The men on the back row, like 4, guard against the opponent crowning, so when to move them is a hard decision.","16":"White goes 21-17, bringing a left-side man forward to support the man on 14 from behind.","17":"Black plays 16-19, pushing towards White's camp and pressing on White's right. The man on 19 stands between White's 23 and 24 and limits White's moves.","18":"White goes 17-13, to the left edge. A man on the edge is harder to capture, but it has fewer directions to move in.","19":"Black plays 12-16, putting a man behind 19 to back it up firmly. Black now holds White's right side with the two men on 16 and 19.","20":"White plays 25-21, the move that is said to have led to the loss. The Spectator's report gave it a \"?\" and said Tinsley regretted it almost at once and felt 26-22 would have been better. This site's engine also reads 26-22 as the best move here. Both views agree that this was the turning point.","21":"Black plays 18-22, offering the man to White's 26 so that it can be won back. It is a move to break up White's defensive shape.","22":"White takes 26x17. A capture is compulsory, so this is the only move.","23":"Black recaptures 9x18 and a man stands on 18 again. After the exchange the man on 18 is an even stronger wedge, and holes are left around White's 22 and 26.","24":"White plays 29-25, moving up a back man to fill the gap.","25":"Black goes 16-20, to the right edge, getting ready to advance to 24. It prepares the plan to come.","26":"White plays 17-14, putting a man on 14 again and aiming at Black's 10 and 9.","27":"Black goes 2-7, bringing a back man forward to join the next plan.","28":"White plays 21-17. In the engine's reading Black's advantage becomes clear with this move. White has fewer and fewer men that can move freely.","29":"Black plays 19-24. The Spectator's report marked it \"!\" and called it the winning manoeuvre, noting that the audience did not see it coming. Going to 24 makes the shot 7-10 possible next, and White's right side collapses. In this site's engine's reading, too, the position is now close to a win for Black.","30":"White plays 30-26. Even with the engine, there is no good move left for White. White cannot stop what Black is aiming at.","31":"Black plays 7-10, letting White's man on 14 capture it. Giving a man in order to take back more is called a \"shot\" in checkers.","32":"White takes 14x7. The capture is compulsory, so White has no choice.","33":"Black jumps twice, 3x10x19: from 3 to 10, then on to 19, taking White's men on 7 and 15. Black is now a man ahead.","34":"White plays 27-23, hitting back with a move that lets Black capture so White can jump twice in return.","35":"Black takes 18x27. This capture is compulsory too.","36":"White jumps twice, 32x23x16, and the count is even again. Even so, Black's men are close to White's king row (29–32), and Black looks set to crown first.","37":"Black plays 24-27, letting White's 31 capture so that 20 can take back and a man reaches 27.","38":"White takes 31x24. It is White's only move.","39":"Black recaptures 20x27. Black's man on 27 is one step from the king row, and White has no back men left to guard it.","40":"White plays 26-22. There is no way to stop Black crowning, so White pushes its own man forward.","41":"Black plays 8-12, tidying up before crowning. The engine also likes crowning at once with 27-31, but either way Black stays on top.","42":"White goes 16-11, pushing deep into Black's camp to try to crown a king of its own.","43":"Black goes 27-31 and crowns the first king of the game. A king can move both forward and backward, so it is far stronger than a man.","44":"White plays 11-7. From here the man can crown next by going to 3 or 2.","45":"Black's king goes 31-26, straight back to the front line to hunt White's men.","46":"White plays 25-21. White's men are bunched on the left, and few of them can move freely.","47":"Black plays 26-30, pulling the king back once to watch White's men. Black does not hurry and waits for White to run out of useful moves.","48":"White plays 22-18.","49":"Black's king goes 30-26, coming forward again to attack White's men on 18 and 21 from behind.","50":"White plays 18-14. According to the Spectator's report, Tinsley resigned after this move without waiting for Black's reply. It adds that Chinook could have forced the win from here.","51":"In Chinook's own record the last move is 26-22, and the game is a win for Chinook. Tinsley is said to have lost only seven games in the 45 years from 1950, and two of them were to Chinook in this match. Even so, Tinsley went on to win four games and took the match 4–2 with 33 draws. The Chinook team kept working, and in 2007 announced that checkers had been proved a draw if both sides play perfectly."}},"logistello1997":{"t":"Logistello sweeps the world champion","w":"Logistello (computer)","b":"Takeshi Murakami","ev":"Game 6 of the six-game match Takeshi Murakami v. Logistello","d":"7 August 1997","pl":"USA (Princeton, NEC Research Institute)","lead":"In August 1997 the reversi world champion Takeshi Murakami played a six-game match against Logistello, a program written by Michael Buro. Logistello won the first five games, then took the sixth 37–27 for a 6–0 result. This was the closest game of the match. Follow it move by move, from the openings known as the Tiger, the Rose-bill and the Goldfish to the very last square.","c":{"0":"7 August 1997, the NEC Research Institute in Princeton, New Jersey: the last day of a six-game match. Black is the program Logistello; White is Takeshi Murakami, world champion since the year before. Logistello has won all five games so far, and Murakami is playing for one win. In reversi you flip your opponent's discs by trapping them between two of your own. The game goes on until all 64 squares are filled, and whoever has more discs at the end wins.","1":"Black starts with f5. The four possible first moves all give the same shape once the board is turned or mirrored, so it makes no difference which one is played. By custom, records are written starting with f5, and games that start elsewhere are usually turned into the f5 shape to compare them. Logistello was written by Michael Buro. It grew out of his doctoral research at the University of Paderborn in Germany.","2":"White plays d6. The new white disc stands in a straight line with the black disc it takes, so this is called the perpendicular opening, the most common second move. The other two are the diagonal (f6) and the parallel (f4). Murakami was 32 at the time and taught English in Tokyo. He is said to have taken up the game in 1981, at about 16.","3":"Black plays c4. Most players play c3 first, but here the order is swapped. When c3 comes two moves later, the game reaches a well-known position by another route. Reaching the same position in a different order is called a transposition. Logistello used book learning: it kept learning opening moves from the games it played.","4":"White plays d3, in the middle of Black's row of discs, taking a central disc. Each side has four discs. In the opening it matters much more to keep many moves available for yourself, and few for your opponent, than to have many discs. Murakami finished second in the All-Japan Championship in 1987 and became one of Japan's top players.","5":"With c3 the game reaches the famous Tiger. The Tiger is the shape of f5, d6, c3, d3, c4, and it is among the most played openings by experts and beginners alike. Many continuations branch from it, each with its own name. In May that same year, IBM's Deep Blue had beaten world chess champion Garry Kasparov, and machine against human was big news.","6":"White plays f4, one of the main continuations of the Tiger. White takes e5 and gains a foothold on the right. Another well-studied line goes f6 here. Murakami won the All-Japan Championship in 1996, which gave him a place in that year's World Championship in Tokyo, and he won that too.","7":"Black plays c5, flipping d5 and e5. Black lines up discs on the left and starts to limit where White can play. Everything so far is opening theory that both sides have studied thousands of times. The prize money was announced as $3,000 for the winner and $1,000 for the loser.","8":"White plays b3, a quiet move that flips only one black disc at the upper left. Moves that flip few discs rarely give the opponent new places to play, so in the opening they are often the best. The tournament director was David Parsons of New York, a strong reversi player and a chess master.","9":"With c2 the game reaches the line called the Rose-bill: from the Tiger, f4, c5, b3, c2. Black flips two discs at the upper left and keeps White's discs shut inside. The games were also relayed live on an Internet game server, where strong players and programs from around the world are said to have watched and commented move by move.","10":"White plays e6, flipping d5 and c4 and bringing white back to the centre. Theory goes on from here: in Japanese opening lists the coming line is named the Goldfish. Logistello ran on a single PentiumPro 233 MHz computer, not far from an ordinary home computer of the day.","11":"Black plays c6, flipping only d5. A black wall forms on the left, and White is pushed to play near the left edge. Forcing the opponent onto squares they would rather avoid is a key idea in reversi. In the four years before this match, Logistello had played 23 international tournaments and won 17.","12":"White plays b4, flipping c5. White plays next to the left edge but not yet on it. Squares in the b and g files and the 2nd and 7th ranks sit just inside an edge, and discs there often hand the edge to the opponent. The first time a world champion lost a game to a computer was in 1980, against a program called The Moor.","13":"Black plays b5, flipping c5 and c4 and thickening the black wall on the left. Squeezed between White's b4 and b3, the move takes away even more of White's options. The match used long time limits for each game, so that the human would not be at a disadvantage in thinking time.","14":"White plays d2, flipping four at once: d3, d4, d5 and c3. Flipping many discs early usually gives the opponent more places to play, but this is still theory, and White retakes the central column. The count is now White 10, Black 8, but in the opening the disc count says almost nothing about who will win.","15":"Black plays e3, flipping only d4. Black keeps slipping single discs into the centre with quiet moves. Within opening theory, neither side usually gains a big advantage. Logistello judged positions by scoring the patterns of discs on the board with more than a million numbers.","16":"White plays a6, stepping onto the left edge and flipping b5 and c4. Edge discs are hard to flip from inside and can become strong. But moving onto an edge too early can also let the opponent use that edge. This is all still the Goldfish line, which White must have studied many times.","17":"Black plays c1, stepping onto the top edge and flipping d2. White took a place on the left edge, so Black takes one on the top edge: each side now holds an edge. In 2023, 26 years after this game, a computation showed that 8×8 reversi is a draw when both sides play perfectly.","18":"White plays b6, flipping c6 and gathering white discs along the left side. These first 18 moves match the line called the Goldfish in Japanese opening lists. Both players have followed a well-studied road so far. From the next move the battle leaves the book.","19":"Black plays f3 and leaves known theory. It is a quiet move that flips only f4 and gives Black a foothold at the upper right. Now White has to search for a good square. Choosing moves that flip few discs and leave the opponent fewer options was typical of Logistello's play.","20":"White plays f6, flipping e5 and d4 and spreading white towards the lower right. White tries to flip inner discs so as not to give Black new moves. But more white discs on the outside, next to empty squares, also give Black places to play.","21":"Black plays f7, flipping f6. Black moves down the board and closes off the outside of White's discs. The fewer of your discs touch empty squares and the more they sit inside, the harder it is for the opponent to find moves. This is a core idea of reversi.","22":"White plays e1, stepping onto the top edge beside Black's c1 and flipping d2. The fight over the edges has begun. More discs on the edges lead to the fight over the corners. The four corners are the strongest squares: a disc there can never be flipped.","23":"Black plays e2, flipping d2 back. Placed just inside White's e1, it makes it harder for White to extend along the top edge. The second rank leads to squares near the corners, so it calls for care. Again Black flips only one disc.","24":"White plays f1, flipping e2 and putting two white discs side by side on the top edge. On the top edge, c1 is black and e1 and f1 are white. The shape of an edge can decide the fight for its corners later. White stretches the edge so that Black finds it hard to play there.","25":"Black plays e7, flipping e6, e5 and d6. Black takes the central column and shrinks White's group by trapping it. Along the bottom, Black's f7 and e7 now stand together, and White will find it hard to reach the bottom edge. The count is Black 15, White 14, close to even.","26":"White plays g3, flipping f3 and e3 and spreading white to the right. The g file sits just inside an edge, but g3 is not next to a corner, so it is not yet dangerous. White keeps places to play while breaking up Black's foothold on the right.","27":"Black plays c7, flipping c6. Black reaches the lower left. c7 is inside the edge but far from the a8 corner, so it is fairly safe. According to Logistello's log, after White's next move the position left its opening book. Until now it had probably been playing learned moves.","28":"White plays g4, flipping f4 and e4. Here the position leaves Logistello's book. From now on Logistello reads ahead and chooses each move by itself. White has spread widely on the right, but more of White's discs now face empty squares.","29":"Black plays g5, flipping f4 and e3. Logistello's log shows it rated its own chance of winning at 57% here. Black slips outside White's wall on the right and makes it hard for White to reach the right edge. A small edge for Black, in the machine's judgement.","30":"White plays d1, flipping d2 and c2 and lining up d1, e1 and f1 on the top edge. White is going for that edge. But the longer a row of white discs on an edge, the greater the risk that Black traps it from both ends.","31":"Black plays g1! It flips f1, e1 and d1 and turns the whole top edge from c1 to g1 black, trapping White's row from the end in one move. After this Logistello put its chance of winning at 68%. But g1 sits next to the h1 corner, so White may still take h1 later.","32":"White plays f2, flipping e3. White moves onto the second rank, inside Black's top edge. It makes it harder for Black to extend the edge and gives White a foothold for taking the h1 corner later. White is running short of places to play.","33":"Black plays h3, stepping onto the right edge and flipping g4. The right edge runs between the h1 and h8 corners and matters a great deal. h3 is two squares from h1, so there is no immediate danger of losing the corner. Logistello still rated its chances at 68%.","34":"White plays h4, flipping g4 and f4 and joining the right edge as well. Black's h3 and White's h4 stand side by side, and the right edge is now contested. White holds on so as not to give Black that whole edge.","35":"Black plays h5! It flips h4, g4, f3 and e2. At this moment Logistello read all 26 remaining squares to the end and found that, with best play, Black wins by 6 discs. Such complete reading to the end is beyond any human. From here on Logistello did not make a single mistake. The question was no longer whether White could win, but by how much Black would win.","36":"White plays c8, stepping onto the bottom edge and flipping c7, c6 and c5. Logistello had expected d8, and its log shows that c8 widened the best-play margin by two discs. White chose a move that flips many discs and may have given Black too many places to play. Still, 24 squares remain, and Murakami fights on to the end.","37":"Black plays b1, flipping c2, d3 and e4 on a diagonal. b1 joins the black row from c1 to g1 and lengthens the top edge. b1 is next to the a1 corner, but White can only play a1 if a white disc can trap the black discs along the edge. For now White cannot play a1.","38":"White plays d7, flipping d6. Down at the bottom White flips black discs one at a time, trying to make places to play. In the endgame, the side that runs out of moves first is the one in trouble.","39":"Black plays h2, flipping g3 and f4 and joining the right edge from h2 to h5. h2 is next to the h1 corner and usually a risky square. But Logistello has read to the end and has checked that Black loses nothing even if White takes h1.","40":"White plays e8, flipping e7, e6, e5 and e4 down the column. White now has c8 and e8 on the bottom edge and leaves d8 empty, hoping Black will have to play there.","41":"Black plays d8, flipping d7, d6, d5 and d4 up the column and e7 as well, five discs in all. It slips between White's c8 and e8 and turns White's plan around. Black also takes the whole central column, and leads 27 to 18.","42":"White plays g6, flipping f6, f7 and f5. White moves into the empty lower right to cut down Black's moves. The area around the h8 corner is where the game will be decided now.","43":"Black plays f8, flipping e8, f7, f6 and f5. Black steps onto the bottom edge and takes a group of white discs. With c8, d8, e8 and f8, Black is on top along the bottom.","44":"White plays g8, flipping f8, e8, d8 and f7 and winning the bottom edge back: c8 to g8 is white. But g8 is next to the h8 corner. Once Black can play h8, this whole white edge will turn black.","45":"Black plays h6, flipping g6. The right edge is now black from h2 to h6. Two squares remain at the lower right, h7 and h8. Whether the number of empty squares in a region is even or odd decides who plays there last. This is called parity, a key endgame idea.","46":"White plays g7, flipping f6. g7 is diagonally next to the h8 corner, an X-square, and playing there is usually very dangerous. White had hardly any better moves and probably had to play here. Now Black is ready to take the h8 corner.","47":"Black plays a2, flipping b3 and c4, and steps onto the left edge. Black does not rush to take h8 but first uses its moves on the left. A corner can be taken later, and not hurrying is the strong way to play.","48":"White plays a3, flipping b3 and lining up on the left edge too. Now a2 is black and a3 and a6 are white: the fight for the left edge begins.","49":"Black plays a4, flipping b4, a3, b5, c6 and b3, five in all. It traps White's a3 on the edge and takes inner discs along with it. The count is Black 34, White 19, a big lead for Black.","50":"White plays b7, flipping c6 and d5. b7 is the X-square next to the a8 corner, so this lets Black take a8. White has almost no safe squares left.","51":"Black plays a8, taking the corner and flipping b7, c6, d5 and e4 on the diagonal. A corner disc can never be flipped again. Black also gains the long diagonal from the corner and leads 37 to 18.","52":"White plays b8, flipping b7. On the bottom edge White's row now runs beside Black's a8. White uses up its few remaining moves one by one.","53":"Black plays b2, flipping c3. b2 is the X-square next to the a1 corner, and it lets White take a1. Logistello has read to the end and knows that giving White a1 does not change the result. It is probably arranging the remaining squares so that it can make the last moves itself.","54":"White plays a1, taking the corner and flipping b2, c3 and d4. But according to Logistello's log, a5 would have been two discs better for White. Corners are always valuable, but in the endgame taking a corner is not always right, and that is what makes reversi hard. With this move the best-play margin grew from 6 to 10.","55":"Black plays h8, taking the corner and flipping six discs along the bottom at once: g8, f8, e8, d8, c8 and b8. White's moves at g7 and g8 now pay off for Black. The count is Black 42, White 17.","56":"White plays h1, taking the corner and flipping six back along the top: g1, f1, e1, d1, c1 and b1. The whole top edge is white. Each side now owns two corners: Black a8 and h8, White a1 and h1.","57":"Black plays h7, flipping g7 and f7 and making the right edge black from h2 to h8 (h1 is white). In the endgame, edges and corners are settled one after another.","58":"White plays g2, flipping f3, e4, d5 and c6 on a diagonal. Only two empty squares remain, a5 and a7 on the left edge.","59":"Black plays a5, flipping b6 and c7. One square, a7, remains, and White plays last.","60":"White plays a7, flipping b6, and all 64 squares are filled. Black 37, White 27: Logistello wins. The match ended 6–0, a complete win for the computer over the reversi world champion. Five months later, in January 1998, Buro is said to have retired Logistello from tournament play. This was the closest of the six games, and Murakami kept the fight nearly even with the machine to the end. Twenty-six years later, in 2023, it was shown by computation that 8×8 reversi is a draw when both sides play perfectly."}},"koyasan1948":{"t":"The Mount Kōya Showdown: 'Illusion bad, look carefully good'","w":"Masuda Kōzō","b":"Ōyama Yasuharu","ev":"7th Meijin-sen, challenger final (best of three), game 3","d":"March 1948","pl":"Japan (Mount Kōya, Wakayama)","lead":"Masuda Kōzō and Ōyama Yasuharu grew up under the same teacher, like older and younger brothers. Their best-of-three match for the right to challenge Meijin Tsukada Masao stood at one win each, and the final game was played on Mount Kōya, where snow still remained. Masuda reached a winning position, but on the last check he chose the wrong escape square for his king and lost in a huge turnaround. The words he said then are still told in the shogi world today.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. Masuda Kōzō was born in 1918 in Hiroshima Prefecture, and Ōyama Yasuharu in 1923 in Okayama Prefecture. Both were disciples of Kimi Kinjirō in Osaka, and Masuda was the senior disciple, five years older. At that time, Masuda was thought to be the stronger player. This game was game 3 of the best-of-three match to decide who would challenge Meijin Tsukada Masao; Ōyama had won game 1 and Masuda game 2. It was three years after the war, food was still scarce, and it is said the sponsor, the Mainichi newspaper, provided the ingredients for sukiyaki, Mount Kōya provided white rice, and a supporter provided sake to keep the match going.","1":"P-2f. Masuda pushes the pawn in front of his rook. This is the start of a 'static rook' game, using the rook straight ahead. It is told that as a boy Masuda wrote on the back of his mother's ruler his resolution to 'beat the Meijin even giving a lance handicap' and left home.","2":"P-8d. Ōyama also pushes his rook pawn, and both sides stretch their rook files. If it continues like this, the game becomes a 'Double Wing' or a 'Side Pawn Capture' battle.","3":"P-2e. The rook pawn goes further. If Sente next plays P-2d, he can exchange pawns and bring his rook forward.","4":"P-8e. Ōyama stretches his pawn in the same way. In those years right after the war, even games between top players often featured static rook games where both sides pushed their rook pawns like this.","5":"P-7f. Opening the bishop's diagonal. Now Sente's bishop starts working too, and the real piece-building of the opening begins.","6":"G-3b. The gold protects the head of the bishop. It also prepares to guard the 2c square for when Sente exchanges his rook pawn. In the summer of this year, 1948, the Olympic Games were held in London, but because of the war Japan was not allowed to take part.","7":"B-7g. The bishop rises to meet Gote's attack along file 8. Defending against the opponent's rook file with the bishop is a common shape in Double Wing-type openings.","8":"P-3d. Gote opens his bishop's diagonal too. Now the pawn on 3d stands exposed to the side of Sente's rook, and whether Sente can take it with the rook becomes a big turning point.","10":"S-6b. Ōyama also lifts a silver. He is preparing to use it on file 5 in the center.","12":"P-5d. Ōyama pushes his file-5 pawn. He seems to plan to advance a silver into the center to restrict Sente's rook.","13":"P-2d. At last Masuda clashes his rook pawn. Side Pawn Capture and Double Wing battles begin with this pawn exchange.","15":"Rx2d. The rook takes back, and Sente's rook comes out to 2d. By exchanging the rook pawn, Sente gains one pawn in hand.","16":"P*2c. Gote drops a pawn to push the rook back. Usually the rook retreats to 2f or 2h, but here Sente has an important choice.","17":"Rx3d. Masuda moves the rook sideways and takes the pawn on 3d. This is the 'Side Pawn Capture'. You win a pawn for free, but the rook easily becomes isolated near the enemy camp; there is even an old saying that 'taking the side pawn brings three years of illness'. It is a strategy with both risk and reward. Masuda was a player who invented new moves one after another and lived by the motto 'a lifetime of new moves', and he jumps into such risky lines by himself.","18":"S-5c. Rather than chasing the rook, Ōyama lifts his silver into the center. In ordinary Side Pawn Capture games Gote often harasses the rook with moves like B-3c, so this is Ōyama's own way of making the center thick.","19":"R-3f. The rook steps back one square to a safe place. Keeping the extra pawn, Sente calms his rook down.","20":"P-5e. Ōyama pushes the file-5 pawn further and tries to take control of the center. Having lost a pawn, he wants to make up for it with active pieces.","22":"S-4d. The silver comes out to 4d, moving closer to Sente's rook on 3f and also watching the center.","23":"Px5e. Masuda takes the pawn on 5e. Now Sente is two pawns up.","24":"R-5b. Ōyama swings his rook to file 5. Putting the rook in the center makes a 'Central Rook' shape, aiming to win back the pawn on 5e. It is a flexible use of the rook, typical of Ōyama, who later became known as a master of ranging rook openings.","25":"K-6i. Masuda moves his king to the left to prepare for an attack from the center.","26":"Sx5e. The silver takes back the pawn on 5e. Gote's silver and rook line up on file 5 and press on Sente's camp from the center.","28":"P*5f. Gote drops a pawn and bites into Sente's camp. If Gote gets to play P-5g+ next, Sente's camp will break down.","29":"P*5g. Sente also drops a pawn to defend. Pawn meets pawn, and a small battle is happening in the center.","30":"Px5g+. Gote takes the pawn and makes a tokin. Sente can take it back right away, so this is just an exchange of pawns.","32":"K-4a. Ōyama moves his king to 4a on the right. Since the rook is used on file 5, he castles the king on the right, away from the rook.","33":"P*5f. Sente drops a pawn to chase away the silver on 5e. A move typical of Sente, who has many pawns in hand.","34":"S-4d. The silver retreats to 4d. It goes back near its own camp and waits for the next chance.","35":"R-2f. The rook returns to file 2, tidying up Sente's formation.","37":"P-6f. Pushing the file-6 pawn makes a road for the silver to come out.","38":"S-5c. Gote lifts another silver into the center, making file 5 even thicker.","39":"S-6g. The silver rises to 6g, strengthening the area around the king while supporting the center.","41":"R-2h. The rook goes back to its original square. Keeping the extra pawns from the center, Sente sets up a solid defense.","43":"G-5h. The gold rises and joins the defense of the center.","45":"P-4f. Pushing the file-4 pawn, Masuda starts preparing an attack from the right side.","46":"R-8b. Ōyama brings his rook back to 8b. Now that the fight in the center has calmed down, he aims to attack along file 8.","49":"B-5i. The bishop retreats to 5i, planning to use it on another diagonal. It is preparing to swing the bishop from 7g toward files 4 and 3.","51":"N-7g. The left knight jumps up, getting ready to join the attack.","52":"P-8f. Ōyama clashes pawns on file 8 to exchange his rook pawn.","54":"Rx8f. The rook takes, and Gote's rook comes out to 8f. By exchanging the rook pawn, Gote also adds a pawn to his hand.","55":"P*8g. Sente drops a pawn to push the rook back.","57":"B-2f. The bishop comes out to 2f and glares at the right side of the enemy camp.","58":"B-4d. Ōyama also brings out his bishop to face Sente's bishop.","59":"B-3g. The bishop steps back one square, avoiding a bishop trade.","61":"R-4h. The rook swings to file 4. It prepares to push the file-4 pawn and attack from the right side.","62":"P-6e. Ōyama pushes his file-6 pawn and starts a counterattack from the left side.","63":"P-4e. Masuda attacks from file 4 too. Both attacks are about to collide: a tense position.","64":"P-6f. A pawn clash that pokes at the front of Sente's silver.","66":"P*6e. Gote drops another pawn to chase the silver. Using pawns skillfully, he breaks Sente's formation little by little.","68":"P*5e. Gote drops a pawn in the center as well, hitting Sente's pawn on 5f.","69":"S-4f. The silver comes out to 4f and joins the attack on file 4.","70":"P-7e. Gote clashes the file-7 pawn too, putting pressure on Sente's left side as well.","72":"S-6d. The silver moves forward to support the pawn on 6e.","73":"P-4d. Masuda pushes his file-4 pawn in and closes in on the area in front of Gote's king.","75":"S-3e. The silver advances to 3e, adding another attacking piece.","76":"N-3c. The knight jumps up to block the silver's path.","77":"Px5e. Sente takes the central pawn. Gote's pawn in the center disappears, and the fight on file 5 grows fierce.","78":"Bx5e. The bishop takes back, and Gote's bishop comes out to the center.","79":"P*5c. A pawn drop that forces Gote's silver to change position.","81":"Bx5e. Masuda goes for the bishop trade. He takes Gote's bishop in the center and adds a bishop to his hand.","83":"Nx6e. The knight jumps and takes the pawn on 6e, coming closer to Gote's camp.","85":"P*5c. A pawn drop that threatens Gote's gold.","87":"P*2d. Sente drops a pawn on file 2 as well, preparing an attack from the right side.","88":"P*5g. Ōyama drops a pawn into Sente's camp too, creating a foothold for a counterattack.","92":"B*4e. Gote drops his bishop from hand and watches the center of Sente's camp.","93":"B*9f. Masuda also drops a bishop. From 9f it is a far-reaching bishop that looks diagonally toward Gote's rook and king. This bishop will play an important role in the final scene as well.","94":"P*8e. Gote drops a pawn to block the movement of Sente's silver.","98":"K-3a. Ōyama moves his king one more square deeper, away from Sente's attack.","99":"N-3g. The right knight also joins the attack.","100":"B-5f. The bishop advances to 5f, aiming deep into Sente's camp.","102":"P*7g. A pawn drop that lures the gold out and opens the bishop's path.","104":"B-8i+. The bishop promotes at the very back of Sente's camp and becomes a horse. A horse moves like a bishop and can also move one square up, down, left or right: a strong piece.","105":"Px2c+. Masuda also promotes a pawn near Gote's king and joins the race of attacks.","106":"+Bx2c. Ōyama moves the horse from 8i all the way to 2c along the long diagonal and takes the tokin. This move shows how a horse can reach from one end of the board to the other.","107":"R-2h. The rook swings to file 2 to continue the attack there.","110":"+B-8i. The horse goes back deep into Sente's camp. It is a flexible move, typical of Ōyama, using one piece for both defense and attack.","112":"Sx6e. Ōyama takes the knight on 6e with his silver. He removes the knight that was the center of Sente's attack and adds to his own hand.","115":"S-7d. The silver advances to 7d, aiming at Gote's knight.","116":"N*6e. Gote drops a knight and attacks the area around Sente's king.","118":"S-5f. The silver moves forward, closing in on Sente's king.","119":"Sx7c+. Masuda takes the knight and makes a promoted silver. The promoted silver also casts its eye toward Gote's king.","120":"+Bx7h. Here Ōyama gives up his horse to take a gold. It is a do-or-die move, throwing away a big piece to strip away the defenders of Sente's king.","121":"Kx7h. The king takes the horse. Sente's king has lost a defending gold, and the area around it starts to get thin.","122":"G*6g, check. Ōyama drops a gold from hand, and his attack continues.","124":"Gx5h. The gold takes a gold and closes in further on Sente's king.","126":"N-5g+. The knight promotes and chases Sente's king.","128":"S-4g+. The silver also promotes, trying to surround Sente's king.","130":"+N-4h, check. The promoted knight gives check. Gote is throwing every piece he has into the attack.","133":"Kx4h. The king takes back. Here Gote's attacking pieces are almost exhausted. Sente has a bishop, a gold, two silvers, two knights and many pawns in hand: a position where Sente is far ahead. If he just gets his king safely away, Masuda wins.","134":"R*8h, check. Ōyama drops his last rook and gives check. It works sideways from far away: his final act of resistance.","135":"K-4g. Masuda moves his king up. He could also have blocked with a piece, but Masuda chose to run toward the middle of the board.","136":"Rx8g+. The rook takes a pawn, becomes a dragon, and gives check again.","137":"Bx8g. The bishop on 9f takes the dragon. The bishop dropped on move 93 proves useful here.","138":"Rx8g+. Ōyama takes back the bishop with the rook from 8b, makes another dragon and gives check.","139":"K-4f. This was the fateful move. Masuda moved his king up to 4f, but this square stands on a diagonal where Gote can drop a bishop. It is said that if he had blocked with a piece from his hand or run in another direction, Masuda would have won. It was a reflex move, made with victory right in front of him.","140":"B*6d, check. Ōyama drops his bishop on 6d. Its diagonal reaches the king on 4f.","141":"N*5e. Masuda drops a knight to block the bishop's line. He uses the knight from his hand to cut off the bishop.","142":"G*4g, check. The gold is protected by the dragon and cannot be taken, and Masuda admitted defeat here. His king could no longer be saved, and Masuda, who should have won, announced his resignation in a joking tone: 'Illusion bad, look carefully good.' With this victory, Ōyama became the challenger for the Meijin title while still in his mid-twenties, and began his path to becoming a great master who would hold the Meijin title 18 times in total. Inoue Yasushi of the Mainichi newspaper, who watched this game, later became a novelist and wrote the short story 'Shōbu' (The Contest) based on it. The long rivalry between the two brothers-in-training really started to move here on Mount Kōya."}},"fischer1972":{"t":"The Game Spassky Applauded","w":"Bobby Fischer","b":"Boris Spassky","ev":"World Championship, game 6","d":"23 July 1972","pl":"Iceland (Reykjavik)","lead":"Game 6 of the World Championship in which the American Fischer challenged the Soviet world champion Spassky in the middle of the Cold War. Fischer, who almost always opened with e4, began with c4 and, in a Queen's Gambit he had never played before, broke through his opponent's favourite defence head-on. The game is famous because the loser, Spassky, joined the audience in applauding. With this win Fischer took the lead in the match for the first time.","c":{"0":"23 July 1972, Reykjavik, the capital of Iceland. This is game 6 of the World Championship, played in a sports hall. White is the American Bobby Fischer, 29. Black is Boris Spassky of the Soviet Union, world champion since 1969. In the Cold War era, the match drew the whole world's attention as a “battle of minds” between America and the Soviet Union. After five games the score was level at 2½ each. Fischer had lost game 1 and forfeited game 2 by not showing up, but he had caught up since then.","1":"White plays c4! Fischer almost always began with e4; he even wrote that e4 was “best by test”. It is said that this was only the third time he had opened with c4 in a serious game. The audience and Spassky alike saw this move with great surprise. Starting with c4 is called the “English Opening”.","2":"Black plays e6. He keeps a flexible setup so he can play d5 whatever happens. It is said that Spassky's team had prepared for Fischer's e4 or d4 and had not studied much for c4.","3":"The knight goes to f3. A natural move that controls e5 and d4 and helps guard the king. White still does not reveal which setup he is heading for.","4":"Black plays d5. He takes a central square and faces the c4 pawn. Going on like this, the game comes close to a Queen's Gambit.","5":"White plays d4. Here the game joins the setup called the Queen's Gambit. Reaching the same position with the moves in a different order is called a “transposition”. It is said that Fischer had never played the Queen's Gambit in a serious game before this one.","6":"Black plays ♘f6. The knight that guards the king comes out and supports d5. Black does not take the c4 pawn; he chooses the “Queen's Gambit Declined”, keeping a solid centre.","7":"The knight goes to c3. It increases the pressure on the d5 pawn. This setup has been played in thousands of games since the 19th century: one of the most traditional ways to fight.","8":"Black plays ♗e7. A calm move that prepares castling. Spassky often used this classical setup as his weapon.","9":"White plays ♗g5. The bishop faces the f6 knight and ties down a piece that guards d5. This is the most common setup in the Queen's Gambit.","10":"Black castles. The king moves to safety on g8. Up to here, everything has been known for decades.","11":"White plays e3. It opens the f1 bishop's path and supports d4. White's setup is also solid and unhurried.","12":"Black plays h6, asking the bishop: take or retreat? From here Black heads into the “Tartakower Variation”, with b6 and the c8 bishop going to b7 or e6. The name comes from Tartakower, the loser of “Réti's Mate” in this room.","13":"White plays ♗h4. The bishop retreats and keeps the pin on the f6 knight. Trading now would let Black's pieces breathe more freely.","14":"Black plays b6. This is the trademark move of the Tartakower Variation. The c8 bishop will come out to b7 or another square. It is said that Spassky had never lost a game with this setup.","15":"White plays cxd5. Pawns are traded in the centre, and Black must decide on his structure. From here several pieces leave the board in quick succession.","16":"Black plays ♘xd5. The knight recaptures and offers trades to White's bishop and knight. By trading pieces, Black tries to get rid of his cramped position.","17":"White plays ♗xe7. The bishops are traded. Now Black's queen comes out to e7.","18":"Black plays ♕xe7, recapturing with the queen. With fewer minor pieces, the position becomes clearer.","19":"White plays ♘xd5. The knights are traded too, which fixes the shape of Black's d5 pawn.","20":"Black plays exd5. He recaptures with the e-pawn, and a black pawn stays on d5. This pawn holds the centre, but it has few friendly pawns around it, so it can become a target later.","21":"White plays ♖c1. The rook goes to the c-file to prepare pressure for when Black plays c5. White aims at Black's queenside from the c-file.","22":"Black plays ♗e6. The bishop guards d5 and prepares c5. This is a common setup in the Tartakower Variation.","23":"White plays ♕a4. From a4 the queen aims at Black's queenside. It is a slightly unusual post that puts pressure on squares like a7 and c6.","24":"Black plays c5. As planned, two pawns on c5 and d5 support the centre. Two pawns side by side like this are called “hanging pawns”: they can be strong or weak.","25":"White plays ♕a3! From a3 the queen aims at the c5 pawn. Behind that pawn, on the diagonal up to f8, stands Black's queen, so the pawn finds it hard to move.","26":"Black plays ♖c8. The rook guards c5. So far, Black has followed the well-known pattern.","27":"White plays ♗b5! This move was much talked about. It is said to borrow an idea from a game the Soviet master Furman played in 1970. The bishop keeps Black's knight from going to d7 or c6 and tries to disturb Black's setup. Later the Soviet grandmaster Geller is said to have remarked that Black should have played ♕b7 here.","28":"Black plays a6, asking the bishop to retreat or trade. White's bishop cannot stay on b5 for long.","29":"White plays dxc5. Pawns are traded, and Black must recapture with the b-pawn. Now Black's pawns stand side by side on c5 and d5 as “hanging pawns”.","30":"Black plays bxc5. Black has the two pawns on c5 and d5, but no pawns around them for support. White will keep aiming his pieces at these two.","31":"White castles. The king is safe and the rook can now move. White does not hurry; he prepares to attack his opponent's weaknesses patiently.","32":"Black plays ♖a7. The rook comes out to the 7th rank where it can also move sideways. It is an unusual square, but the idea is to defend while guarding squares like a6 and c7.","33":"White plays ♗e2. The bishop drops back to look for a better square. The moves of this match were reported almost every day in newspapers and on television around the world, and many people followed each one.","34":"Black plays ♘d7. The knight comes out, ready to go to b6 or f6. Black still holds space with his central pawns.","35":"White plays ♘d4! The knight jumps into the centre and offers itself for Black's bishop on e6. This bishop is an important piece that guards Black's pawns.","36":"Black plays ♕f8. The queen goes to the f-file, thinking of a counterattack there. If White takes on e6, Black plans to recapture with the f-pawn and open the f-file.","37":"White plays ♘xe6. The knight is traded for the bishop. Now Black has one less piece to guard his pawns.","38":"Black plays fxe6. He recaptures with the f-pawn and the f-file opens. But the e6 pawn also becomes a weak target for White's pieces.","39":"White plays e4! He strikes in the centre and challenges Black's d5 pawn. If Black trades, the d5 pawn becomes even more lonely and Black's structure falls apart.","40":"Black plays d4. The pawn advances to avoid the clash. Now Black has a “passed pawn” on d4 inside White's camp, but White can keep it blocked while he fights.","41":"White plays f4. He advances on the kingside, preparing to go on to e5 or f5. Since Black has many pieces on the queenside, White decides to attack on the other side.","42":"Black plays ♕e7. The queen on e7 watches e5. It prepares for White's pawns coming forward.","43":"White plays e5. The pawn reaches e5 and narrows the movement of Black's pieces. Black's knight can no longer come to f6, and Black's king has fewer defenders.","44":"Black plays ♖b8. The rook goes to the b-file and aims at the b2 pawn. Black tries to counterattack from his queenside.","45":"White plays ♗c4. The bishop stares at the e6 pawn and also controls d5. This bishop will later become an important attacker against Black's king.","46":"Black plays ♔h8. The king steps into the corner, off the bishop's diagonal. There is no danger yet, but Black's pieces are becoming passive.","47":"White plays ♕h3. The queen swings to the kingside and aims at the e6 pawn. The queen that was on the queenside finally moves into attacking position.","49":"White plays b3. The pawn supports the bishop and removes any threat against b2. White first gets rid of his weaknesses, then attacks.","51":"White plays f5! He strikes with a pawn to open lines around Black's king. From here White's attack really begins.","53":"White plays ♖xf5. The rook steps forward and takes over the open f-file. White's pieces are gathering close to Black's king.","55":"White plays ♖cf1. The other rook joins it on the f-file, doubling the pressure there. It is not quite Alekhine's gun, but it is the same idea of stacking on one file.","57":"White plays ♕g3. The queen enters the kingside and comes close to Black's king. All of White's pieces are taking up attacking posts.","59":"White plays h4. One pawn step stops Black's knight from coming to g5. It is a small move, but a careful one that takes away one of Black's defences.","61":"White plays e6! The pawn steps forward and cuts Black's camp in two. The e6 pawn is protected by White's bishop, and Black's rooks and knight can barely move.","63":"White plays ♕e5. The queen comes to the centre and aims at Black's pawns and pieces. Black has his hands full just defending.","65":"White plays a4. He stops Black's queenside pawns and removes any counterplay from a5. Removing every defensive worry before attacking is very typical of Fischer.","67":"White plays ♖1f2. He reorders his rooks to prepare the next idea. Black can only shuffle between ♕d8 and ♕e8; he can do nothing on his own.","71":"White plays ♗d3. The bishop moves to the diagonal that aims at h7. If the queen comes to the same diagonal next, there will be huge pressure on h7, right in front of Black's king.","73":"White plays ♕e4. Queen and bishop line up on the same diagonal and aim at h7. White threatens to put a rook on f8 and then mate on h7.","74":"Black plays ♘f6. The knight hits the queen and blocks the diagonal. But this move allows White's next blow.","75":"White plays ♖xf6! He gives a rook for the knight: an “exchange sacrifice”. The f6 knight disappears, and the squares around Black's king open up.","76":"Black plays gxf6. He must take. Otherwise White breaks in on f8 or h7.","77":"White plays ♖xf6. The other rook enters f6 and wins the pawn back. Black's king is now surrounded by White's pieces.","79":"White plays ♗c4. The bishop supports the e6 pawn and also aims at squares near Black's king. Black has almost no good defence.","81":"White plays ♕f4. The queen aims at the h6 pawn and the f8 square, and White's attack can no longer be stopped. Spassky resigned here. And it is said that, moved by the quality of the game, Spassky joined the audience in applauding. Fischer now led the match for the first time, 3½–2½, and in the end he became world champion by 12½–8½. The title, held by Soviet players for 24 years since 1948, had finally passed to someone else."}},"kanju1754":{"t":"Kanju beats his brother and reaches 8-dan","w":"Itō Kanju","b":"Ōhashi Sōkei VIII","ev":"Castle shogi (the yearly game at Edo Castle)","d":"17th day of the 11th month, 1754 (Hōreki 4)","pl":"Japan (Edo Castle)","lead":"A castle game, played on even terms, between Itō Kanju, famous for the tsume-shogi masterpiece 'Shōgi Zukō', and his own elder brother, Ōhashi Sōkei VIII. The two were great rivals who met many times at the castle. Kanju had beaten his brother the year before, won this game too, and so reached 8-dan, the rank of 'the next Meijin', ahead of his brother. Against Gote's Fourth-File Rook, Kanju attacks fast, wins material with his bishop, and at the end drags the enemy king into the middle of the board and mates it. Kanju won in 137 moves.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. Today's game is the castle game of 1754 (Hōreki 4). Sente, Itō Kanju, and Gote, Ōhashi Sōkei VIII, were actually brothers. Their father was the 5th Meijin, Itō Sōin II; Sōkei was the third son and Kanju the fifth. Sōkei was adopted into the Ōhashi main house at 11, so as head of the Ōhashi house, and Kanju as heir of the Itō house, they fought many times, each carrying his family's name. The year before, Kanju had beaten his brother on even terms, and if he won this game too he would reach 8-dan, which meant 'the next Meijin'. Let's watch this important game between brothers.","1":"P-7f. The first move opens the bishop's diagonal. In this year, in Kyoto, the doctor Yamawaki Tōyō watched the dissection of a human body, the first one in Japan done with official permission. It was a big step for Japanese medicine that later led to the famous anatomy book 'Kaitai Shinsho'.","2":"P-3d. Gote opens its bishop's diagonal too. Around the same time in North America, the 'French and Indian War' between Britain and France began. Japan was ruled by the 9th shōgun, Tokugawa Ieshige, and castle shogi was still held every year on the 17th day of the 11th month.","3":"P-2f. Sente pushes the rook's pawn. Kanju was born in 1719 and was 36 by the old way of counting age. He lost his father at five and is said to have learned shogi while being raised by his elder brothers.","4":"P-4d. Gote closes the bishop's diagonal. In Edo-period castle games, Gote very often closed the diagonal here, and the game then took shape depending on whether Gote kept a Static Rook or swung it.","5":"P-2e. Sente pushes the rook's pawn further. It is said that at about seven Kanju read a tsume-shogi book and made such a sharp remark that he amazed his brother Sōkan.","6":"B-3c. The bishop rises to guard 2d. Sōkei was four years older than Kanju, and at the castle the elder brother had always held the position of the stronger player.","7":"S-4h. Sente lifts the right silver, an important piece for a fast attack. Kanju is said to have composed, at the age of 13, a long tsume-shogi problem that takes 611 moves to solve.","9":"P-5f. Sente pushes the centre pawn. Later this pawn will march to 5e and 5d and help break up Gote's camp.","11":"P-3f. Sente pushes the pawn on file 3 to make a path for the knight to jump to 3g. In a fast attack this knight plays an important part.","12":"R-4b. Gote swings its rook to file 4. This is the 'Fourth-File Rook'. It is the classic Ranging Rook opening, played since the Edo period, and it is still popular today with amateurs and professionals alike. Sōkei plans to swing his rook to the left and castle his king on the right.","13":"G-5h. Sente lifts a gold to build the king's defence. Having golds around the king makes it easier to push back the opponent's attack.","14":"K-6b. Gote's king moves to the side away from the rook. In Ranging Rook play, the basic rule is to castle the king far away from the rook.","15":"K-6h. Sente's king also goes left. Because Sente wants to start fighting early, it keeps the castle simple. In exchange, it will fight with a slightly thin defence.","17":"K-7h. The king has reached 7h. The castle is thin, but Sente can attack that much sooner. This is the idea of a 'fast attack'.","19":"P-9f. Sente pushes the edge pawn. This edge pawn will be useful later when attacking Gote's king. Gote answers next with P-9d.","23":"N-3g. The knight jumps, getting ready to attack. When the fight starts on files 3 and 4, this knight will jump to 4e and join in.","24":"P-4e. Gote pushes its pawn on file 4 first. If Sente jumped N-4e and took the pawn, the rook on 4b could take back. Pushing the pawn in front of the Fourth-File Rook like this prepares for Sente's fast attack.","26":"K-8b. Gote's king goes deep to 8b. Next a gold will come to 7b, and together with the silver on 6b they will guard the king.","27":"R-2f. The rook rises one square and becomes a floating rook that can also move sideways. The idea is to swing the rook to file 3 or 4 to attack.","29":"P-3e. The attack begins. Sente clashes pawns on file 3, aiming at the head of the bishop on 3c. Against the Fourth-File Rook, starting a fight by clashing pawns on file 3 or 4 is a common pattern of the fast attack.","31":"P-2d. Sente clashes pawns on file 2 as well. The idea is to weaken the bishop's defence on 3c from both files 2 and 3.","33":"Bx3c+. Sente takes the bishop with its bishop: a bishop trade. Now each side holds a bishop in hand and can drop it anywhere.","35":"P-5e. Sente pushes the centre pawn into Gote's pawn on 5d. After a bishop trade, shaking the opponent's camp from the centre is effective.","36":"P-3f. Gote pushes its pawn from 3e to 3f, attacking the knight on 3g. It is a counter that pokes at Sente's attacking pieces.","38":"P*3d. Gote drops a pawn to block file 3. Now there is a wall of Gote's pawn in front of Sente's rook on 3f.","39":"Px5d. Sente takes in the pawn on file 5. This pawn aims at 5c in Gote's camp. When a pawn gets close to the enemy camp, it becomes a foothold for dropping a bishop or silver.","40":"B*2h. Gote drops its bishop deep in Sente's camp, on 2h. The idea is to take the lance next with B-1i+.","41":"P-9e. Sente clashes the edge pawn. Ignoring the bishop on 2h, it attacks from the edge on the left, where Gote's king lives. Attacking from the edge near the king is one of the key ways to fight a Ranging Rook.","42":"Bx1i+. Gote's bishop takes the lance and promotes to a horse. A horse is a strong piece, but stuck in the corner it cannot work right away.","43":"Px9d. Sente takes in the edge pawn and comes closer to Gote's king on file 9. Even though Gote's horse took the lance, Sente calmly carries on attacking.","44":"P*9b. Gote drops a pawn to guard the edge. File 9 is stopped for now, but later this pawn will make the escape route of Gote's king narrower.","45":"B*5c. Sente drops its bishop on 5c. Along its diagonals this bishop attacks both the silver on 6b and the rook on 4b at the same time: a 'fork'. One of them will be lost, so Gote is in big trouble.","46":"P-4f. Gote pushes the pawn on file 4 to counter-attack Sente's camp. It advances near Sente's rook to slow Sente's attack even a little.","47":"Bx6b+. The bishop takes the silver on 6b and promotes to a horse. Now Sente has a silver in hand for the next attack.","48":"Gx6b. Gote takes back the horse with the gold. Bishop and silver have been traded, but Sente's attack goes on.","49":"S*5c. Sente drops the silver on 5c. The pawn on 5d supports it, and this silver aims at both the rook on 4b and the gold on 6b.","51":"Sx4b+. The silver takes the rook on 4b and promotes. Kanju has won the rook, the strongest piece his opponent had.","53":"P-5c+. The pawn on 5d promotes to a tokin. This tokin is a hard worker that aims at silvers and golds inside Gote's camp.","54":"Sx5c. Gote takes the tokin. So far Sente has won a rook and is clearly ahead in material. This site's shogi AI also sees Sente as better.","56":"L*4b. Gote drops a lance to face the rook on file 4. A lance can move straight ahead as far as it likes, so it is set up in front of the rook to clash with it.","59":"N-4e. The knight on 3g jumps to 4e and joins the attack. A knight jumps over other pieces, so it can attack even where there are many defenders.","60":"+B-6d. The horse on 1i comes back along a long diagonal to 6d. This horse protects its own king and also watches Sente's rook. People say a horse 'is strong when pulled back to your own camp'.","61":"P*2b. Sente drops a pawn on 2b, planning to promote it next into a tokin. A tokin works just like a gold inside the enemy camp.","62":"B*4d. Gote drops its bishop on 4d. This bishop aims at the pawn on 2b, and along the other diagonal it also looks via 5e and 6f toward Sente's king.","63":"S-6f. Sente lifts the silver from 5g to 6f to cut the diagonal of the bishop on 4d. A calm move that protects the area around its own king.","64":"Bx2b. Gote's bishop takes the pawn on 2b. Gote does not give in either: it clears away the pawn Sente dropped and tidies up its defence.","65":"P*3c. Sente drops another pawn to make a foothold in Gote's camp. This pawn aims at the area around 3b and also gets in the way of Gote's bishop.","66":"Lx4e. Gote's lance takes the knight on 4e. Sente has lost one attacking knight, but it still has a rook in hand.","67":"R*4c. Sente drops its other rook on 4c. With two rooks it aims at Gote's camp. Sente can use both rooks because it won one through the bishop fork from move 45.","68":"P*4b. Gote drops a pawn to block the rook. But a single pawn cannot hold back a rook's great power for long.","69":"Rx4e+. The rook on 4c takes the lance on 4e and promotes to a dragon. A dragon moves like a rook and can also step one square diagonally: a very strong piece.","71":"P*5d. Sente drops a pawn on file 5 to hold down Gote's camp. This pawn stops Gote's silver from coming out toward 5e, and later it will become a tokin.","72":"N*4d. Gote drops a knight to aim at the rook on 5f. A knight jumps two squares forward and one to the side, so from 4d it hits the rook on 5f.","73":"R-4f. The rook steps from 5f to 4f and supports the dragon on 4e from behind. The rook and the dragon now stand in a line and protect each other.","74":"S*3e. Gote drops a silver to aim at the rook on 4f, diagonally. A silver can also move diagonally forward, so from 3e it reaches 4f.","75":"+Rx3e. Instead of moving the rook away, Sente takes the silver on 3e with the dragon on 4e. The dragon will be taken back by a pawn, but Kanju seems to have chosen not to give his opponent time to regroup, rather than to count material. This site's shogi AI still sees Sente as better.","77":"P*3d. Sente drops a pawn to attack the bishop on 3c. A bishop can only move diagonally, so it is weak against a pawn coming straight at it.","79":"L*2c. Sente drops a lance to attack the bishop on 2b. If the bishop runs away, Sente can take the knight next with L-2a+. This site's shogi AI sees Sente's lead growing around here.","80":"P*5g. Gote drops a pawn in Sente's camp to start a counter-attack. Taking it pulls Sente's silver back, which changes Sente's defence a little.","83":"Lx2a+. The lance takes the knight and promotes. The right corner of Gote's camp has crumbled, and Sente now has a knight in hand.","85":"+Lx1a. The promoted lance takes the lance too. Sente has more pieces in hand and now heads for Gote's king. The lance and knight in hand will be key pieces for attacking Gote's king.","87":"L*8f. Sente drops the lance from its hand on 8f. Straight up file 8 stands the pawn on 8c, and beyond it Gote's king on 8b, so this lance glares at the king's head. From here Kanju's attack on the king begins.","88":"P-3g+. Gote also makes a tokin in Sente's camp. A tokin moves like a gold and is very strong in the enemy camp.","89":"N*9e. Sente drops a knight to aim at 8c. Working with the lance on 8f, it attacks the head of Gote's king. 8c is just in front of Gote's king; break it and the king is left bare.","90":"R*2h. Gote drops a rook to aim at Sente's king. Gote uses its two rooks to make a last bid against Sente's king.","91":"S-6f. Sente lifts the silver, a move that thinks about both defending its own king and attacking. This silver also covers 5e and 7e and keeps Gote's pieces from coming near.","93":"G-6h. Sente brings the gold over to protect its own king. When the opponent's attack is getting close, defending with just one move and then attacking all at once is important.","95":"Rx4d. The rook on 4f takes the knight on 4d. From here the rook also presses on Gote's camp from the side, toward 4b and 4a.","97":"P-5c+. Sente promotes the pawn on 5d into a tokin. If a tokin is taken you only lose a pawn, but the opponent's piece is forced to move to take it.","99":"Nx8c+. The knight on 9e takes the pawn on 8c and promotes. This is just in front of Gote's king. Now the pawn that guarded Gote's king is gone.","101":"N*7e. Sente drops its other knight, aiming at the gold on 8c and at 6c. A knight moves in a way that ordinary pieces find hard to block, so near the king it is very strong.","103":"Lx8c+. The lance on 8f takes the gold on 8c and promotes. The lance dropped on move 87 does its job here.","104":"K-6a. Gote's king flees further to the right. The king is moving away from its own defenders.","105":"S*7b. A silver drop with check, driving the king toward the centre. A silver can also move diagonally forward, so from 7b it reaches the king on 6a.","106":"K-5b. Gote's king comes out to the centre. When the king comes out to the middle, there are few defenders around it, which is very dangerous.","107":"Nx6c+. The knight on 7e takes the pawn on 6c and promotes. A promoted knight moves like a gold, so this is check on the king on 5b.","109":"Sx6c+. Sente takes with the silver from 7b and promotes. The captures go on, and Gote's defenders are slowly disappearing.","111":"S*7b. Sente drops another silver, aiming at the horse on 6c. A silver can also move diagonally backward, so from 7b it reaches 6c.","112":"+B-5c. Gote's horse steps to 5c and joins the king's defence. Next to its own king, a horse is a very strong defender.","113":"G*6c. Sente drops a gold right onto the horse on 5c. The silver on 7b supports this gold, so if the horse takes it, the silver takes back.","115":"Sx6c+. The silver on 7b takes the horse and promotes. Sente has removed the horse, the heart of Gote's defence.","116":"Kx6c. Gote takes the promoted silver with the king. Gote's king has had to come all the way out to 6c in the centre.","117":"B*7b. A bishop drop from hand gives check. A bishop reaches far along diagonals, and from 7b it aims at the king on 6c.","119":"P*5c. A pawn drop with check. Sente lets the king take it to pull the king up even further. Sacrificing a pawn on purpose to call the enemy king to a dangerous square is the idea.","121":"B-5d+. The bishop on 7b comes out to 5d, promotes to a horse and gives check. When a horse comes right next to the king, it is a very strong attacker.","123":"P*5c. Another pawn drop with check, driving Gote's king to 5a. Chasing the king by dropping pawn after pawn like this is typical of Kanju, the master of tsume-shogi.","124":"K-5a. Gote's king has dropped back to the bottom rank. A king on the bottom rank cannot escape when many pieces press down on it from above.","125":"P-3c+. The pawn on 3d promotes on 3c into a tokin. Next this tokin will move into 4b and come closer to Gote's king.","126":"+Px6h. Gote takes Sente's gold with the tokin and makes a last counter-attack. Even when your own king is in danger, playing moves that get closer to the enemy king is the art of the endgame.","128":"P-5h+. Gote makes another tokin and presses on, but Sente is one step faster. This tokin is close to Sente's king, but it is too late.","129":"+Px4b. The tokin on 3c takes the pawn on 4b. If a tokin is taken you lose only a pawn, so it is perfect for stripping away the opponent's defenders.","131":"Rx4b+. The rook on 4d takes the gold on 4b, promotes to a dragon and gives check. The rook that had waited there since move 95 does its job here.","135":"P-5b+. The pawn on 5c promotes on 5b with check. Tokin, gold and horse wrap up Gote's king. A tokin moves like a gold, so from 5b it reaches the king on 4a.","137":"G-3b. The gold on 4c steps to 3b: checkmate. The horse on 5d reaches 3b along the diagonal, so the king cannot take the gold. The escape squares 2a, 2b, 4a and 4b are all covered too. Kanju won a rook with a bishop fork, gave up a dragon to keep his attack fast, and from the lance and knight on the edge he dragged Gote's king into the middle of the board and mated it. With this win Kanju became 8-dan, and the next year, 1755, he presented his tsume-shogi collection 'Shōgi Zukō' to the shogunate. But in 1760 Kanju died at 42 (by the old way of counting) before he could become Meijin, and the title of Meijin was given to him after his death."}},"masuda1957":{"t":"Masuda, the First Triple Crown: Winning the Meijin Title","w":"Masuda Kōzō","b":"Ōyama Yasuharu","ev":"16th Meijin-sen, best-of-seven title match, game 6","d":"July 10-11, 1957","pl":"Japan (Yoyogi, Tokyo)","lead":"Nine years after the Mount Kōya showdown, the brothers-in-training Masuda Kōzō and Ōyama Yasuharu faced each other again, this time in the best-of-seven Meijin title match. Masuda led 3-2 going into game 6, showed a sharp attack in a Double Yagura battle, and won in 95 moves. With this win Masuda became Meijin and, together with the Ōshō and Kudan titles he already held, became the first 'triple crown' holder in the history of shogi.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. After losing to Ōyama in the Mount Kōya showdown of 1948, Masuda went through a long period in which he could hardly beat Ōyama. Ōyama became Meijin in 1952 and held the title for five years in a row. Meanwhile, in the Ōshō-sen of the 1955 season Masuda beat Ōyama, and under the rules of the time he pushed Ōyama down to a 'lance handicap' game; it is said this fulfilled his childhood dream to 'beat the Meijin even giving a lance handicap'. Then in the 1957 Meijin-sen, Masuda led 3-2 and came to game 6 at the 'Hatsuhana' inn in Yoyogi, Tokyo. Ōyama was 34 and Masuda 39.","1":"P-7f. Masuda opens his bishop's diagonal. All six games of this title match became 'Double Yagura' battles, and this game follows the same course. 1957 was also the year Japan's Antarctic expedition opened 'Shōwa Station' in Antarctica.","2":"P-8d. Ōyama pushes his rook pawn. He meets Masuda with a static rook game, using the rook straight ahead.","3":"S-6h. The left silver comes up. This is the first step toward building the castle called Yagura. Yagura is a fortress-like shape that guards the top and sides of the king with three golds and silvers, a way of playing with a long history going back to the Edo period.","5":"S-7g. The silver goes to 7g and closes the bishop's diagonal. It shows the intention to avoid a bishop trade and build the Yagura slowly. In the Meijin title matches of this era, 'Double Yagura', where both sides build a Yagura like this, was the main battlefield.","6":"S-4b. Ōyama also lifts a silver and aims for a Yagura too.","7":"P-2f. Pushing the rook pawn, Masuda starts preparing his attack as well. In Yagura it is important to build the attacking shape at the same time as the castle.","8":"S-6b. Ōyama lifts his other silver and strengthens the defense from the center to the right.","9":"S-4h. The right silver comes up too. This is the attacking silver, and how it is sent forward is the highlight of this game.","10":"G-3b. The gold rises to build the king's castle.","11":"G-7h. The gold moves over, shaping the Yagura.","12":"K-4a. Ōyama moves his king to 4a. This prepares to bring the king into the castle.","13":"K-6i. Masuda also moves his king to the left. This king will go toward 7i and 8h and enter the Yagura.","14":"P-7d. Ōyama pushes his file-7 pawn to prepare a fight on the left side (seen from Sente).","15":"P-3f. Pushing the file-3 pawn opens a road for the right silver to go up to 3g.","16":"P-5d. Gote pushes the central pawn, making a path to move the bishop to 5c.","17":"P-4f. Sente pushes the file-4 pawn too, laying the foundation for an attack on the right side.","18":"P-6d. Gote pushes the file-6 pawn. The idea is to stretch it to 6e next and take a position above Sente's Yagura.","19":"P-2e. The rook pawn moves one more step. The attack on file 2 is getting closer.","20":"S-3c. The silver goes up to 3c and strengthens the defense of file 2. Even if the pawn on 2e comes to 2d, Gote can defend.","21":"S-3g. The right silver rises to 3g. The plan is to send it forward to 2f and 3e and crash it into the opponent's defense.","22":"B-3a. Ōyama pulls his bishop back to 3a. Next this bishop will move to squares like 5c and restrain Sente's attack along a diagonal. It is a common way to use the bishop in Yagura.","23":"S-2f. The silver comes out to 2f. With the silver in the lead, Sente is preparing to attack from file 3 or file 2.","24":"P-6e. Ōyama stretches his file-6 pawn and takes a position. He pushes a pawn out above Sente's Yagura to narrow the room for Sente's bishop.","25":"P-3e. At last Masuda clashes the file-3 pawn. It is the signal to attack file 3 together with the silver.","27":"Sx3e. The silver takes back, and Sente's silver comes out to 3e. The silver stands on the front line and aims at 4d and 2d.","28":"B-5c. The bishop comes out to 5c and glares at the silver on 3e and Sente's camp. The bishop that retreated on move 22 starts working here.","29":"R-3h. Masuda swings his rook to file 3. Putting the rook behind the silver strengthens the attack on file 3.","30":"S-4d. Ōyama brings his silver out to 4d and hits the silver on 3e. He crashes silver against silver to stop Sente's attack.","31":"S-6h. The left silver steps back and opens the bishop's path. Now that the silver on 7g has retreated, the bishop on 8h can work along its diagonal.","32":"P*3c. Gote drops a pawn to defend file 3.","33":"Sx4d. Masuda goes for the silver trade.","34":"Bx4d. The bishop takes back.","35":"Bx4d. Masuda takes the bishop on 4d with his bishop from 8h. It is a bishop-for-bishop trade.","36":"Px4d. The pawn takes back. After trading a silver and a bishop each, both sides now have a silver and a bishop in hand. With more pieces in hand, it becomes a fierce fight with many places to drop them.","37":"R-4h. The rook swings to file 4. The aim is to push the file-4 pawn and attack on file 4 this time.","38":"G-5b. Ōyama brings his gold over and strengthens the area around his king.","39":"K-7i. Masuda also puts his king one square deeper, making it safe inside the Yagura.","40":"P-8e. Ōyama stretches his file-8 pawn, preparing a counterattack toward Sente's king.","41":"P-4e. Masuda clashes the file-4 pawn.","43":"Rx4e. The rook takes back. Sente's rook comes out to 4e, a strong square where it works both sideways and up and down.","44":"P*4c. Gote drops a pawn in front of the rook to defend file 4.","45":"G-5i. The gold steps back to rebuild the defense. It is a calm move by Masuda, adding a defensive move before attacking.","46":"P-8f. Ōyama clashes the file-8 pawn and begins his counterattack.","48":"N-7c. The knight jumps up, supporting the pawn on 6e and preparing to join the attack.","49":"P-2d. Masuda clashes the file-2 pawn as well.","51":"P*2b. Here Masuda's sharp attack begins. He drops a pawn on 2b to drag the gold out.","52":"Gx2b. The gold takes. When the gold moves to 2b, it gets a little farther from the king's defense.","53":"P*2c. Another pawn drop. Dropping pawns one after another to pull the defending gold farther and farther from the king is a technique called the 'tapping pawn'.","54":"Gx2c. The gold has been pulled out to 2c. The defense of Gote's king has become thin.","55":"B*6a. Masuda drops his bishop from hand at 6a, deep inside Gote's camp. It hits the gold on 5b and glares deep into Gote's camp along its diagonal: a sharp drop.","56":"S-5a. Ōyama pulls his silver back to 5a to support the gold on 5b.","57":"S*6d. Masuda drops a silver from hand on 6d. It aims at the knight on 7c, and with the bishop and silver together he presses on Gote's left side.","58":"B*3d. Ōyama also drops a bishop from hand on 3d. It aims at the rook on 4e and restrains Sente's attack.","59":"Sx7c. The silver takes the knight on 7c. Capturing as it goes, the silver gets into Gote's camp and also threatens Gote's rook.","61":"Rx6e. The rook takes the pawn on 6e. It works widely sideways close to Gote's camp.","62":"P*6d. Ōyama drops a pawn to chase the rook.","64":"P*6c. Gote drops another pawn to chase the rook. He is using pawn after pawn to stop the rook's movement.","65":"Rx5d. The rook moves to 5d, taking the pawn there.","66":"P*5c. Gote drops yet another pawn to chase the rook.","67":"Rx3d. Here Masuda takes the bishop on 3d with his rook. Gote will take the rook with the pawn, but Masuda decides to get the bishop and secure attacking pieces. Using a big piece boldly like this is typical of Masuda's attacks.","68":"Px3d. The pawn takes the rook. It is a rook-for-bishop trade, but Sente has a bishop, a knight and pawns in hand, and the bishop on 6a and the silver on 7c are biting into Gote's camp.","69":"S-7b+. The silver promotes and closes in on the back of Gote's camp.","70":"Rx8f. Ōyama's rook takes the pawn on 8f and counterattacks Sente's camp.","71":"N*1e. Masuda drops a knight on the edge. The idea is N-2c+ next, taking the gold, threatening Gote's defense from afar.","72":"G-3c. The gold escapes to 3c.","73":"P*8g. A pawn drop pushes the rook back and guards the area above Sente's own king.","75":"P*2b. Another pawn drop. The aim is P-2a+ next, taking the knight.","76":"S*3b. Gote drops a silver to defend.","77":"Px2a+. The pawn promotes and takes the knight on 2a.","78":"Sx2a. The silver takes back.","79":"P-7e. Pushing the file-7 pawn stops the sideways movement of the rook on 8e. It is now harder for Gote's rook to get into Sente's camp.","80":"G-4b. The gold moves over and strengthens the area around the king.","81":"P*4d. Masuda drops a pawn that hits the pawn on 4c. It closes in on Gote's king from above with a pawn.","82":"S-3b. The silver rises to defend.","83":"Px4c+. The pawn promotes to a tokin and gets right next to the king.","85":"N*4e. Masuda drops a knight. The idea is to jump to 3c or 5c next and close in on Gote's king.","86":"P*8h. Ōyama drops a pawn near Sente's king and tries a last counterattack.","87":"Nx3c (no promotion). Masuda takes the gold on 3c with the knight and chooses not to promote. An unpromoted knight attacks 2a and 4a, so it gives check to the king on 4a. If it promoted, it would move like a gold and this check would disappear, so not promoting is correct here.","89":"B*7f. Masuda drops his bishop on 7f. This bishop runs diagonally through 6e and 5d to 4c and aims at the silver on 4c. Together with the bishop on 6a, two bishops focus on the 4c square.","90":"P-8i+. Ōyama promotes the pawn and gives check to Sente's king.","92":"Rx7e. The rook takes the pawn on 7e and attacks the bishop on 7f.","93":"Bx4c+. Masuda's bishop from 6a takes the silver on 4c and becomes a horse.","95":"Bx4c+. The bishop from 7f takes the gold on 4c, and a second horse is born. A horse is right next to Gote's king, and Sente has two golds and a silver in hand: there is no defense left. Ōyama admitted defeat here, and Masuda won in 95 moves. Masuda became Meijin with 4 wins and 2 losses, and together with the Ōshō and Kudan titles he gained the first triple crown in history. It is said that Masuda then left the words 'I have climbed this far, yet I am still at the foot of the mountain', meaning that even when you think you have reached the top, you are still only at the foot. In October of this year, the Soviet Union launched the world's first artificial satellite. Nine years after Mount Kōya, this is the game in which Masuda seized his glory."}},"ck1992g18":{"t":"Chinook freezes and resigns a drawn game","w":"Marion Tinsley","b":"Chinook (University of Alberta computer)","ev":"Man vs. Machine World Checkers Championship, game 18","d":"22 August 1992","pl":"United Kingdom (London)","lead":"The world championship between the champion Marion Tinsley and the program Chinook. In game 18, in a quiet, textbook drawn position, Chinook suddenly stopped moving. With almost no time left on its clock, the team chose to resign \"in the interest of sportsmanship.\" It was a loss without losing on the board, and Chinook, which had led 2–1, was pulled back to 2–2.","c":{"0":"Saturday, 22 August 1992, the Park Lane Hotel in London: game 18 of the Man vs. Machine World Checkers Championship. Black (moving first, squares 1–12 at the top) is Tinsley; White (21–32 at the bottom) is Chinook. The time control was 20 moves per hour. Chinook ran on an SGI 4D/480 supplied by the sponsor, Silicon Graphics: one large machine holding eight computers. This game would be decided not on the board but inside that machine.","1":"Tinsley plays 9 to 13, toward the left edge. This first move has a name: the \"Edinburgh\". In tournaments the first three moves are drawn by lot (the ballot) and played as assigned. The U.S. championship has used this three-move restriction since 1934.","2":"White, Chinook, plays 23 to 19, into the centre. Chinook was begun in 1989 at the University of Alberta in Canada by Jonathan Schaeffer and his team. At the 1990 U.S. Open in Tupelo, Mississippi, it finished second behind Tinsley and earned the right to challenge for the world title.","3":"Black plays 6 to 9, completing the ballot 9-13 23-19 6-9. At first the checkers federations would not let a computer play for the title. Tinsley, as if in protest, gave up his world title and then signed a contract to play Chinook anyway.","4":"From here the moves are free. White brings 27 up to 23, backing up the man on 19. Richard Pask's opening manual Key Openings, compiled around 1999, also recommends this attack. The playing table had been used for the 1986 Kasparov–Karpov world chess championship.","5":"Black plays 9 to 14, nearer the centre, watching White's route to 18. This is also the defence Pask recommends. Chinook's opening knowledge had been checked over many years by the team's checkers expert, Norman Treloar.","6":"White plays 22 to 18, standing right in front of the man on 14 and asking Black to decide. Schaeffer wrote that Chinook's machine looked like \"a refrigerator with blinking lights.\" On the big screen in the hall, lifelike computer-drawn hands moved the pieces and amazed the audience.","7":"Black plays 11 to 15. Now White must capture from 18, taking either 14 or 15. Pask lists this position as one of the \"key landings\", crossroads where many openings meet. The prize fund was 10,000 dollars, to be split 60–40 between winner and loser.","8":"White plays 18x9, jumping 14 and landing on 9. Taking 15 with 18x11 was also possible, but this site's engine also prefers 18x9. The man on 9 will be recaptured at once.","9":"Black recaptures 5x14, jumping 9 and landing on 14. Capturing is compulsory, so this is Black's only move. Material is level again.","10":"White plays 25 to 22, bringing a back man forward to organise the centre. In checkers it is vital to move men in groups so no holes are left after exchanges. This is still the book line.","11":"Black plays 7 to 11, filling in behind 15 and firming up the shape. If White ever attacks 15, the man on 11 will support it. Still theory.","12":"White plays 30 to 25, lifting a man off the back row to guard behind 22. Under the 20-moves-an-hour control, Chinook was playing its book moves smoothly.","13":"Black plays 1 to 5. It moves a back-row man, but filling 5 behind 14 prepares for the coming exchange. At the opening ceremony, Tinsley reportedly said several times, \"I cannot lose.\"","14":"White plays 22 to 17, stepping in front of 13 to offer a capture. Black must take with 13x22. White plans to take two back in return.","15":"Black plays 13x22, jumping 17 and landing on 22. When you can capture, you must. White's recapture is ready.","16":"White plays 25x18x9, taking 22 and 14 in one double jump. Chinook judged this a \"sizable advantage,\" about a third of a man. But the man on 9 will be taken straight back.","17":"Black recaptures 5x14, and nothing has been won or lost. In Pask's book the same line continues for many more moves. Tinsley, it is said, could recall move sequences from his own games of the 1940s without hesitation.","18":"White plays 26 to 22, filling the empty centre and preparing routes to 17 and 18. On paper, Chinook's assessment as White kept creeping upward.","19":"Black plays 11 to 16, inviting White's 24-20. Black judges that the exchanges will not break his shape. This too is Pask's main line.","20":"White plays 24 to 20, stepping beside 16 and forcing Black to capture 15x24. Chinook's score rose to more than half a man ahead, and Schaeffer wrote: \"Marion is teasing us again.\" But that number came from looking very far ahead, and with best play Black could still draw.","21":"Black plays 15x24, taking 19 and landing on 24. Capturing is compulsory, so there is only this move. A black man is now deep inside White's camp.","22":"White plays 20x11, jumping 16 and landing on 11. 28x19 was also possible, but the engine chooses 20x11. The white man on 11 will soon be taken.","23":"Black plays 8x15, recapturing the man on 11. Because Black used the back man on 8, his top-row guard is now down to 2, 3 and 4.","24":"White plays 28x19, taking the black man on 24. The black man that had dug in deep is gone for now.","25":"Black plays 15x24, jumping 19 again and landing on 24. The exchanges are over and material is level. The black man on 24 will be a nuisance, cramping White's right side.","26":"White plays 22 to 18, coming into the centre again and eyeing the black man on 14. This is still Pask's sequence.","27":"Black plays 12 to 16, deliberately letting 14 be taken. The engine sees this as almost the only move; others such as 4-8 are much worse. In return, Black gains a path down the right side.","28":"White plays 18x9, taking 14 as the rules require. The white man has reached 9 and is close to the row where it would become a king.","29":"Black plays 16 to 20, setting it beside 24 so the two men support each other. White's right side is now blocked by these two black men.","30":"White plays 23 to 19. Pask's main line ends here. Schaeffer wrote that all of this was published play and that Black can draw.","31":"Black plays 10 to 14, advancing a centre man and opening the way to 18 while keeping an eye on White's man on 9. From here we are out of the book.","32":"White plays 31 to 26. At this moment Chinook saw an advantage of about 0.7 of a man. But with deeper searching, that number would soon fall.","33":"Black plays 14 to 18, pushing into White's centre. White can answer 26-22, letting 18 capture and then taking back.","34":"The last move in the record, 26-22, is Chinook's 17th move. Chinook had played 16 moves in 27 minutes and had 33 minutes for 4 more, but here it kept thinking and never moved. A message said \"move in 95 seconds,\" yet minutes passed with nothing. With two minutes left the team forced a move to see whether the program was alive, stopped the clock, and asked the organisers and Tinsley for a meeting. They were allowed to continue on a backup machine, but the team chose to resign \"in the interest of sportsmanship.\" The position was a published draw, and Schaeffer later wrote that the pain of resigning a drawn game in a world championship never faded. This loss levelled the match at 2–2; Tinsley went on to win game 25 and the final game, keeping his crown 4–2."}},"polgar2002":{"t":"Polgar's First Win over Kasparov","w":"Judit Polgar","b":"Garry Kasparov","ev":"Russia vs the Rest of the World (rapid), round 5","d":"9 September 2002","pl":"Russia (Moscow)","lead":"The game in which Judit Polgar, said to be the strongest woman player in history, beat the world number one Kasparov for the first time. Kasparov uses the Berlin Defence, the very opening that had helped cost him his world title two years before, but Polgar slowly widens her edge in a queenless endgame and finally hunts down Black's king with two rooks. It was played in a lively event where a Russian team and a Rest of the World team met with ten players each.","c":{"0":"9 September 2002, Moscow, Russia. This is round 5 of the team event “Russia vs the Rest of the World”, in which ten Russian players faced ten top players from other countries. It was a rapid game: 25 minutes per player, with 10 seconds added for every move. White is Judit Polgar of the World team, aged 26. Black is Garry Kasparov of Russia, the world number one. Polgar had played Kasparov many times before, but she had never beaten him.","1":"White plays e4: the most common first move, taking the centre. Polgar is the youngest of three sisters; their father László believed that “geniuses can be raised” and taught them chess from early childhood.","2":"Black plays e5, an old reply that meets e4 head-on. Kasparov became world champion in 1985 at the age of 22 and kept the title for many years.","3":"The knight goes to f3 and aims at the e5 pawn. It is one of the oldest move orders in chess.","4":"Black plays ♘c6. The knight guards e5. This position has been played for hundreds of years.","5":"White plays ♗b5. The bishop hits the c6 knight, the defender of e5. This is the “Ruy Lopez” (Spanish Game), named after Ruy López, a 16th-century Spanish priest.","6":"Black plays ♘f6: the “Berlin Defence”. The name is said to come from Berlin players of the 19th century who studied it. For a long time it was rarely played, but at the 2000 World Championship Kramnik used it, and Kasparov lost his title without winning a single game.","7":"White castles. Instead of guarding the e4 pawn, she first makes her king safe. Even if e4 is taken, White is ready to win it back.","8":"Black plays ♘xe4 and takes the pawn. The setup nicknamed the “Berlin Wall” had tormented Kasparov two years earlier; now he steps into it himself with Black.","9":"White plays d4. She opens the centre to attack while Black's king is still on e8. In this Ruy Lopez line, it is White's most popular choice.","10":"Black plays ♘d6. The knight drops back and hits the b5 bishop. From d6 it watches both c6 and e5.","11":"White plays ♗xc6, trading bishop for knight. White chooses to damage Black's pawn structure.","12":"Black plays dxc6. Recapturing with the d-pawn opens lines for Black's queen and bishop. In exchange, Black now has two pawns stacked on the c-file: “doubled pawns”.","13":"White plays dxe5, taking a pawn and hitting the knight. Next the queens come off: that is the Berlin main line.","14":"Black plays ♘f5. The knight moves to a good square. There is no hurry to win back the e5 pawn.","15":"White plays ♕xd8+. She trades queens with check. With the queens gone, the game turns into something like an endgame very early. The Berlin main line usually passes through this position.","16":"Black plays ♔xd8, recapturing with the king. Black can no longer castle, but with the queens gone his king is rarely in danger, and he has the two bishops.","17":"White plays ♘c3. From here the position is called the “Berlin Endgame”: White plays with her extra kingside pawns, Black with his two bishops.","18":"Black plays h6, a standard move that keeps White's knight and bishop off g5. Black's king will look for a safe home on c8 or e8.","19":"White plays ♖d1+. The rook gives check and takes the open d-file. Black's king must decide where to go.","20":"Black plays ♔e8. The king goes back to e8. Going to c8 is also possible, but here Black chooses to guard the kingside.","21":"White plays h3, stopping Black's bishop from coming to g4 and trading for the f3 knight. This event took place in 2002; in June of that year Japan and South Korea had hosted the football World Cup.","22":"Black plays ♗e7. The bishop comes out and makes the king's area solid. Black is thinking about how to use his two bishops.","23":"White plays ♘e2. The knight swings round to e2, aiming at Black's camp from d4 or f4. It is on its way to a good central square.","24":"Black plays ♘h4, offering to trade knights. Without White's strong knight, Black's bishops will have an easier time.","25":"White plays ♘xh4, trading knights. White fights on with her other knight.","26":"Black plays ♗xh4, recapturing with the bishop. Black keeps his two bishops.","27":"White plays ♗e3. The bishop comes out and eyes squares like a7 and c5. White's pieces are joining the game one by one.","28":"Black plays ♗f5. The other bishop also comes out and aims at the c2 pawn. Black's two bishops are working on both sides of the board.","29":"White plays ♘d4. The knight enters the centre and hits the f5 bishop. From here it looks for gaps in Black's pawns.","30":"Black plays ♗h7. The bishop drops back to avoid a trade. It keeps aiming at c2 along the long diagonal.","31":"White plays g4! The pawn advance narrows the escape route of the h4 bishop. If ♔g2 and f4 follow, that bishop could end up with nothing to do.","32":"Black plays ♗e7, bringing the bishop back before it gets shut in. White has gained space on the kingside.","33":"White plays ♔g2. The king steps toward the centre. In an endgame without queens, the king becomes an important fighting piece.","34":"Black plays h5. He strikes with a pawn to shake White's kingside. Black wants to open the h-file for his rook.","35":"White plays ♘f5! The knight lands on the fine f5 square. It eyes the g7 pawn and gets in the way of Black's bishops.","36":"Black plays ♗f8. The bishop drops back to guard g7. Black's pieces are slowly being pushed back.","37":"White plays ♔f3. The king goes further forward and supports the g4 pawn. A king can walk forward like this only because the queens are gone.","38":"Black plays ♗g6. The h7 bishop comes out and shows the idea of trading itself for the f5 knight.","39":"White plays ♖d2. The rook goes to the second rank to prepare to connect the two rooks. White is not in a hurry; she organizes her pieces.","40":"Black plays hxg4+. He trades pawns with check. The h-file opens and Black's rook has a road.","41":"White plays hxg4, recapturing with a pawn. The h-file is now a line Black's rook can use.","42":"Black plays ♖h3+. The rook enters along the h-file with check. Black activates his pieces and looks for counterplay.","44":"Black plays ♖h7. The rook goes back and keeps the h-file. Black sees that just giving checks would gain nothing.","45":"White plays ♔g3. The king stands in front of the rook and blocks the h-file. White's king also works as a defender.","46":"Black plays f6. The pawn attacks e5, going after White's central pawn. Black changes the structure himself to open up the position.","47":"White plays ♗f4. The bishop defends the e5 pawn. White's pieces now all support one another.","48":"Black plays ♗xf5. The bishop takes the knight. Black chooses to get rid of White's strong knight.","49":"White plays gxf5, recapturing with a pawn. The f5 pawn also blocks the road of Black's bishop.","50":"Black plays fxe5 and wins the pawn. By count Black has caught up, but White has a strong move ready.","51":"White plays ♖e1! The rook goes to the e-file and aims at the e5 pawn and the king on e8 behind it. Because of his king, Black's pawn is stuck.","53":"White plays ♗xe5. The bishop wins back the pawn. White's rook on the e-file stares at Black's king on e8.","54":"Black plays ♔d7. The king leaves the e-file and tries to connect the rooks.","55":"White plays c4! The pawn advances, stops Black's c5 from moving and also controls d5. From here White begins a plan to win pawns.","57":"White plays ♗xd6, trading bishops. Black must recapture with the c-pawn, and that pawn will be a target for White's rooks on d6.","59":"White plays ♖e6. The rook attacks the d6 pawn a second time. Black cannot hold this pawn.","61":"White plays ♖exd6+, taking the pawn with check. White is now a pawn up, and her two rooks are working close to Black's king.","63":"White plays ♖2d5. She stacks both rooks on the d-file and looks for a way into Black's camp. Black hits back with checks against White's king.","67":"White plays ♔f3. Dodging the checks, the king steps forward little by little. As the king advances, Black's rook checks will run out.","69":"White plays ♔e4! The king escapes the rook checks and heads to the centre. With the king forward, Black's checks soon come to an end.","71":"White plays ♖c6+. The rook checks from the c-file and drives Black's king toward the corner.","73":"White plays ♖d7. A rook reaches the 7th rank. A rook on the 7th rank is a strong piece that can take pawns from the side and shut in the king.","75":"White plays ♔e3. The king steps back to avoid Black's rook checks. White puts her king somewhere safe and attacks with her rooks.","77":"White plays ♖cc7. Both rooks now stand on the 7th rank. This setup is nicknamed “pigs on the seventh” and is known as a very strong formation.","78":"Black plays ♖xf5. He wins back a pawn, and the pawns are now equal. But White's two rooks are closing in around Black's king.","79":"White plays ♖b7+. The rook gives check and makes Black's king move. With two rooks, White shuts Black's king in on the back rank.","81":"White plays ♖dc7+. The other rook also gives check and drives Black's king to d8.","83":"White plays ♖xg7. She takes a pawn, and the rook arrives on g7. If White plays ♖g8+ next, Black is all but mated.","84":"Black plays ♔c8, and here Kasparov resigned. White is threatening mate starting with ♖g8+, taking the a7 pawn and more, and Black cannot stop everything. Polgar, from Hungary, had become, at 15 years and 4 months, the youngest person ever to earn the grandmaster title up to that time, beating Fischer's record. Here, for the first time, she defeated the world's best player. The match as a whole was won by the Rest of the World, 52 to 48."}},"yang1957":{"t":"Yang Guanlin's Houji Game","w":"Yan Zongjin","b":"Yang Guanlin","ev":"2nd National Xiangqi Individual Championship","d":"9 November 1957","pl":"Shanghai","lead":"Yang Guanlin, who had become the first national champion the year before, plays Black and uses the Houji Game, a plan born in the teahouses of Guangzhou. He pulls his central cannon back on purpose, chases Red's chariots around the board with it, and wins in 25 moves. Yang won his second national title at this tournament, and it is said that the name Houji Game became widely known after it.","c":{"0":"This game comes from the 2nd National Xiangqi Individual Championship, held in Shanghai in 1957. Red is Yan Zongjin from Urumqi in Xinjiang; Black is Yang Guanlin of Guangdong, winner of the first championship the year before. Yang was born in Dongguan in 1925; it is said he was called the village chess king at ten and played as a professional in Hong Kong from 1949 to 1951. In October of this year the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, and the space age had just begun. Playing Black, Yang uses the Houji Game, a plan born in Guangzhou's teahouses.","1":"Red moves the right cannon to the middle. The Central Cannon aims straight at Black's general and is the most basic attacking start in xiangqi.","2":"Black also brings the cannon on the same side to the middle, meeting the attack head-on. When both sides set up central cannons in the same direction it is called Same Direction Cannons. It is a very old way to fight; it already appears in the game record of the Song-era Shilin Guangji.","3":"Red jumps a horse out to guard the middle soldier. This is the natural, standard move in Same Direction Cannons.","4":"Black also jumps a horse to guard the middle soldier. For a while the two sides mirror each other.","5":"Red moves the right chariot one step sideways. It prepares a Straight Chariot that will charge forward along the file.","6":"Black lifts the chariot one step: a Horizontal Chariot, which can swing to either side. This setup is called Same Direction Cannons, Straight Chariot against Horizontal Chariot, a classic line studied for centuries.","7":"Red raises an advisor early to firm up the palace. It is a calm move that settles the defence before Black's horizontal chariot can attack. Later, this line was given the catalogue name Red Raises the Advisor First.","8":"Black swings the horizontal chariot to the file next to the advisors. It eyes the side of Red's palace, the usual job of the horizontal chariot in this opening.","9":"Red sends the chariot across the river in one go. It presses in front of Black's horse and aims at a soldier: an aggressive move.","10":"Black jumps out the other horse, balancing the defence on both sides.","11":"Red jumps the left horse to the edge of the board, an Edge Horse. The idea is to clear the path for the cannon.","12":"Black pushes a soldier one step. Later this soldier will cross the river and become a foothold against Red's camp.","13":"Red's chariot takes a black soldier. It wins material and also stares at Black's horse. But going so deep into the enemy camp will later make the chariot a target.","14":"Black pulls the central cannon back one step. Retreating the central cannon on purpose is unusual, but it is the key idea of the Houji Game. The retreated cannon can now slide sideways freely and chase the red chariot that has come in so deep.","15":"Red swings the left cannon across to aim at Black's right side. It is said that moving the cannon one file further, to block the exit of Black's horse, was also possible.","16":"Black moves the chariot one step forward. A quiet move, but a subtle one: the chariot stands higher and is ready for whatever Red's cannon and chariot do.","17":"Red develops the left chariot. It looks natural, but it leads to the chariot going deep and being chased by Black's cannons. Some think it was better to wait a move by shifting the right chariot sideways.","18":"Black lifts the edge chariot two steps. This is the move that gives the Houji Game its name. Old manuals protected the cannon by moving the chariot sideways; here the chariot is raised high to guard it instead. It is said the name comes from a Guangzhou teahouse called Houji, where the line was popular. In those days, the teahouses of Guangzhou were where xiangqi players tested their strength.","19":"Red pushes the chariot right up to Black's camp. It is a lively move, but it is said that keeping it one row back, near the river, was safer.","20":"Black slides the cannon it retreated earlier sideways. Using its own horse as a screen, it attacks the red chariot.","21":"Red's chariot steps aside. It is said that stepping the other way would have stopped Black's horse from jumping out.","22":"Black's cannon slides to the other side. Using its other cannon as a screen, it now attacks Red's second chariot. The retreat of the central cannon is paying off.","23":"Red's chariot steps aside.","24":"Black swings the rear cannon across and, using a horse as a screen, attacks the red chariot yet again. The retreated cannon is running freely all over the board.","25":"Red's chariot returns to its old file. Each time it is chased by a cannon, Red loses time.","26":"Black's horse advances towards the river. While Red's chariot is being chased, Black brings more pieces forward.","27":"Red swings the right chariot across to watch Black's left side. The runner-up of this tournament was Wang Jialiang of Heilongjiang, who finished second behind Yang three times: in 1956, 1957 and 1959.","28":"Black's soldier crosses the river. From here Black's counterattack begins.","29":"Red's chariot drops back one step to watch Black's horse and soldier.","30":"Black raises an elephant to firm up the position, tidying the defence while preparing the next plan.","31":"Red's chariot moves in front of Black's horse to attack it. There were few good moves left, and this is a difficult one.","32":"Black brings the cannon back to the same file as the other cannon. Using the front cannon as a screen, it attacks Red's chariot. Red must decide whether to run or to trade.","33":"Red's chariot takes Black's cannon. Instead of running, Red chooses to trade.","34":"Black's chariot takes Red's chariot at once. Trading a chariot for a cannon is good business for Black.","35":"Red takes Black's horse with the chariot. Red has given a chariot for a cannon and a horse, so the material is roughly level. But now a black soldier will push deep into Red's camp.","36":"Black's soldier takes a red soldier and threatens Red's cannon. Once it has crossed the river, a soldier can also move sideways, so it becomes a nuisance in Red's camp.","37":"Red's cannon slides away.","38":"Black moves the cannon onto the same file as the soldier. Using the soldier as a screen, it aims deep into Red's camp: a tight, forceful move.","39":"Red's central cannon takes a soldier with check. In a hard position, Red looks for a way to hit back.","40":"Black raises an advisor to block the check.","41":"Red raises an elephant to strengthen the defence.","42":"Black's horse advances and attacks Red's central cannon.","43":"Red's horse takes Black's soldier. It is said that even if the central cannon had escaped, Black would have won another piece, so Red chose to get something back.","44":"Black's chariot dashes into Red's camp and attacks Red's horse and cannon at the same time.","45":"Red's horse runs to the edge.","46":"Black's horse takes Red's central cannon. Now Black is clearly ahead in material.","47":"Red's chariot drops back one step to attack Black's horse.","48":"Black's horse retreats and tidies the position. Black keeps the extra material and takes no risks.","49":"Red pushes a soldier, trying to find any material for a counterattack.","50":"Black's chariot goes to the very back of Red's camp. Next it can swing sideways to attack Red's cannon again. Here Red admitted defeat. Yang Guanlin became national champion for the second time at this tournament and won again in 1959 and 1962. Later, as coach of the Guangdong team, he was also known for raising champions such as Lü Qin and Xu Yinchuan."}},"alphago2016":{"t":"The Divine Move — Lee Sedol Strikes Back at AlphaGo","w":"AlphaGo (DeepMind's program)","b":"Lee Sedol 9-dan","ev":"Google DeepMind Challenge Match, Game 4 (best of five)","d":"13 March 2016","pl":"South Korea (Seoul, Four Seasons Hotel)","lead":"Game 4 of the five-game match between Lee Sedol, one of the strongest players in the world, and the go AI AlphaGo. After three straight losses the match was already lost, but with White 78, a wedge in the centre, Lee broke through from outside the AI's reading and won by resignation in 180 moves. It is known as the only game a human won against AlphaGo in an official match.","c":{"0":"Seoul, South Korea, 13 March 2016. Black is AlphaGo, White is Lee Sedol 9-dan. AlphaGo is a go program made by DeepMind, a British company bought by Google in 2014; it learned from human game records and then grew stronger by playing millions of games against itself. Lee Sedol had won 18 international titles and was one of the very best players in the world. The match began on 9 March, and after three straight losses Lee had already lost it the day before. Even so, the whole world was watching this fourth game. The rules are Chinese, komi (the handicap Black gives White for moving first) is 7.5 points, and each side has 2 hours plus three 60-second byo-yomi periods. Watch for move 78, where a human strikes back against the AI.","1":"Black 1 (Q16) is the star point in the upper right. The star point, where the fourth lines from the edges cross, takes a light hold on the corner in one move and lets Black head for the wide areas quickly. AlphaGo first learned from strong players' records a feeling for \"where a human would play,\" then polished it by playing against itself. In January 2016 a paper describing this method appeared in the science journal Nature and caused a great stir.","2":"White 2 (D4) is the star point in the lower left. Lee Sedol was born in 1983 on Bigeumdo, a small island in south-western Korea. He turned professional in 1995 at the age of 12, and later came to be called \"Ssen-dol,\" the Strong Stone. Fast reading and stubborn fighting were his trademarks.","3":"Black 3 (C16) is the 3-4 point (komoku) in the upper left, where the third and fourth lines cross. It takes corner territory more easily than the star point. AlphaGo did not just imitate human moves; it also had a part that estimated \"who is likely to win,\" and that was what set it apart from earlier go programs. In October 2015 it had beaten the European champion Fan Hui 2-dan 5–0.","4":"White 4 (R4) is the 3-4 point in the lower right. White has taken both corners along the bottom. Before the match, Lee is said to have predicted a landslide win over AlphaGo, but he lost the first three games, and astonishment spread around the world. These first four moves are a perfectly ordinary start, still common today.","5":"Black 5 (P4) approaches the 3-4 stone at R4 high, one space away: the \"one-space high approach.\" It stops White from spreading from the lower-right corner along the bottom. Compared with the low approach (P3), it faces the centre more easily and is harder to pincer later along the bottom.","6":"White 6 (P3) attaches under the approaching stone at P4. Attaching underneath a one-space high approach to guard the corner territory is the start of an old, well-known joseki. The person placing AlphaGo's stones on the board was Aja Huang of DeepMind, a researcher from Taiwan and an amateur 6-dan, who played the AI's moves as they appeared on his screen.","7":"Black 7 (O3) is a hane. It holds down White's spread along the bottom, and Black plants the seed of a framework there. Earlier in this match, at move 37 of Game 2, AlphaGo played a shoulder hit on the fifth line that astonished the commentators. A move most humans would never consider was later praised as brilliant.","8":"White 8 (Q3) draws back. It connects P3 to the corner stone at R4 and secures the corner territory firmly. After Game 3, Lee is reported to have said, in effect, that it was Lee Sedol who lost, not humanity. With the match already decided, perhaps he could play this game with a lighter heart.","9":"Black 9 (C6) leaves the lower right and approaches White's D4 in the lower left, coming in a small knight's move from the side: the \"small knight approach.\" Black seems to judge that the stones at O3 and P4 are already light and cannot be attacked at once. On the English broadcast, the commentator was Michael Redmond 9-dan, an American-born professional of the Nihon Ki-in.","10":"White 10 (F3) answers with a small knight's move from the star point and guards the corner. Before Black can pincer, White holds the bottom side and makes the corner firm. Under Chinese rules, you count your stones on the board together with the territory you surround. The komi is 7.5, with a half point, so that there can be no draws.","11":"Black 11 (N4) firms up the stones at O3 and P4 in the lower right while spreading one step along the bottom. Black comes back to the corner it had left and puts its shape in order. For this match, AlphaGo ran on many connected computers, reported to use more than a thousand processors (CPUs).","12":"White 12 (Q5) is a diagonal step out from R4. It strengthens White's lower-right stones toward the centre and blocks Black P4 from moving up the right side. Inside AlphaGo there was one part that narrowed down candidate moves and another that looked at a position and scored how likely it was to win; it read ahead using the two together.","13":"Black 13 (J3) is a wide extension along the bottom. It gives Black's lower-right stones a base (footing for life) and aims to make the bottom Black's area. There is still a wide gap to White's F3 in the lower left, and whether White will invade there becomes a point to watch later.","14":"White 14 (E17) is a small knight approach from above to Black's 3-4 stone in the upper left. White touches the last big corner left. After this match, the Korea Baduk Association awarded AlphaGo an \"honorary 9-dan.\" Giving a professional rank to a program was something very unusual.","15":"Black 15 (H16) pincers the approaching White E17 along the top: a two-space high pincer. By pincering instead of answering, Black forces White to choose between going into the corner and heading out. Pincers have long been a common joseki choice for humans too. In Korea go is called \"baduk,\" and this match was shown on television and on big screens in the streets.","16":"White 16 (C13) leaves E17 for now and comes close to Black's corner once more, from the left side. Rather than rescue E17 and fight along the top, White chooses to build on the left side. From here E17 gets surrounded by Black and plays something close to the role of a sacrifice.","17":"Black 17 (E16) attaches right under White E17. It blocks E17's way out to the centre and at the same time strengthens Black's upper-left corner. Black starts to gather the top and the upper left into one large sphere of influence.","18":"White 18 (C10) is a wide extension down the left side from C13. It builds an area between C13 and the lower-left stone at D4 and gives White's left-side stones a base. White's plan is to let Black have the top in exchange for making the whole left side its own.","19":"Black 19 (D17) blocks White E17 from the left and makes the corner firm. E17 can now hardly move either outward or into the corner. Black secures the upper-left corner territory, and White keeps sente (the right to play wherever it likes next) and moves on.","20":"White 20 (B4) slides in under the lower-left corner. It firms up the corner territory from beneath Black C6 and denies C6 a base. White's left-side stones at C10 and C13 link up with the corner, and White's territory takes shape on the left.","21":"Black 21 (O17) is a small knight's enclosure from the upper-right star point. It firms up the upper-right corner and at the same time spreads toward Black's H16 on the top. Black has turned the whole top into a big moyo (a wide framework not yet fully enclosed).","22":"White 22 (R11) goes to the middle of the right side. It spreads upward from White's lower-right group (R4, Q3, P3, Q5) and stops Black's upper right from extending its framework down the right side. Against Black's moyo on top, White now has footholds on both the right and left sides.","23":"Black 23 (E4) attaches in the lower left, next to White's D4. It is not a move to live at once but a kind of probe, touching White's shape to see how White will answer. E4 later gets surrounded by White, but it stays on the board as a foothold for things Black aims at in the lower left.","25":"Black 25 (D9) presses in just below White's C10 on the left side. It cuts in between White's lower-left stones and the left-side stones at C10 and C13, trying to make White's left-side area smaller. At the same time it tries to link up with Black's C6.","28":"White 28 (D10) supports White's C10 from the side. It keeps C10 from becoming weak under pressure from Black's C9 and D9. On the left side the roles become clear: White connects its stones and guards its area, while Black lines up stones on the outside, facing the centre.","31":"Black 31 (F11) blocks the head of White's E11. White extends upward along the left side, and Black builds a wall outside it. The likely result: the left side becomes White's territory and the outside becomes Black's influence.","34":"White 34 (B10) firmly reinforces White's left side. Without it, Black could get in around B10 and threaten the eyes (living space) of White's left-side group. It is a quiet move, but an important one so that White need not worry about the left side in the fighting to come.","37":"Black 37 (G13) extends Black's wall from F12 upward. Black's stones now stand in a long line outside the left side, from F9 to G14, facing the centre. Combined with the framework on top, it is clear that Black is trying to build an enormous area from the upper half of the board into the centre.","40":"White 40 (N17) attaches to Black's O17 and goes into Black's huge framework on top. If Black were allowed to enclose the top as it is, Black's territory would grow too large. By touching Black's stone, White tries to build shape out of the exchanges that follow.","42":"White 42 (M17) extends to the left after Black blocks at N16. Stretching sideways on the third line of the top, White looks for a place to live while chipping away at Black's area. Black keeps White low from above and thickens the outside.","44":"White 44 (J16) jumps right next to Black's H16 on top to gain width for life. With M17, N17 and J16, White has made a small base on the top side. But its eyes are not yet secure, and it remains a target for Black's attack.","46":"White 46 (K13) steps lightly out from the top group into the centre. Rather than living small on the top, White pokes its head out and plants a foothold in the very middle of Black's framework. The plan is to shrink Black's big area from the inside, but White's stones are also thinly scattered, which is risky.","47":"Black 47 (Q10) presses on White's R11 on the right side from the inside. R11 is not yet clearly connected to White's lower-right stones, and Black attacks it to widen its central framework further. Building your own area while attacking is a basic idea of strong go.","49":"Black 49 (P10) extends left after White blocks at Q11. With Q10 and P10 in a row toward the centre, Black tries to separate White's right-side stones from whatever White builds in the centre. White lines up above with P11 and ends up being pushed along.","53":"Black 53 (N12) blocks to the left of White O12's head. White cannot get past Black's O11 and is pushed further and further up. The flow is that Black lines up stones toward the centre on the outside, while White forms a long, thin column toward the upper right.","56":"White 56 (N11) cuts into the gap between Black's O11 and N12. Just being pushed would make Black's centre thick, so White gets a hand into the seam of Black's stones to create weaknesses for Black too. N11 will be put in atari by Black M11, but White has that in its plans.","58":"White 58 (N14) is a hane on the head of Black's N13. It gives White's column on the upper right (O12 to O15) a way to show its face toward the centre and puts a crack into Black's central framework. Playing the important spot first even while N11 is about to be captured is a bold choice typical of Lee Sedol.","60":"White 60 (O15) is a forcing move played before rescuing N11 from atari. After drawing Black's answer at O16, White runs N11 out with White 62 (N10). White's central stones become two pillars, N9 to N11 and O12 to O15, standing solidly inside Black's framework.","63":"Black 63 (M14) blocks to the left of White's N14 and links Black's central wall. With M11, N12, N13 and M14, Black has closely sealed the left side of White's two pillars. The whole wide centre to the left of here looks set to become Black's territory.","64":"White 64 (N9) extends White's central pillar further down. If Black sealed the bottom side, White's central stones would have no room to make eyes. By stretching down, White tries to secure a link with the lower right and an escape route toward the bottom.","68":"White 68 (R10) firmly guards White's R11 on the right side from below. Black's O10, O11, P10 and Q10 are squeezed between White's right side and White's centre, and with R10 played, these black stones find it hard to get out to the right side. What happens to these stones will matter a great deal later.","69":"Black 69 (L9) closes off the lower left of White's central pillar. It stops White from heading out into the wide centre on the left and protects Black's huge framework. Around this point many commentators judged that Black was clearly ahead. Black's centre, soon joined by K11 and L9 and others, is turning into a very large area.","70":"White 70 (J9) jumps in beyond Black's L9. It puts a stone on the lower rim of Black's framework, a foothold for wrecking it from within. With K13 and J9, White now has stones above and below Black's framework and is watching for a chance to split the middle.","72":"White 72 (G12) touches a seam in Black's wall outside the left side (F12 and G13). It is not meant to cut at once but to prepare a flaw in Black's wall. White then also touches the upper left with White 74 (G15) and White 76 (F16), scattering sparks all around the rim of Black's framework.","73":"Black 73 (H10) firms up the lower left of the central framework. Black holds the space between White's J9 and G12 firmly and thickens the fence of its framework. Black's centre is now a large space surrounded by K11, L9, H10 and other stones. To win, White must somehow break in here.","77":"Black 77 (F17) puts White E17 on top in atari and cuts it off from White's F16. Black has guarded the upper left firmly. Looking at the board now, Black's huge central framework is untouched, and White seems to have no decisive move. Lee Sedol is said to have thought for a long time over his next move.","78":"White 78 (L11). A wedge played between Black's K11 and M11, this is the move that made the game famous. L11 is surrounded by black stones and at first sight looks easy to capture, but however Black answers, a cut remains somewhere in the fence around Black's centre. Black's huge framework starts to crumble from this single point. China's Gu Li 9-dan praised it as a \"divine move,\" and it later came to be called that in Korea and Japan as well. AlphaGo is thought not to have expected this move.","79":"Black 79 (K10) supports Black's K11 from below. But this was AlphaGo's big mistake. Later analysis says that if Black had answered around L10, it could have kept the centre almost intact. According to DeepMind, AlphaGo still rated its winning chances at about 70 percent at this point and had not yet noticed the danger. After K10, the sequence starting with White M10 brings the cuts in Black's fence out into the open one after another.","80":"White 80 (M10) touches the underside of Black's M11 and reduces the liberties (adjacent empty points) of Black's central stones. Before L11 can be captured, White adds weaknesses to the black stones around it. Black's central stones also start to be surrounded by White stones linked to N10 and N9.","82":"White 82 (K12) plays to the left of Black's L12 and cuts Black's K11 and K10 off from the black stones above. Even if L11 is captured, White's K12 and K13 stay inside Black's fence. Black's central territory has now been split into several pieces from the inside.","83":"Black 83 (N8) goes round to the underside of White's central pillar to attack it. Black wants to win back what it lost in the centre by attacking White's large group. But White links down with O9 and P9, heading for a connection with the lower right.","85":"Black 85 (P8) adds another stone under White's pillar to obstruct White's connection. Black also hopes to rescue its own stones on the right, such as O10 and P10, from below. It is a difficult scene in which both sides' cutting and connecting are tangled together.","87":"Black 87 (Q9). According to DeepMind, it was around this move that AlphaGo finally noticed its estimated chances of winning had suddenly dropped. Black pushes right with Q9 and R9 (Black 89), trying to lead O10, P10 and the other black stones out to the right side, but White's R10 and R11 block the way and it does not work. AlphaGo's moves from here to move 101 were seen by many professionals as \"very bad moves,\" and became a talking point about the AI's weakness. When its position turned bad, it could play what looked like desperate moves.","90":"White 90 (O8) connects White's pillar solidly at the bottom. White holds the point between Black's N8 and P8, and White's large central group, now spread widely downward, is almost out of danger. White has wrecked the centre and made its own stones safe as well.","91":"With Black 91 (L10), Black finally captures White's L11. But in the meantime White has already played key points such as M10, K12 and O8, and Black's centre can no longer become big territory. By letting one stone be captured, White gains many times as much. The wedge at White 78 was exactly that kind of stone.","92":"White 92 (J11) plays to the left of Black's K11 and links White's central stones. White's K12, K13, J11 and J9 come together and split Black's central framework clean in two. Black's stones at K10, K11 and L10 are now surrounded by White and must look for a place to live.","93":"Black 93 (S9) crawls further right along the right side. Black wants to lead the row of stones from O10 to R9 out to the right side, but White, on the side of R10 and S10, holds their heads, and Black can only advance along the first and second lines: a painful shape. It was said to be a move a human professional would hardly choose.","94":"White 94 (P7) captures Black's P8. The links among White's stones from the centre down toward the lower right are now much stronger. Black is doing well neither with its right-side stones nor with its central territory.","95":"Black 95 (Q13) plays in the upper right beside White's large central group. Black tries to win back what it lost in the centre by attacking White from the right side toward the top. But White's stones are already thickly connected, and it is not decisive.","97":"Black 97 (C4) plays inside White's lower-left corner. Between White's B4 and D4, it is a stone that will clearly be captured, and it left the commentators puzzled. It was later understood that when AlphaGo judged its position bad, it could play moves with almost no prospects. Strong when ahead, it became erratic when it had to chase: this was a weakness of AlphaGo at the time, revealed in this game.","99":"Black 99 (P15) is a hane on the head of White's O15 in the upper right. It blocks the top of White's big central group and tries to reduce White's eye space even a little. White's big group is widely connected and will not die at once, but Black keeps aiming at it to the very end.","101":"Black 101 (T9) crawls along the first line on the right side. It probably meant to extend the liberties of the stones connected to R9 and S9, but crawling on the first line makes no eyes, and White's S10 and R10 still hold them down from above. AlphaGo's run of \"bad moves\" is generally seen as lasting until about here. These eight black stones on the right will all be captured at once by White 176 (T10) near the end.","104":"White 104 (J10) closes off the left of Black's central stones at K10, K11 and L10. With J9, J10 and J11 in a vertical line, White firmly shuts these black stones' way out. White takes advantage of the opponent's poor moves and plays the sure points one by one.","106":"White 106 (G11) plays on the inner side of Black's wall outside the left side (F11, F12). It connects to White 72 (G12) and cuts the link between Black's wall and Black's centre. The wall on the left that was once Black's pride is now being eaten into by White from the inside.","108":"White 108 (K8) moves into the upper side of Black's area along the bottom. Having split the centre, White pushes further down to make Black's bottom territory (around J3 and N4) smaller too. Black answers with L7 and L8 and is on the defensive here as well.","112":"White 112 (G7) links White's stones from the lower left toward the centre. Extending down with G8 and G7, White closes the distance to its lower-left stones (E5, F4 and others). Black's outside stones, on the contrary, are getting squeezed between White's stones.","115":"Black 115 (E18) captures White's E17 in the upper left. The stone that Black put in atari at move 77 is finally taken here. Black tries to catch up by piling up small gains, but the gap lost in the centre is still large.","118":"White 118 (E13) puts White's shape on the left side in order. Black has come into the upper part of the left side with C12, D13 and others, so White connects its stones to protect its left-side territory and the safety of its group. Leading now, White answers each of the opponent's moves carefully without forcing anything.","122":"White 122 (D12) connects the middle of White's left-side group firmly. Even under pressure from Black's D13 and D14 above, White holds everything from C10 to E13 together and removes any worry about eyes. It is a plain move, but erasing weak points one at a time like this is how the side that is winning plays.","126":"White 126 (J14) connects between White's J16 on the top and White's K13 in the centre. The white stones that went into the top are also becoming safe by linking up with the centre. Black's framework on top has hardly turned into any territory because of White's invasion starting at move 40.","130":"White 130 (J12) connects White's central stones even more thickly. With J11, J12, K12 and K13, White has raised a sturdy pillar right in the middle of what used to be Black's framework. Almost nothing remains of the big black territory that should have been in the centre.","133":"Black 133 (H5) plays on the left part of the bottom, trying to attack White's lower-left stones from below. White's stones at E5 and F5 do not yet have clear eyes. Black wants to make a big gain here and close the remaining gap even a little.","138":"White 138 (C3) captures Black's C4, the stone that went in with Black 97. White's lower-left corner is now completely safe. In the lower left Black has built a wall outside White with B6, C6, D6 and E6, but there is not much room left for that wall to enclose.","141":"Black 141 (S14) firms up the border between the upper-right corner and the right side. White's S13 has come up the right side, so Black protects its upper-right corner territory. From around here the game enters the yose (endgame, where both sides settle the borders of their territories).","144":"White 144 (K17) tidies up the shape of White's stones on the top. White's top stones now stand side by side at J16, K17, M17 and N17, leaving Black less room to attack them. It looks as if none of the white stones that went into Black's framework in the middle game will be captured.","150":"White 150 (B8) is an endgame move that slides in under Black's stones on the lower part of the left side. It enlarges White's territory on the left while threatening Black's stones around C9 and D9. Even while pressed by byo-yomi, Lee Sedol kept steadily piling up small gains.","156":"White 156 (H4) goes into Black's area along the bottom. With stones at H6 and H4, White reduces Black's bottom territory further. The area Black built along the bottom with J3 and N4 also turned out smaller than hoped.","163":"Black 163 (L16) presses on the underside of White's top stones to attack them once more. White's top group still seems to have only one clear eye, so Black stakes the game on it. But White calmly answers with J15, L18 and J18 and makes a living shape.","166":"White 166 (L18) makes an eye for White's top group. Answering Black's L17 with L18, White secures the makings of a second eye on the top. White's invasion of the top that began at move 40 is now almost sure to live to the end.","169":"Black 169 (R12) cuts in between White's stones on the right side. Black tries to break the link between White's R11 and Q11 and the white stones above at R13 and S13. White answers with S12 and keeps the connection.","175":"Black 175 (P14) squeezes White's big central group (O12 to O15 and N14) until only one liberty, P13, is left. Black judged that capturing this group would turn the game around and went for one last fight. But on the right side, a big capture was still waiting for White.","176":"White 176 (T10) captures the eight black stones on the right side (O10, O11, P10, Q10, Q9, R9, S9 and T9) all at once. The stones Black had been trying to lead out to the right since move 87 have all vanished from the board. This capture also empties O11 and other points, giving White's big central group liberties again, so the aim of Black 175 disappears too. With one move White gets both safety and a big gain: the move that decides the game.","178":"White 178 (O10) captures Black's O11 (Black 177), which had been played into an emptied point after the capture. Black aimed at White's big group to the very end, but White calmly took back one stone at a time and made sure the group was safe. Black has no more chances left to turn the game around.","180":"With White 180 (K4), AlphaGo resigned. White wins by resignation. It is reported that AlphaGo was set to resign once its estimated chance of winning fell below 20 percent. From the wedge at White 78, Black's huge central framework crumbled, and AlphaGo went on to pile up bad moves. The hall burst into applause, and Lee is said to have called it, in effect, a priceless win he would not trade for anything. The match ended 1–4, but this was the first and last time a human beat AlphaGo in an official game. In 2019 Lee Sedol retired, leaving words to the effect that there is now an entity, AI, that cannot be defeated."}},"maruoka1978":{"t":"Maruoka, 16, wins the final 64–0","w":"Hidenori Maruoka","b":"Carol Jacobs","ev":"2nd World Championship, final, game 2","d":"October 1978","pl":"USA (New York)","lead":"The 2nd World Reversi Championship was held in New York in 1978. The two finalists were Hidenori Maruoka of Japan, said to be 16 years old, and Carol Jacobs of the USA. Maruoka had won the first game 50–14, and in this second game he turned over every one of his opponent's discs to win 64–0. It is said to be the only wipe-out ever played in a World Championship final. Follow it move by move, from the openings \"Cow\" and \"Ben-Hur\" to the moment White runs out of places to play.","c":{"0":"October 1978, New York, USA: the final of the 2nd World Reversi Championship. Nine players came, one from each of nine countries, and the last two standing were Hidenori Maruoka of Japan and Carol Jacobs of the USA. Maruoka is said to have been 16 years old, and he had already won the first game of the final 50–14 playing White. In reversi you flip your opponent's discs by trapping them between two of your own, and whoever has more discs at the end wins. This second game would set a record that still stands out in the championship's history.","1":"Black starts with f5. The four first moves are all the same shape once the board is turned or mirrored, so records are written starting from f5 by custom. The game is said to come from \"Reversi\", played in England at the end of the 19th century. Today's rule of starting with four discs in the centre spread around the world from Japan in the 1970s.","2":"White plays f6, taking on the diagonal from Black's disc, so it is called the \"diagonal opening\". The other choices are d6 (perpendicular) and f4 (parallel), and each one leads to a different family of openings. A year earlier, at the 1st World Championship in Tokyo in 1977, Hiroshi Inoue of Japan beat Thomas Heiberg of Norway to win.","3":"Black plays e6, flipping e5. It is the most common answer to the diagonal opening. Black gathers discs in the centre and avoids giving White new places to play. The event was organised by the American company Anjar, and the tournament director was James Becker.","4":"White plays f4, flipping e4 and f5. White lines up discs on the right and looks at where Black can play next. That year national qualifiers were held in 14 countries, and in Sweden more than 300 people took part in five qualifying events.","5":"Black plays e3, flipping e4. The five moves so far make the opening called the \"Cow\". It is one of the most played shapes of the diagonal opening, and many lines branch off from here. The preliminary round had three groups of three, with only two games each, and players were ranked not by wins but by the total number of discs they won.","6":"White plays c5, flipping d5 and e5. White takes the middle row and gets a foothold on the left. Black now has to choose from which side to trap the white discs in the centre. In the preliminaries Maruoka won 112 discs over his two games and went through in first place, far ahead of the rest.","7":"Black plays c4, flipping d4 and d5. This line is called the \"Rose-v-Toth\", one of the main branches of the Cow. Black builds on the left and keeps White from spreading there. In the Swedish qualifiers no clocks were used: each game lasted 12 minutes, and when time was up the discs were simply counted.","8":"White plays d3, flipping only e4. This shape is named \"Ben-Hur\". It is a quiet move that flips little and gives Black few new places to play. In game 1 of the final Jacobs had Black and Maruoka White, and Maruoka won 50–14, so he came into this game one win ahead.","9":"Black plays f3, flipping e4. Continuing the Ben-Hur with f3 and e2 was a popular line at the World Championships of this time. The records show that game 1 of this final and game 1 of the next year's final, in 1979, began with the same ten moves. From here the players start to go their own ways.","10":"White plays e2, flipping e3 and e4. White moves inside Black's discs at the top right and spreads upward. White still has ten places to play and is free to choose. 1978 was also the year the video game \"Space Invaders\" became a huge craze in Japan.","11":"Black plays f2, flipping e3. Black now has a column on the f-file that closes White's way out to the right. Black has 14 places to play at this point and plenty of room. Three years later, in 1981 in Brussels, Maruoka would win the World Championship again.","12":"White plays d2, flipping e3. White firms up the top of the centre with d2, e2, d3 and e3. But the more white discs sit on the outside, the easier it is for Black to find ways to trap them. The World Championship began in Tokyo in 1977 and from then on was held every year, in a different country each time.","13":"Black plays c3, flipping d3 and e3. A diagonal line of black discs runs from the upper left to the right. Black plays around the outside of White's discs and leaves White to choose where to go. For now the number of places each side can play is about even: 13 for Black, 12 for White.","14":"White plays d6 and flips four discs at once: e6, d5, d4 and d3. White now has 13 discs to Black's 5. But in the opening of reversi, having more discs is not necessarily good. With White's discs spread to the outside, Black's choices grow from here while White's slowly shrink. This is one of the turning points of the game.","15":"Black plays d1, entering the top edge and flipping e2. Discs on an edge are hard to trap from inside, so this becomes a base for Black. Black now sits on top of White's central wall, and White has fewer ways out toward the top.","16":"White plays b5, flipping c4. White looks for room on the left. White is down to ten places to play, and most of them are next to an edge or a corner, squares White would rather avoid.","17":"Black plays c1, flipping d2, so c1 and d1 stand side by side on the top edge. Black keeps playing quiet moves that flip one disc at a time. The fewer discs you flip, the fewer new places you give the opponent.","18":"White plays c2, flipping c3. White moves in just under Black's top edge. The second row is right inside the edge, and a disc there makes it easier for the opponent to extend along the edge.","19":"Black plays b3, flipping c3, d3 and c2. Black takes back the foothold White made with c2 right away. White is down to seven places to play, many of them near a corner.","20":"White plays e1, entering the top edge and flipping e2 and e3. White stands next to Black's c1 and d1, and the fight for the edge begins. It is one of the few safe squares White has left.","21":"Black plays f1, flipping e1 and e2, and joins the top edge in black from c1 to f1. Black traps White's edge disc from the side straight away. White once more has to search for a place to play.","22":"White plays a3, entering the left edge and flipping b3, c3 and d3 along the row. White looks for an escape on the left. But a lone disc on an edge can be trapped from above and below by Black.","23":"Black plays a6, entering the left edge and flipping b5, c4 and d3 on the diagonal. Black is now on the same edge as White's a3, and the left edge becomes a battleground. Black has ten places to play, White only six, and the gap is widening.","24":"White plays b4, flipping c4. White had only six places to play, and three of them, b1, g1 and g2, were dangerous squares next to a corner. White picks the relatively safe b4.","25":"Black plays a4, flipping b3, so a4 and a6 are black on the left edge. White's a3 is now easy to trap from above and below. Black wipes out White's places to play one at a time.","26":"White plays b6, flipping b5. White plays just inside the left edge and aims at a5, between Black's a4 and a6. Even so, almost all of White's remaining places are next to corners.","27":"Black plays a2, flipping a3. A2 is next to the a1 corner, usually a risky square that can hand the corner to the opponent. But White has no line of discs with which to play a1, and Black has seen that. On the left edge, a2, a3, a4 and a6 are now black.","28":"White plays g3, flipping f3. White had just five places to play, and b1, g1, b2 and g2 were all next to a corner. White chose the remaining g3, but the next turn will be even harder.","29":"Black plays a5, flipping b4 and c3, and turns the left edge black from a2 to a6. It takes a5, the square White was aiming for, first. Now White is left with only three places to play: b1, g1 and b2. All of them are right next to a corner, squares no one wants to play.","30":"White plays b2, flipping b3, b4 and c3. B2 sits diagonally next to the a1 corner; it is called an \"X-square\" and is usually one of the worst squares to play. All three of White's moves were next to a corner, so White had to pick one. Black's plan of squeezing White's choices is starting to pay off.","31":"Black plays h3, entering the right edge and flipping g3, f3 and e3. Black traps White's row on the right from the edge in one go. White's choices are again b1, g1, g2 and h2, all four next to a corner.","32":"White plays h2, flipping g3. H2 is next to the h1 corner, and this move lets Black take h1 next. But every one of White's four choices would have given away a corner.","33":"Black plays h1, taking the top-right corner and flipping h2. A disc in a corner can never be flipped again; it is the strongest disc on the board. From here Black can firm up the right and top edges.","34":"White plays g1, flipping f2 and e3. White enters the top edge and tries to fill the gap next to Black's h1. But White had only three places to play, and the others, b1 and g2, were dangerous too.","35":"Black plays g2, flipping f2. G2 is diagonally next to the h1 corner, but h1 is already black, so there is no danger. Once you own a corner, the squares around it become safe. After this move White has just one place to play: b1.","36":"White plays b1, flipping six discs: c1, d1, e1, f1, c2 and d3. It was the only move White had. The top edge is now white from b1 to g1, and White leads on discs, 27 to 13. But that white row is caught between Black's h1 and the empty a1.","37":"Black plays h4, flipping g3. Black could have taken a1 at once, but extends the right edge first. White is left with just one place to play: g4.","38":"White plays g4, flipping g3, g2, f3 and e2. Again it was the only move. White gains discs, but Black's moves keep growing while White's disappear.","39":"Black plays a1! Taking the top-left corner, Black flips the six white discs from b1 to g1 at once. The whole top edge is black, and Black owns two of the four corners. Black has 18 discs to White's 25, yet White has nowhere at all to play. This position shows clearly the reversi idea that the number of moves matters more than the number of discs.","40":"White passes. When you have no legal move, you pass and the opponent plays again. White still has many discs in the centre, but there is no square where they can be used. From here Black attacks again and again.","41":"Black plays h5, flipping g4, f3 and e2. The right edge is black from h1 to h5. White gets just one place to play: g5.","42":"White plays g5, flipping g4. It was the only move. White comes close to the right edge, but Black has already sealed the outside.","43":"Black plays b7, flipping five discs down the b-file: b6, b5, b4, b3 and b2. White's disc on b2 turns black here too. White's only place to play is a7, right next to the a8 corner.","44":"White plays a7, flipping b6. It is next to the a8 corner, but there was nowhere else to go. Next, Black can take the a8 corner.","45":"Black plays a8, taking the bottom-left corner and flipping a7. Black now owns three of the four corners. The whole left edge, from a1 to a8, is black.","46":"White plays b8, flipping b7. Again the only move. White enters the bottom edge, but a8 right beside it is Black's corner.","47":"Black plays c8, flipping b8 and b7. The bottom edge is black from a8 to c8. White has no move left and must pass again.","48":"White passes, the second pass of the game. Black can keep playing while White is unable to move.","49":"Black plays c6 and flips seven discs: b6, c5, c4, c3, c2, d5 and e4. It is a huge move, and it still gives White no place to play. The disc count is now 38 to 13.","50":"White passes again. With no legal move, White can only watch Black play. Black now looks for a way to leave no white disc on the board.","51":"Black plays h6, flipping g5, f4 and e3. The right edge is black from h1 to h6. White can now play g6, but that too is a square Black will take back.","52":"White plays g6, flipping g5. It was the only move. White's last discs are huddled near the centre at the lower right.","53":"Black plays c7, flipping d6 and e5. Black surrounds White's discs from the lower left. White's only place to play is d7.","54":"White plays d7, flipping d6 and d5. It was the only move. White wins back a little of the d-file, but Black blocks the outside.","55":"Black plays d8, flipping five discs down the d-file: d7, d6, d5, d4 and d3. White has only eight discs left. White again has no move and must pass.","56":"White passes for the fourth time in the game. Only a few white discs remain near the lower-right centre. Black works out the order that will flip every last one.","57":"Black plays g7 and flips six discs: g6, g5, g4, g3, g2 and f6. The whole white g-file turns black, and White has just two discs left. White gets two places to play: f7 and h7.","58":"White plays f7, flipping f6, and is back to four discs. Playing h7 would have invited Black into the h8 corner, which is probably why White chose this. Still, White cannot find a square safe from being trapped.","59":"Black plays f8, flipping f7, f6 and f5 down the file. White has only one disc left, on e6. A complete wipe-out is very close.","60":"White plays g8, flipping f7, and has three discs again. Once more g8 was the only move. Black looks for a way to trap the rest.","61":"Black plays e7, flipping f7 and e6, and White is down to one disc, on g8. White's only move is e8.","62":"White plays e8, flipping f8, so e8, f8 and g8 are white on the bottom edge. This is White's last move of the game. Those three discs lie between Black's d8 and the empty h8 corner.","63":"Black plays h8! Taking the last corner, Black flips g8, f8 and e8, and not a single white disc is left on the board. Black 63, White 0. H7 is still empty, but neither side can play, so the game is over. Empty squares are counted for the winner, so the result was recorded as 64–0. It is said to be the only time a World Championship final has ended with every disc turned over. Winning the final 2–0, the 16-year-old Maruoka became the second World Champion."}},"logistello1997g4":{"t":"Logistello takes all four corners, 55–9","w":"Logistello (computer)","b":"Takeshi Murakami","ev":"Game 4 of the six-game match Takeshi Murakami v. Logistello","d":"6 August 1997","pl":"USA (Princeton, NEC Research Institute)","lead":"In August 1997 the reversi world champion Takeshi Murakami played a six-game match against Logistello, the program written by Michael Buro. Game 4 ended with the widest margin of the six: Logistello won 55–9. At move 48 Murakami had 24 more discs than the machine, yet he lost all four corners and finished with only nine. Musowl follows this game move by move, from the Cow and Tanida openings to the end. It shows better than any other that in reversi what counts is not how many discs you have, but how many moves you can make.","c":{"0":"The afternoon of 6 August 1997, at the NEC Research Institute in Princeton, USA. This is Game 4 of the six-game match. Game 3 had been played that same morning from 9 o'clock, and this game began at 3 in the afternoon. Black is the program Logistello, White is world champion Takeshi Murakami. Logistello has won all three games so far. In this game White has more discs for a long time, yet it ends 55–9. Why that happens is the story of the game.","1":"Black opens with f5. The original record of this game is written with the board turned another way; here it is shown the way the world game database WTHOR stores it, starting from f5. Turning or flipping the board changes nothing in the game itself. The game reversi grew from appeared in England in the 1880s, and two men, Lewis Waterman and John Mollett, are said to have argued over which of them invented it.","2":"White plays f6, diagonal to Black's disc: the 'diagonal opening'. It is played less often than the most common 'perpendicular' reply (d6), but it has been studied for a long time. Today's rules (four discs set crosswise in the centre, Black moves first) were shaped and spread in Japan by Goro Hasegawa in the early 1970s.","3":"Black plays e6, turning e5 and lining up three black discs in a column. This is Black's most popular reply to the diagonal opening. The first World Championship was held in 1977 at the Imperial Hotel in Tokyo, and Hiroshi Inoue became the first world champion. Twenty years later, Murakami, the best human player, faces a machine.","4":"White plays f4, turning e4 and f5. White had only two moves here, f4 and d6, and both lead to well-known openings. Some days of the match had two games, and Games 3 and 4 were both played on 6 August. Murakami had just lost Game 3 that morning and came straight to this one.","5":"With e3 the game reaches the opening called the Cow. It is one of the best-known lines that start from the diagonal opening. Black places a disc above the centre and starts to shut White's discs inside. Buro began writing Logistello in 1992; in only five years it had come far enough to play a match against the world champion.","6":"White plays c5, turning d5 and e5. White moves to the left to probe where Black can play. Logistello split a game into 13 stages by the number of discs on the board, and scored positions with a different yardstick for each stage, from opening to endgame.","7":"Black plays c4, turning d4 and d5. This shape is the Rose-v-Toth, a standard line after the Cow. Logistello's yardstick was made of about 1.2 million numbers (weights), which it learned by itself from millions of positions. That nobody set those numbers by hand was what made it new.","8":"White plays e7, turning just e6. This is the opening called Tanida. White adds a single disc below the centre, a quiet move that gives Black little to turn. On 1 July that year, Hong Kong had been handed back from Britain to China, big news around the world.","9":"Black plays c6, turning only c5. It extends Black's wall on the left, so White finds it harder to play there. On 4 July that year, NASA's Mars Pathfinder had landed on Mars, and its little rover Sojourner was studying rocks. It was a summer full of machines at work.","10":"White plays e2, turning e3 and e4 and pushing a white disc upward. It wins back central discs, but it also adds white discs on the outside, next to empty squares, which is a worry. Logistello could fix its own opening book after a loss, so that it would 'never lose the same way twice'. It was the first program to use such book learning.","11":"Black plays f3, turning only e4. White has come out at the top, so Black plays just inside and makes use of White's outside discs. Keeping your own discs inside and making the opponent's discs face outward is a key idea in the opening. Logistello is said to have sharpened its play in more than 100,000 games against itself.","12":"White plays g4, turning f3. White moves to the right and pinches Black's disc from inside. This was not the first contest between humans and computers: at events in Paris in 1992 and 1994, the computers had won 29.5–19.5 and 32–4. People were already starting to see how strong the machines were.","13":"Black plays g5, turning f5 and e5. Black builds a foothold on the right too and covers the outside of White's g4. Logistello had long been number one at computer tournaments, but in 1996 a strong rival program called Hannibal had appeared.","14":"White plays d6, turning e5. White drops a disc below the centre and opens a gap in Black's wall. Another idea was to play on the right, at h5 or h4. In checkers, the program Chinook had won the human world championship title for the first time in 1994. Machines were catching up with people in one game after another.","15":"Black plays e8, entering the middle of the bottom edge and turning e7, e6 and e5. An early edge move can let the opponent use that edge, so players usually treat it with care. Logistello seems to have judged that White could not make good use of this edge. The bottom edge will fill with white discs for a while, but in the end every one of them turns black. Princeton is also known as the town where Albert Einstein spent his last years.","16":"White plays h6, turning g5 and entering the right edge. According to Logistello's game log, this position was not in its opening book, so from here it had to search on its own. White places a disc on the edge to cut down Black's moves. The logs of this match are still published on Buro's home page, where you can see the depth and score of each search.","17":"Black plays c7, turning d6. It closes at once the gap White opened with d6. Black stretches to the lower left and keeps White's discs shut inside.","18":"White plays d7, turning e6 and f5. White wins back two central discs, and the count is even at 11 each. But the more white discs reach the outside, the more moves Black gets.","19":"Black plays f7, turning e6. Black places a disc outside on the lower side, like a lid over White's discs. It turns just one disc and gives White almost no new moves.","20":"White plays d8, turning e7 and settling next to Black's e8. White builds a foothold on the bottom edge to pinch Black's edge disc. Entering at c8 was another option.","21":"Black plays f8, turning e7. Black adds to the edge too, with e8 and f8 side by side. The fight for the bottom edge begins.","22":"White plays g8, turning f8 and e8, so the bottom edge from d8 to g8 is white. But g8 sits right next to the corner h8. Edge squares next to a corner are called C-squares, and a disc there often lets the opponent take the corner later. Black cannot play h8 yet, but late in the game this white row will turn black all at once.","23":"Black plays g6, turning f6 and f5. Black is back in the middle of the right side. By turning inner discs, Black keeps White short of moves.","24":"White plays h5, turning g6 and f7, and lines up h5 and h6 on the right edge. White widens its edge to squeeze Black's choices.","25":"Black plays g3, turning f4. It gives Black a foothold at the upper right, and also tempts White to play higher up the right edge, at h2 or h4.","26":"White plays d3, turning five discs at once, d4, d5, d6, e4 and f5: now White 20, Black 10. Turning many discs feels good, but in reversi the more you turn, the more moves you tend to hand the opponent. This game shows that on a very large scale.","27":"Black plays d2, turning only e3. Black quietly places a disc near the top. It keeps playing moves that turn few discs and give White few chances.","28":"White plays h2, turning g3, f4 and e5. h2 is also a C-square, next to the corner h1. White now holds much of the right edge from h2 to h6, but the area around the h1 corner has become weak.","29":"Black plays c3, turning d3, d4 and e5. Black sets up at the upper left and cuts a diagonal through White's centre.","30":"White plays d1, turning d2, d3 and d4 and entering the top edge. White adds edge discs to cut Black's moves further.","31":"Black plays f2, turning f3, f4 and f5. Black plays inside near the top, steering White towards g1 or g2.","32":"White plays g1, turning f2 and e3. g1 is a C-square next to h1 as well, so White now has two discs, g1 and h2, beside that corner. When the opponent's discs gather around a corner, it is very good news for the side that will take it.","33":"Black plays c2, turning d3 and e4. Black firms up the upper left and waits for White's next move. There is no hurry: Black waits for White to step onto a dangerous square.","34":"White plays g2, turning f3 and e4. g2 is diagonally next to the corner, an 'X-square', one of the most dangerous squares of all. With White there, Black can play h1 and take the whole diagonal. White had other moves, but each seems to have given Black something elsewhere. Next move, Black enters the h1 corner.","35":"Black takes the first corner with h1, turning the diagonal g2, f3, e4, d5. A corner disc can never be flanked from any side, so it never turns over again. Discs like this are called stable discs. From here Black slowly makes the top and right edges, which join h1, stable too.","36":"White plays g7, turning f6 and e5. g7 is the X-square diagonal to the h8 corner, White's second dangerous square. Surrounded by black discs, White has few safe moves left.","37":"Black plays c1, turning d2 and e3 and entering the top edge. According to Logistello's log, while choosing this move it searched the last 24 empty squares to the very end and confirmed the win by calculation. From here on, every black move is the best move found by a complete search.","38":"White plays b3, turning eight discs at once: c3, d3, e3, f3, c4, d5, e6 and c2. Now White has 31 and Black 11. By numbers alone, White is far ahead. But White's discs are spread on the outside of the board, and Black still has plenty of moves.","39":"Black plays f1, turning g1, f2 and f3. On the top edge f1, g1 and h1 are now black, and joined to the h1 corner they are stable. The g1 disc White put on a C-square has become a stable black disc.","40":"White plays e1, turning f2 and d2. White adds to the top edge too, but cannot get outside Black's f1, so Black controls that edge.","41":"Black plays c8, turning d7 and e6 and entering the bottom edge. The edge is white from d8 to g8, and Black grabs hold of its left end.","42":"White plays b8, turning c8 and c7. Now all six squares of the bottom edge from b8 to g8 are white. Yet both corners, a8 and h8, are still empty. If Black takes either corner, the whole row may turn black: a dangerous shape.","43":"Black plays b4, turning c4 and d4. Black plays on the left to push White to play even further outside.","44":"White plays b6, turning c6, c5 and d4. b6 is just inside the left edge too. Looking for moves, White is being pushed further and further outward.","45":"Black plays b5, turning c5, d5, e5 and c6. Black wins back the middle on the left and cuts White's moves again.","46":"White plays a5, turning six discs, b5, c5, d5, e5, f5 and b4, and entering the left edge. It turns a lot, but with more white discs on the outside, Black gets more moves.","47":"Black plays a4, turning b4 and b5. Black enters the left edge as well, next to White's a5.","48":"White plays a3, turning a4 and b4. White has 38 discs and Black 14, a lead of 24. Yet White's only moves here were b1, a3, a6 and b7, four in all. Each is on an edge or next to a corner, and White is running out of good moves. More discs but in trouble: a very reversi kind of position.","49":"Black plays a7, turning the diagonal b6, c5, d4, e3, f2. a7 is a C-square next to a8, but White cannot flank it soon, so it is safe for Black. From here Black aims at the lower-left corner.","50":"White plays b7, turning b6, b5 and c6. b7 is the X-square diagonal to the a8 corner. White's only moves were b1, a6 and b7, all next to corners. White had been driven to the point where only dangerous squares were left. Having no good moves is called being 'out of moves', and Logistello was a master at creating it.","51":"Black plays h3, turning g3, h2, g4 and f5. Black enters the right edge and step by step makes the edge from h1 downward black.","52":"White plays h4, turning seven discs, g4, f4, e4, d4, c4, g3 and f2: White 41, Black 15. White's only moves were b1 and h4. White has even more discs now, but from here the corners fall to Black one by one.","53":"Black takes the second corner with a8, turning the diagonal b7, c6, d5, e4. The corner was won through the disc White had put on the X-square b7. Now the white row on the bottom edge is flanked from the a8 side.","54":"White plays a6, turning b7. White's only moves were b1 and a6. White places a disc on the left edge, but the edge running from the a8 corner is already becoming stable black.","55":"Black takes the third corner with h8 and turns six discs at once: g8, f8, e8, d8, c8 and b8. The whole bottom edge from a8 to h8 is black. The disc White put on the C-square g8 at move 22 turns black here. Edge discs joined to a corner become stable. The count is still Black 26, White 33, but Black's stable discs keep growing.","56":"White plays b1, turning c1. b1 was White's only move. It is a C-square next to the a1 corner, so White has to put a disc beside the last corner too.","57":"Black plays h7, turning six discs, g7, f7, h6, h5, h4 and g6: Black 32, White 29, so Black is finally ahead in discs too. The right edge from h1 to h8 is all black.","58":"White passes. There is not a single legal move, so White must hand the turn to Black. In reversi you pass only when you have no move. White has nowhere left to flank a black disc.","59":"Black plays b2 and turns 13 discs at once: Black 46, White 16. b2 is the X-square diagonal to a1, but White no longer has any way to use it to take the corner. Before the last corner, Black collects every disc it can.","60":"White plays a2, turning b3. a2 was White's only move. It is next to the last corner a1, and when Black plays a1 the left edge joins up as well.","61":"Black takes the fourth corner with a1, turning b1, c1, d1, e1, a2, a3, a4, a5 and a6, and all 64 squares are full. Black 55, White 9: a crushing win for Logistello. At move 48 White had 24 more discs, yet in the last dozen moves the count swung right round. What decided the game was not the number of discs but the number of moves, the corners and the stable discs. Logistello took all four corners, and the match stood at 4–0. Logistello won the last two games as well, and the match ended 6–0."}},"ck1927":{"t":"Old Heffner squeezes ex-champion Ferrie","w":"August Heffner","b":"James Ferrie","ev":"Second International Match (USA v. Great Britain)","d":"1927","pl":"USA (New York)","lead":"A game from the 1927 international checkers match between the United States and Great Britain, played in New York. Black is August Heffner, captain of the American team; White is James Ferrie, the former world champion from Scotland. Both were veterans close to seventy. There is hardly any fighting: Heffner slowly takes away every useful move from White's left wing and wins in only 35 moves. It is a model of the checkers 'squeeze', winning by leaving the opponent nothing safe to play.","c":{"0":"From 28 February to 10 March 1927, the United States and Great Britain met at the Hotel Almac in New York. Britain had won the first such match, in Boston in 1905, so for America this was a return match. Black is the American captain August Heffner (born 1858); White is Scotland's James Ferrie (born 1857), a former world champion who took the title from James Wyllie, 'the Herd Laddie', in 1894. In checkers Black moves first, with Black's men on squares 1-12 at the top and White's on 21-32 at the bottom. Moves are written with square numbers, like '11-15', and an 'x' marks a capture.","1":"Black plays 11-15. It is the most popular first move, long nicknamed 'Old Faithful'. The man on 15 takes the centre and watches White's 22, 23 and 24. Heffner worked as a railway brakeman in Boston and also edited checkers columns for local newspapers.","2":"White replies 21-17. The pair 11-15 21-17 is the opening called the 'Switcher'. In 1880 Wyllie and Robert Martins played twenty games of a single Switcher line in Glasgow, the famous 'Switcher match'. White starts from the left edge and avoids a head-on clash with the man on 15.","3":"Black plays 9-13, standing right in front of White's man on 17 and holding back White's left side. The three moves 11-15 21-17 9-13 later became one of the openings drawn by lot under the 'three-move restriction'. Heffner helped shape that system, and in January 1931 the American Checker Association is said to have issued a three-move list based on his suggestions.","4":"White plays 25-21, backing up the man on 17 and gathering men on the left. Now 17, 21 and 22 make a solid group. But a side crowded with men can later run short of moves, which is exactly what will happen here.","5":"Black plays 8-11, supporting the man on 15 from behind. With 11 and 15 in a line, Black can recapture if White ever exchanges on 15. A quiet, natural developing move.","6":"White plays 17-14, offering a man so that he can recapture from 21. In Richard Pask's modern opening book 'Key Openings' the main line here is 30-25, so 17-14 leaves that line. White wants a foothold on 14 inside Black's camp, but this very man will become White's headache.","7":"Black takes 10x17. Captures are compulsory in checkers, so this is the only legal move. The man jumps from 10 over 14 and lands on 17.","8":"White recaptures 21x14 and the men are level at 11 each. The man on 14 is a thorn in Black's side, but it has no support behind it and can become a target. From here on, this man is the main character of the game.","9":"Black plays 6-10, standing right in front of the man on 14 so that it cannot advance. The man on 10 is guarded by 7 and 11, so White cannot simply take it. Black is already showing his plan: freeze the man on 14.","10":"White plays 22-17, the second exchange: Black takes with 13 and White recaptures from 26. White wants a supporting man on 17 behind 14. After it, White's left again has a pair, 14 and 17.","11":"Black takes 13x22. The capture is forced; there is no other move.","12":"White recaptures 26x17, and the count is ten men each. The first round of exchanges is over. So far this is a familiar Switcher pattern found in the books of the time.","13":"Black plays 15-18, stepping into the middle of White's camp. The man on 18 is a wedge that eyes 22 and 23, so White's right side can no longer move freely. An advanced man can be cut off, though, so Black will need to think about supporting it.","14":"White plays 24-20, moving a man to the right edge and leaving the 19 area open. The engine rates 24-19 about the same. This match was also a stage for future champions such as Newell Banks, Asa Long and Samuel Gonotsky. Asa Long, then 22, is said to have tied for first place in the match.","15":"Black plays 1-6, bringing a back-rank man forward to support the man on 10. Back-rank men guard against White crowning a king, but the man on 1 is usually considered one of the easier ones to release.","16":"White plays 28-24, filling in behind the man on 20 and tidying the right side. White is lining up men on the right so that he can advance to 19 whenever he likes.","17":"Black plays 6-9, and the theme of the game becomes clear. The man on 9 blocks the man on 14 from advancing to 9, and if it goes to 10 it will be captured by 7 or 11. White's 14 and 17 on the left are hemmed in by Black's 5, 9 and 10 and are turning into 'dead' men.","18":"White plays 32-28, bringing up a man from the back on the right. Since the left side is hard to move, White must find his moves on the right. In checkers, running out of moves leads straight to defeat, so counting how many safe moves each side has left is essential.","19":"Black plays 3-8, bringing a back man forward to support 11 and 12. Black still has plenty of safe moves in reserve and calmly improves his position.","20":"White plays 30-26, gathering men in the centre to prepare 26-22, which would offer an exchange for Black's man on 18. If the wedge on 18 stays, White's right side remains tied up for good.","21":"Black plays 9-13, again placing a man in front of White's 17. If White now carelessly plays 24-19, the engine finds a Black shot that costs White a great deal. While improving his position, Black is also setting traps on White's right.","22":"White plays 26-22, giving Black the man to take on 18 and recapturing from 29. This finally removes the annoying wedge on 18. The engine also rates this as the best move here.","23":"Black takes 18x25. The capture is compulsory.","24":"White recaptures 29x22, and it is nine men each. White's back rank now has only the man on 31, so he must watch out for Black getting a king later.","25":"Black plays 10-15, putting a man on the central square 15 again. If White now plays 23-19, the engine shows that Black offers a man with 15-18 and wins one back with interest, leaving White a man down. Black keeps setting such traps as he goes.","26":"White plays 23-18, standing in front of Black's 15 and holding the centre. The engine says this is almost the only good move here; the others all lose material. White's choices are getting narrower.","27":"Black plays 12-16, advancing on the right and lining up beside 15. The man on 16 also watches White's man on 20. If White now plays 31-26, the engine again finds a big loss for White. White's safe moves can now be counted on one hand.","28":"White plays 27-23, supporting 18 from behind. The engine rates this best and keeps the game level. White is still finding correct moves, but he is defending on a knife-edge.","29":"Black plays 8-12, supporting 16 and at the same time a waiting move that reduces White's choices. The engine sees that if White now played 31-27, Black would win by squeezing the left side with 2-6. White looks for air with the exchange 24-19.","30":"White plays 24-19, offering a man to be taken from 15 and recapturing from 28. Fewer men may loosen White's cramped position a little. The engine also rates this best here.","31":"Black takes 15x24. The capture is compulsory.","32":"White recaptures 28x19, and it is eight men each. The count is equal, but White's left-side men on 14, 17 and 22 are boxed in by Black's 5, 13 and others and can hardly move. Only a few of White's men on the right are free.","33":"Black plays 2-6, preparing 6-9 to lock in the man on 14 for good. At this point every White move except 31-27, such as 14-9 (allowing the double jump 5x14x21), loses heavily. From outside White's camp looks full of men, but inside it is full of men that cannot move.","34":"White plays 31-27, advancing his last back-rank man. The site's engine rates the game a draw before this move, but after it finds Black's 6-9 and changes its verdict to a win for Black. With the man on 31 gone, White has used up his last 'waiting' moves. Since every other move lost material at once, White may already have been in serious trouble.","35":"Black plays 6-9. If White's man on 14 goes to 10 it is taken by 7x14, and 17, 22 and 23 are blocked all around. White has only about four moves, such as 27-24, and the engine shows that after Black waits with 4-8, White must give up a man with 19-15 or similar. The record ends here and the game is scored as a win for Black, Heffner. America won the match decisively, avenging the 1905 defeat. Captain Heffner led the work on the annotated book of the match games, and died four years later, in 1931, aged 72."}},"ck1992g25":{"t":"Chinook lost off the book; the champion leads again","w":"Chinook (University of Alberta computer)","b":"Marion Tinsley","ev":"Man vs. Machine World Checkers Championship, game 25","d":"25 August 1992","pl":"United Kingdom (London)","lead":"The match stood at 2–2 going into the final stretch. In game 25, Chinook was led into an opening it knew, but by a move order it did not know. The defence it found on its own grew steadily worse, and after an 11-minute think the champion judged the game \"over.\" Chinook threw away a man to try for counterplay, but Tinsley caught it with a beautiful sequence and took the lead again, 3–2.","c":{"0":"Tuesday, 25 August 1992, the Park Lane Hotel in London: game 25 of the Man vs. Machine World Checkers Championship. Black (moving first, squares 1–12 at the top) is Chinook; White (21–32 at the bottom) is Tinsley. The score was 2–2, with 15 games left including this one. Before the match Schaeffer had predicted a 4–0 or 5–1 win for Tinsley. By that measure, Chinook was holding on far better than expected.","1":"Chinook plays 9 to 13. This first move, toward the left edge, is called the \"Edinburgh.\" The first three moves are drawn by lot, so this was not Chinook's own choice. In those days there were 144 possible three-move ballots.","2":"White, Tinsley, plays 23 to 18, into the centre, watching Black's next move. Tinsley was born in Ohio, USA, in 1927. It is said he skipped four grades at school and finished high school at fifteen.","3":"Black plays 11 to 15, completing the ballot 9-13 23-18 11-15. The man on 15 meets the one on 18 head-on, so White must capture at once. It is a ballot that begins with an exchange straight away.","4":"White plays 18x11, taking 15. Capturing is compulsory, so this is White's only move. The white man on 11 will be taken straight back.","5":"Black recaptures 8x15. Taking with 7x16 was also possible, but this site's engine rates that much worse. Coming up from behind, Black holds the centre square 15 again.","6":"White plays 24 to 20. Richard Pask's opening manual Key Openings recommends 22-17, but Tinsley chose 24-20 on the right. Tinsley was a mathematician with a doctorate in combinatorial analysis from Ohio State University, and he taught at university.","7":"Black plays 7 to 11, firming up behind 15. So far this is a normal set-up. Tinsley reportedly said that as a graduate student he spent around 10,000 hours studying checkers.","8":"White plays 28 to 24. Schaeffer called this move \"a tragedy.\" Chinook knew this opening, but only when White played 27-23 first and 28-24 after. Just by swapping the order, Tinsley pushed Chinook out of its book, and it had to play on its own analysis.","9":"Black plays 4 to 8. Chinook found this move on its own, but according to Schaeffer the correct move was 5-9. After 5-9, if White played 27-23, Chinook would have been back in a position it knew from its book. Searching 19 moves ahead, it saw itself \"slightly worse, a tenth of a man.\"","10":"White plays 27 to 23, strengthening the right of the centre. Thanks to the sponsor's SGI machine, Chinook could now search two moves deeper than before. When Tinsley heard that, he said \"Good!\", which left Schaeffer a little uneasy.","11":"Black plays 5 to 9, supporting 13 and gathering the men on the left. Playing 5-9 now no longer brings Chinook back to its book.","12":"White plays 32 to 28, lifting the back right corner man to guard behind 24. White's right now has a solid column: 20, 24 and 28.","13":"Black plays 1 to 5, emptying one back square to fill behind 9. For the endgame, Chinook's database held every position with seven or fewer pieces, about 20 billion of them.","14":"White plays 22 to 18, stepping in front of 15 to offer an exchange. Black has to take with 15x22.","15":"Black plays 15x22, jumping 18 and landing on 22. White recaptures at once.","16":"White recaptures 25x18. Material is level, and White has rebuilt his central outpost on 18.","17":"Black plays 10 to 15, stepping in front of the man on 18. The struggle for the centre begins.","18":"White plays 26 to 22, backing up 18. Schaeffer called this \"the critical position.\" Until now Chinook had seen itself only slightly worse and planned the proper 9-14. But searching 23 moves deep, it suddenly saw serious danger in 9-14.","19":"Black plays 6 to 10. Chinook went into \"panic mode,\" searching for an alternative, and after 11 minutes found nothing better. 6-10 also lost value as the search went deeper, and Tinsley later said that at this point the game was already over. The engine, too, rates 9-14 as much the better move.","20":"White plays 30 to 26, bringing up another back man. White hurries nothing, steadily shrinking the room Black has to move.","21":"Black plays 9 to 14. Chinook plays 9-14 after all, but a move too late. White will capture with 18x9.","22":"White plays 18x9, jumping 14 and landing on 9. Capturing is compulsory.","23":"Black recaptures 5x14. Material is still level. But Black's back left corner (1, 5 and 9) is now empty.","24":"White plays 23 to 19, approaching the black man on 15 head-on and taking hold of the centre. The engine, too, now sees White clearly easier to play.","25":"Black plays 14 to 18, pushing into the middle of White's camp. It looks aggressive, but the man on 18 has no support and is easy to target.","26":"White plays 22 to 17, stepping in front of 13 to make Black capture 13x22. The engine rates 21-17 even higher, but this move keeps White's advantage too.","27":"Black plays 13x22, as the rules require. White recaptures from 26.","28":"White recaptures 26x17. Material is 8 against 8. But Black's man on 18 is loose, and White can target it next.","29":"Black plays 12 to 16, the move that made the audience gasp. It hands White a free man with 19x12; Chinook hoped to win \"some\" counterplay. Schaeffer wrote that 18-23 was the move to play, forcing Tinsley to show the long winning line. The engine also prefers 18-23.","30":"White plays 19x12, taking 16. White is now a man ahead. After the game, Tinsley showed a long and beautiful line in which Chinook lost whatever it did.","31":"Black plays 18 to 22. Having lost a man, Black hurries toward a king. From 22 the road leads to 26 and then the back row at 31.","32":"White plays 31 to 27, bringing a back-row man forward to watch the exit of Black's future king.","33":"Black plays 22 to 26, one step from White's back row. Schaeffer called this the high point of Black's counterplay, but it did not last.","34":"White plays 27 to 23, advancing a central man even though it opens Black's road to the king row. White's own man on 12 can also become a king one day.","35":"Black plays 26 to 31, reaching White's back row and becoming a king. A king can move both forward and backward. But Black is still a man down.","36":"White plays 23 to 19, firming the centre while eyeing Black's man on 15.","37":"Black plays 31 to 27; the king comes back, planning to chase White's men from behind.","38":"White plays 17 to 14, offering a man to the black man on 10. Black must take 10x17. White then takes back with 19x10.","39":"Black plays 10x17, as required.","40":"White plays 19x10, jumping 15 and landing on 10. One for one, and now a white man stands deep in Black's camp on 10.","41":"Black plays 17 to 22, trying to make a second king while keeping an eye on 21.","42":"White plays 10 to 7, the finishing touch: it offers the man on 7. Black can only take 3x10, and that empties square 3.","43":"Black plays 3x10. Capturing is compulsory, and now square 3 is empty.","44":"White plays 12x3, taking 8 and landing on the back row at 3: a king. Give one man, gain a king: a clean, Tinsley-like sequence.","45":"Black plays 11 to 15, pushing a man forward, but White's king is closing in from behind.","46":"White plays 24 to 19, stepping in front of 15 to force a capture. Black must take 15x24.","47":"Black plays 15x24.","48":"White recaptures 28x19. Staying a man up, White keeps simplifying the board. The fewer pieces there are, the more a one-man edge counts.","49":"Black plays 10 to 14, advancing a central man, but White's king chases it.","50":"White plays 19 to 16, heading for a second king. Via 12 it can reach the back row, for example at 4.","51":"Black plays 2 to 6, bringing out even the man that guarded the back row. Black seems to have judged there was little left worth guarding.","52":"White plays 16 to 11. The road to another king is open. Each time White gains a king, it can hunt down Black's men one by one.","53":"The record ends with Black's 14-18, and here the Chinook team resigned. White has a king and four men, Black a king and three, and White's man on 11 is about to crown. Schaeffer wrote that if you give Marion a chance, he takes everything he can, and praised the champion's beautiful play. Tinsley led 3–2. The next thirteen games, 26 to 38, were all drawn, and in the final game, 39, Tinsley added one more win."}},"meihua":{"t":"Meihuapu: Screen Horses beat the Central Cannon","w":"Red (name not recorded)","b":"Black (name not recorded)","ev":"Meihuapu, chapter “Screen Horses beat the Central Cannon”","d":"Qing dynasty, Kangxi era (17th–18th century)","pl":"China (Qing)","lead":"A game from the most important chapter of Meihuapu (the Plum Blossom Manual), written by Wang Zaiyue in the Kangxi era of the Qing dynasty: “Screen Horses beat the Central Cannon”. Against Red's Central Cannon, Black sets up two horses side by side like a folding screen, traps the red chariot that has gone too far, and wins a chariot in 19 moves. This chapter showed that the second player's Screen Horses could beat the first player's Central Cannon, and it changed xiangqi opening theory for good.","c":{"0":"This game is from Meihuapu (the Plum Blossom Manual), written by Wang Zaiyue during the reign of the Kangxi Emperor of the Qing dynasty (1661–1722). The exact year is unknown; it is said the book was never printed in the Qing era but passed on in handwritten copies, and only came out in print in 1917. Together with the Ming manual Juzhongmi it is called “Ju-Mei”, the two great xiangqi manuals. This game is in a chapter where the stronger player gives up the first move, so Black is the stronger side. In old books the game is sometimes printed mirrored left to right.","1":"Red moves the right cannon to the middle: the Central Cannon, aiming straight at Black's general. In the time of Juzhongmi, attacking first with this cannon was the main way to play.","2":"Black jumps a horse to guard the middle soldier. From here Black builds the Screen Horses: two horses standing side by side like a folding screen. In an age when people answered a central cannon with a central cannon, Meihuapu showed how strong this quiet horse defence was.","3":"Red also jumps a horse, guarding the middle soldier while bringing pieces out.","4":"Before the second horse, Black pushes a soldier one step. With the soldier forward, the horse will later have more room to jump. In the time of the Kangxi Emperor, when Wang Zaiyue lived, Newton was publishing his Principia (1687) far away in England.","5":"Red brings the right chariot out to the side, preparing to send it forward.","6":"Black also brings out a chariot, facing Red's chariot on the same file. The chariot protects Black's cannon.","7":"Red moves the chariot up to its own bank of the river. A chariot watching the river like this is called a River Chariot. From here it can go after Black's soldiers.","8":"Black jumps the second horse, and the Screen Horses are complete: the two horses guard the middle soldier together, a solid shape. In later times this setup became the main defence against the Central Cannon.","9":"Red pushes a soldier into Black's soldier. The idea is to trade soldiers and open a path for the chariot.","10":"Black's soldier takes Red's soldier.","11":"Red's chariot moves sideways and takes that soldier back. Now it stands in front of Black's horse and attacks it. The River Chariot taking a soldier is the scene that gives this chapter's game its theme.","12":"Black pushes a soldier on the other side. Although the right side is in danger, Black deliberately advances on the left. While Red's chariot has moved right, Black prepares to bring pieces out on the left; it is said this is a very Wang Zaiyue kind of move.","13":"Red moves the left cannon behind the chariot. Using the chariot as a screen, the cannon also attacks Black's horse: a double threat. If Black just guards the horse, an elephant becomes a target.","14":"Black's horse jumps forward. It escapes and heads towards Red's camp at the same time.","15":"Red's chariot steps forward and chases the horse again.","16":"Black raises the cannon to its side of the river. Using its own soldier as a screen, it attacks the red chariot along the row.","17":"Red's chariot takes that soldier. It removes the cannon's screen and now attacks both Black's horse and cannon.","18":"Black's horse crosses the river into Red's camp. Rather than worry about losing pieces, Black chooses speed of attack.","19":"Red's chariot takes Black's horse and now eyes Black's cannon. Red could have firmed up the defence here, but chose to grab material. It is a bold choice, but it leaves Red's own camp thin.","20":"Black raises an elephant and blocks the chariot's path to the cannon. It defends while preparing the next attack.","21":"Red's chariot drops back to attack the black horse that has crossed the river.","22":"Black's horse jumps deeper, aiming to give check next. Even with fewer pieces, Black chooses to hunt Red's general.","23":"Red brings out the other horse. It guards the camp and frees the left chariot to move.","24":"Black's horse jumps into the corner of Red's palace with check, right next to Red's general.","25":"Red's general steps forward to escape the check. When the general walks up inside the palace, it becomes easier for many pieces to attack it.","26":"Black moves the cannon onto the file behind the horse. Using the horse as a screen, it blocks the general's escape to the left, and it also prepares to chase the general with a chariot.","27":"Red's general runs to the right. The left is blocked by the cannon, so there is no other way.","28":"Black sends the other cannon deep into Red's camp, to the second row from the back. It is not a check yet, but it controls the row around the general: a move with a big plan.","29":"Red brings out the left chariot to chase Black's cannon.","30":"Black lifts the edge chariot one step. This chariot too will later swing across to chase the general.","31":"Red moves the chariot onto the general's file, in front of it, to block Black's attack.","32":"Black's chariot charges deep into Red's camp with check. Red's general must run again.","33":"Red's general steps forward again and is driven to the very front of the palace.","34":"Black's horse jumps to the middle and gives check again.","35":"Red drops the central cannon one step to block the horse's leg (the point the horse must pass) and stop the check. But now this cannon becomes a screen for Black's cannon.","36":"Black's chariot swings across with check. Black's cannon, using Red's cannon as a screen, protects this chariot, so the general cannot take it.","37":"Red's general escapes to the middle.","38":"Black's chariot pulls back and takes the red chariot that stood in front of the general. This is the last scene, recorded in the book as “Black wins a chariot and wins”. The second player's Screen Horses have beaten the first player's Central Cannon. This chapter argued that the Screen Horses can beat the Central Cannon, and it is said to have helped make the Screen Horses the leading defence against the Central Cannon in later times. The book, copied by hand in the Qing era, was published in movable type in Shanghai in 1926, proofread by the historian Lü Simian."}},"peng1931":{"t":"Peng Shusheng, the sage of the northwest, comes to Beiping","w":"Zhang Dekui","b":"Peng Shusheng","ev":"A match in Beiping (Peng Shusheng's journey to Beiping)","d":"1931","pl":"Beiping (today's Beijing)","lead":"Peng Shusheng, nearly 60, travelled alone from Lanzhou in Gansu to Beiping (today's Beijing) and played the northern masters one after another. This record is handed down as a game from that journey. His opponent was Zhang Dekui, the strongest player in Beiping. Against Red's Central Cannon, Peng defended with the Screen Horses, and on move 28 his horse jump cut the link between Red's chariot and cannon. He kept trading pieces into a winning attack, and Red resigned after move 48.","c":{"0":"Beiping, 1931. Black, Peng Shusheng, was born in Gansu province in 1874. He learned by watching grown-ups play in the teahouses of Lanzhou and became the best player in the northwest. That spring his friend Wang Hesheng helped pay for the trip: down the Yellow River on a raft of sheepskins, on by camel and horse, then by train from Zhangjiakou to Beiping. The journey is said to have taken more than a month. Red, Zhang Dekui, was the leading player of Beiping, an attacking master later nicknamed after a fierce hero of the Three Kingdoms.","1":"Zhang moves the right cannon to the middle. The Central Cannon has long been the classic attack, and Zhang is said to have liked pairing it with a quickly developed chariot. By the way, the capital had moved to Nanjing by then, so Beijing was called Beiping, \"northern peace\".","2":"Peng jumps a horse out to guard the middle soldier. Bringing out the second horse too, so that two horses guard the centre, is called the Screen Horses, and Peng's Screen Horses had a fearsome reputation. Central Cannon against Screen Horses is still the most common battle in xiangqi today.","3":"Red also brings a horse out to guard the middle soldier. In the Central Cannon attack, guarding the middle soldier with a horse before bringing out the chariot is the basic order.","4":"Black already pushes the soldier on file 3. It opens the road for the right horse. Modern theory usually brings a chariot out first here, and this game soon heads for an early exchange of soldiers.","5":"Red moves the right chariot one step sideways, onto a file where it can run straight ahead. A chariot only becomes strong once it has an open road.","6":"Black brings out the left chariot too, facing Red's chariot. The chariot is the strongest piece, so both sides want it out early.","7":"Red's chariot advances to just before the river. A chariot here is called a river-patrol chariot: it can slide sideways to hunt soldiers. It is one step more modest than crossing the river, and can always come home to defend.","8":"Black brings out the second horse, and the Screen Horses are complete. The two horses protect each other and hold the centre. This is the formation Peng is said to have used again and again on the journey, to Zhang's great trouble.","9":"Red pushes the soldier on file 7 and offers to trade soldiers. Red wants to open file 7 so that the river-patrol chariot can swing across.","10":"Black takes the soldier. Black's soldier seems to have crossed the river, but Red's chariot can take it back at once. If Black did not take, Red's soldier would take first.","11":"Red's chariot takes the soldier back. Now it stares straight at Black's horse on file 3, and Black must decide where that horse should go.","12":"Black pushes the soldier on file 7, opening the road for the left horse too. Peng is said to have taught: 'look for just one move of real initiative', and he was famous for playing fast. It is said that on this journey he even asked his opponent's rank before each game and decided in advance whether to win or draw.","13":"Red moves the left cannon to file 7. With the cannon behind the chariot, the cannon can also strike the horse, jumping over the chariot as its screen. The horse is now eyed by both.","14":"Black's horse escapes toward the river. It moves forward instead of back, so nothing is lost. This horse will do great work later.","15":"Red pushes the middle soldier. Red wants to press in the centre and later gather the horses there. Red hopes to break through in the middle at once.","16":"Black raises an elephant to strengthen the centre. Elephants cannot cross the river; their job is to guard the front of the general.","17":"Red's middle soldier advances again and crosses the river. Once over the river, a soldier may also move sideways and becomes much stronger.","18":"Black raises an advisor to guard the general. Before Red's soldier can come in through the centre, Black finishes the defensive shape.","19":"Red's soldier steps sideways toward Black's left side. Red avoids trading it for Black's middle soldier and wants it to keep working.","20":"Black's cannon drives deep into Red's third rank. Using Red's soldier as a screen, it aims at the soldier on the edge. While Red attacks, Black also reaches into Red's camp.","21":"Red pushes the soldier on file 3, removing the screen of Black's cannon and offering a trade. It also protects the soldier on the edge.","22":"Black's soldier takes. If Black did not, Red's soldier would take Black's and open the way in front of Black's left horse.","23":"Red's chariot runs sideways and takes the soldier back. But the chariot has now left file 7, which is suddenly wide open.","24":"Black moves the right chariot onto file 7, staring straight at Red's cannon there. A small move, but it punishes the chariot's departure at once.","25":"Red advances the cannon and lets the chariot beside it guard it. Red's chariot and cannon are linked along the rank and support each other.","26":"Black's horse reaches the riverbank, ready to leap forward. It is watching the link between Red's chariot and cannon.","27":"Red brings out the last horse. Red's aim is the 'coiled horses' shape, two horses linked in the centre. Zhang is said to have liked combining the Central Cannon with this shape.","28":"The turning point: horse to the fourth file. Black's horse jumps right between Red's chariot and cannon and cuts their link. Red's cannon is now hit by Black's chariot, and the horse also threatens Red's middle cannon and left horse. A biography describes a game in which Peng cut Red's main forces in two with exactly this horse move, but it is not certain that it is this game.","29":"Red escapes with the horse to the middle, and that horse now guards the cannon on file 7. One move removes two worries; Red survives a dangerous moment.","30":"Black's horse jumps into Red's camp. With this horse as a screen, Black's cannon can now strike Red's middle horse.","31":"Red's chariot slides over to take Black's horse. Normally this would put the horse in trouble.","32":"Black's cannon takes Red's horse, with check! It jumps over its own horse to capture, then gives check through Red's middle cannon. A cannon using two screens in one move: very xiangqi.","33":"Red takes the cannon with the right horse. Cannon and horse are traded, but Black's attack goes on.","34":"Black's horse leaps next to the general with check. A xiangqi horse can be stopped by putting a piece right beside it in the direction it moves (the horse's leg).","35":"Red pulls the chariot back to block the horse's leg. The check is stopped, but now the chariot is tied to defence.","36":"Black's chariot dashes deep into Red's camp and attacks Red's middle horse from the side. Red has two worries at once.","37":"Red's chariot takes Black's horse. Red reduces Black's pieces too, and the trading goes on.","38":"Black's chariot takes Red's horse back. Material is about even, but Black's chariot sits in the centre and freezes Red's middle cannon: if the cannon moves, the chariot checks the general.","39":"Red's chariot comes back toward the river, trying to rebuild the defence. Red needs it near home now.","40":"Black's horse takes the soldier on the edge. Picking up soldiers as it goes, this horse heads toward Red's general.","41":"Red raises an elephant to the edge, blocking one of the horse's roads. Red wants to slow the horse's approach to the general even a little.","42":"Black's horse goes deep into Red's camp, to a nasty square from which it can attack the general's advisors.","43":"Red finally moves the left chariot to attack the horse. That chariot had not moved once all game: attacking with the Central Cannon, Red never found time to bring it out.","44":"Black's cannon goes to the very back of Red's camp. If Red's chariot takes the horse, the cannon checks over the advisor, so the chariot cannot touch it.","45":"Red raises an advisor to guard the general.","46":"Black's chariot runs sideways behind the cannon and horse. Chariot, horse and cannon gather around Red's general.","47":"Red's chariot moves sideways looking for counterplay. But Black's attack is much faster.","48":"Black's chariot enters Red's second rank, and Red resigns. Next the chariot takes an advisor with check, protected by the horse. Hu Lansun, who supervised the games, wrote that Zhang lost seven or eight games in a row without a single win. Others say Zhang did win some, and the story of their match is still told today."}},"ryusetsu1837":{"t":"Ryūsetsu v. Ishimoto Kengyō: a Side-Pawn battle","w":"Ishimoto Kengyō","b":"Ōhashi Ryūsetsu","ev":"The 21-game match between Ryūsetsu and Ishimoto Kengyō","d":"1837 (Tenpō 8)","pl":"Japan (where it was played is not recorded)","lead":"A game from the 21-game match, near the end of the Edo period, between Ōhashi Ryūsetsu, who has been called 'a pioneer of modern shogi', and Ishimoto Kengyō, known as a blind shogi player. Sente, the Kengyō, takes the side pawn, and in the middlegame Sente has the easier game. But in a wild fight full of captures, Ryūsetsu turns it around and finally catches Sente's king with a silver drop. Ryūsetsu won in 114 moves.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. Today's game is from 1837 (Tenpō 8). Gote, Ōhashi Ryūsetsu, was raised as heir of the Ōhashi branch house and became 6-dan while still young. But because of an illness of his ears, in 1830 he gave up his place as heir and went on playing shogi outside the shogi houses. Sente, Ishimoto Kengyō, held 'kengyō', the highest rank in the guild of blind people, and is said to have played strong shogi without his sight. From 1837 to 1839 the two played a 21-game match, and Ryūsetsu won it by a wide margin, 19 wins to 2. Let's look at one of those games.","1":"P-7f. The first move opens the bishop's diagonal. In the spring of this year, in Osaka, Ōshio Heihachirō, a former official, led an uprising to help people suffering from famine. The 'Great Tenpō Famine', when rice harvests failed all over Japan, was still going on: a hard time.","2":"P-3d. Gote opens its bishop's diagonal too. In this year Tokugawa Ieyoshi became the 12th shōgun in Edo. It was also the year when, in Britain, Victoria became queen at 18.","3":"S-4h. Sente lifts the right silver. How a blind player followed the board during a game is not known in detail. Even so, the Kengyō was strong enough to play a 21-game match against a master like Ryūsetsu.","4":"P-8d. Gote pushes its rook's pawn. Ryūsetsu was born in Edo in 1795 and was 42 at this time. It is said that even after leaving the shogi house, he taught shogi to many people.","6":"P-8e. Gote pushes the rook's pawn further. A game where both sides push their rook's pawns at each other is called 'Double Wing Attack' (aigakari). Ryūsetsu studied it deeply and is known for writing a book on it, 'Hirate Aigakari Jōseki Ōgi'.","7":"G-7h. Sente lifts a gold to prepare for an attack on file 8. The gold on 7h guards the square 8g, which is very important.","8":"G-3b. Gote also lifts a gold, preparing for an attack on file 2. In the Double Wing Attack both sides get ready to meet the opponent's rook's pawn.","10":"P-8f. Gote is the first to clash pawns on file 8. Trading the rook's pawn gives you an extra pawn in hand and lets the rook come forward and work more easily.","11":"Px8f. Sente takes with the pawn. When you take an opponent's pawn, it becomes a piece in your hand.","12":"Rx8f. Gote's rook comes out to 8f. It gains a pawn in hand and glares at Sente's camp.","13":"P-5e. Sente pushes its centre pawn to 5e and takes 'the high ground' in the centre. The pawn on 5e also blocks the diagonal of Gote's bishop. It turns the centre into Sente's territory and makes it easier to move pieces.","14":"R-8b. Gote's rook goes back home. Having gained a pawn, it calmly retreats here.","16":"S-7b. Gote lifts a silver, getting ready to use it in the attack on file 8. When file 8 is attacked with the power of both rook and silver, it is hard to defend.","17":"P-2f. Sente pushes its rook's pawn too. Since Gote traded pawns on file 8, Sente aims to do the same on file 2.","18":"S-8c. Gote's silver goes to 8c. Next it will go to 7d or 8d and attack together with the rook. Bringing a silver forward to work with the rook is a common way of attacking in the Double Wing Attack.","20":"S-7d. Gote's silver comes out to 7d. The idea is to go on to 6e or 7e and press on Sente's camp.","22":"Px2d. Gote takes with the pawn. If it didn't, Sente would make a tokin next with P-2c+.","23":"Rx2d. Sente's rook comes out to 2d. Now Sente has gained a pawn too, so each side is one pawn up from the trades.","24":"P*2c. Gote drops a pawn to block file 2. That leaves Gote's pawn on 3d, right beside Sente's rook.","25":"Rx3d. Sente's rook moves sideways and takes the pawn on 3d. This is the 'Side Pawn Capture' (yokofu-dori). Sente wins a pawn, but the rook comes out close to the enemy camp, so if Gote attacks well the rook can get into danger. There is even a saying, 'taking the side pawn means three years of illness', and people have studied for a very long time whether to take it or not. Ryūsetsu's 'Hirate Aigakari Jōseki Ōgi' is thought to be the oldest book that writes about the Side Pawn Capture.","26":"K-4a. Gote moves its king one step to the right and tidies up its defence. Ryūsetsu lets Sente take the side pawn, and in return he builds up his position while Sente's rook is away from home.","27":"R-3f. Sente pulls the rook back to 3f. On 3d the rook is easily targeted by Gote's bishop and silver, so Sente moves it to a safe place early.","29":"R-2f. The rook goes back to 2f. Keeping its extra pawn, Sente starts to arrange its camp.","30":"B-4d. Gote brings its bishop out to 4d. This bishop glares diagonally at Sente's camp and also aims at the pawn on 5e.","31":"R-5f. Sente swings its rook to the centre to protect the pawn on 5e. Sente's plan is to hold on to the high ground in the centre.","37":"K-6i. Sente moves its king to the left and starts to firm up its defence. A fight is likely on the right side, so the king escapes to the left.","40":"B-2b. Gote pulls its bishop back to a square where it can also defend. On 4d it was easily targeted by Sente's rook and silver, so Gote pulls it back in advance.","41":"P-6f. Sente pushes the pawn on file 6 to open a path for the silver to 6g. Pushing the pawn on file 6 also changes the bishop's diagonal a little.","43":"S-6g. The silver rises to 6g, and the king's surroundings become sturdier. With silver 6g, gold 7h and king 6i, a simple castle is ready.","45":"S-4f. The other silver comes out to 4f. This silver supports the pawn on 5e and also aims to advance to 3e or 4e.","46":"P-5d. Gote clashes its centre pawn to break Sente's high ground on 5e. The pawn on 5e is a key pawn holding Sente's centre, so this becomes the centre of the fight.","47":"Px5d. Sente takes with the pawn. The centre pawns have been traded, and the fight on file 5 begins.","48":"P*5e. Gote drops a pawn to block the rook on 5f. This pawn becomes a wall that stops Sente's rook from working in the centre.","49":"R-5i. Sente's rook drops back to 5i. From 5i it can later move sideways toward 2i.","50":"Sx5d. Gote's silver takes the pawn on 5d. In the centre, Gote has pushed back a little.","51":"P-3f. Sente pushes the pawn on file 3 to open a path for the knight to 3g. To use a knight in an attack, you first have to move the pawn in front of it.","53":"N-3g. The right knight jumps too. Both knights are getting ready to join the attack. Later this knight will jump to 2e or 4e and press on Gote's camp.","55":"G-4h. Sente brings a gold over to firm up its camp. A gold toward the centre can guard both the middle and the right side.","56":"N-7c. Gote also jumps its left knight to aim at 6e. Gote wants this knight to work on 6e, close to Sente's camp.","59":"R-2i. Sente brings the rook back to file 2. The idea is to attack near Gote's king from file 2.","60":"P-7e. Gote starts the fight first. It clashes the pawn on file 7 to break the front of Sente's knight on 7g.","61":"Px7e. Sente takes with the pawn. If it didn't, Gote would push P-7f and win the knight.","62":"P-6e. Gote clashes the pawn on file 6 as well. From both files 6 and 7, Gote comes to break up the area around Sente's king.","63":"Px6e. Sente takes this one too. It is drawing Gote's attacking pieces forward little by little.","64":"Nx6e. Gote's knight jumps to 6e. Next it aims to take the knight on 7g. A knight can only move forward, so jumping is a move made knowing there is no way back.","65":"P*6f. Sente drops a pawn to attack the knight on 6e. According to this site's shogi AI, around here Sente, the Kengyō, has the better game. Gote's attack is a little too much, and Sente is in a good shape to win material.","66":"Nx7g+. Gote's knight takes the knight on 7g and promotes. Now the defender on 7g is gone from Sente's camp.","67":"Bx7g. Sente takes the promoted knight with the bishop. Knights have been traded. The board is tidier, and Sente's bishop has come out to a good square.","68":"N*3d. Gote drops a knight from its hand on 3d, aiming at the silver on 4f. A knight can aim at two squares at once, so where you drop it matters a lot.","69":"S-5g. Sente pulls the silver from 4f back to 5g. Bringing the silver back near the king also makes the defence firmer.","70":"P*3e. Gote drops a pawn to clash with Sente's pawn on 3f. The idea is to open file 3 and bring the silver on 4d forward.","71":"Px3e. Sente takes with the pawn. The pawns on file 3 have been traded, and a path has opened for Gote's silver to come forward.","72":"Sx3e. Gote's silver comes out to 3e. The silver looks toward 3f and 4f in Sente's camp and wants to join the attack.","73":"N*4f. Sente drops a knight to attack both the knight on 3d and the silver on 5d. Knights are good at aiming at two pieces at once.","74":"Nx4f. Gote takes the knight on 4f with the knight from 3d. If it didn't, it would lose either the knight or the silver next.","75":"Px4f. Sente takes back with the pawn. Sente has traded knights for the cost of one pawn and built a pawn wall on 4f.","76":"P*3f. Gote drops a pawn to attack the knight on 3g. The head of a knight, the square just in front of it, is a weak spot for a knight.","77":"N-2e. Sente jumps the knight away to 2e. From 2e the knight also aims at 1c and 3c in Gote's camp.","78":"P-2d. Gote pushes a pawn to attack the knight on 2e. The knight is about to be taken, so Sente has to hurry.","79":"P*3d. Sente drops a pawn and presses on Gote's camp. Later this pawn may promote on 3c and become a tokin near Gote's king.","80":"Px2e. Gote's pawn takes the knight on 2e. According to this site's shogi AI, the game is now roughly level again. Ryūsetsu has patiently narrowed the gap by winning back pieces bit by bit.","81":"Rx2e. Sente takes the pawn with the rook. The rook is back on file 2, but Gote's camp is still solid.","82":"S-4d. Gote pulls the silver from 3e back to 4d for defence. The silver that went out to attack comes back to guard its own king.","83":"N*2d. Sente drops a knight. Next it aims at both N-1b+ and N-3b+.","84":"G-2c. Gote lifts a gold to attack the knight. While the gold goes after the knight, Sente's knight can jump in and promote.","85":"N-1b+. Sente's knight jumps in and promotes on 1b, in front of the lance. The promoted knight moves like a gold, and next it aims at the bishop on 2b.","86":"P*2d. Gote drops a pawn to block the rook on 2e. With file 2 blocked, Sente's rook can no longer go straight up the file.","87":"+Nx2b. The promoted knight takes the bishop on 2b. Sente has won Gote's bishop, a big piece.","88":"Px2e. Gote's pawn takes the rook on 2e. In this bishop-for-rook trade, Gote has gained a strong piece.","89":"+Nx2c. The promoted knight goes on to take the gold on 2c as well. Both sides are taking big pieces from each other: a very wild fight.","90":"P-3g+. Gote promotes the pawn on 3f into a tokin and comes into Sente's camp. A tokin works like a gold and is very strong inside Sente's camp.","91":"Gx3g. Sente takes the tokin with the gold. Because the gold has gone up to 3g, the right side of Sente's king has become a little thin.","92":"R*3i. Gote drops the rook it won on 3i. From the bottom rank of Sente's camp, the rook attacks sideways.","93":"B-5i. Sente pulls its bishop back to 5i to defend. Using the bishop for defence weakens the attack, but it firms up the area around the king.","94":"Rx1i+. The rook takes the lance and promotes to a dragon. From the bottom rank of Sente's camp, the dragon reaches far to the side.","95":"P*5h. Sente drops a pawn to firm up its defence. The pawn on 5h is a small wall guarding the side of the king.","96":"P-5f. Gote pushes its centre pawn into Sente's silver. The idea is to give up a pawn to make Sente's silver move.","97":"Sx5f. Sente takes the pawn with the silver. But the silver is about to be targeted by Gote's knight right away.","98":"N*6d. Gote drops a knight to attack the silver on 5f. Dropping the knight puts a second attacker on the silver.","99":"P-3c+. Sente promotes the pawn on 3d into a tokin. It is a move that gets closer to Gote's king, but checked with this site's shogi AI, this was the big turning point. The tokin is taken by a knight right away, and meanwhile Gote's attack moves ahead. Another move, such as dropping a piece for defence, seems to have been better.","100":"Nx3c. Gote takes the tokin with the knight. Now Gote, Ryūsetsu, is clearly better.","101":"P*2i. Sente drops a pawn to cut off the dragon on 1i from the side. If Sente does not stop the dragon's line, the king will be attacked from the side.","102":"P*7g. Gote drops a pawn deep in, on 7g near Sente's king. A nasty pawn: taking it moves the gold, and leaving it lets the gold on 7h be attacked.","103":"Gx7g. Sente takes with the gold. But because the gold moved to 7g, the area around the king has become a little thin.","104":"+Rx2i. The dragon takes the pawn on 2i and enters the bottom rank of Sente's camp. Deep in Sente's camp, the dragon gets ready to press on the king from the side.","105":"+Nx3c. Sente's promoted knight takes the knight on 3c. Sente does not give in and goes after Gote's defenders too.","106":"Sx3c. Gote takes the promoted knight with the silver. Gote's defence still has silver and gold, and Sente's attack pauses for breath.","107":"S-4g. Sente brings the silver over to firm up its defence. It pulls the silver back from 5f to guard the right side of the king.","108":"P*6h. Gote drops a pawn to start dragging Sente's king out. If the king takes the pawn, it moves up one rank and becomes easier to attack.","109":"Kx6h. Sente's king takes the pawn and moves up to 6h. When the king moves up, it has more escape squares, but it also gets closer to the opponent's pieces.","110":"N*5f. Gote drops a knight with check. From 5f the knight reaches the king on 6h, and no other piece can block it.","111":"Sx5f. Sente takes the knight with the silver from 4g. The king could have run instead, but Sente chose to remove the knight giving check.","112":"Nx5f. Gote takes back the silver with the knight from 6d. This knight also reaches the king on 6h from 5f, so it is check again.","113":"Sx5f. Sente takes the knight with the silver. Now Sente has more pieces, but what matters is the area around the king.","114":"S*7i. Gote drops a silver from its hand on 7i with check. Here Ishimoto Kengyō admitted defeat. Sente has many pieces in hand, but its king has no defenders left around it, and it is trapped by the dragon on 2i and the silver. The Kengyō had the better game in the middlegame, but in a wild fight full of captures Ryūsetsu calmly narrowed the gap and, seizing on his opponent's one slack move, turned the game around. Ryūsetsu died two years later, in 1839, at 45, but his ideas about the Double Wing Attack and the Side Pawn Capture were passed down to the players who came after him."}},"ralle1984":{"t":"Ralle becomes Europe's first World Champion","w":"Ryoichi Taniguchi","b":"Paul Ralle","ev":"8th World Championship, final, game 2","d":"October 1984","pl":"Australia (Melbourne)","lead":"The 8th World Reversi Championship was held in Melbourne, Australia, in 1984. The final was a two-game match between Ryoichi Taniguchi of Japan and Paul Ralle of France. Ralle won the first game 34–30, and in this second game, playing White, he took away Black's places to play one by one and won 49–15. It was the game that made the first World Champion from Europe. Halfway through, Black leads 30–15 on discs, but that is exactly when White's comeback begins.","c":{"0":"October 1984, Melbourne, Australia: the final of the 8th World Reversi Championship. Seven players from seven countries took part, and the final was a two-game match between Ryoichi Taniguchi of Japan and Paul Ralle of France. Ralle had already edged the first game 34–30 with Black. Here Taniguchi has Black and Ralle White. If White wins this game, Europe will have its first World Champion.","1":"Black starts with f5. All four first moves give the same shape, so records begin with f5. The World Championship had been held every year since Tokyo in 1977, moving on to New York, Rome, London, Brussels, Stockholm and Paris. Melbourne was the first time it was held in the southern hemisphere.","2":"White plays d6, the \"perpendicular opening\", which lines up with Black's disc; it is the most common second move. Ralle had won the French championship for the first time that same year, 1984. He would go on to win it seven times and became France's leading player from the 1980s into the early 1990s.","3":"Black plays c5, flipping d5. It is one of the answers to the perpendicular opening, making a diagonal line of black in the centre. The final standings of this championship were Ralle first, Taniguchi second, Ted Landau of the USA third and David Sharman of Great Britain fourth.","4":"White plays f4, flipping e4 and e5. White stretches out to the right and probes where Black can play. White has 5 discs to Black's 3, but in the opening the disc count has almost nothing to do with who wins.","5":"Black plays e3, flipping d4. The five moves so far form the opening called the \"Rabbit\". It is one of the main shapes after the perpendicular opening, and many lines such as the \"Rose\" branch off from here. In game 1 of the final, Ralle as Black had started with the opening called \"Tanida\", said to be named after Kunihiko Tanida, the 1982 World Champion.","6":"White plays c6, flipping d5. White steps outside Black's discs on the left and squeezes the centre from above and below. At this point White has eleven places to play and plenty of choice.","7":"Black plays d3, flipping e4. This is the main line after the Rabbit, adding a black disc above the centre. 1984 was also the year the Summer Olympics were held in Los Angeles, USA.","8":"White plays f3, flipping e4. In the main line of the Rabbit, White usually plays f6 here and goes into the famous shape called the \"Rose\". White picks f3 instead and steps a little off the main road of opening theory. It is a quiet move that flips only one disc, carefully choosing where Black will be allowed to play.","9":"Black plays e6, flipping e5 and e4. Black joins up the central column. From 1977 to 1983, every World Champion had been Japanese, except Jonathan Cerf of the USA in 1980.","10":"White plays f7, flipping e6. White extends toward the lower right. Ralle is said to have been 16 at the time, the same age as Hidenori Maruoka when he won in 1978.","11":"Black plays g4, flipping f4. Black comes out on the right and goes around the outside of White's discs. But discs on the outside can later give the opponent places to play.","12":"White plays g5, flipping f5 and e5. White also lines up on the right and takes the square below Black's g4. Ralle would reach the World Championship final again in 1986, 1987 and 1991, and stayed at the top for many years.","13":"Black plays h6, entering the right edge and flipping g5. Edge discs are hard to trap from inside, so this becomes a base for Black. Black has twelve places to play and still has room. In 1990 Ralle also became a team World Champion as part of the French side.","14":"White plays h3, entering the right edge and flipping g4. Black's h6 and White's h3 are now on the same edge, and the fight for the right edge begins. The shape of an edge often decides the fight for the corners later.","15":"Black plays h4, flipping g4. Black lines up right below White's h3. Black is looking for a chance to trap White's edge disc from above and below.","16":"White plays g3, flipping f4. White firms up the upper right and, together with h3, surrounds Black's h4. White keeps playing quiet moves that flip one disc at a time.","17":"Black plays f6, flipping e5. Black adds a disc to the lower right of the centre. Black is gaining discs in the middle, while White tries to surround Black from the outside.","18":"White plays h5 and flips three discs: g5, h4 and g4. On the right edge, h3, h4 and h5 are now white. Only Black's h6 is left on that edge, stranded below the white row.","19":"Black plays e7, flipping e6 and d6. Black comes out below the centre, next to White's f7. Black has ten places to play, and the game is still close to even.","20":"White plays h7, flipping h6, and the right edge is white from h3 to h7. H7 is next to the h8 corner, but Black would need a disc on h2 to take h8. White judges that this will not happen soon and takes the edge.","21":"Black plays g6, flipping f5. Black moves inside White's right edge and blocks the inside of White's wall. Black has eleven places to play and still keeps some choice.","22":"White plays c7, flipping d6 and e5. White swings round to the lower left and wraps Black's central discs from outside. White is aiming for a shape with its own discs inside and Black's outside.","23":"Black plays c4, flipping d5. Black plays at the upper left and adds to the centre. From around here, a gap slowly opens in the number of places each side can play.","24":"White plays f8, entering the bottom edge and flipping e7. White makes a foothold on the bottom edge. White now has twelve places to play, more than Black's nine.","25":"Black plays d8, entering the bottom edge and flipping e7 back. Black stands next to White's f8, and the fight for the bottom edge begins.","26":"White plays e8, flipping e7 and e6, so e8 and f8 on the bottom edge are white. Black's d8 is left to the left of the white row. Black is down to seven places to play.","27":"Black plays c8, flipping c7 and c6. Black now has c8 and d8 side by side on the bottom edge. But with few places to play, Black's choices are becoming limited.","28":"White plays b6, flipping c6. White comes out on the left and goes around the outside of Black's c-file discs. White still has eleven places to play.","29":"Black plays d7, flipping d6 and e6. Black wins back discs below the centre. Black had nine places to play, f2, g2, h2, a5, a6, a7, b7, d7 and g8, but most were near an edge or a corner.","30":"White plays b8 and flips four discs: c8, d8, c7 and d6. The bottom edge is white from b8 to f8. B8 is next to the a8 corner, usually a dangerous square. But White judged that taking this edge and cutting Black's choices was worth more.","31":"Black plays b5, flipping c6. Black had eight places to play, and a7, b7, g7 and g8 were dangerous squares near corners. Black chooses the relatively safe b5.","32":"White plays a4, entering the left edge and flipping b5, c6 and d7 on the diagonal. White now has a foothold on the left edge too. Black's places to play keep shrinking.","33":"Black plays f2, flipping f3 and f4. Black plays into the empty space at the upper right. Black has seven places to play, and many of them, such as g2, a7, b7 and g8, are next to corners.","34":"White plays g2, flipping f3, e4 and d5. G2 is the \"X-square\" diagonally next to the h1 corner, normally a square you must not play. But for Black to take h1, there would have to be a black disc at the end of White's line running from g2 toward the centre, and there is none now. Ralle plays the dangerous square only after making sure his opponent cannot take the corner.","35":"Black plays a6 and flips four discs: b6, c6, d6 and b5. Black enters the left edge. But Black has only six places to play, and g1, h2, a7 and g8 were next to corners.","36":"White plays b7, flipping c6. B7 is the X-square diagonally next to the a8 corner. After this move Black has only three places to play: g1, h2 and g8. All of them are next to a corner. Ralle's plan of cornering his opponent through the number of moves is now clear.","37":"Black plays g1, flipping four discs down the file: g2, g3, g4 and g5. G1 is next to the h1 corner and makes it easier for White to take h1. But all three of Black's choices were next to corners, so Black had to pick one. On discs it is almost even, 20 to 21, but on places to play White is far ahead.","38":"White plays h2, flipping g3 and f4. White joins the right edge from h2 to h7. White has twelve places to play; Black has three.","39":"Black plays a8! Black takes the bottom-left corner and flips five discs on the diagonal: b7, c6, d5, e4 and f3. Because White had played b7, the diagonal line was complete and Black could reach the corner. A corner disc can never be flipped again. Still, Black has few places to play, and White's attack goes on.","40":"White plays a7, flipping b7. A7 is next to the a8 corner, but the corner already belongs to Black, so White is not giving anything away. After this move Black has only one place to play: g8.","41":"Black plays g8 and flips six discs, f8, e8, d8, c8, b8 and f7, so the bottom edge is black from a8 to g8. It was the only move. On discs Black leads by a lot, 30 to 15. But White has ten places to play, and Black will again have only one next turn: the position favours White.","42":"White plays h1, taking the top-right corner and flipping five discs on the diagonal: g2, f3, e4, d5 and c6. Black's g1 had made it possible for White to play h1. With the corner, White can firm up the top and right edges.","43":"Black plays e2, flipping f3. It was the only move. Black is surrounded by white discs and has nowhere free to play.","44":"White plays d1, entering the top edge and flipping e2, f3 and g4. White makes a foothold on the top edge as well. Black's places to play are e1, f1 and d2.","45":"Black plays e1, flipping e2. Black enters the top edge, but between White's d1 and the h1 corner. Almost no safe squares are left for Black.","46":"White plays c2, flipping d3. White moves inside Black's top edge and cuts Black's choices further. Black's options were b1, c1, d2 and c3.","47":"Black plays c1, flipping d1. Black now has c1, d1 and e1 on the top edge. But f1 to the right is empty, and if White plays there, g1 between it and the h1 corner will be flipped.","48":"White plays f1 and flips three discs: g1, f2 and e2. The right side of the top edge is white. White's discs joined to the h1 corner can no longer be flipped.","49":"Black plays b3, flipping c2. Black had four places to play, but all were on the top edge or near the top-left corner. Black picks the slightly safer square at the upper left.","50":"White plays d2, flipping e3. White flips Black's discs above the centre one by one and erases Black's places to play. After this move Black has just one: c3.","51":"Black plays c3, flipping d2. It was the only move. Black flips white discs, but cannot create new places to play.","52":"White plays b1 and flips four discs, c1, d1, e1 and c2, so the top edge is white from b1 to h1. All of Black's discs on the top edge are gone. Black's only place to play is b2, diagonally next to the a1 corner.","53":"Black plays b2, flipping c2. It is an X-square, but there was nowhere else to play. This lets White play into the a1 corner.","54":"White plays a1, taking the top-left corner and flipping b2, c3 and d4 on the diagonal. It is White's second corner. White now holds the whole top edge and the top-left corner.","55":"Black plays a2, flipping b2. Black had only two places to play, a2 and b4. Black enters the left edge, but right above is White's corner.","56":"White plays a5 and flips four discs: b5, c5, a6 and b6. White enters the middle of the left edge and traps Black's edge discs. The disc count is White 40, Black 20.","57":"Black plays b4 and flips five discs: b5, b6, b7, c5 and c3. Black gathers its last strength and wins discs back. Black's only choices were a3 and b4.","58":"White plays a3 and flips seven discs: b3, c3, a2, b4, c5, d6 and b2. The left edge is white from a1 to a5. After this move Black has no place to play.","59":"Black passes. When you have no legal move, you pass and the opponent plays again. Only two empty squares remain: g7 and h8.","60":"White plays g7 and flips four discs: f7, g6, g5 and f6. H8 is still empty, but neither side can play there, so the game is over. White 48, Black 15; the empty square is counted for the winner, so the result was recorded as 49–15. Ralle won the final 2–0 and became the first World Champion from Europe. At move 41 Black led 30–15, yet White, who had more places to play, turned it around: a game that shows what reversi is all about."}},"xu1993":{"t":"Xu Yinchuan beats Hu Ronghua for the first time","w":"Hu Ronghua","b":"Xu Yinchuan","ev":"1993 National Xiangqi Individual Championship, round 5","d":"5 August 1993","pl":"Qingdao","lead":"Hu Ronghua, who became national champion at 15 in 1960, is challenged by 18-year-old Xu Yinchuan of Guangdong, playing Black. Xu punishes a slip by Hu in the opening, gets the upper hand by trading one chariot for two cannons, and then carefully wins a long endgame. It was his first win over Hu in a major event; Xu went on to win his first national title at this tournament, and the game is seen as the start of the Xu Yinchuan era.","c":{"0":"Round 5 of the 1993 National Xiangqi Individual Championship in Qingdao. Red is Hu Ronghua of Shanghai, aged 48. He first became national champion at 15 in 1960 and was the great star of xiangqi, nicknamed “Commander Hu”. Black is Xu Yinchuan of Guangdong; according to the records, this was his 18th birthday. Xu had just lost in round 4, and it is said he had never beaten Hu in an important game before. It is the same story of a new generation as the 1960 game in which 15-year-old Hu beat Yang Guanlin.","1":"Hu moves the right cannon to the middle: the Central Cannon. Hu was a player who used many openings freely, not just the Central Cannon but also the Elephant Opening and the Immortal Points the Way.","2":"Xu jumps a horse to guard the middle soldier. From here he builds the Screen Horses, two horses side by side. Screen Horses against the Central Cannon is today's standard defence, the shape whose strength the Qing manual Meihuapu showed.","3":"Red also jumps a horse to guard the middle soldier.","4":"Black brings the left chariot out sideways, facing Red's chariot on the same file.","5":"Red brings out the right chariot too. The chariots stare at each other: a familiar picture in Central Cannon against Screen Horses.","6":"Black jumps the second horse, and the Screen Horses are complete.","7":"Red jumps the left horse to the edge, an Edge Horse, preparing to move the left cannon sideways.","8":"Black pushes a soldier to open the way for the left horse. This shape is called Screen Horses with the 7th soldier advanced, and it is very often played.","9":"Red moves the left cannon across. With the central cannon, this makes the setup called the Five-Seven Cannons: two cannons aiming at Black's right side.","10":"Black develops the right chariot. Many players advance a cannon here, but Xu brings the chariot out first.","11":"Red pushes a soldier, putting pressure on Black's right horse.","12":"Black sends the cannon across the river into the row of Red's soldiers, blocking the path in front of Red's left chariot.","13":"Red pushes the middle soldier. This sharp attack is called the Rushing Middle Soldier, and it is said to be rare in big tournaments. Hu seems to have wanted to build a big lead against his young opponent right from the opening.","14":"Black raises an advisor to firm up the middle: a calm move against Red's middle-soldier attack.","15":"Red brings out the left chariot, facing Black's cannon.","16":"Black moves the left cannon one step forward, to the edge of the river, eyeing Red's camp.","17":"Red pushes yet another soldier: the third soldier advance, after the 7th and the middle. It was a bold attempt to open the closed position quickly. Some think Hu, with one win and three draws in the first four rounds, was in a hurry for points.","18":"Black's soldier takes Red's soldier.","19":"Red advances the chariot to attack Black's cannon across the river. But this was a big miscalculation. It is said that such a mistake had almost never happened to Hu before. Another way of attacking, pushing a soldier, is considered better here.","20":"Black's cannon slides sideways. Using Red's horse as a screen it aims at an elephant, and at the same time it invites a trade of chariots.","21":"Red jumps the horse to the middle. But this is a second oversight that gives Black a neat sequence. Here Red could have traded chariots first.","22":"Black's chariot takes Red's chariot. The exchanges begin.","23":"Red takes back Black's chariot with the horse. One chariot for one chariot.","24":"Black moves the cannon to the middle. Using Red's soldier as a screen, it attacks the central horse. Now Red's chariot and horse are both under attack.","25":"Red's chariot takes Black's cannon.","26":"Black's soldier takes the chariot back. Red has lost a chariot.","27":"Red's soldier takes Black's central cannon.","28":"Black's soldier takes that soldier.","29":"Red's central cannon takes a soldier with check. In this series of exchanges, after one chariot was traded for one chariot, Red gave the other chariot for Black's two cannons: one chariot for two cannons.","30":"Black jumps a horse to the middle and blocks the check.","31":"Red's horse jumps forward, looking for counterplay.","32":"Black raises an elephant to strengthen the defence. Red has no chariot, while Black has a chariot and a soldier across the river. The number of pieces is similar, but Black is clearly better. “The side with a chariot is stronger” is an important endgame idea.","33":"Red's cannon takes a black soldier. Red tries to reduce material and aim for a draw.","34":"Black's horse jumps out, aiming at Red's edge soldier.","35":"Red's cannon slides sideways to watch Black's camp.","36":"Black's horse jumps further forward, closing in on Red's camp.","37":"Red's horse comes near Black's camp. Hu looks for chances to strike back with horse and cannon.","38":"Black moves an advisor, getting ready for the red horse's jumps.","39":"Red pulls the central cannon back one step so that it can also help defend.","40":"Black brings the other advisor to the middle and tidies the palace. Calmly, he sidesteps the attacks of Red's horse and cannon.","41":"Red's other horse moves towards the middle.","42":"Black's horse drops back and offers to trade horses. Removing the opponent's attacking piece is a good move.","44":"Black's horse jumps sideways from the middle and attacks Red's central cannon.","46":"Black's horse takes a red soldier.","47":"Red brings the other horse to the middle. It is said that taking Black's horse here would have let Black win even more easily, so Red avoids it.","48":"Black's soldier, already across the river, steps sideways. Inside Red's camp it attacks a red piece.","49":"Red's horse takes Black's horse.","50":"Black's chariot comes forward. Now the chariot joins the attack.","52":"Black's soldier takes Red's central horse. The soldier that went deep has done great work.","54":"Black's chariot swings across to attack Red's cannon.","56":"Black's chariot advances to the river and attacks a red soldier.","58":"Black moves the general sideways and changes the shape of the defence: a careful move to avoid any check by Red's cannon in advance.","60":"Black's chariot takes a red soldier.","61":"Red's cannon takes Black's horse. Red also gets something back.","62":"Black's elephant takes that cannon.","63":"Red's horse takes a black soldier. Now Black's chariot faces Red's horse, cannon and soldier.","64":"Black's chariot goes deep into Red's camp and takes an elephant. Red's defence keeps getting thinner.","66":"Black brings an elephant back to the middle and tidies the defence. Even close to victory he leaves no gap for a check, which is typical of Xu Yinchuan.","70":"Black pushes the edge soldier one step. The soldier will help press Red's last soldier.","72":"Black's chariot drops back and controls the middle of Red's camp.","76":"Black's chariot comes right in front of Red's horse and attacks it.","78":"Black's chariot chases Red's horse once more.","80":"Black's chariot goes deep into Red's camp. Red's edge soldier can no longer be saved, and here Hu Ronghua admitted defeat. Xu Yinchuan won his first national title at this tournament, becoming the youngest national champion after Hu himself. He later became a great master with six national titles and three world titles, and this game is seen as the victory that opened the Xu Yinchuan era."}},"ck1995laf":{"t":"Chinook's only win against Lafferty","w":"Don Lafferty","b":"Chinook (University of Alberta computer)","ev":"Man vs. Machine World Checkers Championship (32-game match), game 31","d":"17 January 1995","pl":"United States (Petal, Mississippi)","lead":"After Tinsley withdrew through illness, Chinook became the Man vs. Machine world champion. The challenger was grandmaster Don Lafferty, often described as Tinsley's protégé. In a match where 31 of 32 games were drawn, this was the only decisive game. Chinook surprised Lafferty with an old move added to its book two days before the match, then used its endgame databases to see all the way to the end.","c":{"0":"17 January 1995, the International Checker Hall of Fame in Petal, Mississippi, USA: game 31 of the 32-game Man vs. Machine World Checkers Championship. Black (moving first, squares 1–12 at the top) is Lafferty; White (21–32 at the bottom) is the champion, Chinook. The time control was 30 moves per hour, and failing it meant an immediate loss. Chinook's creator Jonathan Schaeffer published the whole game with notes in AI Magazine (1996).","1":"Lafferty plays 10 to 14. This first move has a name: the \"Denny.\" The first three moves are decided by lot (the ballot). At the time there were 144 possible ballots.","2":"White plays 22 to 18, stepping in front of 14. In 1994 Chinook became the Man vs. Machine world champion under the rules when the champion Tinsley withdrew mid-match for health reasons. Tinsley was diagnosed with cancer soon afterward.","3":"Black plays 7 to 10, completing the ballot 10-14 22-18 7-10. Richard Pask lists it among the 16 three-move ballots most worth careful study. The man on 10 supports 14 from behind.","4":"White plays 25 to 22. From here the moves are free. It is the most natural move, firming up behind 18. Lafferty was known as a careful grandmaster who rarely lost.","5":"Black plays 11 to 16, developing on the right and keeping an eye on White's 24 and 19. In 1994 Chinook and Lafferty had played an 18-game match, and every game was drawn.","6":"White plays 24 to 19: the move Chinook had prepared. According to Schaeffer it was added to the book two days before the match, and it had not been used in grandmaster play for more than 50 years. The main line in Pask's book is 29-25. Lafferty was caught by surprise.","7":"Black plays 3 to 7. Lafferty thought for over 10 minutes on this move. Asked later why he did not play the natural 8-11, he said both looked equally attractive and he found it hard to decide. In its pre-match preparation, Chinook had proved that 8-11 leads to a draw.","8":"White plays 27 to 24, and here Chinook leaves its book. Searching 21 moves deep, it switched from the planned 28-24 to 27-24 and judged itself clearly better. The engine also picks this move first.","9":"Black plays 16 to 20, going to the right edge to watch the white man on 24. The man on 20 will cramp White's right side for a long time.","10":"White plays 31 to 27, lifting a back-row man to guard behind 24. The 1994 Chinook searched deeper than the 1992 version, judged positions better, and had twelve times as much opening knowledge.","11":"Black plays 8 to 11. Schaeffer wrote that this move appears to be a mistake. Chinook had expected 9-13, and from here its assessment grew with every move. The man on 11 becomes a \"loose man,\" easy to attack from behind.","12":"White plays 19 to 16, stepping in front of 12 to offer a capture. Black must take 12x19. White then takes two back at once.","13":"Black plays 12x19, as required. White's recapture is ready.","14":"White plays 24x15x8, taking 19 and 11 in a double jump and landing on 8. The man on 8 will be taken straight back. But this exchange has thinned Black's upper right defence.","15":"Black recaptures 4x11, and material is level. But Black has had to use the back-row man on 4.","16":"White plays 28 to 24. White aims to push a man along 23-19-16-12-8-3 to make a king. Schaeffer wrote that unless Black stops it, a white king can then attack Black's men from behind at leisure.","17":"Black plays 9 to 13. Schaeffer calls it \"apparently forced.\" It lets the man on 18 capture, setting up a recapture.","18":"White plays 18x9, taking 14 and landing on 9. Capturing is compulsory.","19":"Black recaptures 5x14. Material is level, but Black's back left is growing thin too.","20":"White plays 22 to 18, coming into the centre again in front of 14.","21":"Black plays 14 to 17. Some annotators called this the losing move, but Chinook disagreed. If Chinook is right, the game was already lost at 8-11, Black's sixth move. Schaeffer wrote that a player as strong as Lafferty being lost in six moves showed the power of opening preparation.","22":"White plays 21x14, taking 17 and landing on 14. Capturing is compulsory.","23":"Black recaptures 10x17. Material is still level.","24":"White plays 24 to 19, advancing in the centre and teaming up with 23 to hold Black's middle.","25":"Black plays 6 to 10. Chinook had expected 1-5, but a deep search while Lafferty was thinking showed that 1-5 also lost. Every road is hard for Black now.","26":"White plays 19 to 15, stepping in front of 10 to force a capture. Black must take 10x19.","27":"Black plays 10x19, as required.","28":"White recaptures 23x16, landing on 16. Schaeffer wrote that it is obvious White is getting a king: the road 16, 12, 8, 3 is in sight.","29":"Black plays 17 to 22, fighting back by pushing into White's camp.","30":"White plays 26x17, as required.","31":"Black recaptures 13x22. A black man has also come close to White's back row.","32":"White plays 18 to 14. Here Chinook announced that it thought the game was won. The man on 14 is also heading for a king.","33":"Black plays 2 to 6, bringing out a back-row man to stop White's man on 14.","34":"White plays 16 to 12, two steps from a king. Black has no way to stop it.","35":"Black plays 11 to 16, placing a man beside White's path.","36":"White plays 12 to 8. Here Chinook announced that it had seen to the end of the game. From now on it follows a road that its databases know to be a win.","37":"Black plays 16 to 19, pushing forward to aim for a king too.","38":"White plays 8 to 3, reaching the back row and becoming a king. A king can move both forward and backward.","39":"Black plays 7 to 11, putting a man in front of the king to get in its way.","40":"White plays 30 to 26, offering a man to Black's 22. Black can take 22x31 and become a king.","41":"Black plays 22x31, capturing and crowning on 31. Now both sides have a king.","42":"White plays 3 to 7, bringing the king forward to target Black's men on 11 and 19.","43":"Black plays 31x24, taking 27 and landing on 24. Black's king enters White's right side.","44":"White plays 7x16x23, taking 11 and 19 in a double jump. Material is four against four. Lafferty said afterward that he played for this ending: he knew it was bad but thought it drawable. Chinook, though, had foreseen this position many moves earlier.","45":"Black plays 1 to 5. From here Chinook plays a string of moves, each the only one that keeps the win, all instantly.","46":"White plays 29 to 25, the only winning move, advancing the edge man toward 21 and 17.","47":"Black plays 24 to 27, moving the king to target White's men.","48":"White plays 23 to 18, again the only move: the king takes the centre and cuts Black's king off from its men.","49":"Black plays 27 to 31, pulling the king back.","50":"White plays 25 to 21. According to Schaeffer, 18-22 also won here. White's man edges closer to a king.","51":"Black plays 20 to 24, hurrying a man toward the king row.","52":"White plays 18 to 22, the only move, eyeing 26, where Black's king on 31 would come out.","53":"Black plays 24 to 28, one step from White's back row, but the white man on 32 blocks the way.","54":"White plays 22 to 18, the only move; the king returns to watch the centre again.","55":"Black plays 31 to 26, bringing the king out.","56":"White plays 21 to 17, the only move, marching toward another king.","57":"Black plays 26 to 30, pulling the king back.","58":"White plays 18 to 15, the only move; the king goes to hold Black's men from above.","59":"Black plays 30 to 25. Lafferty completed this move with only seconds left on his clock. Failing to make 30 moves in the hour meant an immediate forfeit.","60":"White plays 17 to 13, the only move. This man too is heading for the far row to crown.","61":"Black plays 25 to 22, bringing the king into the centre.","62":"White plays 14 to 10, the only move. The man on 10 nears the king row too.","63":"Black plays 22 to 18, and now Black's king can be captured. The engine also sees White's win clearly from here.","64":"White plays 15x22, capturing Black's king. The only move.","65":"Black plays 6x15, taking White's man on 10. Black's king for one white man: Black has lost his king and White keeps his.","66":"White plays 22 to 17, the only move; the king goes to stop Black's men.","67":"Black plays 15 to 18.","68":"White plays 17 to 14, the only move. With 13 and 14 side by side, White secures the road to a new king.","69":"The record ends with Black's 18-23, and here Lafferty resigned. Black's man on 28 is stuck behind White's man on 32, and White can make another king at once. Chinook kept its title, 1 win and 31 draws. That January Tinsley had said he wanted to play Chinook again in the summer to regain his title, but his illness returned and he died on 3 April 1995. In 2007 the team announced it had proved that checkers is a draw with perfect play by both sides."}}};
